export interface CombatNote { path: string; folder: string; title: string; body: string }
export const COMBAT_NOTES: CombatNote[] = [{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/50 50 Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"50 50 Guard","body":"[[Single-Leg X Guard]]\n[[Saddle]]\n[[Cross Ashi]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n50/50 Guard is a mirrored leg-entanglement position where both athletes have one leg threaded inside the other’s legs, creating shared access to control, off-balancing, rotation, and leg attacks.\n\nIt should be understood as a contested lower-body control position rather than a neutral stall position.\n\nThe defining feature is reciprocity.\n\nBoth athletes may have access to similar structures, so small differences in knee-line depth, hip angle, upper-body control, and secondary-leg management can determine who has the stronger position.\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control the opponent’s knee line more deeply than they control yours\n- win the battle for hip angle\n- control the secondary leg\n- limit the opponent’s ability to rotate freely\n- create asymmetry inside an otherwise mirrored position\n- expose the foot or heel without sacrificing positional safety\n- use off-balancing to create top-position or passing opportunities\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- heel exposure where ruleset permits\n- straight ankle lock\n- Achilles lock\n- sweep to top position\n- back exposure\n- transition to outside ashi\n- transition to saddle\n- secondary-leg attacks\n- disengagement into passing position\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- protect the knee line\n- deny heel exposure\n- prevent the opponent from controlling the secondary leg\n- maintain useful hip rotation\n- recover inside position when the opponent creates angle\n- clear the entanglement before committing to a vulnerable turn\n- avoid allowing the opponent to convert mirrored control into one-sided dominance\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- relative knee-line depth\n- hip angle\n- secondary-leg control\n- foot position\n- rotational freedom\n- upper-body connection\n- ability to create asymmetry\n- access to the opponent’s base\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- one athlete gaining deeper knee-line control\n- secondary-leg control breaking the symmetry of the position\n- one athlete exposing the heel while their own knee line remains vulnerable\n- off-balancing creating a clean top-position conversion\n- defensive rotation worsening leg exposure\n- athlete disengaging safely into a passing position\n- prolonged mirrored control without meaningful positional progression\n\nSynochi should recognise 50/50 as a position where symmetry must be broken.\n\nThe athlete who creates better knee-line control, hip angle, secondary-leg control, or upper-body connection usually gains the stronger attacking relationship."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Alignment Breaking.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Alignment Breaking","body":"[[Pinning Mechanics]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nA pin becomes stronger when the top athlete prevents the bottom athlete from maintaining useful skeletal alignment.\n\nKey forms of alignment breaking include:\n\n- turning the head away from the hips\n- separating shoulders from pelvis\n- rotating the spine\n- extending one side while compressing the other\n- preventing knee-elbow connection\n\nGood pinning does not only hold position.\n\nIt changes the opponent's body shape so that meaningful force production becomes harder."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Americana.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Americana","body":"## Type\nShoulder Lock / Bent Arm Submission\n\n## Purpose\nThe Americana is a bent-arm shoulder lock that isolates the opponent’s arm and applies rotational pressure through the shoulder while keeping the elbow controlled.\n\nIt commonly appears from dominant top positions where the attacker can pin the opponent’s upper body and separate the arm from the torso.\n\nThe submission depends on controlling:\n\n- wrist position\n- elbow position\n- shoulder rotation\n- torso movement\n- available escape direction\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe attacker bends the opponent’s arm and uses a figure-four grip to control the wrist and elbow.\n\nThe shoulder is then rotated while the opponent’s torso is prevented from moving freely with the pressure.\n\nThe submission becomes stronger when the elbow is kept close to the mat and the wrist remains controlled.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- opponent’s arm bent near ninety degrees\n- attacker controlling the wrist\n- secondary arm threaded underneath the opponent’s upper arm\n- figure-four grip established\n- opponent’s elbow pinned or closely controlled\n- torso movement restricted\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- pin the wrist\n- prevent the elbow from drifting\n- keep the opponent’s shoulder connected to the mat or controlled surface\n- maintain tight figure-four structure\n- separate the arm from the torso\n- restrict whole-body rotation\n\nThe submission becomes weaker when the opponent can move the elbow freely or turn the torso with the pressure.\n\n## Wrist and Elbow Relationship\nThe Americana is not simply a wrist movement.\n\nThe wrist and elbow must be controlled together.\n\nIf the wrist moves while the elbow remains free, the opponent can change the shoulder angle and reduce pressure.\n\nIf the elbow is controlled but the wrist escapes, the submission structure breaks.\n\nThe attacker should treat the arm as one connected lever.\n\n## Offensive Pathways\nThe Americana often appears from:\n\n- mount\n- side control\n- pressure passing transitions\n- upper-body pinning exchanges\n- reactions where the opponent frames with an exposed arm\n\nIt may also create reactions that open other attacks.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- reconnect the elbow to the torso\n- straighten the arm\n- turn onto the side\n- move the elbow away from the mat\n- control the attacker’s hands\n- bridge or rotate the torso\n- change the shoulder angle before the figure-four becomes stable\n\nEarly elbow recovery is usually easier than escaping once the shoulder is fully isolated.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Elbow not controlled\nThe attacker moves the wrist but allows the elbow to travel.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent rotates the shoulder and reduces pressure.\n\n### Arm remains too close to the torso\nThe attacker never creates enough separation.\n\nResult:\nthe defender retains structural strength.\n\n### Lifting instead of rotating\nThe attacker pulls the arm upward rather than creating shoulder rotation.\n\nResult:\nthe finish becomes inefficient.\n\n### Loose figure-four\nThe attacker’s grip structure is weak.\n\nResult:\nthe defender straightens or retracts the arm.\n\n### Ignoring torso movement\nThe opponent turns their whole body with the pressure.\n\nResult:\nshoulder tension decreases.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify an Americana when:\n\n- one athlete pins the opponent’s wrist near the mat\n- the opponent’s arm is bent\n- the attacker threads a second arm beneath the upper arm\n- a figure-four grip is established\n- the defender tries to straighten or retract the arm\n- the attacker rotates the bent arm while controlling the elbow\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the wrist is secured\n- whether the elbow remains controlled\n- whether the opponent can rotate the torso\n- whether the arm is sufficiently separated from the body\n- whether the attacker maintains a compact figure-four\n- whether the movement is rotational rather than simply upward\n- what defensive movement breaks the structure\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf the Americana repeatedly fails, inspect elbow control before applying more force.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the elbow actually pinned?\n- Is the wrist secure?\n- Can the opponent turn their torso?\n- Is the arm separated enough from the body?\n- Is the attacker creating rotation or merely lifting?\n\nThe submission should become mechanically tight before significant force is required.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Americana is a bent-arm shoulder-isolation system.\n\nThe attacker controls both ends of the lever while preventing the opponent’s torso from rotating freely with the applied pressure.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nisolate arm,\nsecure wrist,\ncontrol elbow,\nrestrict torso movement,\ncreate shoulder rotation,\nfinish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Anaconda Choke.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Anaconda Choke","body":"# Anaconda Choke\n\n## Type\nFront Headlock Submission / Arm and Neck Compression Choke\n\n## Purpose\nThe Anaconda Choke is a front headlock submission that traps the opponent’s head and one arm inside a connected arm structure.\n\nThe choke works by compressing the neck while also controlling the trapped shoulder, usually after the opponent’s posture has already been broken.\n\nIt commonly appears from:\n\n- front headlock\n- turtle\n- sprawls\n- failed takedowns\n- scrambles\n- snapdowns\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe attacker threads one arm underneath the opponent’s neck and through the space around the trapped arm, then connects the hands or arms into a locking structure.\n\nThe finish depends on combining:\n\n- head control\n- trapped shoulder compression\n- angle\n- chest pressure\n- rotational control\n- reduction of available space\n\nThe opponent’s own shoulder contributes to the choking structure.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- the opponent’s head and one arm trapped together\n- the attacker’s arm threaded deeply beneath the neck\n- the choking structure wrapping around both neck and shoulder\n- the opponent’s posture collapsed\n- the attacker positioned at an angle rather than directly square\n- rotational movement used to tighten the structure\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- keep the opponent’s head low\n- trap the shoulder deeply\n- eliminate space around the neck\n- maintain a compact arm connection\n- prevent the opponent from turning toward open space\n- use body position to reinforce the choke\n\nThe submission becomes weaker if the opponent can separate the head and trapped shoulder.\n\n## Rotational Component\nThe Anaconda often becomes stronger when the attacker rotates or moves around the opponent.\n\nThis movement can:\n\n- tighten the trapped shoulder\n- reduce neck space\n- collapse posture further\n- prevent the defender from facing the attacker\n- improve finishing angle\n\nThe rotation should reinforce control rather than loosen the lock.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- create space around the neck\n- pull the trapped arm free\n- raise posture\n- turn toward the attacker\n- prevent rotational tightening\n- control the attacker’s locking hands\n- widen the shoulder and neck relationship\n\nEarly defence usually focuses on preventing the head and arm from being fully compressed together.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Shallow threading arm\nThe attacker’s arm does not travel deeply enough beneath the neck.\n\nResult:\nthe choke remains loose.\n\n### Trapped shoulder not controlled\nThe opponent’s shoulder remains mobile.\n\nResult:\nspace remains inside the choking structure.\n\n### Staying square\nThe attacker remains directly in front without improving angle.\n\nResult:\ncompression is weaker.\n\n### Loose arm connection\nThe locking structure opens during movement.\n\nResult:\nthe defender regains space.\n\n### Rolling without control\nThe attacker rotates before the head and shoulder are secured.\n\nResult:\nthe defender follows the motion and escapes.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify an Anaconda Choke when:\n\n- the opponent’s head and one arm are trapped together\n- the attacker threads an arm underneath the neck\n- the attacker connects the arms around the neck and shoulder\n- the defender is in turtle, front headlock, or a collapsed wrestling posture\n- the attacker rotates or circles to tighten the choke\n- the defender attempts to pull the trapped arm free or create neck space\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the threading arm is deep enough\n- whether the trapped shoulder is compressed\n- whether the opponent can regain posture\n- whether the attacker maintains angle\n- whether rotation improves or weakens the structure\n- whether the defender can turn into open space\n- at what moment the choke becomes structurally tight\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf an Anaconda repeatedly fails, inspect the head-and-shoulder connection before squeezing harder.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the opponent’s posture truly broken?\n- Is the trapped shoulder compressed?\n- Is the threading arm deep enough?\n- Is the attacker creating angle?\n- Is there still space inside the lock?\n\nThe submission should become compact before maximal pressure is applied.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Anaconda is a head-and-shoulder compression system.\n\nThe attacker combines cervical control with trapped-shoulder positioning to reduce available space and movement.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nbreak posture,\ntrap head and arm,\nthread deeply,\ncompress shoulder,\ncreate angle,\ntighten,\nfinish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Armbar.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Armbar","body":"# Armbar Position\n\n## Type\nSubmission Control Position / Arm Isolation System\n\n## Purpose\nThe armbar position is a control structure used to isolate an opponent’s arm while controlling the shoulder line and limiting their ability to rotate, posture, or reconnect the elbow to the body.\n\nThe finishing mechanism is elbow extension, but the position itself is broader than the submission.\n\nA strong armbar position controls:\n\n- shoulder orientation\n- elbow position\n- opponent posture\n- rotational escape\n- distance between the arm and torso\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- the opponent’s arm isolated between the attacker’s legs\n- the attacker’s hips positioned close to the opponent’s shoulder\n- the knees controlling the opponent’s upper body\n- the elbow line separated from the torso\n- the attacker controlling the wrist or forearm\n- the opponent’s shoulder rotation restricted\n\nThe exact leg configuration may differ depending on whether the armbar is entered from mount, guard, back control, or a scramble.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe armbar works by separating the elbow from the opponent’s defensive structure.\n\nThe attacker attempts to create a lever around the shoulder and elbow while removing the opponent’s ability to rotate with the pressure.\n\nThe key relationship is not simply wrist control.\n\nThe attacker must control the larger chain:\n\nshoulder,\nelbow,\nwrist.\n\nIf the shoulder is free to rotate, the opponent often gains access to escape routes even when the arm remains trapped.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- keep the elbow above or across the hip line\n- control the opponent’s shoulder\n- keep the knees connected\n- prevent the opponent turning into the legs\n- prevent the elbow slipping back toward the torso\n- maintain close hip connection\n- control the opponent’s hands when defensive grips are present\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defending athlete commonly attempts to:\n\n- reconnect the elbow to the body\n- rotate the shoulder\n- turn toward the attacker\n- stack the attacker\n- clear one of the controlling legs\n- connect the hands\n- move the elbow below the attacker’s hip line\n- create enough space to withdraw the arm\n\nThe defence often begins before the arm is fully extended.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Loose knees\nThe attacker allows excessive space around the opponent’s shoulder.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent rotates and begins escaping.\n\n### Hips too far from the shoulder\nThe attacker controls the arm but lacks close body connection.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent can move the elbow and recover alignment.\n\n### Pulling only on the wrist\nThe attacker focuses on extending the arm without controlling the shoulder.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent rotates with the pressure.\n\n### Crossing the feet unnecessarily\nThis can weaken directional control in some armbar configurations.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent gains additional rotational space.\n\n### Losing the elbow line\nThe opponent pulls the elbow past the attacker’s controlling hips.\n\nResult:\nthe submission structure collapses.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify an armbar position when:\n\n- one athlete has isolated an opponent’s arm between the legs\n- the attacker’s hips are positioned near the opponent’s shoulder\n- the defender is connecting the hands\n- the attacker is separating the opponent’s defensive grip\n- the defender is stacking, turning, or pulling the elbow free\n- the exchange centres around control of the elbow line\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the elbow remains controlled\n- whether the attacker’s hips stay close to the shoulder\n- whether the defender can rotate\n- whether the attacker’s knees maintain upper-body control\n- how the defender is connecting their hands\n- whether the attacker is controlling the shoulder or only the wrist\n- what moment causes the elbow line to be lost or secured\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nWhen an armbar fails, the finishing mechanics may not be the primary problem.\n\nInspect:\n\n- shoulder control\n- elbow line\n- knee connection\n- rotational control\n- hip distance\n- defensive hand fighting\n\nA mechanically strong finish usually emerges from a stable positional structure.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe armbar is an isolation problem before it is an extension problem.\n\nThe attacker progressively disconnects the arm from the opponent’s torso, controls the shoulder’s ability to rotate, and preserves the elbow inside the attacking structure.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nisolate,\nseparate,\ncontrol rotation,\nsecure elbow line,\nfinish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Back Control.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Back Control","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nBack control is a dominant control position where the attacker is connected behind the opponent’s shoulders and hips, limiting their ability to face the attacker while creating sustained access to the neck and upper body.\n\nBack control should be understood as a control system rather than simply the presence of hooks.\n\nCommon configurations include:\n\n- double hooks\n- body triangle\n- one hook with upper-body control\n- seated back control\n- rear body lock during transition\n- back control during turtle breakdown\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- maintain chest-to-back connection\n- control shoulder rotation\n- control the opponent’s ability to turn toward the attacker\n- manage the relationship between upper-body control and hip control\n- isolate defensive hands\n- progressively expose the neck\n- prevent the opponent from sliding below the controlling line\n\nThe attacker should seek to create a narrowing defensive environment.\n\nControl reduces the defender’s available reactions.\n\nEach defensive reaction should expose another control or attacking pathway.\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- rear naked choke\n- short choke\n- arm trap\n- hand fighting sequences\n- body triangle pressure\n- transition to mount\n- return to turtle control\n- crucifix transitions\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- protect the neck\n- win hand-fighting exchanges\n- move the head and shoulders toward the safe side\n- bring the back toward the floor where possible\n- clear controlling hooks or leg configuration\n- recover hip rotation\n- turn to face the attacker\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- chest-to-back connection\n- head position\n- shoulder line control\n- hip alignment\n- hook or body-triangle control\n- defensive hand availability\n- attacker’s ability to follow rotation\n- defender’s ability to slide below or turn inside\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- attacker losing chest-to-back connection while chasing the neck\n- defender winning inside hand position\n- head moving to the safe side\n- body triangle or hooks restricting hip rotation\n- attacker successfully trapping a defensive arm\n- defender beginning to place shoulders on the mat\n- repeated defensive reactions being converted into new attacking opportunities\n\nSynochi should recognise back control as a reaction-driven control system.\n\nThe strongest back attacks progressively remove defensive options rather than relying on immediate submission attempts."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Backside 50 50.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Backside 50 50","body":"[[50 50 Guard]]\n[[K Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nBackside 50 50 is a leg entanglement reached when the attacker controls the opponent’s leg from behind the line of the hips, usually after creating an angle that prevents the opponent from easily squaring back toward them.\n\nIt should be understood as a control and rotational dominance position rather than simply a heel hook entry.\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control the opponent’s knee line\n- stay behind the opponent’s hip line\n- prevent the opponent from turning back to square\n- control the secondary leg where possible\n- manage distance between the hips\n- expose the heel without sacrificing positional control\n- use the opponent’s defensive rotation to maintain or improve the entanglement\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- heel exposure where ruleset permits\n- transition to 50 50 Guard\n- transition to Saddle\n- secondary leg attacks\n- sweep to top position\n- back exposure\n- disengagement into passing position after control is established\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- recover the knee line\n- turn the hips back toward the attacker\n- clear secondary leg control\n- separate the attacker’s legs and hip connection\n- deny heel exposure\n- rebuild base before attempting full extraction\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- position relative to the opponent’s hip line\n- knee line depth\n- hip angle\n- secondary leg control\n- rotational freedom\n- heel exposure\n- ability to prevent the opponent from squaring\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- attacker clearly positioned behind the opponent’s hip line\n- opponent unable to rotate back toward square\n- knee line deeply controlled before heel exposure develops\n- secondary leg being used to limit defensive movement\n- opponent recovering square hip alignment and weakening the position\n- attacker maintaining control while following defensive rotation\n\nSynochi should recognise Backside 50 50 as a position where angle creates control.\n\nIts value comes from remaining behind the opponent’s hip line, limiting their ability to square up, and using that positional advantage to create leg attacks, sweeps, or further transitions."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Body Lock Passing.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Body Lock Passing","body":"# Body Lock Passing\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Half Guard]]\n[[Side Control]]\n[[Mount]]\n\n## Type\nPressure Passing System / Upper Body Connection Pass\n\n## Purpose\nBody lock passing is a guard passing system built around controlling the opponent’s torso and hips with a tight upper-body connection while progressively removing their ability to retain guard with the legs.\n\nThe defining feature is sustained connection.\n\nRather than creating distance to move around the guard, the passer closes distance and prevents the bottom athlete from freely rotating, framing, or reconfiguring their hips.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- chest-to-chest or chest-to-upper-torso connection\n- arms locked around the opponent’s torso, waist, or hips\n- head position used to reinforce directional control\n- hips kept close enough to prevent easy re-guarding\n- progressive clearing of hooks and knee lines\n- pressure applied while the passer advances incrementally\n\nThe exact hand configuration can vary.\n\nThe key is that the passer maintains a closed connection around the opponent’s centre while dismantling the lower-body guard.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nBody lock passing works by controlling the opponent’s ability to separate upper-body and lower-body movement.\n\nThe passer attempts to make the opponent’s torso and hips behave as one constrained unit.\n\nThis reduces:\n\n- hip mobility\n- rotational freedom\n- space for frames\n- ability to insert hooks\n- ability to create strong angle changes\n\nThe passer then clears the legs while preserving torso control.\n\n## Entry Conditions\nCommon entries include:\n\n- seated guard\n- butterfly guard\n- half guard\n- failed wrestling exchanges\n- transitions from front headlock or snapdown situations\n- reactions to underhook battles\n- pressure from standing into seated opponents\n\nBody lock passing is especially effective when the bottom athlete allows close upper-body access without first establishing strong frames or inside controls.\n\n## Head Position\nHead position is central to the pass.\n\nThe passer may use the head to:\n\n- block the opponent from turning\n- reinforce chest pressure\n- deny posture\n- prevent upper-body pummelling\n- steer the opponent’s shoulders away from recovery\n\nPoor head position often allows the bottom athlete to turn, frame, or create an angle.\n\n## Lower Body Progression\nThe legs are usually cleared progressively rather than in one movement.\n\nCommon tasks include:\n\n- flattening or redirecting butterfly hooks\n- stepping over or around knees\n- freeing a trapped foot\n- collapsing knee lines\n- forcing half guard\n- clearing the final leg into [[Side Control]] or [[Mount]]\n\nThe passer should avoid rushing leg clearance if it compromises the body lock.\n\n## Bottom Player Defensive Priorities\nThe bottom athlete generally wants to break connection before the passer settles.\n\nUseful priorities include:\n\n- establishing frames early\n- creating shoulder rotation\n- recovering inside position\n- pummelling for underhooks\n- keeping knees between the bodies\n- using butterfly hooks to elevate or redirect\n- turning onto a useful angle rather than accepting flat posture\n- separating the passer’s hands when possible\n\nOnce the body lock becomes stable, recovery becomes significantly harder.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Loose upper-body connection\nThe passer has an apparent body lock but leaves enough space for the opponent to rotate.\n\nResult:\nthe bottom athlete recovers frames or inserts hooks.\n\n### Hips too far away\nThe arms remain connected but the passer’s hips drift back.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent regains leg mobility.\n\n### Head on the wrong side\nThe passer allows the opponent to turn toward their strongest recovery direction.\n\nResult:\nunderhooks, frames, or guard recovery become available.\n\n### Clearing legs too aggressively\nThe passer prioritises stepping past the legs over maintaining torso control.\n\nResult:\nconnection breaks and the guard resets.\n\n### Remaining static\nThe passer achieves a strong lock but does not progressively remove hooks or knee position.\n\nResult:\nthe position stalls and the bottom athlete can begin rebuilding structure.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify body lock passing when:\n\n- the top athlete’s arms are locked tightly around the torso or hips\n- the passer maintains chest connection while moving around the legs\n- the bottom athlete is attempting to frame, pummel, or recover knees inside\n- the passer incrementally clears butterfly hooks or half guard\n- the exchange remains close-range throughout\n- the pass completes into chest-to-chest top control\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the lock is genuinely tight\n- where the passer’s head is positioned\n- whether the bottom athlete can rotate\n- whether the passer’s hips stay connected\n- how the hooks are being removed\n- whether the passer is forcing half guard or clearing directly\n- whether hand position changes as the pass progresses\n- what exact moment the bottom athlete loses meaningful guard retention\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf body lock passing repeatedly fails, do not assume the issue is leg clearing.\n\nFirst inspect:\n\n- torso connection\n- head position\n- hip distance\n- ability to stop rotation\n- whether the bottom athlete has recovered inside frames\n\nA weak body lock creates a difficult leg-clearing problem.\n\nA strong body lock simplifies it.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nBody lock passing is a connection-dominant control system.\n\nThe passer reduces the opponent’s independent movement options by binding torso and hips into a constrained structure.\n\nThe lower-body pass then becomes a process of progressively removing residual guard components while preserving that central connection.\n\nThe strategic priority is therefore:\n\nconnection first,\nclearance second."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Butterfly Ashi.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Butterfly Ashi","body":"[[Single-Leg X Guard]]\n[[Butterfly Guard]]\n[[Saddle]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nButterfly Ashi is a leg-entanglement configuration where the attacker controls one of the opponent’s legs while using a butterfly-style inside hook to control the far hip or thigh and influence the opponent’s ability to rotate, stand, or withdraw.\n\nIt should be understood as a control, off-balancing, and transitional leg-entanglement position rather than simply a variation of Single-Leg X.\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control the opponent’s knee line\n- maintain strong hip-to-leg connection\n- use the butterfly hook to influence the opponent’s far hip\n- restrict effective rotation\n- disrupt the opponent’s base\n- control distance between the hips\n- expose the primary leg while limiting support from the secondary leg\n- create predictable reactions that lead into finishing or positional transitions\n\nThe butterfly hook provides additional control over the opponent’s overall lower-body structure.\n\nRather than controlling only the isolated leg, the attacker can use the far-side connection to influence how the opponent’s hips rotate and where their weight can move.\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- straight ankle lock\n- Achilles lock\n- heel exposure where ruleset permits\n- sweep to top position\n- transition to Single-Leg X\n- transition to X Guard\n- transition to Saddle\n- transition to Cross Ashi\n- secondary-leg attacks\n- back exposure when the opponent rotates defensively\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- recover the controlled knee line\n- remove or neutralise the butterfly hook\n- regain freedom of hip rotation\n- separate the attacker’s knees and hip connection\n- recover the secondary leg as a usable post\n- rebuild base before attempting full disengagement\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- knee-line depth\n- butterfly-hook placement\n- far-hip control\n- hip connection\n- orientation of the controlled knee\n- secondary-leg availability\n- rotational freedom\n- distance between the athletes’ hips\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- attacker controlling the primary leg while the butterfly hook disrupts the far hip\n- opponent unable to freely rotate because both sides of the lower body are being influenced\n- butterfly hook being lost and the position degrading toward weaker Single-Leg X control\n- defensive weight shift creating elevation or sweeping opportunity\n- attacker chasing the foot while losing control of the knee line\n- opponent clearing the hook before beginning leg extraction\n- transition into Saddle or another ashi configuration as the opponent rotates\n\nSynochi should recognise Butterfly Ashi as a two-sided lower-body control configuration.\n\nIts value comes from combining primary-leg isolation with influence over the opponent’s opposite hip and base, allowing off-balancing, rotational control, and leg-attack transitions to operate together."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Butterfly Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Butterfly Guard","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nButterfly guard is a seated or semi-seated guard where the bottom athlete uses one or both insteps as inside hooks against the opponent’s legs while controlling upper-body connection and balance.\n\nButterfly guard should be understood as an elevation, off-balancing, and connection system rather than simply a guard with hooks inserted.\n\nPrimary bottom-player objectives include:\n\n- establish useful upper-body connection\n- keep inside position with the legs\n- control distance\n- prevent the opponent from flattening the hips\n- create kuzushi before attempting elevation\n- connect upper-body control to lower-body hooks\n- force the opponent to post\n- expose pathways to sweeps, wrestle-ups, or leg entanglements\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- butterfly sweep\n- shoulder crunch sweep\n- arm drag\n- wrestle-up\n- single-leg entry\n- SLX entry\n- X-guard entry\n- saddle entry\n- back exposure\n- front headlock transitions\n\nPrimary top-player objectives include:\n\n- deny strong inside hooks\n- control the bottom athlete’s head and shoulders\n- flatten or extend the seated posture\n- keep hips outside effective elevation range\n- prevent meaningful upper-body attachment\n- force the bottom athlete onto a weaker angle\n- clear hooks and begin passing\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- inside leg position\n- hip proximity\n- upper-body connection\n- head position\n- angle of the spine\n- ability to create elevation\n- opponent’s available posts\n- direction of off-balance\n- connection between hook and upper-body control\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- hooks present without meaningful upper-body connection\n- athlete attempting to elevate before creating kuzushi\n- top player successfully flattening seated posture\n- opponent forced to post before the sweep\n- arm drag creating back exposure\n- failed elevation converting into wrestle-up or leg entry\n- butterfly hook being used to redirect rather than simply lift\n\nSynochi should recognise butterfly guard as a reaction-driven off-balancing system.\n\nIts effectiveness comes from combining inside position, upper-body connection, and directional kuzushi so that elevation or transition occurs after the opponent’s base has already been compromised."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Butterfly Half Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Butterfly Half Guard","body":"[[Half Guard]]\n[[Butterfly Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nButterfly half guard is a hybrid guard where the bottom athlete controls one of the opponent’s legs in a half-guard relationship while using an inside butterfly hook to create elevation, off-balancing, and directional movement.\n\nIt should be understood as a transitional and attacking guard rather than simply half guard with a hook inserted.\n\nPrimary bottom-player objectives include:\n\n- maintain inside position with the butterfly hook\n- avoid being flattened\n- preserve hip mobility\n- create upper-body connection\n- control the opponent’s trapped leg\n- use elevation to disrupt base\n- build height when the opponent reacts\n- create pathways to sweeps, wrestle-ups, back exposure, or leg entanglements\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- butterfly sweep\n- underhook wrestle-up\n- dogfight\n- back exposure\n- SLX entry\n- X-guard entry\n- saddle entry\n- knee-tap style reversals\n- transition to full butterfly guard\n\nPrimary top-player objectives include:\n\n- flatten the bottom athlete\n- clear or neutralise the butterfly hook\n- control head and shoulder position\n- win the underhook battle\n- prevent the bottom athlete from building height\n- free the trapped knee line\n- transition into passing pressure\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- butterfly hook depth\n- hip angle\n- underhook position\n- trapped-leg control\n- upper-body connection\n- head position\n- ability to elevate\n- ability to build onto elbow or hand\n- opponent’s available posts\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- butterfly hook present but disconnected from upper-body control\n- bottom athlete being flattened before elevation becomes available\n- opponent’s weight loaded onto the hook\n- successful elevation forcing a post\n- underhook converting elevation into a wrestle-up\n- top player clearing the hook and collapsing the hips\n- transition from butterfly half into SLX, X-guard, or dogfight\n\nSynochi should recognise butterfly half guard as a hybrid control system combining half-guard leg entanglement with butterfly-style elevation and off-balancing.\n\nIts effectiveness depends on maintaining hip mobility and using the hook to create reactions rather than treating the hook as a static frame."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Closed Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Closed Guard","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nClosed guard is a guard configuration where the bottom athlete controls the opponent with the legs closed around the torso while maintaining the hips high enough to meaningfully control the opponent’s pelvis and posture.\n\nThe position should not be understood as simply wrapping the legs around the opponent’s body.\n\nA strong closed guard creates a relationship where the bottom athlete’s hips are positioned high relative to the opponent’s hips, giving the bottom player greater ability to control posture, create angles, and attack the upper body.\n\nPrimary bottom-player objectives include:\n\n- control posture\n- maintain strong hip connection\n- keep the hips high relative to the opponent’s pelvis\n- limit the opponent’s ability to freely stand or disengage\n- create upper-body attachment\n- break alignment between head, shoulders, and hips\n- create angles\n- isolate arms\n- expose the neck\n- force predictable defensive reactions\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- triangle\n- armbar\n- omoplata\n- kimura\n- guillotine\n- hip-bump sweep\n- pendulum-style sweep\n- back take\n- transition to open guard when the opponent stands or creates distance\n\nPrimary top-player objectives include:\n\n- recover posture\n- lower and control the bottom athlete’s hips\n- maintain elbow position\n- prevent angle creation\n- control the pelvis\n- deny dominant grips and head control\n- create a safe opening sequence\n- stand or open the guard without exposing limbs or balance\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- relative hip height\n- posture control\n- pelvic connection\n- hip angle\n- head position\n- elbow position\n- shoulder alignment\n- distance between chest and hips\n- ability to create asymmetric control\n- top player’s base during opening attempts\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom player’s hips sitting too low to meaningfully control posture\n- top player successfully driving the bottom athlete’s hips down\n- bottom player climbing the guard higher to increase pelvic and upper-body control\n- angle creation preceding triangle or armbar threats\n- arm isolation causing defensive reactions\n- failed guard opening due to poor posture\n- bottom player losing control as the top player stands\n- guard opening creating immediate transition to another guard rather than neutral separation\n\nSynochi should recognise closed guard as a hip-dominant control and attacking system.\n\nIts effectiveness depends heavily on the bottom athlete’s ability to keep the hips high, control the opponent’s pelvis and posture, and use that connection to create angles and isolate limbs."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Connection Before Separation.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Connection Before Separation","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nSome escape attempts fail because the athlete tries to create distance before establishing useful connection.\n\nUseful connections may include:\n\n- frame against the opponent's shoulder\n- grip on a controlling limb\n- foot connected to the floor\n- knee connected to elbow\n- shoulder connected to the mat\n\nConnection provides structure from which separation can be created.\n\nWithout connection, movement may become loose, reactive, and mechanically weak.\n\nSynochi should identify whether the athlete has built a usable structure before attempting to generate space."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Constraint Removal Sequencing.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Constraint Removal Sequencing","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nSuccessful escapes often occur through sequential constraint removal.\n\nThe athlete should identify which controlling mechanism is preventing the next useful movement.\n\nExample sequence:\n\nhead control removed\n→ shoulder rotation recovered\n→ elbow returns inside\n→ hip can rotate\n→ knee enters\n→ guard is recovered\n\nThe athlete does not escape the entire position at once.\n\nThey remove enough constraints for the next layer of movement to become available.\n\nSynochi should model escapes as progressive constraint reduction rather than isolated techniques."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Cross Ashi.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Cross Ashi","body":"[[Saddle]]\n[[Outside Ashi]]\n[[Single-Leg X Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nCross Ashi is a leg-entanglement configuration where the attacker controls one of the opponent’s legs across the centerline while using the legs and hips to restrict knee-line escape, rotation, and access to base.\n\nIt should be understood as a control configuration that links positional dominance, secondary-leg management, and finishing access.\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- maintain deep control of the opponent’s knee line\n- keep strong hip-to-leg connection\n- prevent the opponent from freely turning the knee out\n- control or monitor the secondary leg\n- separate the controlled leg from the opponent’s upper-body base\n- use the opponent’s reactions to expose the foot, heel, or transition pathway\n- preserve positional control before escalating to a finish\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- straight ankle lock\n- Achilles lock\n- inside heel exposure where ruleset permits\n- transition to Saddle\n- transition to Outside Ashi\n- secondary-leg control\n- sweep to top position\n- back exposure from defensive rotation\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- recover the knee line\n- remove secondary-leg control\n- regain rotational freedom\n- separate the attacker’s hips from the controlled leg\n- turn the knee toward a safer orientation\n- rebuild base before attempting full extraction\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- knee-line depth\n- hip connection\n- orientation of the controlled knee\n- control of the secondary leg\n- rotational freedom\n- ability to follow defensive turning\n- distance between the opponent’s hips and available base\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- attacker controlling the foot while losing the knee line\n- strong secondary-leg control preventing escape\n- opponent successfully rotating the knee free\n- defensive turning increasing heel exposure\n- attacker transitioning between Cross Ashi and Saddle as the opponent changes orientation\n- opponent rebuilding base after separating hip connection\n\nSynochi should recognise Cross Ashi as a control-first leg-entanglement position.\n\nIts effectiveness depends on maintaining knee-line control, limiting rotation, and controlling the opponent’s supporting structure before prioritising the finish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Cross-Body Control.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Cross-Body Control","body":"[[Pinning Mechanics]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nCross-body control links pressure across different regions of the opponent's body.\n\nExamples include controlling:\n\n- head and far hip\n- near shoulder and far knee\n- upper torso while blocking opposite pelvic rotation\n\nThis limits whole-body rotation.\n\nStrong pins often work because the top athlete controls more than one rotational segment at once."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Crucifix.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Crucifix","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nThe crucifix is a control configuration where the attacker isolates one of the opponent’s arms with the legs while controlling the upper body and head.\n\nIt commonly appears from:\n\n- turtle\n- back exposure\n- front headlock exchanges\n- failed wrestling transitions\n- defensive arm posts\n\nThe key strategic feature of the crucifix is asymmetric limb control.\n\nOne arm is removed from meaningful defensive use while the attacker retains access to the neck, opposite arm, and rotational control of the torso.\n\nPrimary control objectives include:\n\n- isolating the trapped arm\n- controlling head and shoulder alignment\n- limiting the opponent’s ability to turn toward the attacker\n- preventing the opponent from rebuilding base\n- maintaining chest and hip connection during rotation\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- rear naked choke variations\n- short choke\n- armbar\n- wrist control into secondary attacks\n- transitions to back control\n- transitions to seated or side crucifix configurations\n\nCommon defensive priorities include:\n\n- protecting the neck\n- freeing the trapped arm\n- turning toward the attacker where mechanically available\n- rebuilding base\n- preventing further rotational collapse\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- arm successfully isolated by the legs\n- head controlled away from defensive turning\n- opponent unable to post with the trapped side\n- attacker maintaining connection during rotation\n- defender exposing neck while attempting to recover the arm\n\nSynochi should recognise the crucifix as both a submission platform and a control system rather than treating it as a single finishing position."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/D'Arce Choke.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"D'Arce Choke","body":"## Type\nFront Headlock Submission / Arm and Neck Compression Choke\n\n## Purpose\nThe D'Arce Choke is a head-and-arm choke that traps the opponent’s neck and one arm inside a connected upper-body structure.\n\nIt commonly appears from:\n\n- front headlock exchanges\n- turtle\n- half guard\n- sprawls\n- scrambles\n- failed takedowns\n- top pressure positions\n\nThe choke becomes effective when the attacker combines deep arm penetration with control of the opponent’s shoulder and posture.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe attacker threads an arm deeply underneath the opponent’s near-side arm and across the neck, then connects the hands or arms to create compression.\n\nThe finish depends on combining:\n\n- neck control\n- trapped shoulder position\n- deep arm penetration\n- torso angle\n- chest pressure\n- reduced rotational freedom\n\nThe opponent’s own shoulder helps complete the choking structure.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- the opponent’s head and one arm trapped together\n- the attacker’s arm threaded deeply through the armpit and across the neck\n- the attacker positioned at an angle beside the opponent\n- the defender’s posture collapsed or constrained\n- the trapped shoulder compressed inward\n- the choking structure reinforced by the attacker’s chest and upper body\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- thread the choking arm deeply\n- keep the opponent’s head low\n- compress the trapped shoulder\n- prevent the opponent from turning toward open space\n- keep the upper-body connection compact\n- improve angle before applying maximal pressure\n\nA shallow D'Arce often feels close but lacks enough control to finish reliably.\n\n## Depth of the Choking Arm\nArm depth is one of the most important variables.\n\nIf the arm is too shallow:\n\n- the grip becomes weak\n- neck compression decreases\n- the opponent can create space\n- finishing pressure becomes strength-dependent\n\nThe attacker should seek enough depth that the choking structure remains compact without excessive pulling.\n\n## Angle and Chest Pressure\nThe D'Arce becomes stronger when the attacker uses body position rather than arm strength alone.\n\nUseful adjustments may include:\n\n- moving the torso toward the opponent’s head\n- compressing the trapped shoulder\n- changing hip angle\n- using chest pressure to reduce space\n- preventing the defender from rotating underneath\n\nThe choke should tighten through positioning, not just squeezing.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- posture up\n- pull the trapped arm free\n- create space around the neck\n- turn toward the attacker\n- widen the shoulder\n- control the attacker’s locking hands\n- move the head toward open space\n\nEarly defensive structure is usually more effective than trying to escape after the shoulder and neck have been fully compressed.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Choking arm too shallow\nThe attacker cannot create enough compression.\n\nResult:\nthe defender survives or escapes.\n\n### Trapped shoulder remains mobile\nThe defender can separate the shoulder from the neck.\n\nResult:\nspace remains inside the choke.\n\n### Poor angle\nThe attacker stays badly aligned with the opponent’s torso.\n\nResult:\npressure is distributed inefficiently.\n\n### Pulling with the arms\nThe attacker attempts to finish by squeezing harder.\n\nResult:\nthe position becomes tiring without becoming tighter.\n\n### Allowing rotation\nThe defender turns toward a safer alignment.\n\nResult:\nthe choking structure opens.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a D'Arce Choke when:\n\n- one athlete threads an arm underneath the opponent’s armpit and across the neck\n- the opponent’s head and one arm are trapped together\n- the attacker connects the arms around the neck and shoulder\n- the defender is in turtle, half guard, or a collapsed wrestling position\n- the attacker adjusts torso angle to increase compression\n- the defender attempts to pull the trapped arm free or posture up\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- how deep the choking arm is\n- whether the trapped shoulder is compressed\n- whether the defender can posture\n- whether the attacker creates useful angle\n- whether chest pressure contributes to the choke\n- whether the defender can rotate into open space\n- what exact adjustment creates or loses compression\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a D'Arce repeatedly fails, inspect depth and shoulder control before grip strength.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the choking arm deep enough?\n- Is the trapped shoulder actually compressed?\n- Can the opponent posture?\n- Is the attacker creating angle?\n- Is the torso helping the choke or are the arms doing everything?\n\nA strong D'Arce should become structurally tight before maximal squeezing is required.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe D'Arce is a head-and-shoulder compression system built around deep upper-body penetration.\n\nThe attacker uses the trapped arm and shoulder to reduce space around the neck while controlling posture and rotation.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\ncollapse posture,\nthread deeply,\ntrap shoulder,\ncontrol rotation,\ncreate angle,\ncompress,\nfinish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/De La Riva Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"De La Riva Guard","body":"[[Supine Open Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nDe La Riva Guard is an open guard configuration built around controlling the outside of one of the opponent’s legs while using the legs and grips to manage distance, disrupt stance, and create rotational access around the opponent’s hips.\n\nIt should be understood as a position for angle creation, stance disruption, and transition rather than simply a hook placed around the leg.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- establish meaningful control of the near leg\n- use the outside hook to influence hip and knee direction\n- maintain control of distance\n- prevent the opponent from freely squaring the hips\n- create off balance through push pull relationships\n- access the far leg, upper body, or backside\n- transition into sweeps, wrestle ups, back takes, or other guards\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- technical stand up sweep\n- tripod style sweep\n- wrestle up\n- single leg\n- back exposure\n- berimbolo style rotation where appropriate\n- transition to X Guard\n- transition to Single Leg X Guard\n- transition to K Guard\n- transition to 50 50 Guard\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- remove or neutralise the De La Riva hook\n- control the bottom athlete’s free leg\n- square the hips\n- prevent the bottom athlete from controlling the far leg\n- close distance without allowing underneath access\n- redirect into a passing angle\n- prevent rotational access around the hip\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- depth and direction of the De La Riva hook\n- control of the near leg\n- far leg access\n- hip angle\n- distance\n- upper body grip or frame connection\n- opponent stance width\n- ability to prevent the hips from squaring\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- hook present without meaningful control of the opponent’s stance\n- opponent freely turning the knee and clearing the hook\n- bottom athlete using the hook to create rotational off balance\n- far leg control creating a sweep or backside entry\n- opponent stepping close enough to expose X Guard or Single Leg X Guard\n- transition from De La Riva into K Guard or 50 50 Guard as the opponent turns\n- top player successfully controlling the free leg before beginning the pass\n\nSynochi should recognise De La Riva Guard as an angle and stance control position.\n\nIts value comes from using outside leg control to prevent the opponent from remaining square and stable, then exploiting the resulting rotation to access sweeps, wrestle ups, backside positions, or other guard systems."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Deep Half Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Deep Half Guard","body":"[[Half Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nDeep Half Guard is a bottom position where the athlete moves underneath the opponent’s center of mass and controls one leg while positioning the hips beneath or beside the opponent’s hips to create off balancing, rotational access, and pathways to come on top.\n\nIt should be understood as an underneath guard and sweep system rather than simply a variation of Half Guard.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- get underneath the opponent’s hips\n- control the trapped leg\n- prevent the opponent from freely turning the knee outward\n- stay connected to the opponent’s center of mass\n- manage upper body pressure\n- create rotational imbalance\n- expose pathways to come on top\n- avoid being flattened before the underneath position is established\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- waiter sweep\n- rotational sweep\n- back door style recovery\n- technical stand up\n- transition to X Guard\n- transition to Single Leg X Guard\n- transition to wrestle up\n- back exposure from defensive posting\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- keep weight distributed away from the bottom athlete’s strongest angle\n- control the bottom player’s head and shoulders\n- prevent deep hip penetration underneath the center of mass\n- free the trapped leg\n- turn the knee and hips toward a safer passing angle\n- prevent the bottom athlete from rotating underneath\n- flatten the bottom athlete before the sweep structure develops\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- depth of hip position\n- control of the trapped leg\n- relationship to the opponent’s center of mass\n- shoulder position\n- head position\n- ability to rotate underneath\n- opponent’s available posts\n- direction of the opponent’s knee\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom athlete successfully positioning underneath the opponent’s hips\n- opponent’s weight loaded over the bottom athlete’s frame\n- trapped leg controlled while the free leg is used to influence balance\n- top player successfully flattening the bottom athlete\n- rotational movement creating a sweep\n- opponent posting and exposing a secondary pathway\n- bottom athlete losing hip depth and returning to standard Half Guard\n\nSynochi should recognise Deep Half Guard as an underneath control and off balancing system.\n\nIts effectiveness depends on controlling the opponent’s leg while positioning beneath their center of mass deeply enough to create rotational instability and a pathway to come on top."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Dogfight.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Dogfight","body":"[[Half Guard]]\n[[Z Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nDogfight is a half guard and wrestling transition where the bottom athlete comes up onto a knee or elbow with an underhook and begins contesting upper body position, hip position, and base against the top athlete.\n\nIt should be understood as a wrestle up and positional transition system rather than a single sweep.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- secure and maintain the underhook\n- build height from the floor\n- keep the head in strong inside position\n- connect the shoulder to the opponent’s torso\n- control the opponent’s near hip or leg\n- prevent being flattened back down\n- force the opponent to post or widen their base\n- convert the exchange into a sweep, takedown, back exposure, or top position\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- knee tap\n- far knee pick\n- single leg finish\n- body lock\n- back take\n- limp arm style back exposure\n- roll through or rotational sweep\n- transition to standing wrestling\n- return to Half Guard if the exchange collapses\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- deny or weaken the underhook\n- win head position\n- apply whizzer pressure\n- keep the bottom athlete from building height\n- widen the base\n- square the hips\n- force the bottom athlete back to the mat\n- transition into front headlock, back exposure, or passing control when the bottom athlete overcommits\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- underhook depth\n- head position\n- shoulder connection\n- hip height\n- control of the near leg\n- whizzer pressure\n- ability to build onto elbow, hand, or knee\n- opponent’s available posts\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom athlete reaching the underhook without building height\n- head position lost to the whizzer side\n- strong shoulder connection allowing the athlete to come up\n- opponent posting and exposing a knee tap\n- bottom athlete being flattened back to the mat\n- failed wrestle up converting into back exposure\n- successful transition from guard into standing wrestling\n\nSynochi should recognise Dogfight as the bridge between Half Guard and wrestling.\n\nIts effectiveness depends on converting the underhook into height, head position, and control of the opponent’s base rather than treating the underhook as an endpoint."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Double Under Pass.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Double Under Pass","body":"## Type\nPressure Passing System / Leg Elevation Pass\n\n## Purpose\nThe Double Under Pass is a pressure passing system built around controlling underneath both of the opponent’s legs, elevating the hips, and progressively folding the opponent’s lower body toward the upper body.\n\nThe passer removes the opponent’s ability to freely use the legs as defensive frames and converts the guard into a compressed structure.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- both of the passer’s arms positioned underneath the opponent’s legs\n- control around the thighs, hips, or lower back\n- the opponent’s hips elevated from the mat\n- the knees driven toward the torso\n- the passer maintaining close pressure while moving toward one side\n- the opponent’s ability to rotate or reinsert the legs progressively reduced\n\nThe position often develops from open guard when the passer gains access underneath both legs.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe Double Under Pass works by folding the opponent around their own hips.\n\nNormally, the guard player needs hip mobility and leg extension to maintain distance and recover position.\n\nThe passer takes away both.\n\nBy lifting the hips and compressing the knees toward the torso, the passer reduces:\n\n- leg mobility\n- hip rotation\n- ability to frame with the feet\n- ability to establish hooks\n- ability to square back toward the passer\n\nThe passer then moves around the compressed structure.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe passer generally wants to:\n\n- secure control underneath both legs\n- elevate the hips\n- prevent the opponent from extending the legs\n- keep close torso connection\n- control the direction of the opponent’s hips\n- progressively move around the guard\n- establish upper-body control before releasing the legs\n\n## Stack Pressure\nStacking commonly appears within the pass.\n\nThe goal is not simply to drive the opponent’s knees toward their face.\n\nEffective stack pressure should reduce the opponent’s usable hip movement while helping the passer control direction.\n\nPoor stacking can create unnecessary force without improving the position.\n\nUseful stack pressure should create:\n\ncompression,\nhip elevation,\nand reduced rotational freedom.\n\n## Directional Progression\nOnce the opponent’s hips are elevated and compressed, the passer usually begins moving toward one side.\n\nThe passer should maintain enough control that the opponent cannot simply follow the movement with the hips.\n\nThe progression should feel like moving around a controlled structure rather than racing around free legs.\n\n## Bottom Player Defensive Priorities\nThe guard player commonly attempts to:\n\n- prevent both arms entering underneath the legs\n- widen the knees\n- create frames against the passer’s shoulders or head\n- lower the hips back toward the mat\n- establish hip rotation\n- pummel a leg back inside\n- invert or redirect before the compression becomes stable\n- create enough distance to rebuild open guard\n\nThe position becomes significantly harder to escape once the hips are fully elevated and the knees are compressed.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Hips remain on the mat\nThe passer controls underneath the legs but never meaningfully elevates the pelvis.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent retains hip movement and can recover guard.\n\n### Arms too shallow\nThe passer’s control remains near the knees rather than connecting more deeply to the hips.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent can extend the legs and create distance.\n\n### Excessive forward pressure\nThe passer focuses on stacking without controlling direction.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent may rotate, invert, or redirect the pass.\n\n### Moving around too early\nThe passer begins circling before the hips are controlled.\n\nResult:\nthe guard follows the movement.\n\n### Releasing the legs before upper-body control\nThe passer clears the lower body but creates a moment of separation.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent recovers guard before the pass stabilises.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a Double Under Pass when:\n\n- both of the passer’s arms are underneath the opponent’s legs\n- the opponent’s hips are visibly elevated\n- both knees are compressed toward the torso\n- the bottom athlete is being stacked\n- the passer progressively moves around one side\n- the defender is trying to widen the knees or lower the hips\n- the pass completes after the legs have been compressed and cleared\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether both underhooks are secure\n- whether the hips are actually elevated\n- how deeply the passer controls the legs\n- whether the opponent can rotate the pelvis\n- whether stack pressure is creating meaningful constraint\n- when the passer begins moving around the guard\n- whether upper-body control is established before leg control is released\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf the Double Under Pass repeatedly stalls, inspect whether the athlete has actually controlled the hips.\n\nThe visible problem may appear to be difficulty moving around the legs.\n\nThe deeper problem is often that the opponent’s pelvis remains free.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Are the hips elevated?\n- Can the opponent extend their legs?\n- Can the pelvis rotate?\n- Is the passer connected deeply enough?\n- Is the passer moving around a controlled structure or chasing moving legs?\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Double Under Pass is a compression-and-elevation passing system.\n\nThe passer reduces the opponent’s lower-body freedom by lifting the pelvis and folding the legs toward the torso.\n\nThis converts a mobile guard into a constrained structure that can be navigated around.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nenter underneath,\nelevate,\ncompress,\ncontrol direction,\ncircle,\nstabilise.\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Seated Guard]]\n[[Butterfly Guard]]\n[[Side Control]]\n[[Smash Pass]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Dynamic Pinning.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Dynamic Pinning","body":"[[Pinning Mechanics]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nHigh-level pinning is not static.\n\nThe top athlete continuously adjusts:\n\n- pressure\n- wedges\n- base\n- hip position\n- chest angle\n- head position\n- limb control\n\nDynamic pinning means maintaining control while adapting to the bottom athlete's escape attempts.\n\nThe goal is not to freeze movement completely.\n\nThe goal is to ensure that movement occurs inside a controlled range."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Escape Direction Selection.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Escape Direction Selection","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nEscape direction should emerge from the opponent's available posts, pressure direction, hip orientation, head position, and trapped limbs.\n\nThe same technical escape may succeed or fail depending on direction.\n\nA useful escape direction usually moves toward the opponent's weakest ability to post, follow, or redirect force.\n\nSynochi should avoid prescribing an escape as a fixed movement independent of directional context."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Escape Priority.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Escape Priority","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nEscaping is not simply creating movement.\n\nIt is identifying **which constraint must be solved first** so that meaningful movement becomes possible.\n\nWhen multiple controls are present, some are more important than others.\n\nThe key question is:\n\nWhat is currently preventing the escape from beginning?\n\n## **Constraint Order**\n\nA trapped athlete may be dealing with several problems at once:\n\n- head control,\n- shoulder control,\n- hip control,\n- lost inside position,\n- collapsed posture,\n- lack of base,\n- blocked rotation,\n- or strong connection.\n\nTrying to solve all of them simultaneously often creates wasted effort.\n\nA better approach is:\n\n**Identify dominant constraint → remove it → rebuild structure → advance escape**\n\n## **Primary Constraint**\n\nThe primary constraint is the control that currently prevents the next useful movement.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf the athlete cannot turn because the head is fixed, recovering hip movement may not be enough.\n\nIf the hips are pinned but the upper body is relatively free, solving head position first may have little effect.\n\nEscape priority is therefore context-dependent.\n\n## **Structure Before Movement**\n\nA common mistake is trying to move before rebuilding enough structure to support the movement.\n\nUseful escape preparation may include:\n\n- recovering inside frames,\n- reconnecting elbow and knee,\n- rebuilding posture,\n- recovering head position,\n- creating a usable post,\n- or shifting onto a stronger base.\n\nThe escape often becomes easier once structure has been restored.\n\n## **Inside Position**\n\nRecovering [[Inside Position]] is frequently a high-priority defensive objective.\n\nIf the opponent occupies all meaningful inside space, direct movement may be ineffective.\n\nRecovering even one inside frame can:\n\n- create separation,\n- protect the torso,\n- block reconnection,\n- and begin restoring mobility.\n\n## **Breaking Connection**\n\nIf the opponent has strong [[Connection]], escape may first require disrupting that connection.\n\nThis can mean:\n\n- creating a frame,\n- clearing head control,\n- separating hips,\n- changing angle,\n- or inserting a wedge.\n\nOnce connection weakens, the bottom athlete gains independent movement.\n\n## **Restoring Base**\n\nSometimes the athlete has space but cannot use it because they have no meaningful [[Base]].\n\nA post, foot placement, elbow position, or hip angle may need to be restored before a powerful bridge, turn, or recovery becomes possible.\n\n## **Rotation**\n\nMany escapes depend on restoring rotational freedom.\n\nIf both shoulders and hips are fixed, rotation may be impossible.\n\nThe athlete may first need to free:\n\n- one shoulder,\n- one hip,\n- the head,\n- or an inside frame\n\nbefore attempting to turn.\n\n## **Escape Sequences**\n\nEscapes often follow an ordered progression.\n\nExample:\n\n**Recover frame → break chest connection → turn onto side → recover knee → rebuild guard**\n\nThe final movement works because the earlier constraints were solved first.\n\nSkipping a stage may cause the athlete to fight against the opponent’s strongest control.\n\n## **Defensive Efficiency**\n\nGood escape mechanics reduce unnecessary effort.\n\nRather than repeatedly bridging or shrimping against a structurally secure pin, the athlete identifies what makes the pin secure and dismantles it.\n\nThis creates a more efficient question:\n\nWhat do I need to recover before the escape becomes mechanically available?\n\n## **Re-Pinning Risk**\n\nAn escape is not complete simply because space appears.\n\nIf the athlete creates space without rebuilding useful structure, the opponent may reconnect immediately.\n\nSuccessful escapes should therefore include:\n\n**Break control → create space → rebuild structure → establish new position**\n\nnot simply:\n\n**Create space**\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- which constraint is actually preventing movement,\n- whether the athlete attacks the correct problem first,\n- whether they attempt large movements before rebuilding structure,\n- whether inside position is recovered,\n- and whether newly created space is converted into a stable defensive position.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou kept trying to shrimp while their crossface still controlled your head. Until that control was cleared, your hips could move but your torso could not rotate with them.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of:\n\n“Move more.”\n\nFRAME should identify the priority:\n\n“Clear the head control first, then turn onto your side.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Recover the inside frame before trying to bring the knee through.”\n\nThis gives the athlete an ordered solution.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Escapes become easier when constraints are solved in the correct order.**\n\nThe athlete should not ask only:\n\nHow do I get out?\n\nThey should ask:\n\nWhat is stopping the next useful movement, and what must I recover first?\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Pinning Mechanics]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Base]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Escape Progress Preservation.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Escape Progress Preservation","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nEscape progress should be protected once created.\n\nA common defensive failure is repeatedly gaining small improvements and then surrendering them while attempting the next movement.\n\nExamples:\n\n- recovering an elbow inside, then allowing it to be isolated again\n- creating hip space, then flattening voluntarily\n- removing one hook, then allowing immediate reinsertion\n- recovering half guard, then abandoning knee position\n\nEach successful micro-recovery should become part of the new defensive structure.\n\nSynochi should recognise whether the athlete is accumulating escape progress or repeatedly resetting to the original problem."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Escape Readiness.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Escape Readiness","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nEscape Readiness describes whether the conditions required for a meaningful escape are currently present.\n\nAn athlete may know the correct escape but still be unable to execute it because the necessary mechanical prerequisites have not yet been restored.\n\nCommon escape-readiness variables include:\n\n- immediate submission safety\n- inside position\n- frame integrity\n- head and shoulder freedom\n- hip mobility\n- floor connection\n- opponent base disruption\n- usable escape direction\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\nknowing the escape\n\nand\n\nbeing mechanically ready to escape.\n\nWhen escape readiness is low, coaching should target the missing prerequisite rather than prescribe more effort."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Escape Timing Windows.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Escape Timing Windows","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nEscapes are often timing-dependent rather than continuously available.\n\nA control position may briefly weaken when the top athlete:\n\n- changes grips\n- transitions between pins\n- attacks a submission\n- shifts weight\n- posts to maintain balance\n- follows a previous escape attempt\n\nThese moments create escape windows.\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\nmechanical possibility\n\nand\n\ntiming opportunity.\n\nA technically correct escape attempted against settled structure may fail, while the same movement may succeed during a transition.\n\nCoaching should therefore identify when the opponent's control is temporarily reorganising."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Failed Escape Diagnostic.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Failed Escape Diagnostic","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nA failed escape provides information about the unresolved controlling structure.\n\nRepeated failure may indicate:\n\n- wrong priority\n- incorrect direction\n- absent inside position\n- weak framing\n- opponent base not disrupted\n- poor timing\n- trapped shoulder or hip\n- excessive force against stable structure\n\nSynochi should treat repeated failed escapes as diagnostic evidence rather than simply recommending greater effort."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Front Headlock.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Front Headlock","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nThe front headlock is a control position built around control of the opponent’s head and usually one arm while remaining in front of, above, or slightly beside the opponent.\n\nIt commonly appears from:\n\n- failed shots\n- turtle\n- sprawls\n- snapdowns\n- scrambles\n- seated wrestling exchanges\n- guard passing reactions\n\nThe front headlock is both a control position and a branching attacking system.\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control head position\n- control one arm where possible\n- keep the opponent’s posture broken\n- prevent clean re-guard or standing recovery\n- create angle around the opponent’s shoulders\n- expose the neck\n- transition behind the hips\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- guillotine\n- high-elbow guillotine\n- arm-in guillotine\n- anaconda choke\n- darce choke\n- go-behind\n- back take\n- crucifix\n- snapdown to turtle control\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- recover posture\n- protect the neck\n- regain inside hand position\n- square the shoulders\n- deny angle creation\n- recover base\n- clear the controlling arm before turning aggressively\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- head height\n- chin position\n- shoulder angle\n- arm isolation\n- chest pressure over the upper back\n- distance between attacker’s hips and defender’s shoulders\n- ability to circle\n- opponent’s available posts\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- attacker controlling head without useful angle\n- defender successfully rebuilding posture\n- elbow separation creating choke access\n- attacker circling behind while maintaining head control\n- failed choke attempt opening a go-behind\n- defender exposing back while trying to clear the headlock\n\nSynochi should recognise the front headlock as a transition hub connecting wrestling control, submissions, turtle attacks, back takes, and crucifix entries."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Guillotine.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Guillotine","body":"# Guillotine\n\n## Type\nFront Headlock Submission / Neck Compression Choke\n\n## Purpose\nThe guillotine is a submission family built around controlling the opponent’s head and neck from a front-facing or angled position.\n\nIt commonly appears from:\n\n- front headlock exchanges\n- failed takedowns\n- wrestling shots\n- seated guard\n- standing clinches\n- scrambles\n- snapdowns\n\nThe choke is effective when the attacker controls both the opponent’s neck position and their ability to posture or rotate out.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe guillotine works by creating compression around the neck while controlling posture.\n\nDepending on the variation, the finishing mechanics may emphasise:\n\n- arm position under the neck\n- wrist orientation\n- elbow position\n- chest connection\n- torso angle\n- hip position\n\nThe choke becomes significantly stronger when the opponent cannot lift the head or turn into a safer alignment.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- the opponent’s head controlled underneath the attacker’s arm\n- the attacker’s choking arm positioned around the neck\n- the opponent’s posture broken\n- close connection between chest, shoulder, and head\n- the defender prevented from freely rotating or pulling the head out\n\nThe opponent’s arm may be trapped inside or outside the choking configuration depending on the variation.\n\n## Arm In and Arm Out Variations\nGuillotines can be broadly divided into arm-in and arm-out structures.\n\n### Arm Out\nThe opponent’s neck is isolated without their arm inside the choking loop.\n\nThis often allows more direct neck compression.\n\n### Arm In\nOne of the opponent’s arms remains inside the configuration.\n\nThis changes:\n\n- available finishing angles\n- grip structure\n- posture control\n- defensive pathways\n\nFRAME should distinguish the configuration when visible.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- keep the opponent’s head below their own shoulder line\n- prevent posture recovery\n- maintain close chest and shoulder connection\n- control the opponent’s ability to rotate\n- keep the choking structure tight\n- avoid creating unnecessary space while adjusting\n\n## Posture Control\nPosture is one of the most important variables.\n\nIf the opponent can lift the head and straighten the spine, the choke often weakens rapidly.\n\nThe attacker should therefore treat posture control as part of the submission rather than as a separate problem.\n\n## Offensive Pathways\nThe guillotine may lead to:\n\n- direct choke finish\n- front headlock control\n- sweep\n- transition to top position\n- back exposure\n- transition into another front-headlock attack\n\nA failed guillotine attempt can still produce strong positional advantage if the head remains controlled.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- restore posture\n- create space around the neck\n- control the attacker’s choking arm\n- rotate toward a safer angle\n- move the head toward the open side\n- establish strong upper-body connection\n- reduce the attacker’s ability to close the elbow and chest around the neck\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Head position too high\nThe opponent regains posture.\n\nResult:\nthe choke loses compression.\n\n### Loose elbow position\nThe attacker’s choking arm opens away from the body.\n\nResult:\nspace appears around the neck.\n\n### Pulling with the arms only\nThe attacker relies on arm strength without controlling posture or torso alignment.\n\nResult:\nthe defender survives and begins escaping.\n\n### Poor torso angle\nThe attacker remains badly aligned with the opponent’s neck.\n\nResult:\npressure is distributed inefficiently.\n\n### Sacrificing position for the finish\nThe attacker falls or rotates without enough control.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent clears the head and gains top position.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a guillotine when:\n\n- one athlete controls the opponent’s head underneath an arm\n- the defender’s posture is visibly broken\n- the attacker connects the hands around the neck\n- the defender is trying to lift the head or peel the choking arm\n- the exchange originates from a shot, snapdown, or front headlock\n- the attacker adjusts torso or elbow position to increase neck compression\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the opponent can posture\n- whether the choking elbow remains tight\n- whether the attacker maintains head control\n- whether the opponent’s arm is inside or outside\n- whether the attacker creates useful torso angle\n- whether the defender can rotate toward the open side\n- what happens if the choke fails\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a guillotine repeatedly fails, inspect posture control before grip strength.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the opponent’s head actually controlled?\n- Can they straighten the spine?\n- Is the choking elbow tight?\n- Is the attacker using body position or just pulling?\n- Does the attacker retain positional advantage if the choke does not finish?\n\nA strong guillotine should feel structurally restrictive before maximal squeezing is required.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe guillotine is a head-control submission system.\n\nThe attacker converts control of the opponent’s posture and cervical alignment into a choking opportunity.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\ncapture head,\nbreak posture,\nclose space,\ncontrol rotation,\napply compression,\nfinish or transition."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Half Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Half Guard","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nHalf guard is a position where the bottom athlete controls one of the top athlete’s legs while both players contest upper-body connection, hip position, and the ability to turn or build height.\n\nHalf guard should be understood as a family of positions rather than a single guard.\n\nCommon configurations include:\n\n- knee shield\n- flattened half guard\n- underhook half guard\n- lockdown\n- half butterfly\n- seated half guard\n- deep half guard\n- reverse half guard\n\nPrimary bottom-player objectives include:\n\n- avoid being flattened\n- win useful head and arm position\n- preserve knee-elbow connection\n- create an underhook or inside frame\n- maintain access to hip rotation\n- build to an elbow or hand\n- recover full guard, wrestle up, or enter attacking pathways\n\nPrimary top-player objectives include:\n\n- flatten the bottom athlete\n- control head direction\n- win the underhook battle\n- separate knees and elbows\n- immobilise the trapped leg configuration\n- clear the knee line\n- progress to mount, side control, or passing sequences\n\nCommon bottom pathways include:\n\n- dogfight\n- wrestle-up\n- knee shield attacks\n- butterfly elevation\n- back exposure from underhook battles\n- deep half transitions\n- guard recovery\n\nCommon top pathways include:\n\n- crossface and underhook control\n- half guard smash\n- knee cut progression\n- backstep\n- tripod passing\n- mount\n- side control\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- head position\n- underhook control\n- knee shield integrity\n- hip angle\n- trapped-leg depth\n- ability to build height\n- shoulder alignment\n- top-player chest connection\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom athlete flattened with both shoulders controlled\n- top player winning head and underhook position\n- knee shield collapsing\n- bottom athlete successfully building onto elbow or hand\n- underhook creating access to dogfight\n- trapped knee becoming free\n- top player changing angle rather than forcing directly through frames\n\nSynochi should recognise half guard as a dynamic battle over upper-body alignment, hip freedom, and height.\n\nThe position changes dramatically depending on whether the bottom athlete remains mobile and angled or becomes flattened and structurally compressed."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Head Control As Positional Control.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Head Control As Positional Control","body":"[[Pinning Mechanics]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nControl of the head influences spinal rotation, shoulder movement, and hip direction.\n\nWhen the head is fixed or redirected, the rest of the body often loses rotational freedom.\n\nThis makes head control a central pinning variable rather than merely an upper-body grip.\n\nSynochi should treat head direction as a predictor of available escape direction."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Headquarters Position.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Headquarters Position","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Type\nGuard Passing Hub / Transitional Control Position\n\n## Purpose\nHeadquarters is a standing or crouched passing position used to organise the top player’s relationship to an opponent’s open guard before committing to a pass.\n\nIt is less a single pass and more a control hub from which multiple passing directions can be selected.\n\nThe position matters because the passer has already reduced the bottom player’s freedom enough to begin forcing predictable reactions.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- One of the bottom player’s legs is controlled between or around the passer’s legs\n- The passer maintains enough distance to avoid being fully collapsed into guard\n- The bottom player’s hips are partially constrained\n- The passer can threaten movement to both sides\n- The passer is positioned to transition into knee cut, leg drag, smash, backstep, or other passing systems\n\nThe exact leg configuration varies, but the defining feature is that the passer has converted an open guard into a more organised and controllable passing relationship.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nHeadquarters works through constraint.\n\nThe passer reduces the number of useful directions available to the guard player while preserving several offensive routes for themselves.\n\nThis creates an asymmetry:\n\nBottom athlete:\nfewer viable reactions\n\nTop athlete:\nmultiple connected passing options\n\nThe value of the position is therefore not just positional control, but decision advantage.\n\n## Offensive Pathways\nCommon continuations include:\n\n- [[Knee Cut Pass]]\n- [[Leg Drag]]\n- backstep passing\n- smash passing\n- folding passes\n- torreando-style redirections\n- forcing half guard\n- forcing knee shield collapse\n\nA strong headquarters position should allow the passer to move between these options rather than becoming locked into one attack.\n\n## Bottom Player Objectives\nThe guard player generally wants to prevent headquarters from becoming stable.\n\nUseful priorities include:\n\n- recovering inside position\n- restoring knee elbow connection\n- freeing the trapped leg\n- controlling distance\n- disrupting the passer’s posture\n- creating kuzushi before the passer settles\n- returning to a stronger open guard configuration\n\nOnce headquarters becomes stable, guard retention becomes increasingly reactive.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify headquarters when:\n\n- the top athlete is standing or crouched over open guard\n- one bottom leg is meaningfully trapped or isolated\n- the passer controls the opponent’s knee line or hip direction\n- the passer repeatedly switches between passing directions\n- the bottom player is forced into defensive leg pummelling or frame recovery\n- the exchange has moved beyond neutral open guard but has not yet become a completed pass\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Passer commits too early\nThe athlete attacks a pass before controlling the guard structure.\n\nResult:\nthe guard player retains freely or creates an immediate counterattack.\n\n### Weak leg control\nThe bottom athlete’s trapped leg is not genuinely constrained.\n\nResult:\nthey recover guard before the passer can build momentum.\n\n### Poor posture\nThe passer’s weight drifts too far forward.\n\nResult:\nthe bottom athlete can elevate, enter underneath, or attack upper-body controls.\n\n### Static headquarters\nThe passer reaches the position but treats it as an endpoint.\n\nResult:\nthe guard player has time to rebuild frames and inside position.\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nA useful coaching question is:\n\n**Has the athlete actually established headquarters, or are they simply standing inside open guard?**\n\nThe distinction matters.\n\nTrue headquarters should create:\n\nconstraint,\npredictable reactions,\nand connected passing options.\n\nIf those are missing, the position is not yet functioning as a passing hub.\n\n## Video Analysis\nWhen analysing footage, FRAME should examine:\n\n- which leg is being constrained\n- whether the bottom athlete retains inside position\n- whether the passer controls hip rotation\n- whether the passer can attack both directions\n- whether the bottom athlete is reacting or initiating\n- which pass becomes available from the opponent’s response\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nHeadquarters is best understood as a temporary decision-control state.\n\nThe passer is attempting to compress the guard player’s available action space while maintaining several future branches.\n\nA successful headquarters position therefore increases offensive optionality while decreasing defensive optionality.\n\nThe position becomes strategically valuable before any pass is completed."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Heel Hook.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Heel Hook","body":"# Heel Hook\n\n## Type\nLeg Lock Submission / Rotational Joint Attack\n\n## Purpose\nThe heel hook is a leg lock that applies rotational force through the foot and lower leg while controlling the opponent’s knee line and hip movement.\n\nThe visible grip is on the heel, but the submission depends on controlling the larger leg structure.\n\nA strong heel hook position typically combines:\n\n- heel exposure\n- knee line control\n- hip connection\n- rotational control\n- restricted escape direction\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe heel acts as a rotational handle on the leg.\n\nWhen the foot is controlled and the opponent’s knee cannot freely rotate or withdraw, rotation at the foot transfers force through the ankle and knee.\n\nThe attack therefore depends on two linked problems:\n\ncontrol the leg\n\nand\n\napply rotational finishing mechanics.\n\nWithout positional control, the opponent may rotate, slip the knee line, or disengage.\n\n## Inside and Outside Heel Hooks\nHeel hooks are commonly divided into inside and outside configurations.\n\n### Inside Heel Hook\nThe heel is exposed toward the inside of the attacker’s body.\n\nThis configuration often creates strong rotational control and may produce significant pressure through the knee.\n\n### Outside Heel Hook\nThe heel is exposed toward the outside.\n\nThe finishing direction and surrounding entanglement differ, but the same broad principles remain:\n\ncontrol the knee line,\nlimit rotation,\nexpose the heel,\napply controlled rotational pressure.\n\nFRAME should distinguish the finishing orientation where footage or user description makes it clear.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nBefore attacking, the controlling athlete generally wants to:\n\n- secure the opponent’s knee line\n- keep the hips connected to the trapped leg\n- prevent free defensive rotation\n- control the opponent’s secondary leg when necessary\n- expose the heel without sacrificing the entanglement\n- maintain a stable finishing angle\n\nThe submission becomes less reliable when heel exposure is prioritised over positional control.\n\n## Heel Exposure\nHeel exposure is the process of creating access to the back or side of the opponent’s heel.\n\nThis may occur through:\n\n- rotational movement\n- controlling the toes\n- manipulating the opponent’s knee orientation\n- creating reactions with alternative attacks\n- adjusting the entanglement\n\nThe goal is to expose the heel while keeping the knee trapped.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender generally wants to:\n\n- hide the heel\n- extract the knee line\n- create rotational freedom\n- separate the attacker’s legs\n- control the attacker’s hands\n- disengage the hips\n- move in the correct direction for the specific entanglement\n\nRotating blindly can increase danger.\n\nDefensive movement must match the entanglement and finishing direction.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Chasing the heel before securing the knee\nThe attacker gains heel exposure but the opponent’s knee remains free.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent withdraws the leg.\n\n### Loose hip connection\nThe attacker’s hips drift away from the trapped leg.\n\nResult:\nrotational control decreases.\n\n### Weak knee line control\nThe opponent’s knee clears the controlling structure.\n\nResult:\nthe heel hook loses most of its finishing potential.\n\n### Over-rotating the upper body\nThe attacker attempts to generate force through the arms and torso rather than maintaining positional alignment.\n\nResult:\ncontrol may weaken and the opponent can rotate with the attack.\n\n### Ignoring the secondary leg\nThe defender uses the free leg to clear grips, push away, or create escape space.\n\nResult:\nthe entanglement deteriorates.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a heel hook when:\n\n- one athlete isolates the opponent’s leg\n- the heel is being controlled or exposed\n- the attacker’s forearm wraps around the heel line\n- the defender is attempting to hide the heel or rotate\n- the exchange centres around knee line control\n- the attacker maintains a leg entanglement while applying rotational pressure\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the knee line is controlled\n- whether the heel is genuinely exposed\n- whether the attacker’s hips remain connected\n- whether the defender has rotational freedom\n- whether the secondary leg is influencing the exchange\n- whether the attacker maintains positional control while entering the finish\n- which heel-hook orientation is being used\n\n## Safety Interpretation\nHeel hooks can create serious injury before pain becomes a reliable warning signal.\n\nTraining application should therefore prioritise:\n\n- controlled finishing pressure\n- early tapping\n- partner awareness\n- ruleset awareness\n- avoiding uncontrolled rotational escapes\n\nFRAME should avoid encouraging explosive finishing mechanics.\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a heel hook repeatedly fails, inspect the entanglement before the grip.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the knee line actually trapped?\n- Can the opponent rotate freely?\n- Is the attacker connected at the hips?\n- Is the heel being exposed without losing control?\n- Is the defender’s secondary leg creating the escape?\n\nMany finishing failures are positional failures first.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe heel hook is a rotational isolation system.\n\nThe attacker controls the opponent’s leg strongly enough that rotation applied at the heel cannot be safely dissipated through movement of the hip or knee.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nisolate,\ncontrol knee line,\nrestrict rotation,\nexpose heel,\nsecure grip,\napply controlled finish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/High Wrist Guillotine.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"High Wrist Guillotine","body":"## Type\nFront Headlock Submission / High Wrist Choke Variation\n\n## Purpose\nThe High Wrist Guillotine is a guillotine variation that uses a deep choking-arm position and elevated wrist orientation to create strong compression around the neck while limiting the opponent’s ability to posture or turn out.\n\nThe distinguishing feature is the height and angle of the choking wrist relative to the opponent’s neck and jaw line.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe choke works by combining:\n\n- deep neck control\n- high wrist placement\n- elbow closure\n- shoulder connection\n- posture disruption\n- torso angle\n\nThe attacker attempts to create a compact choking structure in which the opponent’s head is trapped beneath the shoulder while the wrist and forearm remain high enough to maintain strong neck contact.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- the opponent’s head captured under the attacker’s arm\n- the choking wrist positioned high near the opponent’s neck or jaw line\n- the choking elbow kept relatively tight\n- the attacker’s shoulder closing over the opponent’s head\n- posture prevented from fully recovering\n- secondary hand reinforcing the choking structure\n\nThe exact grip may vary, but the high wrist position remains the defining characteristic.\n\n## Why the High Wrist Matters\nA lower wrist position can create more space around the neck and make posture recovery easier.\n\nRaising the wrist can:\n\n- deepen the choking arm\n- improve neck connection\n- increase compression\n- reduce space near the opponent’s jaw\n- make it harder for the head to slip free\n\nThe wrist should remain structurally supported rather than simply being lifted with arm strength.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- capture the head deeply\n- maintain the high wrist position\n- keep the choking elbow connected\n- prevent the opponent from lifting the head\n- close the shoulder over the neck\n- create a useful torso angle\n- maintain enough control to transition if the finish fails\n\n## Entry Conditions\nThe High Wrist Guillotine commonly appears from:\n\n- front headlock exchanges\n- snapdowns\n- defended takedowns\n- standing clinches\n- seated guard\n- scrambles\n- failed wrestling shots\n\nIt is especially useful when the attacker gains deep head control before the opponent can rebuild posture.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- raise posture\n- lower or strip the choking wrist\n- peel the reinforcing hand\n- turn toward the safer side\n- create shoulder space\n- move the head toward the opening\n- prevent the attacker from closing the elbow and shoulder together\n\nOnce the choking structure becomes compact, late defence becomes much harder.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Wrist not high enough\nThe attacker has a guillotine grip but the choking hand sits too low.\n\nResult:\nspace remains around the neck and the defender can posture.\n\n### Elbow flares outward\nThe attacker loses connection between the forearm and neck.\n\nResult:\ncompression decreases.\n\n### Shoulder not connected\nThe wrist is correctly positioned but the attacker does not close the shoulder over the head.\n\nResult:\nthe defender retains movement.\n\n### Pulling instead of closing\nThe attacker tries to drag the opponent’s head upward with the arms.\n\nResult:\nthe choke becomes strength-dependent and unstable.\n\n### Poor angle\nThe attacker remains directly square when a better finishing angle is available.\n\nResult:\nthe defender can align the spine and relieve pressure.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a High Wrist Guillotine when:\n\n- one athlete controls the opponent’s head in a guillotine structure\n- the choking wrist is visibly elevated near the neck or jaw\n- the elbow remains tight rather than hanging low\n- the shoulder closes over the opponent’s head\n- the defender repeatedly tries to lower or peel the wrist\n- the attacker adjusts angle while maintaining deep neck control\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- how high the choking wrist sits\n- whether the elbow remains connected\n- whether the opponent can regain posture\n- whether the shoulder seals the top of the choking structure\n- whether the attacker creates an effective torso angle\n- whether the defender can move the head toward open space\n- whether the grip remains structurally sound during transitions\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf the High Wrist Guillotine repeatedly fails, inspect the quality of the choking structure rather than squeezing harder.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the wrist actually high?\n- Is the elbow connected?\n- Is the shoulder sealing the head?\n- Can the opponent still posture?\n- Is the attacker using angle and body position effectively?\n\nThe variation becomes powerful when the upper-body structure is compact and connected.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe High Wrist Guillotine is a refined head-control compression system.\n\nIts advantage comes from reducing escape space around the neck while improving the attacker’s ability to maintain posture disruption and rotational control.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\ncapture deeply,\nraise wrist,\nclose elbow,\nseal shoulder,\ncontrol posture,\ncompress,\nfinish or transition."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/K Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"K Guard","body":"[[50 50 Guard]]\n[[Single-Leg X Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nK Guard is a guard configuration where the bottom athlete creates an angle underneath and around the opponent’s leg, using inside leg position, hip rotation, and lower-body connection to isolate the leg and access off-balancing, backside entries, and leg entanglements.\n\nIt should be understood as an angle-creation and lower-body entry system rather than a static guard shape.\n\nA defining mechanical feature is the relationship between the bottom athlete’s inside knee and foot.\n\nThe inside knee should penetrate toward the inside of the opponent’s leg rather than remaining outside or loosely attached.\n\nThe knee angle should point downward toward the mat as the athlete rotates underneath, helping the leg form a strong internal wedge around the opponent’s thigh.\n\nThe foot should connect to the opponent’s hip or hip line, creating a frame that helps manage distance, maintain the angle, and prevent the opponent from simply collapsing directly onto the bottom athlete.\n\nThis creates a connected structure:\n\nfoot on hip\n→ knee inside\n→ knee angled downward\n→ hips rotated underneath\n→ opponent’s leg isolated\n\nPrimary bottom-player objectives include:\n\n- establish the foot on the hip or hip line\n- bring the inside knee deeply to the inside\n- orient the knee downward rather than allowing it to flare outward\n- rotate the hips underneath the opponent\n- isolate one leg\n- prevent the opponent from squaring their hips\n- control distance with the foot and knee structure\n- expose the knee line\n- access backside or leg-entanglement pathways\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- backside 50/50\n- 50/50\n- Single-Leg X\n- Saddle\n- heel exposure where ruleset permits\n- wrestle-up\n- sweep to top position\n- back exposure\n- transitions into other open-guard configurations\n\nPrimary top-player objectives include:\n\n- remove or bypass the foot-on-hip frame\n- force the inside knee back to the outside\n- square the hips toward the bottom athlete\n- deny the bottom athlete access underneath the hip line\n- control the legs before the angle becomes established\n- prevent the knee line from being captured\n- create distance or pressure that collapses the K Guard structure\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- foot-on-hip connection\n- inside knee depth\n- downward knee orientation\n- hip rotation\n- knee-line access\n- control of the opponent’s far leg\n- relationship between the bottom athlete’s hips and the opponent’s thigh\n- ability to maintain an off-centre angle\n- opponent’s ability to square the hips\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- foot connected to the hip while the inside knee penetrates underneath\n- knee pointing downward as the hips rotate beneath the opponent\n- top player forcing the knee outward and collapsing the structure\n- K Guard entry creating immediate access to the knee line\n- far-leg control preventing disengagement\n- transition into backside 50/50\n- bottom athlete losing the foot-on-hip frame and being flattened or squared up\n- opponent stepping or rotating in a way that exposes the back or secondary leg\n\nSynochi should recognise K Guard through the combined relationship of foot-on-hip framing, inside knee penetration, downward knee orientation, and hip rotation.\n\nThe guard becomes effective when those structures work together to place the athlete underneath and outside the opponent’s alignment, creating access to the knee line, backside position, and secondary leg."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Kimura Trap.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Kimura Trap","body":"## Type\nUpper Body Control System / Submission and Transition Hub\n\n## Purpose\nThe Kimura Trap is a control system built around isolating an opponent’s arm with a figure-four grip while using that control to influence the shoulder line, torso rotation, and overall direction of movement.\n\nIt is more than a shoulder lock.\n\nThe Kimura connection can be used to:\n\n- control posture\n- force rotation\n- expose the back\n- create sweeps\n- prevent escapes\n- enter submissions\n- transition between top and bottom positions\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- control of the opponent’s wrist\n- a figure-four grip around the opponent’s arm\n- the elbow separated from the torso\n- control of the opponent’s shoulder rotation\n- the attacker positioned so the arm can be used as a lever against the torso\n\nThe grip may remain active through multiple positional transitions.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe Kimura Trap works by converting control of one arm into control of the opponent’s upper-body orientation.\n\nThe isolated arm becomes a lever.\n\nBy controlling the wrist and elbow relationship, the attacker can influence:\n\n- shoulder rotation\n- torso direction\n- posture\n- ability to turn\n- ability to post\n- ability to defend transitions\n\nThis creates pathways that extend far beyond the submission itself.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- maintain wrist control\n- keep the opponent’s elbow separated from the body\n- keep the figure-four connection tight\n- control shoulder rotation\n- position their own body so the arm cannot easily reconnect to the torso\n- follow the opponent through transitional movement\n\nThe Kimura Trap becomes weak when the opponent restores the arm to a strong defensive alignment.\n\n## Offensive Pathways\nCommon continuations include:\n\n- shoulder lock\n- back take\n- sweep\n- armbar\n- top-control transition\n- control during scrambles\n- forcing the opponent into defensive rotation\n\nThe same grip may remain useful even when the original submission disappears.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- reconnect the elbow to the torso\n- straighten or hide the arm\n- grip their own body or clothing where rules allow\n- rotate the shoulder into safer alignment\n- control the attacker’s hands\n- create enough space to break the figure-four\n- move the torso so the isolated arm is no longer functioning as an effective lever\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Elbow remains connected\nThe attacker controls the wrist but never meaningfully separates the elbow from the torso.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent retains strong structural defence.\n\n### Loose figure-four\nThe connection between the attacker’s arms is weak.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent can rotate, straighten the arm, or recover posture.\n\n### Chasing only the submission\nThe attacker ignores positional opportunities because the shoulder lock is not available.\n\nResult:\ncontrol is lost despite having a strong transitional grip.\n\n### Poor body position\nThe attacker has the Kimura grip but their torso is badly aligned.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent can move freely despite the arm being controlled.\n\n### Losing wrist control\nThe primary connection breaks.\n\nResult:\nthe entire control system rapidly deteriorates.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a Kimura Trap when:\n\n- one athlete has a figure-four grip around the opponent’s arm\n- the opponent’s wrist is being controlled\n- the elbow is separated from the torso\n- the attacker uses the grip during a sweep or back take\n- the defender repeatedly tries to hide or straighten the arm\n- the grip remains active through positional transitions\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the wrist is securely controlled\n- whether the elbow is separated from the torso\n- who controls shoulder rotation\n- whether the attacker follows the opponent during transitions\n- whether the grip is being used for submission, sweep, or positional control\n- what reaction the grip creates\n- whether the attacker loses control by overcommitting to the finish\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a Kimura attack repeatedly fails, the issue may not be the shoulder lock.\n\nInspect:\n\n- elbow separation\n- wrist control\n- body position\n- shoulder rotation\n- whether the athlete recognises available transitions\n\nA good Kimura Trap should still create value even when the submission is not immediately available.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Kimura Trap is a limb-to-torso control system.\n\nThe attacker isolates one arm and uses it as a lever to influence the opponent’s larger body structure.\n\nIts value comes from converting local control into global directional control.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nsecure wrist,\nseparate elbow,\ncontrol shoulder,\nforce reaction,\ntransition or finish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Knee Cut Pass.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Knee Cut Pass","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Headquarters Position]]\n[[Half Guard]]\n[[Side Control]]\n\n## Type\nGuard Pass / Pressure Passing Pathway\n\n## Purpose\nThe knee cut pass is a high-percentage passing system built around driving one knee across the opponent’s thigh line while controlling the upper body strongly enough to prevent guard recovery.\n\nIt is not simply a matter of sliding the knee through.\n\nThe pass works when lower-body penetration and upper-body control happen together.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- one knee cuts across the opponent’s thigh\n- the passer controls or clears the opponent’s inside knee line\n- the passer establishes upper-body connection through an underhook, crossface, head position, or equivalent control\n- the opponent’s hips are prevented from freely turning back into guard\n- the passer progresses toward chest-to-chest control\n\nThe exact grips can vary significantly between gi and no-gi, but the structural goal remains similar.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe knee cut separates the opponent’s defensive structures.\n\nThe passer attempts to create:\n\n- control above the hips\n- penetration below the hips\n- directional pressure across the body\n\nThis can break the opponent’s ability to keep their knees connected to their upper-body frames.\n\nThe pass becomes much stronger when the opponent is forced to defend two problems at once:\n\nupper-body flattening\n\nand\n\nlower-body penetration\n\n## Entry Conditions\nCommon entries include:\n\n- [[Headquarters Position]]\n- open guard passing exchanges\n- knee shield half guard\n- standing passing transitions\n- failed leg-entry attempts\n- transitions from body lock or smash passing\n\nThe passer does not always need to begin in a textbook headquarters configuration.\n\nWhat matters is whether the knee line can be crossed without giving the bottom athlete enough space to recover.\n\n## Upper Body Control\nUpper-body control is usually the deciding factor.\n\nUseful controls may include:\n\n- underhook\n- crossface\n- head and arm control\n- far shoulder control\n- near-side elbow control\n- head positioning that blocks the opponent from turning inward\n\nA knee cut with no upper-body control often becomes a temporary leg position rather than a completed pass.\n\n## Lower Body Mechanics\nThe cutting knee should travel across the thigh line while the trailing leg remains capable of maintaining base and preventing re-guarding.\n\nImportant details include:\n\n- controlling the opponent’s knee direction\n- preventing the knee shield from rebuilding\n- avoiding unnecessary entanglement of the trailing foot\n- progressing the hips rather than reaching with the upper body alone\n\n## Bottom Player Defensive Priorities\nThe bottom athlete commonly attempts to:\n\n- maintain knee shield structure\n- recover an underhook\n- frame against the passer’s shoulder or neck\n- turn onto their side\n- insert a butterfly hook\n- recover inside knee position\n- enter single leg or dogfight pathways\n- redirect into [[Half Guard]] or another guard\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Cutting before controlling the upper body\nThe knee clears but the opponent can rotate underneath.\n\nResult:\nguard recovery or reversal.\n\n### Losing the underhook battle\nThe bottom athlete secures strong inside upper-body control.\n\nResult:\nthe passer may be forced into [[Dogfight]] or a wrestling exchange.\n\n### Knee trapped at the hip\nThe passer penetrates partially but cannot clear the opponent’s thigh structure.\n\nResult:\na stalled half guard position.\n\n### Weight too far forward\nThe passer reaches with the chest while the hips remain behind.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent can create space underneath or elevate.\n\n### Weight too far back\nThe passer controls the leg but does not commit pressure through the torso.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent rebuilds frames and knee position.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a knee cut when:\n\n- the top athlete’s knee travels across the opponent’s thigh\n- the opponent is defending with knee shield or half guard structures\n- the passer is trying to establish chest-to-chest connection\n- the top athlete is fighting for an underhook or crossface\n- the exchange repeatedly revolves around clearing the trapped lower leg\n- successful completion leads toward [[Side Control]]\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- who controls the underhook\n- whether the bottom athlete remains on their side\n- whether the passer’s head position blocks rotation\n- whether the cutting knee has actually cleared the thigh\n- whether the trailing leg is trapped\n- whether the passer is controlling the hips or simply moving forward\n- what recovery route the bottom athlete is attempting\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a knee cut repeatedly stalls, the problem may not be the knee.\n\nCheck:\n\n- upper-body control\n- head position\n- hip direction\n- underhook ownership\n- trailing leg freedom\n- the opponent’s ability to turn toward the passer\n\nThis prevents technical diagnosis from becoming overly focused on the visible cutting leg.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe knee cut is a coordinated constraint problem.\n\nThe passer is attempting to deny rotation above the hips while crossing the defensive leg structure below them.\n\nIf either layer remains free, the opponent can reconnect their guard.\n\nA successful knee cut therefore represents simultaneous control of:\n\norientation,\nhip access,\nand defensive reconstruction."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Knee-on-Belly.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Knee-on-Belly","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nKnee-on-belly is a transitional top-control position where the attacker uses the knee and supporting posts to apply directional pressure while retaining mobility around the opponent’s torso.\n\nIt should be understood as a mobile control position rather than simply a pressure hold.\n\nCommon uses include:\n\n- stabilising after a pass\n- forcing defensive reactions\n- transitioning between side control and mount\n- creating arm exposure\n- opening space for movement around the body\n- generating pressure without committing both knees to the floor\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control the opponent’s upper-body orientation\n- keep weight directed through a stable pressure line\n- prevent easy hip escape\n- maintain enough mobility to follow reactions\n- force the defender to frame or turn\n- exploit those reactions to progress position or attack submissions\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- mount\n- technical mount\n- far-side armbar\n- near-side arm isolation\n- baseball-bat style choke entries where ruleset and grips allow\n- back exposure\n- return to side control\n- movement around to north-south\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- frame against the controlling leg and torso\n- protect the near-side arm\n- recover hip movement\n- disrupt the attacker’s balance\n- turn toward a safer angle\n- prevent progression to mount\n- create space for guard recovery\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- knee placement\n- hip height\n- post position\n- pressure direction\n- upper-body control\n- distance between hips and chest\n- ability to transition without losing balance\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- attacker sitting too upright and losing pressure\n- knee placement too low to meaningfully restrict movement\n- defender successfully framing and creating hip space\n- attacker using reaction to step to mount\n- excessive commitment causing balance loss\n- defender turning and exposing back or arm during escape\n\nSynochi should recognise knee-on-belly as a mobile pressure-and-transition platform.\n\nIts value comes from forcing reactions while preserving the attacker’s ability to move, rather than from static pressure alone."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Kneebar.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Kneebar","body":"# Kneebar\n\n## Type\nLeg Lock Submission / Linear Joint Attack\n\n## Purpose\nThe kneebar is a leg lock that isolates the opponent’s leg and applies extension pressure through the knee.\n\nThe visible finish occurs at the knee joint, but the submission depends on controlling the larger relationship between:\n\n- hip\n- thigh\n- knee line\n- lower leg\n- rotational freedom\n\nA strong kneebar position prevents the opponent from freely turning, bending the knee, or withdrawing the leg.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe kneebar functions through controlled hyperextension of the knee.\n\nThe attacker uses their hips and body position to create a fulcrum while controlling the opponent’s lower leg.\n\nThe goal is to align the opponent’s knee with the attacker’s hip line so force transfers efficiently through the joint.\n\nThe finishing mechanic becomes weaker when the opponent can rotate the knee, bend the leg, or move the hip freely.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- one leg isolated between the attacker’s legs\n- the opponent’s knee positioned near the attacker’s hip line\n- the thigh controlled strongly enough to prevent easy withdrawal\n- the lower leg secured with the arms\n- the attacker’s knees controlling the opponent’s thigh\n- the opponent’s hip rotation restricted\n\nThe attacker should control the leg as a whole rather than pulling only on the foot.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- isolate one leg\n- control the knee line\n- keep the thigh connected tightly\n- prevent the opponent from rotating\n- control the lower leg close to the body\n- maintain hip alignment with the knee\n- remove slack before extending\n\n## Rotation Control\nRotational freedom is one of the most important variables in kneebar control.\n\nIf the defender can freely turn the knee inward or outward, they may reduce extension pressure and begin escaping.\n\nThe attacker should therefore monitor:\n\n- hip orientation\n- knee direction\n- foot direction\n- ability of the defender to spin\n\nA kneebar is strongest when the leg is controlled as one aligned structure.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- bend the knee\n- rotate the hip\n- turn the knee away from the finishing line\n- clear the attacker’s knees\n- withdraw the knee line\n- control the attacker’s hands\n- connect to the attacker’s legs\n- spin or move into a safer alignment\n\nEarly rotation is usually easier than late rotation once the thigh is fully controlled.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Knee line too shallow\nThe attacker controls the lower leg but the knee remains outside the strongest part of the hip structure.\n\nResult:\nthe defender can withdraw the leg.\n\n### Loose thigh control\nThe opponent’s leg moves independently inside the attacker’s knees.\n\nResult:\nrotation and bending become easier.\n\n### Pulling on the foot\nThe attacker focuses on the ankle rather than controlling the entire leg.\n\nResult:\nthe defender rotates and relieves pressure.\n\n### Poor hip alignment\nThe attacker’s hips are not positioned directly against the knee line.\n\nResult:\nextension becomes inefficient.\n\n### Overextending before controlling rotation\nThe attacker attempts to finish while the opponent still has rotational freedom.\n\nResult:\nthe defender turns out of the pressure.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a kneebar when:\n\n- one athlete isolates the opponent’s leg between their own legs\n- the opponent’s knee is positioned near the attacker’s hips\n- the attacker controls the lower leg with both arms\n- the defender is trying to bend or rotate the knee\n- the exchange revolves around maintaining or extracting the knee line\n- the attacker extends the hips while keeping the leg aligned\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the knee line is deeply controlled\n- whether the opponent can bend the leg\n- whether the thigh is tightly isolated\n- whether the defender can rotate the hip\n- whether the attacker’s hips align with the knee\n- whether the attacker controls the entire lower leg\n- what movement creates the defender’s escape\n\n## Safety Interpretation\nKneebars can damage the knee rapidly if finishing pressure is applied aggressively.\n\nTraining application should prioritise:\n\n- progressive extension\n- controlled hip pressure\n- early tapping\n- avoiding explosive bridging\n- maintaining awareness during rotational defence\n\nFRAME should treat uncontrolled finishing mechanics as unsafe.\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a kneebar repeatedly fails, inspect positional alignment before adding more extension pressure.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the knee line deep enough?\n- Is the thigh actually trapped?\n- Can the opponent rotate?\n- Is the attacker controlling the whole leg?\n- Are the hips aligned with the knee?\n\nThe finish should emerge from positional control rather than force.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe kneebar is a linear isolation system.\n\nThe attacker removes the opponent’s ability to bend, rotate, or withdraw the leg, then aligns the knee against a controlled extension force.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nisolate,\ncontrol knee line,\nrestrict rotation,\nalign hips,\nremove slack,\napply controlled extension."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Lasso Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Lasso Guard","body":"[[Spider Guard]]\n[[Supine Open Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nLasso Guard is an open guard configuration where the bottom athlete threads one leg around and underneath the opponent’s arm, usually while maintaining wrist, sleeve, or upper body control, to create strong rotational control over the opponent’s shoulder and posture.\n\nIt should be understood as a control and angle creation system rather than simply a leg wrapped around the arm.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- control the opponent’s arm and shoulder line\n- maintain tension through the lasso leg\n- prevent the opponent from freely retracting the trapped arm\n- create asymmetry between the opponent’s shoulders and hips\n- disrupt posture\n- control distance\n- force predictable stepping and posting reactions\n- create angles for sweeps, submissions, or transitions\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- sweep toward the lasso side\n- tripod style sweep\n- technical stand up\n- triangle\n- omoplata\n- armbar\n- back exposure\n- transition to Spider Guard\n- transition to De La Riva Guard\n- transition to Single Leg X Guard\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- free the trapped arm\n- reduce tension in the lasso\n- square the shoulders and hips\n- control the bottom athlete’s free leg\n- prevent the bottom athlete from creating a strong angle\n- collapse or step around the lasso structure\n- recover posture before committing to the pass\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- depth of the lasso\n- shoulder control\n- tension through the lasso leg\n- wrist or sleeve control\n- hip angle\n- control of the opponent’s free arm\n- opponent stance\n- ability to maintain asymmetry\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- lasso leg wrapped without meaningful shoulder control\n- opponent retracting the arm and clearing the structure\n- bottom athlete using the lasso to rotate the opponent’s upper body\n- strong tension forcing the opponent to post\n- sweep developing as the opponent shifts weight away from the trapped arm\n- transition back to Spider Guard when the lasso is cleared\n- top player controlling the free leg before attempting to pass\n\nSynochi should recognise Lasso Guard as an upper body rotational control system.\n\nIts effectiveness comes from using the lasso to distort shoulder alignment and create asymmetry, then converting the opponent’s reactions into sweeps, submissions, or transitions."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Leg Drag.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Leg Drag","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Headquarters Position]]\n[[Side Control]]\n[[Back Control]]\n\n## Type\nGuard Passing Position / Transitional Control System\n\n## Purpose\nThe leg drag is a passing position built around redirecting both of the opponent’s legs to one side while the passer moves around the opposite side of the hips.\n\nIts value comes from creating hip misalignment.\n\nThe bottom athlete’s legs are displaced away from the side they need to defend, while the passer begins occupying the space beside the hips.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- both of the bottom athlete’s legs are directed toward one side\n- the passer controls near the knees, shins, ankles, or hip line\n- the passer positions their body beside the opponent’s hips\n- the bottom athlete is prevented from squaring their knees back toward the passer\n- the passer begins connecting upper-body control to the displaced lower-body structure\n\nThe leg drag can function as a passing position, a transitional control state, or an entry into back exposure.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe leg drag works through rotational mismatch.\n\nThe opponent’s lower body is turned away while the passer advances around the hips.\n\nThis creates a structural problem:\n\nthe legs are pointing one direction\n\nwhile the opponent needs to rotate the torso and hips back toward the passer to recover guard.\n\nThe passer’s objective is to prevent that reconnection.\n\n## Entry Conditions\nCommon entries include:\n\n- [[Headquarters Position]]\n- torreando passing exchanges\n- failed guard retention\n- De La Riva passing\n- reverse De La Riva passing\n- transitions from knee cut reactions\n- redirects from seated or supine open guard\n- defensive reactions to leg-entry attempts\n\nThe leg drag often appears when the passer redirects rather than drives directly through the guard.\n\n## Hip Control\nControl of the hip line is critical.\n\nThe passer may use:\n\n- knee pressure\n- shin control\n- hip posting\n- torso connection\n- near-side underhook\n- waist control\n- head position\n\nThe goal is to stop the bottom athlete from simply rotating back to face the passer.\n\n## Upper Body Progression\nOnce the legs are displaced, the passer should connect to the upper body.\n\nCommon progressions include:\n\n- chest-to-chest control\n- near-side underhook\n- crossface\n- far shoulder control\n- seatbelt-style connection if the back becomes exposed\n- transition to [[Side Control]]\n- transition to [[Back Control]]\n\nWithout upper-body connection, the leg drag may remain temporary.\n\n## Bottom Player Defensive Priorities\nThe bottom athlete generally wants to:\n\n- square the hips back toward the passer\n- recover knee elbow connection\n- frame against the passer’s torso\n- create shoulder rotation\n- insert the near knee back inside\n- free the dragged legs before upper-body control settles\n- turn strategically rather than remaining twisted\n\nEarly recovery is usually easier than late recovery.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Controlling legs without controlling hips\nThe passer moves the legs aside but does not occupy the space beside the hips.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent simply squares back up.\n\n### Staying too far away\nThe passer creates displacement but leaves too much distance.\n\nResult:\nthe bottom athlete rebuilds open guard.\n\n### Reaching for upper-body control too early\nThe passer abandons lower-body control before the hips are stabilised.\n\nResult:\nthe legs return to the centre line.\n\n### Allowing shoulder rotation\nThe bottom athlete turns strongly toward the passer.\n\nResult:\nguard recovery becomes available.\n\n### Driving straight forward\nThe passer treats the leg drag like a pressure pass rather than a redirection.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent regains alignment and defensive structure.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a leg drag when:\n\n- both bottom legs are displaced toward the same side\n- the passer is positioned beside rather than directly in front of the hips\n- the bottom athlete is visibly twisted or attempting to square back\n- the passer controls the knees, shins, ankles, or hip line\n- the exchange progresses toward side control or back exposure\n- guard recovery depends on rotating the hips back toward the passer\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether both legs are genuinely displaced\n- whether the passer has reached the side of the hips\n- whether the bottom athlete can square back\n- how the passer controls the near hip\n- whether upper-body connection is established\n- whether the bottom athlete turns toward or away\n- whether the leg drag becomes [[Side Control]] or [[Back Control]]\n- at what moment the bottom athlete loses alignment\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a leg drag repeatedly fails, inspect whether the athlete is actually controlling the hip line.\n\nMoving the legs is only the visible entry.\n\nThe pass becomes effective when the passer occupies the displaced side and prevents the opponent from rebuilding alignment.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe leg drag is an alignment-disruption passing system.\n\nThe passer separates the orientation of the opponent’s lower body from the direction needed for effective guard retention.\n\nThe resulting advantage comes from forcing the bottom athlete to spend time restoring alignment while the passer advances into control.\n\nThe critical transition is:\n\ndisplace,\noccupy,\nconnect,\nstabilise."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Mount.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Mount","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nMount is a dominant top position where the attacker controls the opponent from above the torso while restricting hip movement, upper-body rotation, and access to meaningful defensive frames.\n\nMount should be understood as a pressure-and-isolation system rather than simply a scoring position.\n\nCommon configurations include:\n\n- low mount\n- high mount\n- technical mount\n- S-mount\n- grapevine mount\n- cross-body mount\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control the head and shoulders\n- separate elbows from the ribs\n- limit hip rotation\n- climb higher as defensive structure breaks down\n- force predictable bridging or elbow-escape reactions\n- isolate arms\n- expose the neck\n\nA strong mount progressively reduces the defender’s available structure.\n\nAs the defender reacts, the attacker should improve position rather than chase submissions prematurely.\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- arm triangle\n- mounted triangle\n- armbar\n- Americana\n- gift wrap\n- back take\n- high mount progression\n- S-mount transitions\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- protect the neck and arms\n- recover elbow position\n- reconnect elbows toward knees\n- create effective frames\n- establish foot connection to the floor\n- recover hip movement\n- create angle rather than bridging directly into stable base\n- recover half guard or full guard\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- head control\n- shoulder rotation\n- elbow position\n- hip height\n- knee position\n- base width\n- weight distribution\n- ability to follow bridging direction\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- top player sitting too upright without controlling upper-body movement\n- knees too wide or too low to restrict hip recovery\n- defender recovering elbow-knee connection\n- bridge attempts occurring without control of posting structure\n- attacker climbing from low mount to high mount as elbows separate\n- arm isolation created by defensive framing\n- transition to technical mount when the defender turns\n\nSynochi should recognise mount as a progressive control system.\n\nThe objective is not simply to remain on top, but to use pressure, alignment disruption, and defensive reactions to climb toward increasingly restrictive control and higher-value attacks."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/North-South.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"North-South","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nNorth-south is a top-control position where the attacker rotates around the opponent’s head and upper body, using chest, hip, and shoulder positioning to limit turning while opening new attacking angles.\n\nIt is both a pinning position and a transition hub.\n\nCommon entries include:\n\n- side control transitions\n- reactions to frames\n- movement around the head during guard passing\n- failed underhook recovery\n- defensive turning from side control\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control head and shoulder orientation\n- deny the opponent easy turning toward the attacker\n- maintain chest connection\n- keep the hips far enough away to reduce leg involvement\n- isolate arms as the defender frames\n- use rotational control to create submission or positional pathways\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- north-south choke\n- kimura\n- armbar\n- return to side control\n- transition to mount\n- back exposure when the defender turns\n- spin-behind control\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- recover head movement\n- build effective frames\n- turn onto a useful side rather than remaining flat\n- create enough space to reconnect knees and elbows\n- recover guard or come to a seated position\n- avoid exposing isolated arms during framing\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- chest position\n- head control\n- shoulder angle\n- hip distance\n- arm isolation\n- ability to follow rotational movement\n- connection during transitions\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- top player rotating without maintaining chest connection\n- defender recovering strong frames against the shoulders\n- excessive hip proximity allowing leg recovery\n- attacker isolating an arm during defensive framing\n- defender turning and exposing back access\n- north-south used as a temporary transition rather than a stable pin\n\nSynochi should recognise north-south as a rotational control position that connects side control, submissions, mount transitions, and back exposure."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Octopus Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Octopus Guard","body":"[[Half Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nRuleset:\nBoth\n\nRuleset Notes:\n\n- Gi: belt and fabric grips can strengthen control around the waist and hips.\n- No Gi: relies more heavily on body connection, waist control, hip position, and rotational pressure.\n\nOctopus Guard is a Half Guard configuration where the bottom athlete comes around the opponent’s back or side, usually using a deep waist or body connection while staying connected to the trapped leg.\n\nIt should be understood as a rotational control and wrestle up position rather than simply a variation of Half Guard.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- get around the opponent’s upper body rather than remaining square underneath\n- establish strong connection around the waist or torso\n- keep control of the trapped leg\n- prevent the opponent from turning fully back toward the bottom athlete\n- build height\n- disrupt the opponent’s base\n- create rotational pressure\n- convert the position into a sweep, wrestle up, or back exposure\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- wrestle up\n- hip bump style reversal\n- back exposure\n- rear body lock\n- single leg finish\n- transition to Dogfight\n- transition to standing\n- sweep to top position\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- turn back toward the bottom athlete\n- deny the waist or body connection\n- flatten the bottom athlete\n- free the trapped leg\n- control the head and shoulders\n- prevent the bottom athlete from building height\n- redirect into chest to chest Half Guard or a passing position\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- depth of waist connection\n- hip angle\n- trapped leg control\n- ability to get around the opponent’s side\n- head position\n- shoulder position\n- rotational control\n- ability to build onto elbow, hand, or knee\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom athlete reaching around the waist without controlling rotation\n- strong body connection preventing the opponent from squaring\n- athlete successfully building height from the position\n- opponent turning back in and collapsing the angle\n- rotational pressure exposing the back\n- transition from Octopus Guard into a wrestle up or rear body lock\n- trapped leg being freed before upper body control is established\n\nSynochi should recognise Octopus Guard as a rotational Half Guard system.\n\nIts effectiveness comes from getting around the opponent’s side, maintaining strong body connection, and using that positional relationship to build height and attack the opponent’s base rather than remaining underneath chest to chest pressure."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Omoplata.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Omoplata","body":"# Omoplata\n\n## Type\nShoulder Lock / Guard Control and Transition System\n\n## Purpose\nThe Omoplata is a shoulder-control system built around trapping one of the opponent’s arms between the attacker’s legs while using the hips and torso to rotate the opponent’s shoulder away from a safe alignment.\n\nAlthough commonly treated as a submission, it also functions as:\n\n- a sweep\n- a posture-breaking system\n- a control position\n- a transition to top position\n- a route into further shoulder attacks\n\nThe position becomes strong when the attacker controls both the isolated shoulder and the opponent’s ability to rotate or posture out.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe Omoplata works by isolating the opponent’s arm and using the attacker’s legs and hips to rotate the shoulder internally while preventing the opponent from turning with the pressure.\n\nThe submission therefore depends on controlling:\n\n- arm isolation\n- shoulder orientation\n- posture\n- hip distance\n- rotational escape\n\nThe leg structure traps the arm, but the attacker’s body position determines whether that isolation becomes meaningful control.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- one of the opponent’s arms trapped between the attacker’s legs\n- the attacker’s thigh controlling across the opponent’s shoulder\n- the opponent’s posture broken forward or sideways\n- the attacker positioned at an angle beside the opponent\n- the opponent prevented from freely rolling or turning\n- the attacker progressing toward sitting up over the trapped shoulder\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- keep the opponent’s arm trapped\n- control the opponent’s hip or waist\n- prevent forward rolling\n- prevent posture recovery\n- rotate their own body toward the opponent’s side\n- move toward an upright finishing position\n- keep the opponent’s shoulder internally rotated\n\n## Angle Creation\nThe Omoplata becomes much stronger when the attacker moves off the opponent’s centre line.\n\nRemaining directly underneath or behind the opponent often leaves too much rotational freedom.\n\nCreating angle helps:\n\n- increase shoulder isolation\n- prevent posture recovery\n- improve hip control\n- make rolling escapes harder\n- create a cleaner finishing line\n\n## Control of the Opponent’s Hips\nHip control is a major part of finishing and stabilising the position.\n\nIf the opponent can freely move the hips, they may:\n\n- roll forward\n- spin\n- posture\n- rotate the shoulder into a safer alignment\n\nThe attacker may use the arms, legs, or torso position to prevent this movement.\n\n## Offensive Pathways\nThe Omoplata may lead to:\n\n- shoulder lock finish\n- sweep to top position\n- transition into another shoulder attack\n- back exposure\n- upper-body control after the opponent rolls\n- re-entry into guard attacks if the position breaks down\n\nA failed submission attempt can still produce strong positional advantage.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- posture before the attacker sits up\n- roll forward\n- rotate the trapped shoulder\n- free the elbow\n- control the attacker’s legs\n- move the hips away from the finishing angle\n- prevent the attacker from controlling the waist\n\nEarly posture recovery is usually easier than escaping after the attacker has sat upright and controlled the hips.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Remaining flat\nThe attacker isolates the shoulder but stays on the back.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent retains more rotational freedom.\n\n### Failing to control the hips\nThe attacker focuses entirely on the trapped arm.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent rolls or spins out.\n\n### Sitting up without angle\nThe attacker comes upright directly behind the opponent.\n\nResult:\nshoulder pressure is reduced.\n\n### Loose leg control\nThe opponent’s elbow slips free from the attacking structure.\n\nResult:\nthe Omoplata collapses.\n\n### Chasing the shoulder lock too early\nThe attacker applies rotational pressure before stabilising posture and hip control.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent uses the movement to escape.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify an Omoplata when:\n\n- one athlete traps an opponent’s arm between the legs\n- the attacker’s thigh sits across the opponent’s shoulder\n- the opponent is folded forward or sideways\n- the attacker begins rotating toward an upright seated position\n- the defender attempts to roll forward\n- the exchange revolves around shoulder rotation and hip control\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the arm remains fully isolated\n- whether the attacker creates angle\n- whether the opponent can posture\n- whether the attacker controls the hips\n- whether the defender can roll freely\n- whether the attacker reaches an upright controlling position\n- whether the attack becomes a submission, sweep, or transition\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf an Omoplata repeatedly fails, inspect control of the opponent’s posture and hips before adding more shoulder pressure.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the arm genuinely trapped?\n- Can the opponent roll freely?\n- Has the attacker created enough angle?\n- Is the attacker sitting up into control?\n- Can the opponent posture before the position stabilises?\n\nThe Omoplata becomes reliable when shoulder isolation is combined with control of the opponent’s larger body movement.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Omoplata is a shoulder-isolation and rotational-control system.\n\nThe attacker uses the legs to isolate the arm, then progressively restricts the opponent’s ability to posture, rotate, or roll safely.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nisolate arm,\nbreak posture,\ncreate angle,\ncontrol hips,\nsit up,\nrestrict rotation,\nfinish or transition."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Outside Ashi.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Outside Ashi","body":"[[Single-Leg X Guard]]\n[[Saddle]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nOutside Ashi is a leg-entanglement position where the attacker controls one of the opponent’s legs while positioning both legs to the outside of that leg, creating a strong connection around the hip and knee line.\n\nOutside Ashi should be understood as a leg-control and finishing platform, not simply an ankle-lock position.\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- maintain control above the opponent’s knee line\n- keep the hips connected to the controlled leg\n- prevent the opponent from freely turning the knee away\n- control the secondary leg or posting structure where possible\n- create separation between the opponent’s leg and their upper-body base\n- expose the ankle and heel without sacrificing positional control\n- use defensive reactions to transition into stronger entanglements where appropriate\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- straight ankle lock\n- Achilles lock\n- outside heel exposure where ruleset and position permit\n- technical stand-up sweep\n- transition to Single-Leg X\n- transition to Saddle\n- transition to Cross Ashi\n- top-position conversion after the opponent loses balance\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- recover the knee line\n- prevent the attacker from controlling hip rotation\n- remove secondary-leg control\n- turn the knee toward a mechanically safer orientation\n- control or separate the attacker’s feet\n- rebuild base before attempting full extraction\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- knee-line depth\n- hip-to-leg connection\n- orientation of the opponent’s knee\n- control of the secondary leg\n- attacker’s ability to extend the opponent’s posture\n- foot and ankle exposure\n- rotational freedom\n- opponent’s ability to build height\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- attacker falling backward onto the foot while losing control of the knee\n- opponent successfully extracting the knee before the finish develops\n- secondary-leg control preventing the opponent from standing or rotating\n- strong hip extension creating ankle-lock pressure\n- opponent turning and exposing a transition to another leg entanglement\n- attacker using the position to sweep rather than forcing a finish\n\nSynochi should recognise Outside Ashi as a control-first leg-entanglement system.\n\nFinishing quality depends on maintaining positional control, knee-line depth, and hip connection before escalating pressure onto the foot or ankle."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Over Under Pass.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Over Under Pass","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Half Guard]]\n[[Side Control]]\n\n## Type\nPressure Passing System / Leg Control Pass\n\n## Purpose\nThe over under pass is a pressure passing system built around controlling both of the opponent’s legs asymmetrically.\n\nOne arm typically controls over one leg while the other arm controls underneath the opposite leg.\n\nThis creates a strong connection to the opponent’s hips and allows the passer to compress the guard while progressively moving around the legs.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- one arm controlling over a leg\n- one arm controlling underneath the opposite leg\n- the passer’s chest connected closely to the opponent’s thighs or hips\n- head position used to reinforce pressure and deny rotation\n- the opponent’s legs compressed together or redirected\n- the passer circling toward the side of the overhooked leg\n\nThe exact grips vary, but the defining feature is asymmetrical control of the legs combined with heavy hip pressure.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe over under pass works by compressing the opponent’s lower-body structure and reducing independent leg movement.\n\nThe passer attempts to:\n\n- bind the legs together\n- limit hip rotation\n- collapse knee position\n- remove space between the opponent’s thighs and torso\n- move around the guard while maintaining compression\n\nThe pass becomes effective when the opponent can no longer freely separate the knees and reconstruct guard.\n\n## Entry Conditions\nCommon entries include:\n\n- open guard\n- butterfly guard\n- half guard transitions\n- failed torreando attempts\n- reactions to standing passing\n- pressure entries from kneeling or crouched positions\n- situations where both legs can be collected close together\n\nThe position is especially useful when the bottom athlete’s legs become extended or compressed into a narrow defensive structure.\n\n## Head Position\nHead position often determines whether the passer can maintain directional pressure.\n\nUseful head placement may:\n\n- block the opponent from turning toward the passer\n- prevent strong upper-body frames\n- reinforce shoulder pressure\n- help pin the hips indirectly\n\nA loose head position can allow the bottom athlete to rotate, frame, or create space.\n\n## Hip Control\nThe passer should remain closely connected to the opponent’s hip line.\n\nImportant goals include:\n\n- preventing the hips from freely turning\n- stopping the knees from separating\n- keeping the opponent’s legs compressed\n- advancing around the legs without creating space\n\nDistance generally weakens the pass.\n\n## Bottom Player Defensive Priorities\nThe bottom athlete usually wants to:\n\n- separate the knees\n- restore hip mobility\n- create frames against the passer\n- rotate toward the passer\n- recover inside leg position\n- establish a knee shield or butterfly hook\n- free the leg controlled by the underhooking arm\n- create enough space to return to open guard\n\nThe longer the legs remain compressed, the harder recovery becomes.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Loose compression\nThe passer controls both legs but does not connect the opponent’s knees and hips tightly enough.\n\nResult:\nthe bottom athlete separates the legs and reconstructs guard.\n\n### Hips too far away\nThe passer’s arms control the legs but the torso remains distant.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent retains rotational freedom.\n\n### Head position lost\nThe bottom athlete can turn toward the passer.\n\nResult:\nframes, underhooks, and guard recovery become available.\n\n### Circling without pressure\nThe passer tries to move around the legs before stabilising the compressed structure.\n\nResult:\nthe guard follows the passer.\n\n### Overcommitting forward\nThe passer places too much weight ahead of the base.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent may create elevation, inversion, or reversal opportunities.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify an over under pass when:\n\n- one arm is visibly over one leg and the other underneath the opposite leg\n- the opponent’s knees are compressed together\n- the passer is driving chest pressure into the thighs or hips\n- the passer circles around the legs rather than attempting to jump over them\n- the bottom athlete is trying to separate the knees or recover hip rotation\n- the exchange progresses toward [[Side Control]]\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the opponent’s legs are genuinely compressed\n- whether the passer stays connected to the hips\n- the direction of the passer’s head\n- whether the bottom athlete can turn toward the passer\n- whether the knees separate during the pass\n- whether the passer loses pressure while circling\n- which defensive frame or hip movement creates recovery\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf an over under pass repeatedly stalls, inspect compression before movement.\n\nThe athlete may be trying to circle around a guard that has not actually been constrained.\n\nThe pass becomes easier when the opponent’s lower body is first converted into a narrow, connected structure.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe over under pass is a compression-based passing system.\n\nThe passer reduces the opponent’s lower-body degrees of freedom by binding both legs into a constrained configuration.\n\nOnce hip mobility and knee separation are reduced, the passer can advance around the guard with fewer defensive variables to manage.\n\nThe strategic sequence is:\n\ncollect,\ncompress,\ndeny rotation,\ncircle,\nstabilise."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Pinning Mechanics.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Pinning Mechanics","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nA pin is not simply holding someone down.\n\nA functional pin limits the opponent’s ability to:\n\n- rotate,\n- reconnect their limbs,\n- rebuild posture,\n- recover inside position,\n- create meaningful frames,\n- or move their centre away from control.\n\nThe central idea is:\n\n**A strong pin controls the movements required for escape.**\n\n## **What Makes a Pin Strong**\n\nA pin becomes stronger when several elements work together:\n\n- connection,\n- pressure,\n- wedges,\n- head control,\n- shoulder control,\n- hip control,\n- base,\n- and directional force.\n\nThe position should not depend on one control point alone.\n\nSeveral controls should combine so that removing one does not immediately restore freedom.\n\n## **Rotation Control**\n\nMany escapes depend on rotation.\n\nThe bottom athlete may need to:\n\n- turn onto a side,\n- bring a knee inside,\n- bridge and rotate,\n- turn toward the opponent,\n- or turn away to expose a new pathway.\n\nA strong pin often controls one or both rotational directions.\n\nThis can be achieved through:\n\n- head position,\n- shoulder control,\n- hip control,\n- wedges,\n- or pressure across opposing body segments.\n\n## **Shoulder–Hip Relationship**\n\nThe shoulders and hips are major rotational structures.\n\nIf both can rotate freely together, escapes become easier.\n\nIf they are controlled in opposing directions, movement becomes much harder.\n\nFor example:\n\nShoulders controlled one way while hips are prevented from following.\n\nThis creates structural tension and reduces rotational freedom.\n\n## **Head Control**\n\nThe head strongly influences spinal rotation.\n\nTurning or fixing the head can reduce the opponent’s ability to turn the rest of the body efficiently.\n\nThis is why head position can significantly strengthen a pin even when it appears to be a small detail.\n\n## **Hip Control**\n\nThe hips are central to:\n\n- bridging,\n- shrimping,\n- guard recovery,\n- turning,\n- and generating space.\n\nA pin that controls the upper body but allows free hip movement may remain unstable.\n\nLikewise, controlling the hips without controlling the shoulders may allow upper-body rotation and framing.\n\nStrong pins often connect both.\n\n## **Inside Position**\n\nMaintaining dominant inside position helps prevent the bottom athlete from rebuilding defensive structure.\n\nThe athlete on top should recognise attempts to recover:\n\n- knee-elbow connection,\n- inside frames,\n- underhooks,\n- or head position.\n\nThe pin should respond before those structures become fully established.\n\n## **Wedges**\n\n[[Wedges]] help maintain positional boundaries.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- knee against hip,\n- elbow against shoulder line,\n- head occupying turning space,\n- thigh preventing knee recovery.\n\nA wedge can stop an escape before the movement develops.\n\n## **Pressure**\n\n[[SPINE/Pressure]] reinforces the pin by degrading posture and making movement more expensive.\n\nHowever, pressure without structural control can be escaped.\n\nThe strongest pins combine pressure with mechanical restriction.\n\n## **Connection**\n\n[[Connection]] allows the top athlete to feel and follow escape attempts.\n\nA connected pin can adapt as the bottom athlete moves.\n\nA disconnected pin may look dominant but create enough space for the opponent to rebuild structure.\n\n## **Base**\n\nThe top athlete still needs sufficient [[Base]] to avoid being reversed.\n\nOvercommitting pressure without adequate support may create instability.\n\nThe pin therefore balances:\n\n**control of the opponent**\n\nwith:\n\n**stability of the controlling athlete.**\n\n## **Dynamic Pinning**\n\nPins are not always static.\n\nA strong pin can change shape while preserving control.\n\nFor example:\n\n- side control to mount,\n- mount to technical mount,\n- north-south to side control,\n- chest-to-chest control into back exposure.\n\nThe goal is not to freeze one position.\n\nIt is to maintain control while the configuration changes.\n\n## **Pinning as Constraint Accumulation**\n\nA strong pin is a practical example of [[Constraint Accumulation]].\n\nThe athlete progressively removes:\n\n- rotation,\n- inside space,\n- posting options,\n- hip mobility,\n- and frame recovery.\n\nAs these options disappear, the opponent’s responses become more predictable.\n\n## **Escape Perspective**\n\nFrom the defensive side, escaping a pin often requires identifying which constraint is most important.\n\nThe athlete should ask:\n\nWhat is currently stopping me from moving?\n\nThat may be:\n\n- head control,\n- shoulder pinning,\n- hip control,\n- loss of inside position,\n- or lack of base.\n\nRemoving the critical constraint can create the rest of the escape.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether shoulders and hips are both controlled,\n- whether rotation remains available,\n- whether the bottom athlete is rebuilding inside position,\n- whether the top athlete’s wedges are maintained,\n- whether pressure is directional,\n- and whether the pin fails because one major control point disappears.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou had strong chest pressure, but their hips remained free. Once they turned onto their side, they rebuilt knee-elbow connection and recovered guard.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only saying:\n\n“Hold side control tighter.”\n\nFRAME should identify the missing component:\n\n“Control the far hip so their shoulders and hips can’t rotate together.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Keep the head turned away while blocking the near hip.”\n\nThis makes the pin mechanically understandable rather than dependent on vague pressure.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**A pin is strongest when escape directions are systematically removed.**\n\nControl is not simply about weight.\n\nIt is about restricting the rotations, connections, and structural recoveries the opponent needs to escape.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Wedges]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Constraint Accumulation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Post Denial.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Post Denial","body":"[[Pinning Mechanics]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nBottom escape movement depends heavily on access to posts.\n\nPosts may include:\n\n- feet\n- elbows\n- hands\n- shoulders\n- knees\n\nPinning improves when the top athlete removes or degrades these posting surfaces.\n\nSynochi should analyse not only where pressure is applied, but which escape posts have been removed."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Progressive Escape Recovery.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Progressive Escape Recovery","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nLarge positional escapes are often built through smaller recoveries that progressively restore freedom of movement.\n\nExamples:\n\n- freeing the head\n- recovering one elbow\n- clearing one knee\n- removing one hook\n- turning one shoulder\n- rebuilding knee-elbow connection\n\nThe athlete does not always need to escape the entire position immediately.\n\nOften the first priority is to recover one piece of structure that makes the next movement possible.\n\nSynochi should recognise whether the athlete is progressively improving the defensive position rather than judging success only by whether full guard, standing, or another neutral position has already been recovered.\n\nEscape quality can therefore be measured by whether each movement creates a better defensive structure and opens the next layer of recovery."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Rear Body Lock.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Rear Body Lock","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Back Control]]\n[[Turtle]]\n\n\n## Type\nStanding Control Position / Wrestling to Back Transition\n\n## Purpose\nThe rear body lock is a standing control position established behind the opponent, typically with the hands connected around the waist or torso.\n\nIt is valuable because the controlling athlete has gained positional alignment behind the opponent while restricting their ability to turn and face.\n\nThe rear body lock can lead to:\n\n- mat returns\n- trips\n- lifts\n- back takes\n- transitions into turtle control\n- riding positions\n- submission entries after the opponent reaches the floor\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- chest connected to the opponent’s back\n- hands locked around the waist or torso\n- head positioned close to the opponent\n- hips connected closely enough to influence balance\n- the opponent prevented from freely rotating to face\n- the controlling athlete positioned behind rather than beside the opponent\n\nThe exact hand configuration can vary.\n\nWhat defines the position is posterior control of the opponent’s centre.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe rear body lock works by controlling the opponent from behind while reducing their ability to generate effective forward-facing defence.\n\nThe controlling athlete gains access to:\n\n- the opponent’s hips\n- their balance\n- their rotational direction\n- their base\n\nBecause the opponent cannot easily see or frame directly against the attacker, the position creates strong control asymmetry.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe controlling athlete generally wants to:\n\n- keep chest-to-back connection\n- control the hips\n- maintain head position\n- prevent the opponent turning inward\n- disrupt the opponent’s base\n- transition to the mat without losing rear alignment\n\nThe body lock should function as a moving control structure rather than a static grip.\n\n## Common Offensive Pathways\nCommon continuations include:\n\n- inside trip\n- outside trip\n- mat return\n- lift and return\n- knee block\n- foot sweep\n- spiral ride\n- transition to [[Turtle Position]]\n- transition to [[Back Control]]\n\nThe correct option depends heavily on the opponent’s stance and reaction.\n\n## Opponent Defensive Priorities\nThe defending athlete usually wants to:\n\n- break or separate the hands\n- lower their centre of gravity\n- widen the base\n- turn to face\n- clear the attacker’s head position\n- peel grips while maintaining balance\n- use the boundary or mat strategically where rules allow\n\nThe most important objective is usually to destroy rear alignment before the attacker progresses.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Hips too far away\nThe hands remain connected but the attacker’s hips are distant.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent retains balance and can begin turning.\n\n### Chest connection lost\nThe attacker controls the waist but not the torso.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent creates rotational space.\n\n### Head position weak\nThe attacker allows the opponent’s shoulders to turn.\n\nResult:\nthe defender may square up.\n\n### Chasing the takedown\nThe attacker forces a trip or lift before stabilising control.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent escapes during the transition.\n\n### Standing directly behind without angle\nThe attacker does not adapt to the opponent’s stance.\n\nResult:\nmat-return options become predictable and easier to defend.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a rear body lock when:\n\n- one athlete is directly behind the other\n- both arms are connected around the waist or torso\n- chest-to-back contact is maintained\n- the defending athlete is trying to peel the hands or turn\n- the exchange leads toward a mat return or back control\n- the attacker repeatedly manipulates the opponent’s base from behind\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the hands are locked securely\n- whether the attacker’s hips remain connected\n- whether the defender can rotate\n- the position of the attacker’s head\n- which leg of the defender is loaded\n- whether the attacker chooses a trip, lift, or controlled descent\n- whether rear control survives the transition to the mat\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf rear body lock attacks repeatedly fail, inspect control before takedown selection.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the attacker genuinely behind the hips?\n- Can the opponent turn?\n- Is chest connection maintained?\n- Are the hands being protected?\n- Is the chosen mat return appropriate for the opponent’s stance?\n\nA strong rear body lock often creates the takedown rather than requiring the takedown to be forced.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe rear body lock is a posterior control state.\n\nThe attacker gains structural advantage by occupying a direction the opponent cannot defend easily while directly influencing the hips and base.\n\nIts strategic value comes from maintaining rear alignment across transitions.\n\nThe core sequence is:\n\nget behind,\nconnect,\ndeny rotation,\nbreak balance,\nreturn,\npreserve rear control."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Reverse De La Riva Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Reverse De La Riva Guard","body":"[[Supine Open Guard]]\n[[De La Riva Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nReverse De La Riva Guard is an open guard configuration where the bottom athlete controls the inside or near side of the opponent’s leg while using the legs and hips to influence knee direction, manage distance, and create rotational access underneath or around the opponent.\n\nIt should be understood as an angle creation and transition guard rather than simply the opposite version of De La Riva Guard.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- control the opponent’s near leg\n- maintain useful inside leg position\n- influence the direction of the opponent’s knee\n- prevent the opponent from freely squaring the hips\n- create space underneath the opponent’s base\n- preserve access to the far leg\n- use rotation to create sweeps, leg entries, or backside pathways\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- transition to K Guard\n- transition to 50 50 Guard\n- transition to Backside 50 50\n- transition to Single Leg X Guard\n- wrestle up\n- inversion underneath the opponent\n- back exposure\n- sweep to top position\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- clear the controlling hook\n- square the hips\n- control the bottom athlete’s free leg\n- deny access underneath the hip line\n- prevent the bottom athlete from rotating underneath\n- free the knee line\n- create a passing angle before the guard becomes deeper\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- hook placement\n- knee orientation\n- hip angle\n- distance\n- inside position\n- access to the far leg\n- opponent stance\n- ability to rotate underneath the center of mass\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom athlete controlling the leg but failing to create angle\n- top player squaring the hips and neutralising the hook\n- reverse De La Riva position creating access underneath the hip line\n- transition into K Guard as the bottom athlete rotates deeper\n- opponent stepping in a way that exposes Backside 50 50\n- bottom athlete losing inside position and being forced into a weaker open guard\n\nSynochi should recognise Reverse De La Riva Guard as a rotational entry position.\n\nIts value comes from controlling the opponent’s near leg while creating an angle underneath the hips, which can open pathways into K Guard, 50 50 Guard, backside positions, wrestle ups, and other lower body systems."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Reverse Half Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Reverse Half Guard","body":"[[Half Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nReverse Half Guard is a half guard configuration where the bottom athlete controls one of the opponent’s legs while facing toward the opponent’s legs rather than directly toward the upper body.\n\nIt should be understood as a rotational guard and transition position rather than simply an unusual Half Guard variation.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- maintain control of the trapped leg\n- prevent the opponent from freely clearing the knee line\n- use hip rotation to stay connected underneath the opponent\n- create access to the far leg\n- disrupt the opponent’s base\n- prevent chest to chest flattening\n- create pathways into sweeps, leg entanglements, or guard recovery\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- transition to Deep Half Guard\n- transition to 50 50 Guard\n- transition to Single Leg X Guard\n- backside leg entry\n- rotational sweep\n- wrestle up\n- recovery back to standard Half Guard\n- transition underneath into leg entanglements\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- free the trapped knee line\n- square the hips away from the bottom athlete’s rotational angle\n- control the bottom athlete’s hips\n- prevent access to the far leg\n- flatten the bottom athlete where possible\n- clear the entanglement before committing weight forward\n- transition into side control, mount, or a passing position\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- knee line control\n- hip angle\n- rotational freedom\n- access to the far leg\n- control of the opponent’s hips\n- ability to stay underneath the center of mass\n- relationship between the bottom athlete’s shoulders and hips\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom athlete maintaining the trapped leg while rotating underneath\n- top player freeing the knee line and squaring the hips\n- far leg becoming accessible during the transition\n- bottom athlete using the position to enter 50 50 Guard or another leg entanglement\n- rotational sweep developing as the opponent’s weight shifts\n- loss of hip connection causing the position to collapse into a weaker guard\n\nSynochi should recognise Reverse Half Guard as a rotational transition position linking Half Guard, Deep Half Guard, and lower body entanglements.\n\nIts value comes from controlling the trapped leg while using hip rotation and access underneath the opponent to create sweeps, recoveries, and leg entry pathways."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Saddle.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Saddle","body":"[[Single-Leg X Guard]]\n[[X Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nSaddle is a leg-entanglement position where the attacker controls one of the opponent’s legs while using the legs and hips to restrict rotation, separate the opponent’s lower body from their upper-body base, and create access to finishing or transition pathways.\n\nSaddle should be understood as a control system rather than simply a heel-hook position.\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control the opponent’s knee line\n- maintain strong hip connection\n- limit free rotation\n- separate the controlled leg from the opponent’s ability to stand or turn\n- control the secondary leg where possible\n- force predictable defensive reactions\n- create finishing access without sacrificing positional control\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- inside heel hook\n- straight ankle lock variations where mechanically available\n- transition to cross ashi\n- transition to outside ashi\n- secondary-leg control\n- back exposure from defensive turning\n- sweep or top-position conversion\n- re-entry to X Guard or Single-Leg X if control is lost\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- protect the knee line\n- prevent heel exposure\n- clear the secondary leg when possible\n- recover rotational freedom\n- separate the attacker’s knees and hips\n- turn only in directions that do not worsen entanglement\n- extract the controlled knee before attempting full disengagement\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- depth of knee-line control\n- hip connection\n- position of the secondary leg\n- direction of the opponent’s knee\n- heel exposure\n- rotational control\n- distance between the opponent’s hips and available base\n- ability to follow defensive turning\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- attacker chasing the foot while losing knee-line control\n- defender freeing the knee before attempting rotation\n- strong secondary-leg control preventing escape\n- defensive turning creating greater heel exposure\n- attacker maintaining entanglement while changing finishing angle\n- opponent successfully separating hips and recovering base\n- transition between Saddle, X Guard, and Single-Leg X as the opponent changes position\n\nSynochi should recognise Saddle as a positional leg-control system.\n\nThe strongest attacks come from maintaining control of the knee line and secondary defensive structures first, then using the opponent’s reactions to expose the finishing pathway."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Seated Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Seated Guard","body":"[[Butterfly Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nSeated Guard is an open guard configuration where the bottom athlete remains upright or semi upright, using the feet, shins, hooks, grips, and upper body connection to manage distance and create entries.\n\nIt should be understood as a mobile attacking platform rather than a passive seated position.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- stay structurally upright\n- maintain useful inside position\n- control distance with the legs\n- deny easy chest to chest connection\n- create upper body attachment where useful\n- force the opponent to step, post, or change stance\n- enter underneath or outside the opponent’s base\n- connect into sweeps, wrestle ups, or leg entanglements\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- Butterfly Guard\n- Single Leg X Guard\n- X Guard\n- K Guard\n- wrestle up\n- arm drag\n- front headlock\n- single leg\n- back exposure\n- technical stand up\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- control the bottom athlete’s head and shoulders\n- deny strong inside hooks\n- force the seated athlete backward\n- control the feet and knees\n- prevent access underneath the hips\n- create passing angles before the bottom athlete establishes connection\n- force the guard player into weaker supine structure\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- posture\n- head position\n- inside leg position\n- distance\n- hip mobility\n- upper body connection\n- opponent stance width\n- access to the legs\n- ability to change level\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- seated athlete being forced flat\n- feet controlling distance without meaningful follow up\n- opponent stepping into range and exposing Single Leg X or Butterfly entries\n- arm drag creating rotational advantage\n- athlete building directly into a wrestle up\n- top player controlling both feet and denying inside position\n- seated posture remaining strong while the opponent circles\n\nSynochi should recognise Seated Guard as a branching entry position.\n\nIts value comes from maintaining posture and inside access while reading the opponent’s stance and movement, then selecting the appropriate pathway into wrestling, sweeping, or leg entanglement systems."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Side Control.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Side Control","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nSide control is a dominant top position where the attacker controls the opponent from the side while restricting shoulder rotation, hip movement, and the ability to rebuild inside position.\n\nSide control should be understood as a family of control configurations rather than one fixed shape.\n\nCommon configurations include:\n\n- chest-to-chest side control\n- crossface and underhook control\n- near-side underhook control\n- reverse crossface variations\n- scarf hold\n- modified scarf hold\n- north-south transitions\n- floating side control\n- hip-blocking side control\n\nPrimary attacking objectives include:\n\n- control head and shoulder alignment\n- prevent the near elbow from returning inside\n- separate shoulders from hips\n- block meaningful hip rotation\n- maintain chest connection\n- force the defender to carry pressure through compromised structure\n- create reactions that expose mount, back, submissions, or further pinning\n\nStrong side control is not simply about applying weight.\n\nPressure should be directed so that the defender’s movement becomes predictable.\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- mount\n- knee-on-belly\n- north-south\n- back exposure from turning reactions\n- arm triangle\n- kimura\n- Americana\n- near-side arm isolation\n- far-side arm attacks\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- protect the neck and inside arm position\n- recover frames\n- regain head movement\n- reconnect elbow toward knee\n- create hip angle\n- recover a knee inside\n- turn toward useful underhook or guard-recovery pathways\n- prevent progression to mount or back exposure\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- crossface strength\n- underhook position\n- head direction\n- near-side elbow access\n- hip blocking\n- chest angle\n- weight distribution\n- ability to follow the defender’s rotation\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- top player applying pressure without controlling head direction\n- defender recovering inside elbow position\n- excessive space between chest and torso\n- hips free enough for knee recovery\n- underhook creating a path to wrestle up\n- top player transitioning as the defender turns\n- pressure becoming static rather than adaptive\n\nSynochi should recognise side control as a dynamic pinning platform.\n\nThe strongest control usually comes from combining head control, upper-body connection, hip restriction, and reaction-based transitions rather than relying on bodyweight alone."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Single-Leg X Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Single-Leg X Guard","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nSingle-Leg X Guard is a leg-control position where the bottom athlete isolates one of the opponent’s legs while using the legs and hips to control distance, disrupt balance, and limit the opponent’s ability to freely rotate or disengage.\n\nSingle-Leg X should be understood as an off-balancing and transition system rather than a static leg entanglement.\n\nPrimary bottom-player objectives include:\n\n- isolate one leg\n- control the opponent’s knee line\n- maintain strong hip connection\n- disrupt posture and base\n- control the distance between hips\n- prevent easy back-stepping or leg extraction\n- create directional kuzushi\n- transition into sweeps, wrestle-ups, or deeper leg entanglements\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- technical stand-up sweep\n- outside sweep\n- inside sweep\n- wrestle-up\n- X-guard\n- saddle\n- straight ankle lock\n- outside ashi transitions\n- back exposure from defensive turning\n\nPrimary top-player objectives include:\n\n- recover balance\n- free the controlled knee line\n- control the bottom athlete’s feet and hips\n- prevent elevation\n- turn the knee and hip toward a safer angle\n- clear the entanglement before committing weight forward\n- begin passing once the leg is no longer isolated\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- knee-line control\n- hip connection\n- foot placement\n- direction of the opponent’s knee\n- distance between the hips\n- ability to extend or elevate the controlled leg\n- opponent’s available posting leg\n- upper-body connection\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom athlete controlling the ankle without controlling the knee line\n- hips drifting too far away from the opponent\n- top athlete successfully turning the knee free\n- off-balancing forcing the free leg to post\n- elevation creating transition to X-guard\n- defensive rotation exposing saddle or outside ashi\n- leg attack attempted without positional control\n\nSynochi should recognise Single-Leg X as a branching leg-control system.\n\nIts value comes from controlling the opponent’s base and creating predictable reactions that connect directly into sweeps, wrestle-ups, X-guard, and leg-entanglement pathways."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Smash Pass.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Smash Pass","body":"# Smash Pass\n\n## Type\nPressure Passing System / Hip Compression Pass\n\n## Purpose\nThe Smash Pass is a pressure passing method built around collapsing the opponent’s knees and hips into a constrained direction while the passer moves into increasingly dominant upper-body control.\n\nRather than trying to move around freely mobile legs, the passer first removes that mobility.\n\nThe central objective is to compress the opponent’s lower body into a position where guard reconstruction becomes difficult.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- the opponent’s knees folded or redirected toward one side\n- the passer controlling the hip line closely\n- heavy torso or shoulder pressure\n- limited space between the passer and the opponent\n- the bottom athlete’s knees prevented from returning to the centre\n- progressive movement toward chest-to-chest control\n\nThe exact configuration can vary depending on the guard being passed.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe Smash Pass works by collapsing the opponent’s lower-body structure.\n\nThe guard player normally needs the hips and knees to move independently enough to:\n\n- frame\n- rotate\n- recover inside position\n- replace hooks\n- square back toward the passer\n\nThe passer reduces those options by compressing the knees toward the torso or floor and controlling the resulting hip orientation.\n\nOnce the hips are constrained, the passer can advance into upper-body control.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe passer generally wants to:\n\n- control the direction of the knees\n- keep the hips pinned or compressed\n- prevent the opponent from squaring back\n- maintain close body connection\n- establish upper-body control as the lower body collapses\n- avoid creating space during transitions\n\nThe pass becomes weaker when the opponent can freely separate the knees or rotate the hips back underneath the passer.\n\n## Creating the Smash\nThe smash may emerge from:\n\n- open guard\n- half guard\n- butterfly structures\n- failed knee cut attempts\n- leg drag reactions\n- standing passing exchanges\n- situations where both knees can be driven toward one side\n\nThe entry matters less than whether the passer successfully converts mobile legs into a compressed structure.\n\n## Upper Body Progression\nOnce the hips are controlled, the passer should progressively connect to the torso.\n\nUseful controls may include:\n\n- crossface\n- underhook\n- shoulder pressure\n- head position\n- far-side upper-body control\n\nThis prevents the opponent from using spinal rotation to rebuild the guard.\n\n## Bottom Player Defensive Priorities\nThe guard player generally wants to:\n\n- prevent both knees being collapsed in the same direction\n- recover knee elbow connection\n- create frames\n- turn onto a useful side\n- regain inside knee position\n- create enough hip mobility to square back up\n- prevent chest-to-chest connection\n\nEarly hip recovery is usually easier than escaping after full compression is established.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Knees moved but hips remain free\nThe passer redirects the legs without controlling the pelvis.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent squares back up.\n\n### Excessive space\nThe passer compresses the legs but stays too far away.\n\nResult:\nthe bottom athlete reconstructs guard.\n\n### Upper body left uncontrolled\nThe passer focuses entirely on the legs.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent rotates the shoulders and creates defensive structure.\n\n### Driving without direction\nThe passer applies pressure straight forward without controlling where the knees and hips are going.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent absorbs the pressure and retains guard.\n\n### Releasing compression too early\nThe passer attempts to transition before upper-body control is established.\n\nResult:\nthe guard reforms.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a Smash Pass when:\n\n- the bottom athlete’s knees are folded toward one side\n- the passer applies heavy pressure into the hips or thighs\n- the bottom athlete struggles to square back toward the passer\n- the passer progressively closes upper-body distance\n- the exchange becomes increasingly compressed rather than mobile\n- the pass finishes after hip movement has been heavily restricted\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the knees are actually collapsed\n- whether the hips remain free to rotate\n- whether the passer maintains close connection\n- whether upper-body control develops alongside lower-body compression\n- whether the opponent can recover inside knee position\n- when the bottom athlete loses the ability to square up\n- whether pressure is directional or simply forceful\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a Smash Pass repeatedly stalls, inspect the relationship between knee control and hip control.\n\nThe important questions are:\n\n- Are the knees genuinely compressed?\n- Is the pelvis controlled?\n- Can the opponent still rotate?\n- Has the passer connected to the upper body?\n- Is the pressure reducing movement or merely adding weight?\n\nThe pass works through compression plus directional control, not pressure alone.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Smash Pass is a lower-body compression system.\n\nThe passer reduces the opponent’s available hip and knee movement until the guard can no longer reorganise efficiently.\n\nThe positional advantage comes from converting a mobile defensive structure into a constrained one.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\ncollapse,\ncompress,\ncontrol hips,\nconnect upper body,\nstabilise."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Spider Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Spider Guard","body":"[[Supine Open Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nSpider Guard is an open guard configuration where the bottom athlete uses foot placement against the opponent’s arms, usually around the biceps or upper arms, together with sleeve or wrist control to manage distance, disrupt posture, and create off balance.\n\nIt should be understood as a limb control and distance management system rather than simply a flexible open guard.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- control the opponent’s arms\n- maintain tension through the legs and grips\n- prevent the opponent from freely closing distance\n- disrupt posture and shoulder alignment\n- create asymmetry between the opponent’s left and right sides\n- force predictable stepping or posting reactions\n- transition into sweeps, triangles, omoplatas, or other open guards\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- tripod style sweep\n- sickle style sweep\n- triangle\n- omoplata\n- armbar\n- lasso transitions\n- De La Riva Guard\n- Single Leg X Guard\n- technical stand up\n- back exposure from overreaction\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- remove or neutralise foot control\n- break sleeve or wrist control\n- keep elbows from being extended away from the body\n- recover posture\n- control the bottom athlete’s legs\n- create an angle around the guard\n- avoid stepping into predictable off balancing patterns\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- foot placement\n- grip integrity\n- leg tension\n- shoulder control\n- elbow extension\n- hip angle\n- opponent stance\n- ability to create asymmetric pressure\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- feet placed on the arms without meaningful tension\n- opponent freely retracting the elbows\n- bottom athlete creating strong push pull asymmetry\n- posture broken before a sweep or submission develops\n- top player successfully controlling the feet before passing\n- transition from Spider Guard into Lasso Guard or another open guard\n- bottom athlete overextending the legs and losing connection\n\nSynochi should recognise Spider Guard as an upper limb control system that uses the legs to disrupt posture, manage distance, and create asymmetric reactions.\n\nIts effectiveness comes from combining grip control with active leg tension and angle rather than relying on foot placement alone."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Straight Arm Lock.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Straight Arm Lock","body":"## Type\nElbow Lock / Linear Arm Submission\n\n## Purpose\nThe Straight Arm Lock is a submission that isolates the opponent’s arm and applies extension pressure through the elbow while restricting the shoulder and torso from moving with the force.\n\nIt can appear from many positions and may use different parts of the attacker’s body as the controlling structure.\n\nThe common objective is always the same:\n\nisolate the arm,\ncontrol alignment,\nremove rotational freedom,\nextend through the elbow.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe straight arm lock works by creating a fulcrum beneath or around the opponent’s elbow while controlling the wrist and upper arm.\n\nThe attacker separates the arm from the opponent’s torso and prevents the shoulder from following the direction of pressure.\n\nThe submission becomes strong when:\n\n- the elbow is isolated\n- the wrist is controlled\n- the shoulder is restricted\n- the arm cannot rotate freely\n- the elbow is aligned against the attacker’s fulcrum\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- the opponent’s arm extended or progressively straightened\n- control near the wrist or forearm\n- the elbow positioned against a fulcrum\n- the shoulder prevented from freely rotating\n- the attacker using hips, chest, arms, legs, or torso position to reinforce control\n\nThe precise configuration may vary considerably.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- separate the elbow from the torso\n- control the wrist\n- maintain elbow alignment\n- prevent shoulder rotation\n- remove slack from the arm\n- keep close body connection\n- apply extension progressively\n\n## Relationship Between Shoulder and Elbow\nThe elbow cannot be treated in isolation.\n\nIf the shoulder can rotate freely, the opponent may follow the direction of pressure and reduce the lock.\n\nThe attacker should therefore control the larger chain:\n\nshoulder,\nelbow,\nwrist.\n\nThe more aligned and constrained this chain becomes, the less force is required.\n\n## Common Applications\nStraight arm locks may appear from:\n\n- top control\n- mount\n- side control\n- guard\n- scrambles\n- failed bent-arm attacks\n- transitions where the opponent straightens the arm defensively\n\nThe submission may emerge opportunistically rather than from a dedicated setup.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- bend the elbow\n- reconnect the arm to the torso\n- rotate the shoulder\n- turn the thumb or forearm\n- withdraw the elbow from the fulcrum\n- control the attacker’s hands\n- move the torso with the direction of pressure\n\nEarly elbow recovery is usually easier than defending after the arm has been fully isolated.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Elbow not isolated\nThe opponent can keep the upper arm connected to the body.\n\nResult:\nthe attacker cannot create meaningful extension.\n\n### Wrist controlled without shoulder control\nThe attacker extends the arm while the shoulder remains free.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent rotates out.\n\n### Fulcrum misplaced\nThe pressure is applied away from the elbow joint.\n\nResult:\nthe attack becomes weak or mechanically inefficient.\n\n### Too much space\nThe attacker extends the arm while disconnected from the opponent.\n\nResult:\nthe defender bends or retracts the arm.\n\n### Using arm strength alone\nThe attacker pulls on the wrist without using body position.\n\nResult:\nthe submission becomes inefficient and easier to defend.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a Straight Arm Lock when:\n\n- one athlete isolates an extended arm\n- the wrist or forearm is controlled\n- the elbow is positioned against a body or limb fulcrum\n- the defender tries to bend or retract the arm\n- the attacker restricts shoulder rotation\n- the exchange centres around maintaining extension through the elbow\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the elbow is genuinely isolated\n- whether the wrist is controlled\n- whether the shoulder can rotate\n- where the fulcrum is positioned\n- whether the defender can bend the arm\n- whether the attacker maintains body connection\n- what defensive motion causes the lock to fail\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a Straight Arm Lock repeatedly fails, inspect isolation before extension.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the elbow separated from the torso?\n- Is the shoulder controlled?\n- Is the fulcrum actually beneath the elbow?\n- Can the opponent rotate the arm?\n- Is the attacker using body position or just pulling?\n\nThe finish should emerge from alignment and control rather than force.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Straight Arm Lock is a linear elbow-isolation system.\n\nThe attacker converts control of the wrist and shoulder into a constrained elbow line, then applies extension through a stable fulcrum.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nisolate,\nalign,\ncontrol rotation,\nremove slack,\nextend,\nfinish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Straight Ashi.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Straight Ashi","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Single-Leg X Guard]]\n[[Outside Ashi]]\n[[Cross Ashi]]\n[[Saddle]]\n\n## Type\nLeg Entanglement / Control Position\n\n## Purpose\nStraight Ashi is a foundational leg entanglement used to control one of the opponent’s legs while creating access to sweeps, positional transitions, and leg attacks.\n\nIt is often closely related to [[Single-Leg X Guard]], but the emphasis here is on the entanglement itself as a control and attacking structure rather than solely as a guard.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- one of the opponent’s legs isolated between the attacker’s legs\n- the attacker’s hips connected closely to the opponent’s leg\n- one leg positioned across or around the opponent’s hip line\n- the second leg reinforcing control lower on the body\n- the opponent’s knee line managed to limit escape\n- the attacker controlling the foot, ankle, knee, or hip relationship\n\nThe exact configuration can vary depending on whether the attacker is seated, supine, sweeping, or entering a submission sequence.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nStraight Ashi works by isolating one leg from the opponent’s base.\n\nThe attacker creates a local control system around that limb while using their own legs and hips to influence the opponent’s posture and balance.\n\nThis can produce:\n\n- reduced base\n- reduced rotational freedom\n- exposure of the ankle\n- sweep opportunities\n- transitions into stronger leg entanglements\n\nThe position becomes weaker if the opponent freely withdraws the knee line or rotates the leg without resistance.\n\n## Relationship to Single Leg X\n[[Single-Leg X Guard]] and Straight Ashi may visually overlap.\n\nA useful distinction is intent and function.\n\nSingle Leg X often describes the guard-based application of the structure used for:\n\n- off-balancing\n- sweeping\n- managing a standing opponent\n\nStraight Ashi describes the leg entanglement as a broader control position that may continue after the opponent has been brought to the mat.\n\nFRAME should therefore use surrounding context rather than treating the names as perfectly interchangeable.\n\n## Offensive Pathways\nCommon continuations include:\n\n- sweep to top position\n- ankle lock attacks\n- transition to [[Outside Ashi]]\n- transition to [[Cross Ashi]]\n- transition to [[Saddle]]\n- technical stand-up\n- leg drag or passing transitions after a sweep\n\nThe entanglement can therefore function as both an attacking endpoint and a transitional hub.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe controlling athlete generally wants to:\n\n- maintain the opponent’s knee line\n- keep strong hip connection\n- prevent the opponent from freely rotating\n- control the secondary leg or upper body when required\n- maintain useful alignment between their hips and the attacked leg\n\nThe opponent generally wants to destroy one or more of these relationships.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nCommon defensive goals include:\n\n- extracting the knee line\n- clearing the controlling foot from the hip\n- controlling the attacker’s legs\n- rotating into a safer alignment\n- removing ankle exposure\n- standing or turning in a way that breaks the entanglement\n- creating distance between the attacker’s hips and the trapped leg\n\nEscaping the knee line often changes the position more decisively than fighting the feet alone.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Losing the knee line\nThe opponent’s knee slips beyond the attacker’s controlling leg structure.\n\nResult:\nthe entanglement rapidly loses control.\n\n### Weak hip connection\nThe attacker’s hips drift away from the opponent’s leg.\n\nResult:\nrotation and extraction become easier.\n\n### Focusing only on the ankle\nThe attacker controls the foot but not the larger leg structure.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent can rotate or withdraw despite apparent submission access.\n\n### Poor control of the secondary leg\nThe opponent uses the free leg to push, stand, clear grips, or create rotational escape.\n\nResult:\nthe attacker loses positional stability.\n\n### Overcommitting to the submission\nThe attacker sacrifices the entanglement to chase the ankle.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent escapes the position entirely.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify Straight Ashi when:\n\n- one opponent leg is isolated between the attacker’s legs\n- the attacker has a foot or leg controlling near the hip\n- the attacker maintains close hip-to-leg connection\n- the opponent is attempting to free the knee line\n- the exchange includes ankle control or leg-lock threats\n- the position transitions between [[Single-Leg X Guard]] and other leg entanglements\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the opponent’s knee line is trapped\n- where the attacker’s outside foot is positioned\n- whether the attacker’s hips remain connected\n- whether the opponent can rotate freely\n- how the secondary leg is being controlled\n- whether the position is being used primarily for sweeping or submission\n- what transition occurs when control begins to weaken\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf Straight Ashi repeatedly fails, inspect the entanglement before the submission.\n\nThe key questions are:\n\n- Is the knee line controlled?\n- Is the hip connection strong?\n- Can the opponent rotate?\n- Is the secondary leg creating problems?\n- Is the attacker sacrificing control to chase the foot?\n\nMany apparent finishing problems are actually positional-control problems.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nStraight Ashi is a limb-isolation control system.\n\nThe attacker reduces the opponent’s ability to use one leg as part of a coordinated base while creating concentrated control around that limb.\n\nIts effectiveness depends less on holding the foot and more on maintaining the larger relationship between:\n\nhip,\nknee line,\nrotation,\nand base.\n\nThe core sequence is:\n\nisolate,\nconnect,\ncontrol rotation,\noff-balance or attack,\ntransition when required."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Structural Recovery Before Escape.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Structural Recovery Before Escape","body":"[[Escape Priority]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nLarge escape movement becomes inefficient when the athlete remains structurally compromised.\n\nUseful structural recovery may include:\n\n- recovering elbow position\n- restoring head-spine alignment\n- freeing a shoulder\n- changing pelvic angle\n- reconnecting feet to the floor\n- rebuilding knee-elbow relationship\n\nSynochi should identify whether the athlete first needs to restore usable structure before attempting positional movement."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Supine Open Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Supine Open Guard","body":"[[Seated Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nSupine Open Guard is an open guard configuration where the bottom athlete is primarily on their back or shoulders while using the legs, feet, shins, and grips to manage distance, preserve inside position, and create attacking angles.\n\nIt should be understood as a dynamic distance management and entry platform rather than simply lying underneath a standing opponent.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- maintain useful foot and shin connections\n- preserve inside leg position where possible\n- control distance between the hips\n- prevent direct chest to chest connection\n- keep the knees active between the opponent and the torso\n- create angles rather than remaining square underneath\n- expose opportunities to off balance, enter underneath, or transition into stronger guards\n- prevent the opponent from freely controlling both legs\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- Single Leg X Guard\n- X Guard\n- K Guard\n- 50 50 Guard\n- Butterfly Guard\n- technical stand up\n- wrestle up\n- tripod style sweep\n- sickle style sweep\n- leg entanglement entries\n- transition back to Seated Guard\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- control the feet and knees\n- move around rather than directly through strong frames\n- deny inside leg position\n- pin one or both legs\n- force the bottom athlete’s knees away from the centerline\n- close distance safely\n- create an angle for passing\n- prevent the bottom athlete from entering underneath the hips\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- foot placement\n- knee position\n- hip angle\n- distance between the athletes’ hips\n- inside position\n- connection to the opponent’s legs\n- ability to create rotation\n- opponent stance and base\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- bottom athlete lying flat without useful leg connection\n- both feet being controlled and moved away from the centerline\n- strong foot placement maintaining distance\n- angle creation exposing K Guard or Single Leg X entries\n- opponent stepping too close and allowing underneath access\n- bottom athlete recovering to Seated Guard when distance increases\n- top player successfully pinning the legs before entering passing range\n\nSynochi should recognise Supine Open Guard as a distance control and transition position.\n\nIts effectiveness depends on keeping the legs structurally active, preserving access to inside space, and using changes in the opponent’s stance to move into stronger guards, sweeps, wrestle ups, or leg entanglements."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Tarikoplata.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Tarikoplata","body":"# Tarikoplata\n\n## Type\nShoulder Lock / Upper Body Isolation System\n\n## Purpose\nThe Tarikoplata is a shoulder lock that uses a leg-assisted control structure to isolate the opponent’s arm and rotate the shoulder into a compromised position.\n\nIt commonly appears when the attacker has already established strong upper-body control and can trap the opponent’s arm close to the torso.\n\nThe position can function as:\n\n- a direct submission\n- a control system\n- a transition from Kimura-style attacks\n- a way to immobilise the shoulder during top control\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe Tarikoplata works by combining arm isolation with lower-body control.\n\nThe attacker uses the legs to reinforce control over the opponent’s arm while rotating the shoulder against restricted torso movement.\n\nThe effectiveness of the position depends on controlling:\n\n- wrist position\n- elbow position\n- shoulder rotation\n- torso movement\n- available escape direction\n\nThe submission becomes stronger when the opponent cannot freely turn with the pressure.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- one of the opponent’s arms isolated close to the attacker’s body\n- the attacker using a leg to reinforce control of the arm\n- the opponent’s elbow separated from a safe position\n- the wrist and forearm prevented from freely rotating\n- the shoulder placed under increasing rotational tension\n- the opponent’s torso movement restricted\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- isolate the arm before attempting the finish\n- keep the wrist controlled\n- prevent the elbow from reconnecting to the torso\n- use the leg to reinforce the upper-body grip\n- control the opponent’s shoulder line\n- prevent the opponent from turning into the pressure\n\n## Relationship Between Arm and Torso\nThe Tarikoplata becomes powerful when the opponent’s arm can no longer move independently of the attacking structure.\n\nThe attacker uses the trapped limb as a lever while denying the opponent enough torso rotation to relieve pressure.\n\nIf the torso is free, the opponent may rotate with the attack and reduce the effect.\n\n## Offensive Pathways\nThe Tarikoplata may develop from:\n\n- Kimura-style control\n- top half guard\n- side control\n- mount transitions\n- scrambles where an arm becomes isolated\n- failed shoulder lock attempts\n\nThe exact entry can vary, but the finishing structure depends on preserving arm isolation throughout the transition.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- reconnect the elbow to the torso\n- straighten the arm\n- rotate the shoulder into safer alignment\n- turn the torso with the pressure\n- free the trapped wrist\n- remove the attacker’s reinforcing leg\n- create enough space to restore posture\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Arm not fully isolated\nThe attacker attempts the submission while the elbow remains connected to the body.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent retains strong structural defence.\n\n### Weak wrist control\nThe opponent can rotate or straighten the forearm.\n\nResult:\nthe shoulder pressure decreases.\n\n### Leg control too loose\nThe attacker’s lower-body reinforcement does not meaningfully trap the arm.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent extracts the limb.\n\n### Torso rotation remains available\nThe opponent can turn with the submission.\n\nResult:\npressure is dissipated through whole-body movement.\n\n### Chasing the finish too early\nThe attacker applies rotation before stabilising the position.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent escapes during the transition.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a Tarikoplata when:\n\n- one athlete has isolated an opponent’s arm in a Kimura-like structure\n- a leg is used to reinforce control of the arm\n- the defender’s wrist and elbow are tightly constrained\n- the attacker rotates the shoulder while maintaining close body connection\n- the defender attempts to straighten the arm or turn with the pressure\n- the submission develops from a strong upper-body control position\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the wrist is genuinely controlled\n- whether the elbow is separated from the torso\n- whether the reinforcing leg is maintaining control\n- whether the defender can rotate the shoulder or torso\n- whether the attacker remains connected during the finishing movement\n- what exact movement allows the defender to create space\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a Tarikoplata repeatedly fails, inspect isolation before rotation.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the arm actually trapped?\n- Can the elbow reconnect to the torso?\n- Is the wrist controlled?\n- Is the leg reinforcing the position effectively?\n- Can the opponent rotate their torso with the attack?\n\nThe finish should emerge from a stable arm-control structure rather than from forcing shoulder rotation.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Tarikoplata is a reinforced shoulder-isolation system.\n\nThe attacker combines upper-body control with lower-body reinforcement to reduce the opponent’s ability to reposition the arm or rotate the torso.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nisolate arm,\nsecure wrist,\nseparate elbow,\nreinforce with leg,\ncontrol torso,\nrotate shoulder,\nfinish."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Toehold.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Toehold","body":"# Toehold\n\n## Type\nLeg Lock Submission / Rotational Foot and Ankle Attack\n\n## Purpose\nThe toehold is a rotational leg lock that uses control of the foot to create torque through the ankle and lower leg.\n\nAlthough the grip is applied to the foot, the effectiveness of the submission depends on controlling the larger leg structure strongly enough that the opponent cannot simply rotate, straighten, or withdraw.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe attacker controls the opponent’s foot with a figure-four style grip and rotates the foot while restricting movement through the knee and hip.\n\nThe submission works by creating torsion through:\n\n- foot\n- ankle\n- lower leg\n- knee orientation\n\nThe more rotational freedom the opponent retains, the easier it is for them to relieve pressure.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- control around the toes or forefoot\n- secondary control around the opponent’s ankle or lower leg\n- a figure-four grip\n- the opponent’s knee line at least partially controlled\n- the attacker positioned so the opponent cannot freely spin\n\nThe exact finishing angle may vary depending on the leg entanglement and orientation.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- control the foot securely\n- keep the opponent’s knee from freely rotating\n- reduce space between their own body and the trapped leg\n- maintain a stable figure-four connection\n- control the opponent’s escape direction\n- apply rotation progressively rather than explosively\n\n## Rotational Control\nThe defender’s ability to rotate is a major variable.\n\nIf the opponent can turn their hip, knee, and foot together, the pressure may dissipate.\n\nThe attacker therefore benefits from controlling:\n\n- knee orientation\n- hip movement\n- lower-leg alignment\n- space around the trapped leg\n\nThe submission becomes stronger when rotation is localised rather than shared across the whole body.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- hide or retract the foot\n- straighten the leg\n- rotate with the pressure\n- free the knee line\n- break the attacker’s grip\n- use the free leg to create space\n- change the angle before the lock becomes tight\n\nThe safest escape direction depends on the exact orientation of the lock.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Foot controlled without leg control\nThe attacker secures the grip but the opponent’s knee and hip remain free.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent rotates out of pressure.\n\n### Grip too shallow\nThe attacker controls only the toes without stable support around the ankle.\n\nResult:\nthe grip slips or produces weak leverage.\n\n### Excessive arm strength\nThe attacker tries to finish by twisting harder with the arms.\n\nResult:\ncontrol deteriorates and the opponent gains space.\n\n### Poor body position\nThe grip is mechanically correct but the attacker’s torso and hips are too far away.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent can move the leg freely.\n\n### Ignoring escape direction\nThe attacker applies pressure without controlling where the opponent can rotate.\n\nResult:\nthe defender follows the motion and escapes.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a toehold when:\n\n- one athlete controls the opponent’s foot with both arms\n- a figure-four grip forms around the foot and ankle\n- the opponent’s foot is rotated while the leg remains trapped\n- the defender attempts to spin or straighten the leg\n- the exchange centres around control of the foot and knee orientation\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the grip is secure\n- how much rotational freedom the defender has\n- whether the knee line is controlled\n- whether the attacker stays close to the trapped leg\n- whether the free leg is affecting the exchange\n- what exact movement allows the defender to escape\n\n## Safety Interpretation\nToeholds can create fast pressure through the ankle and knee.\n\nTraining application should prioritise:\n\n- controlled rotational pressure\n- early tapping\n- avoiding explosive finishing\n- avoiding uncontrolled spinning escapes\n- awareness of the ruleset being used\n\nFRAME should avoid encouraging forceful rotational finishing.\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf a toehold repeatedly fails, inspect control before rotation.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is the foot actually secured?\n- Can the opponent rotate their knee freely?\n- Is the attacker close enough to the leg?\n- Is the grip stable?\n- Is the opponent escaping by moving the entire limb with the pressure?\n\nThe finish becomes much more reliable when the leg is controlled as a complete structure.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe toehold is a rotational distal-control system.\n\nThe attacker uses the foot as a lever while attempting to prevent the rest of the leg from safely following the applied rotation.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nsecure foot,\ncontrol leg,\nrestrict rotation,\nestablish angle,\napply controlled torque."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Torreando Pass.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Torreando Pass","body":"## Type\nOpen Guard Pass / Outside Passing System\n\n## Purpose\nThe Torreando Pass is an outside passing method built around controlling the opponent’s legs and redirecting them away from the direction the passer intends to move.\n\nRather than driving directly through the guard, the passer creates an angle around it.\n\nThe central objective is to move the opponent’s knees away from the defensive centre line while the passer occupies the space beside the hips.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- control of the opponent’s knees, shins, ankles, or lower legs\n- the opponent’s legs redirected away from the passer’s movement\n- lateral movement around the hip line\n- distance maintained long enough to prevent strong upper-body connection from the guard player\n- rapid transition from leg control into upper-body control\n\nThe pass can be performed from standing, crouched, or transitional open-guard exchanges.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe Torreando Pass works through redirection.\n\nThe guard player normally wants their knees and feet positioned between themselves and the passer.\n\nThe passer removes that relationship by moving the legs away from the direction of travel.\n\nThis creates a temporary opening around the outside of the hips.\n\nThe passer must then occupy that opening before the opponent can square back up.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe passer generally wants to:\n\n- control both legs effectively\n- move the knees away from the intended passing direction\n- maintain posture and mobility\n- move laterally rather than directly forward\n- reach the opponent’s hip line quickly\n- transition into upper-body control before guard recovery\n\n## Directional Movement\nThe pass depends heavily on timing between hand movement and footwork.\n\nIf the hands redirect the legs but the feet remain stationary, the opponent can usually recover.\n\nIf the passer moves without sufficiently redirecting the legs, the guard follows them.\n\nThe movement should therefore function as one connected action:\n\nredirect,\nmove,\noccupy.\n\n## Bottom Player Defensive Priorities\nThe guard player commonly attempts to:\n\n- keep the knees facing the passer\n- maintain foot or shin connection\n- recover inside position\n- frame against the passer as they approach\n- invert or rotate with the passing direction\n- establish grips that slow lateral movement\n- recover the knees before chest-to-chest control develops\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Moving the legs without moving the body\nThe passer pushes the knees aside but stays directly in front.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent immediately recovers guard.\n\n### Moving before redirecting\nThe passer circles while the opponent’s knees remain aligned with them.\n\nResult:\nthe guard simply tracks the movement.\n\n### Reaching for the upper body too early\nThe passer releases leg control before reaching a dominant angle.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent replaces their knees.\n\n### Excessive distance\nThe passer successfully moves around the legs but remains too far from the hips.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent has enough time to square back up.\n\n### Driving forward\nThe passer turns an outside pass into a direct pressure battle.\n\nResult:\nthe opponent can reconnect frames and defensive leg structure.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a Torreando Pass when:\n\n- the top athlete controls both of the bottom athlete’s legs\n- the legs are rapidly redirected to one side\n- the passer moves around the opposite side\n- the exchange remains primarily outside the opponent’s knee line\n- the bottom athlete repeatedly attempts to square their knees back toward the passer\n- the pass ends with the passer occupying the space beside the hips\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether both legs are genuinely controlled\n- whether the opponent’s knees are moved away from the passing direction\n- whether the passer’s footwork matches the hand movement\n- how quickly the passer reaches the hip line\n- whether the opponent can square back up\n- whether leg control is released too early\n- what form of upper-body control follows the pass\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nIf Torreando passing repeatedly fails, inspect the relationship between redirection and movement.\n\nThe key questions are:\n\n- Did the passer actually move the knees?\n- Did their feet move at the same time?\n- Did they reach the hip line before releasing control?\n- Did the opponent remain able to track them?\n\nThe pass is rarely just about pushing the legs harder.\n\nIt is about creating and occupying an angle before the guard can reorganise.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe Torreando Pass is a directional displacement system.\n\nThe passer temporarily moves the opponent’s primary defensive structure away from the route they intend to occupy.\n\nThe advantage exists only briefly.\n\nIts effectiveness therefore depends on converting displacement into positional occupation before defensive alignment is restored.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\ncontrol,\nredirect,\nmove,\noccupy,\nstabilise."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Triangle Position.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Triangle Position","body":"## Type\nSubmission Control Position / Head and Arm Isolation System\n\n## Purpose\nThe triangle position is a control structure built around isolating the opponent’s head and one arm inside the attacker’s legs.\n\nAlthough commonly associated with the finishing choke, the position also functions as a strong control hub for:\n\n- posture breaking\n- shoulder isolation\n- arm attacks\n- sweeps\n- transitions\n- positional control\n\nThe position becomes strong when the opponent’s head and trapped arm are compressed into a controlled alignment.\n\n## Structural Characteristics\nTypical features include:\n\n- the opponent’s neck and one arm trapped inside the attacker’s leg structure\n- one leg crossing behind the opponent’s neck\n- the second leg reinforcing the lock\n- the trapped shoulder driven across the opponent’s neck line\n- the attacker controlling posture and rotational freedom\n- the opponent prevented from freely withdrawing the head or arm\n\nThe precise leg configuration may vary during entry and adjustment.\n\n## Core Mechanism\nThe triangle works through asymmetric head and shoulder control.\n\nThe opponent’s trapped arm is pushed across their own neck while the attacker’s legs create compression around the remaining space.\n\nThe position therefore depends on controlling:\n\n- head posture\n- shoulder position\n- arm orientation\n- angle\n- hip distance\n\nThe finish becomes difficult when these relationships are poorly aligned.\n\n## Positional Priorities\nThe attacker generally wants to:\n\n- prevent posture recovery\n- control the trapped arm\n- create an angle rather than remaining square\n- maintain strong leg connection\n- keep the opponent’s shoulder inside the structure\n- stop the opponent from driving directly through the position\n- adjust until unnecessary space is removed\n\n## Angle Creation\nAngle is often more important than raw squeezing force.\n\nRemaining directly underneath the opponent can leave excess space and reduce control.\n\nCreating a lateral angle can:\n\n- improve shoulder compression\n- reduce available neck space\n- make posture recovery harder\n- reduce the opponent’s ability to stack effectively\n\n## Offensive Pathways\nThe triangle position may lead to:\n\n- choke finish\n- armbar\n- shoulder lock\n- sweep\n- back exposure\n- transition to top control\n\nA failed choke does not necessarily mean the position has failed.\n\nThe attacker may still have strong control over the opponent’s posture and upper body.\n\n## Defensive Priorities\nThe defender commonly attempts to:\n\n- restore posture\n- create space around the neck\n- pull the trapped arm back into alignment\n- stack the attacker\n- turn toward a safer angle\n- clear one of the controlling legs\n- change shoulder orientation\n- prevent the attacker from cutting an angle\n\nEarly posture recovery is usually significantly easier than escaping after the position has been fully adjusted.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n### Remaining square\nThe attacker stays directly underneath the opponent.\n\nResult:\ncompression is weaker and stacking becomes easier.\n\n### Weak posture control\nThe opponent can lift the head and spine.\n\nResult:\nspace increases inside the triangle.\n\n### Incorrect shoulder position\nThe trapped shoulder is not driven across the neck line.\n\nResult:\nthe choke structure remains loose.\n\n### Locking too early\nThe attacker closes the legs before achieving useful alignment.\n\nResult:\nthe position feels tight but lacks effective control.\n\n### Chasing the finish\nThe attacker squeezes aggressively without fixing the structural problem.\n\nResult:\nenergy is spent without improving the position.\n\n## Recognition Cues\nFRAME may identify a triangle position when:\n\n- the opponent’s head and one arm are trapped between the attacker’s legs\n- the defender is attempting to posture or stack\n- the attacker is adjusting hip angle\n- the trapped arm is being moved across the opponent’s body\n- the attacker controls the head while repositioning the legs\n- the exchange develops into a choke, armbar, or sweep\n\n## Video Analysis\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the defender can posture\n- whether the attacker creates an angle\n- where the trapped shoulder is positioned\n- whether there is visible space inside the leg structure\n- whether the attacker maintains control while adjusting\n- whether the defender is stacking effectively\n- whether an alternative attack becomes available from the reaction\n\n## Coaching Interpretation\nWhen a triangle repeatedly fails, inspect structure before squeeze.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Is posture actually broken?\n- Is the trapped arm positioned correctly?\n- Has the attacker created enough angle?\n- Is the opponent’s shoulder compressed into the neck line?\n- Is the attacker adjusting position or simply squeezing harder?\n\nA strong triangle should become mechanically efficient before maximal force is required.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe triangle is a head-and-shoulder isolation system.\n\nIts effectiveness emerges from progressively reducing space and controlling the opponent’s ability to restore posture or shoulder alignment.\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\nisolate,\nbreak posture,\ncontrol shoulder,\ncreate angle,\ncompress,\nfinish or transition."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Turtle.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Turtle","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nTurtle is a defensive and transitional position where the athlete is supported primarily through knees, elbows, hands, or forearms while protecting the front of the body and limiting immediate scoring or submission access.\n\nTurtle should not be treated as one fixed shape.\n\nIt can vary from:\n\n- compact defensive turtle\n- active wrestling turtle\n- seated turtle\n- tripod-style turtle\n- referee’s position style base\n- transitional turtle during scrambles\n\nThe position is strategically important because it sits between several major pathways:\n\n- guard recovery\n- standing recovery\n- back exposure\n- front headlock\n- crucifix\n- wrestling reversals\n- mat returns\n\nPrimary defensive objectives include:\n\n- protect the neck\n- deny clean hook insertion\n- keep elbows connected enough to protect inside space\n- maintain a base that allows movement\n- avoid being flattened\n- recover facing position when possible\n- create a route to guard, standing, or reversal\n\nPrimary attacking objectives against turtle include:\n\n- break the defender’s base\n- expose the hips\n- control the near-side or far-side arm\n- secure chest-to-back connection\n- establish seatbelt or body control\n- insert hooks or body triangle\n- transition to front headlock or crucifix\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- head position\n- elbow-knee relationship\n- hip height\n- weight distribution\n- control of wrists and arms\n- ability to rotate\n- access to the opponent’s back\n- ability to post and rebuild base\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- defender becoming flattened\n- hips being pulled away from base\n- elbows separated from knees\n- repeated failed stand-up attempts\n- attacker gaining chest-to-back connection\n- wrist control preventing rotation\n- clean transition into back control, crucifix, or front headlock\n\nSynochi should recognise turtle as a branching transitional position rather than simply a defensive endpoint."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/X Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"X Guard","body":"[[Single-Leg X Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nX Guard is a leg-control position where the bottom athlete places the opponent’s weight over the legs while using opposing hooks to control both the near and far side of the opponent’s lower body.\n\nX Guard should be understood as a balance-breaking and elevation system.\n\nPrimary bottom-player objectives include:\n\n- place the opponent’s weight onto a compromised base\n- control both sides of the opponent’s lower body\n- prevent free hip rotation\n- create extension through the opponent’s stance\n- force posts or weight shifts\n- create access to sweeps, wrestle-ups, back exposure, or leg entanglements\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- technical stand-up sweep\n- backward sweep\n- forward off-balance\n- wrestle-up\n- back exposure\n- transition to Single-Leg X\n- saddle entry\n- outside ashi entry\n- leg drag style passing transitions after coming on top\n\nPrimary top-player objectives include:\n\n- recover posture\n- remove one of the controlling hooks\n- bring weight back over a stable base\n- prevent the bottom athlete from extending the stance\n- free the knee line\n- control the bottom athlete’s feet\n- avoid posting in ways that expose further attacks\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- relationship between both hooks\n- hip proximity\n- knee-line control\n- direction of the opponent’s weight\n- ability to extend or compress the stance\n- access to the far leg\n- control of the opponent’s posting leg\n- upper-body attachment where available\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- X Guard established without meaningful off-balance\n- opponent standing comfortably with posture recovered\n- one hook cleared, reducing control to a weaker configuration\n- weight shifted onto one leg before a sweep\n- bottom player extending the opponent’s stance to break balance\n- transition between X Guard and Single-Leg X as the opponent changes base\n- defensive rotation creating access to saddle or back exposure\n\nSynochi should recognise X Guard as a dynamic balance-control system.\n\nIts effectiveness comes from controlling both sides of the opponent’s base and forcing weight shifts that make sweeps, wrestle-ups, and leg-entanglement transitions available."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/BJJ Intelligence/Z Guard.md","folder":"BJJ Intelligence","title":"Z Guard","body":"[[Half Guard]]\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nZ Guard is a half guard configuration where the bottom athlete uses the top leg as a knee shield across the opponent’s torso while the lower leg maintains control around the opponent’s trapped leg.\n\nIt should be understood as a framing, distance control, and angle creation position rather than simply a defensive Half Guard variation.\n\nPrimary bottom player objectives include:\n\n- maintain the knee shield as a structural frame\n- prevent chest to chest flattening\n- preserve hip mobility\n- control distance\n- create useful upper body connection\n- protect the inside space\n- create an angle for underhooks, arm drags, entries, or transitions\n- prevent the top player from collapsing the shield\n\nCommon attacking pathways include:\n\n- underhook to wrestle up\n- dogfight\n- kimura\n- arm drag\n- back exposure\n- transition to Butterfly Half Guard\n- transition to K Guard\n- transition to Single Leg X Guard\n- full guard recovery\n- sweep from off balancing reactions\n\nPrimary top player objectives include:\n\n- collapse or bypass the knee shield\n- control the bottom athlete’s head and shoulders\n- deny the underhook\n- pin the bottom knee\n- flatten the hips\n- clear the trapped leg\n- force the bottom athlete’s knees away from the centerline\n- progress into chest to chest Half Guard or a passing position\n\nKey mechanical variables include:\n\n- knee shield height\n- shin angle\n- hip angle\n- inside knee position\n- underhook access\n- head position\n- distance between the athletes’ chests\n- ability to keep the shield structurally connected rather than muscularly extended\n\nVideo observation cues:\n\n- knee shield collapsing under pressure\n- bottom athlete keeping the top player’s chest away\n- underhook becoming available as the top player pressures forward\n- top player pinning the shield knee\n- bottom athlete rotating underneath into K Guard\n- transition into Butterfly Half Guard when the top player changes angle\n- loss of hip angle leading to flattening\n\nSynochi should recognise Z Guard as a distance and structure management system within Half Guard.\n\nIts effectiveness comes from using the knee shield to preserve inside space, protect hip mobility, and create the angle needed to transition into stronger offensive pathways."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Intelligence Hub/Combat Intelligence Hub.md","folder":"Combat Intelligence Hub","title":"Combat Intelligence Hub","body":"# Combat Intelligence Hub\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe Combat Intelligence Hub is the central bridge between Synochi’s core intelligence and the Combat Intelligence specialist layer.\n\nIt exists to keep Combat Intelligence structurally distinct from the main Synochi network while still allowing controlled information flow between them.\n\n## Function\n\nThe Hub should:\n\n- Receive relevant context from Synochi core\n- Route combat-specific reasoning into the appropriate Combat Intelligence nodes\n- Prevent unnecessary cross-linking between every combat node and the main Synochi graph\n- Act as the main anchor point for the Combat Intelligence network\n- Preserve the distinction between general intelligence and combat-specific intelligence\n\n## Architecture\n\nSynochi Core\n→ Combat Intelligence Hub\n→ Combat Intelligence Network\n\n## Combat Intelligence Domains\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence — Operating Definition]]\n- Diagnostic Patterns\n- Drill Templates\n- Technique Schemas\n- Video Observation Ontology\n- Combat Models\n\n## Core Bridges\n\n[[Agency Preservation]]\n\n[[SPINE/Stability-First Progression|Stability First Progression]]\n\n[[MODELS/Gradient–Hierarchy Resolution Model|Gradient Hierarchy Resolution Model]]\n\n## Principle\n\nCombat Intelligence should extend Synochi, not fragment it.\n\nThe Hub acts as the controlled interface between the core system and the specialist combat layer.\n\n## Routes by Training Problem\n\n| Task | Existing entry | Useful continuation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Describe what the footage shows | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Combat Video Observation Schema]] | Record events and confidence before interpretation. |\n| Compare explanations | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Competing Diagnostic Hypotheses]] | Identify a discriminating observation or practice task. |\n| Decide what matters first | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Intervention Priority]] | Select a supported, trainable target or continue monitoring. |\n| Practise recognising relevant information | [[Recognition Decision Drill]] | Assess decision quality separately from execution. |\n| Develop access to an intended attack | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Entry Setup Drill]] | Establish viable entry conditions. |\n| Develop a limiting movement component | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Movement-Pattern Drill]] | Assess the component in the full sport task. |\n| Evaluate an intervention | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Intervention Feedback Assimilation]] | Record implementation, response, tradeoffs and learning flags. |\n| Consult previous learning | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Diagnostic Learning Retrieval]] | Reuse or adapt prior interventions only when the context fits. |\n\n\n## Additional Positioning Routes\n\n| Training problem | Existing template | Selection boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unsuitable distance or orientation prevents useful action | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Range and Angle Drill]] | Assess range, recognition and execution separately. |\n| A mat edge, ropes or wall changes available options | [[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Boundary Management Drill]] | Specify boundary type, rules and substitute-environment limitations. |"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Intelligence Hub/Combat Intelligence — Operating Definition.md","folder":"Combat Intelligence Hub","title":"Combat Intelligence — Operating Definition","body":"# **Combat Intelligence — Operating Definition**\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nCombat Intelligence is Synochi’s specialist layer for understanding combat-sport performance.\n\nIt converts observations from training, competition, athlete reports, and video into structured technical understanding, diagnoses, coaching priorities, drills, and future recommendations.\n\n## **Core Function**\n\nCombat Intelligence should answer five questions:\n\n1. What happened?\n2. Why did it happen?\n3. What matters most?\n4. What should change?\n5. How should it be trained?\n\n## **What Happened?**\n\nIdentify observable actions, positions, transitions, reactions, outcomes, and recurring patterns.\n\n## **Why Did It Happen?**\n\nInfer likely technical, tactical, positional, timing, decision-making, or physical causes while preserving uncertainty.\n\n## **What Matters Most?**\n\nDistinguish meaningful patterns from isolated events and rank them by impact on the athlete’s game.\n\n## **What Should Change?**\n\nProduce a useful correction, tactical adjustment, constraint, concept, or focus area.\n\n## **How Should It Be Trained?**\n\nConvert the diagnosis into an appropriate drill, positional round, task constraint, or coaching cue.\n\n## **System Principle**\n\nCombat Intelligence does not replace Synochi’s core reasoning.\n\nIt extends it.\n\nThe Combat Intelligence layer supplies domain-specific combat understanding.\n\nThe Synochi core still governs athlete context, identity, state, uncertainty, guidance style, progression, and agency preservation.\n\n## **Reasoning Flow**\n\nObservation → Pattern → Diagnosis → Priority → Intervention → Feedback → Athlete Model\n\n## **Evidence Hierarchy**\n\nCombat Intelligence should distinguish between:\n\n- Directly observed evidence\n- Repeated athlete-reported evidence\n- Coach-confirmed evidence\n- Inferred patterns\n- Low-confidence hypotheses\n\nAn inference should never be presented with the certainty of an observation.\n\n## **Primary Outputs**\n\n- Technical observations\n- Tactical observations\n- Pattern recognition\n- Strength identification\n- Vulnerability identification\n- Diagnostic hypotheses\n- Coaching cues\n- Drill prescriptions\n- Positional training prescriptions\n- Competition adjustments\n- Questions that reduce uncertainty\n\n## **Design Objective**\n\nThe goal is not to create a technique encyclopedia.\n\nThe goal is to create a system that increasingly understands how this specific athlete fights, why their game succeeds or fails, and what intervention is most likely to improve it.\n\n## **Links**\n\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Agency Preservation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Branch Reliability.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Branch Reliability","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should judge whether a reaction branch inside a combat system is reliable enough to be trusted.\n\nA branch should not be treated as part of the athlete’s core system simply because it exists technically.\n\nIt should earn reliability through evidence.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA useful branch must be:\n\n- Recognisable\n- Repeatable\n- Executable under resistance\n- Connected to a predictable opponent reaction\n- Valuable enough to justify inclusion\n\n## Branch Structure\n\nTrigger\n→ Opponent Reaction\n→ Athlete Recognition\n→ Selected Response\n→ Outcome\n\nSynochi should evaluate each stage.\n\n## Reliability Factors\n\n### Reaction Frequency\n\nHow often does the opponent reaction actually occur?\n\nA branch built around a rare reaction should carry less weight.\n\n### Recognition Accuracy\n\nCan the athlete reliably recognise the cue that activates the branch?\n\n### Execution Reliability\n\nCan the athlete perform the response under realistic resistance?\n\n### Outcome Quality\n\nDoes the branch usually produce a useful result?\n\n### Continuity\n\nDoes the branch keep the athlete inside a strong system, or does it create a disconnected technical detour?\n\n## Reliability Levels\n\n### Experimental\n\nThe branch is technically plausible but has little real evidence.\n\n### Emerging\n\nThe branch has appeared successfully more than once but is not yet dependable.\n\n### Reliable\n\nThe athlete recognises and executes the branch consistently under resistance.\n\n### Core\n\nThe branch is highly reliable and forms an important part of the athlete’s game architecture.\n\n## Weak Branch Detection\n\nSynochi should identify branches that repeatedly fail because of:\n\n- Late recognition\n- Poor timing\n- Technical weakness\n- Incorrect reaction reading\n- Excessive complexity\n- Weak connection to the athlete’s strengths\n\n## Branch Pruning\n\nA combat system should not become larger indefinitely.\n\nSynochi should be willing to reduce or remove branches that:\n\n- Rarely appear\n- Repeatedly fail\n- Create unnecessary decision load\n- Duplicate stronger options\n- Pull the athlete away from their preferred game\n\n## Branch Reinforcement\n\nA strong branch can be reinforced through:\n\n- Repetition under resistance\n- Reaction-specific drilling\n- Positional rounds\n- Cue recognition training\n- Constraint-led practice\n- Competition validation\n\n## Decision Load\n\nMore branches are not always better.\n\nEach additional branch increases the athlete’s decision burden.\n\nSynochi should prefer the smallest number of reliable branches that cover the highest proportion of realistic opponent reactions.\n\n## System Efficiency\n\nA strong system may look like:\n\nPrimary Position\n→ 3 common reactions\n→ 3 highly reliable responses\n\nrather than:\n\nPrimary Position\n→ 10 possible reactions\n→ 10 poorly developed options\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help compress the athlete’s game into high-confidence decision systems.\n\nThe goal is not maximum technical choice.\n\nThe goal is maximum reliability with minimum unnecessary decision load.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Dilemma Construction]]\n\n[[Trilemma Construction]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Constraint Accumulation.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Constraint Accumulation","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nEffective grappling often works by progressively reducing the opponent’s available options.\n\nRather than asking:\n\nHow do I force the finish immediately?\n\nCombat Intelligence should often ask:\n\nWhich options can I remove first?\n\nThe central sequence is:\n\n**Control → Restrict → Narrow → Isolate → Finish**\n\n## **Constraint**\n\nA constraint is anything that reduces the opponent’s ability to move, escape, posture, rotate, frame, attack, or change direction.\n\nConstraints can be created through:\n\n- body position,\n- weight distribution,\n- grips,\n- wedges,\n- limb isolation,\n- head position,\n- hip control,\n- shoulder control,\n- angle,\n- pressure,\n- or threat.\n\nThe more meaningful constraints applied, the fewer viable responses remain.\n\n## **Accumulation**\n\nOne constraint may be easy to solve.\n\nSeveral overlapping constraints are much harder.\n\nFor example:\n\nControlling the upper body alone may still allow strong hip movement.\n\nControlling the hips alone may still allow framing and rotation.\n\nControlling:\n\n- head position,\n- near-side shoulder,\n- hip line,\n- and inside space\n\ncan dramatically reduce the opponent’s available escape routes.\n\nThe strength comes from the **combined effect**.\n\n## **Option Reduction**\n\nConstraint Accumulation should be understood as progressive option reduction.\n\nA position becomes stronger when the opponent has fewer good choices.\n\nThis can move through stages:\n\n**Many viable responses**\n\n↓\n\n**Several responses removed**\n\n↓\n\n**Only predictable reactions remain**\n\n↓\n\n**Reaction becomes exploitable**\n\n↓\n\n**Finish or positional advancement becomes highly probable**\n\nThis connects directly to reaction-based offence.\n\n## **Control Before Exposure**\n\nStrong attacking systems often create exposure indirectly.\n\nInstead of chasing an arm, neck, leg, or back immediately, the athlete first removes the opponent’s ability to protect it.\n\nFor example:\n\nRather than pulling harder on an isolated arm:\n\n- control posture,\n- restrict shoulder rotation,\n- remove defensive framing,\n- limit hip movement,\n- then attack the limb.\n\nThe submission becomes easier because the defence has already been constrained.\n\n## **Dynamic Constraints**\n\nConstraints do not have to be static.\n\nA threat can temporarily restrict behaviour.\n\nFor example:\n\nA credible choke threat may force the opponent to dedicate both hands to defence.\n\nThose hands are now unavailable for posting or framing.\n\nThe threat itself becomes a constraint.\n\nThis means:\n\n**Control can be mechanical or behavioural.**\n\n## **Constraint Trade-Offs**\n\nRemoving one option may open another.\n\nFor example:\n\nBlocking an opponent from turning inward may encourage them to turn away.\n\nThat new direction may expose the back.\n\nThe goal is therefore not always to eliminate all movement.\n\nSometimes the goal is to eliminate the movements you do not want while leaving one predictable route available.\n\n## **Funnel Effect**\n\nThe strongest constraint systems create a funnel.\n\nThe opponent appears to retain choice, but their realistic options progressively narrow toward a predictable response.\n\nThis creates:\n\n**Constraint → Reduced Options → Predictable Reaction → Exploitation**\n\nThe athlete is not simply reacting faster.\n\nThey are shaping which reactions become available.\n\n## **Defensive Application**\n\nConstraint Accumulation also applies defensively.\n\nWhen escaping, the athlete should identify which constraints are currently preventing movement.\n\nThe question becomes:\n\nWhat is actually stopping the escape?\n\nRather than trying to move the entire body at once, the athlete can remove constraints in sequence.\n\nFor example:\n\n- recover inside position,\n- free the head,\n- create hip movement,\n- rebuild frames,\n- then escape.\n\nDefence becomes **constraint removal**.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should identify:\n\n- which constraints the athlete successfully creates,\n- which important constraints are missing,\n- which defensive option remains available,\n- and whether the athlete attempts to advance before sufficient control exists.\n\nA common diagnostic may be:\n\nThe athlete is attempting the next action before enough defensive options have been removed.\n\nThis is more useful than simply saying:\n\n“You lost the position.”\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nCoaching should often focus on the missing constraint.\n\nInstead of:\n\n“Squeeze harder.”\n\nA better intervention may be:\n\n“Control the shoulder first so they can’t rotate into the escape.”\n\nThe principle is to identify the freedom that allows the defence and remove it.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Control improves as meaningful options disappear.**\n\nStrong grappling does not always overpower resistance directly.\n\nIt progressively shapes the opponent’s available choices until the next response becomes predictable and exploitable.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Reaction Mapping]]\n- [[Offence–Defence Cycle]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Constraint Creation.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Constraint Creation","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand the creation of situations where the opponent’s available choices become progressively narrower.\n\nHigh-level combat systems do not only attack positions.\n\nThey create constraints.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA strong combat sequence reduces opponent freedom.\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n“What options does the opponent still have?”\n\nThe fewer viable options remain, the more predictable the exchange becomes.\n\n## Constraint Structure\n\nThreat\n→ Opponent Reaction\n→ Option Removal\n→ Increased Predictability\n→ Secondary Threat\n→ Further Constraint\n\n## Constraint Types\n\n### Positional Constraint\n\nThe opponent’s movement is physically restricted.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Hip control\n- Head control\n- Pinning shoulders\n- Blocking rotation\n- Removing base\n\n### Structural Constraint\n\nThe opponent loses useful alignment or defensive structure.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Broken posture\n- Extended limb\n- Separated elbow-knee connection\n- Collapsed frame\n- Misaligned hips and shoulders\n\n### Tactical Constraint\n\nThe opponent still has movement but only a limited number of strategically useful choices.\n\n### Reaction Constraint\n\nA credible threat forces a small set of predictable reactions.\n\n### Temporal Constraint\n\nThe opponent has very little time to respond before the next action becomes available.\n\n## Constraint vs Control\n\nControl and constraint are related but not identical.\n\nControl asks:\n\n“Can the athlete maintain the position?”\n\nConstraint asks:\n\n“How many useful choices does the opponent still have?”\n\nA position can be controlled without creating much offensive constraint.\n\nA strong system often does both.\n\n## Example\n\nAthlete establishes front headlock pressure.\n\nOpponent can:\n\n- Post\n- Circle\n- Drive forward\n- Pull away\n\nThe athlete then uses position and threat to progressively remove those responses.\n\nAs options disappear, the opponent becomes easier to predict.\n\n## Constraint Cascades\n\nSynochi should identify when one constraint produces another.\n\nExample:\n\nHead control\n→ posture broken\n→ hands forced to mat\n→ arm becomes occupied\n→ back exposure increases\n\nThis is more valuable than treating each event as separate.\n\n## Constraint Quality\n\nSynochi should assess:\n\n- How many opponent options are removed\n- Whether remaining options are predictable\n- Whether the athlete can punish those remaining options\n- Whether constraint can be maintained without excessive energy\n- Whether it connects into existing strengths\n\n## False Constraint\n\nA position may look dominant but still leave the opponent many effective choices.\n\nSynochi should avoid assuming positional appearance equals meaningful control.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Constraint\n\nDifferent athletes may create constraint through different tools:\n\n- Pressure\n- Pace\n- Grip control\n- Threat density\n- Speed\n- Positional wedges\n- Submission threats\n- Scramble pressure\n\nSynochi should learn how the individual athlete most effectively narrows opponent options.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should increasingly understand combat as the management of available choices.\n\nThe goal is to help the athlete create exchanges where the opponent’s reactions become more predictable and easier to exploit.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Initiative Control]]\n\n[[Threat Hierarchy]]\n\n[[Control Before Progression]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Control Before Progression.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Control Before Progression","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should judge whether an athlete has enough control to progress safely and effectively.\n\nCombat Intelligence should not reward constant movement for its own sake.\n\nProgression without control often creates unnecessary instability.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nBefore advancing, Synochi should ask:\n\n“Is the current position sufficiently controlled to justify progression?”\n\nIf not, the better action may be to stabilise first.\n\n## Control Indicators\n\nSynochi should consider whether the athlete has:\n\n- Stable base\n- Useful head position\n- Effective inside position\n- Hip control\n- Upper-body control\n- Frame denial\n- Reduced opponent mobility\n- Reliable connection\n- Predictable opponent reactions\n- Low immediate reversal risk\n\n## Progression Readiness\n\nA position is more ready for progression when:\n\n- The opponent’s major escape routes are limited\n- The athlete can maintain connection during transition\n- The next action does not expose a larger threat\n- The athlete has positional structure\n- The transition connects to a known strength\n\n## Premature Progression\n\nSynochi should recognise when the athlete advances before control is established.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Chasing mount before securing side control\n- Attacking a submission while base is compromised\n- Passing without controlling hip movement\n- Taking the back without securing upper-body connection\n- Shooting a secondary attack before stabilising the first position\n\n## False Activity\n\nHigh movement does not always mean high quality.\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- Productive progression\n- Necessary adjustment\n- Reactive scrambling\n- Uncontrolled movement\n- Activity that creates no meaningful advantage\n\n## Consolidation\n\nConsolidation is the process of making an advantageous state more stable before progressing.\n\nThis may include:\n\n- Settling weight\n- Rebuilding base\n- Controlling frames\n- Securing grips or connections\n- Removing defensive structures\n- Forcing predictable reactions\n\n## Exception\n\nControl before progression is not absolute.\n\nSome situations reward immediate transition because hesitation reduces the opportunity.\n\nSynochi should account for:\n\n- Transitional windows\n- Opponent exposure\n- Time pressure\n- Ruleset\n- Athlete-specific strengths\n- Whether the next action itself creates control\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nPrefer:\n\nControl\n→ Stabilise\n→ Progress\n\nunless:\n\nThe available transition has higher expected value than continued consolidation.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help athletes distinguish between useful aggression and unnecessary positional haste.\n\nThe goal is not slower grappling.\n\nThe goal is progression from positions that are stable enough to support it.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[Threat Hierarchy]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Decision Load Management.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Decision Load Management","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand the cognitive burden created by too many technical choices inside a combat system.\n\nMore options do not automatically create a better game.\n\nA strong system should reduce unnecessary decision load while preserving enough flexibility to deal with realistic opponent reactions.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should prefer:\n\nFewer reliable decisions\n→ faster recognition\n→ cleaner execution\n\nover:\n\nToo many possible options\n→ hesitation\n→ slower reactions\n→ inconsistent execution\n\n## Decision Load Structure\n\nSituation\n→ Available Options\n→ Cue Recognition\n→ Option Selection\n→ Execution\n\nThe more options an athlete must evaluate, the greater the decision burden.\n\n## High Decision Load\n\nDecision load becomes excessive when:\n\n- Too many techniques are available from the same position\n- Branches overlap without clear triggers\n- Athlete does not know which cue activates which response\n- Several options have similar value\n- Athlete repeatedly hesitates\n- Athlete changes decisions mid-sequence\n- Technical knowledge exceeds practical reliability\n\n## Low Decision Load\n\nA well-structured system may contain:\n\n- One primary objective\n- Two or three common opponent reactions\n- One reliable answer for each reaction\n- One safe fallback\n\nThis gives the athlete clarity without making the system rigid.\n\n## Cue-Based Simplification\n\nSynochi should reduce decision load by linking actions to clear opponent cues.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent stays low\n→ attack front headlock\n\nOpponent builds posture\n→ go behind\n\nOpponent drives forward\n→ re-attack\n\nThe athlete does not need to choose between every possible technique.\n\nThey need to recognise the cue.\n\n## Option Redundancy\n\nSynochi should identify when multiple techniques solve the same problem.\n\nIf three options produce similar outcomes, Synochi should ask:\n\n- Which is most reliable?\n- Which best fits the athlete?\n- Which has the strongest continuation?\n- Which creates the lowest decision burden?\n\nLower-value redundant options may be deprioritised.\n\n## Decision Compression\n\nAs evidence accumulates, Synochi should compress complex technical knowledge into simple rules.\n\nExample:\n\nInstead of:\n\n“From this position you can use five different attacks.”\n\nPrefer:\n\n“If they turn in, do this.\nIf they turn away, do this.”\n\nThis creates a more usable combat system.\n\n## Decision Load Under Pressure\n\nCompetition, fatigue, speed, and stress can reduce decision capacity.\n\nSynochi should account for this when recommending systems.\n\nA technically complex system that works in drilling may fail under competition pressure because decision load becomes too high.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Decision Capacity\n\nDifferent athletes may perform well with different levels of complexity.\n\nSynochi should learn whether the athlete performs better with:\n\n- Simple narrow systems\n- Moderate branching\n- High improvisational freedom\n\nThe system should adapt to the athlete rather than force one model of complexity.\n\n## Complexity Threshold\n\nNew branches should only be added when:\n\n- Existing branches are reliable\n- A meaningful recurring problem remains unsolved\n- The new branch has a clear cue\n- The athlete can recognise and execute it\n- The added complexity produces enough value\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help the athlete build systems that are simple enough to execute under pressure and flexible enough to handle realistic resistance.\n\nThe goal is not maximum technical variety.\n\nThe goal is maximum usable intelligence.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]\n\n[[Dilemma Construction]]\n\n[[Trilemma Construction]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Game Pillar Architecture]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Decision Point Recognition.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Decision Point Recognition","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should identify the moments in a combat sequence where the athlete has multiple meaningful options and the quality of the decision determines what happens next.\n\nCombat Intelligence should not only recognise techniques.\n\nIt should recognise choice points.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA decision point occurs when:\n\n- More than one viable action is available\n- The opponent’s position or reaction changes the best option\n- Timing matters\n- The athlete’s choice meaningfully affects the next phase of the exchange\n\nSynochi should learn not only what the athlete did, but what they could reasonably have done.\n\n## Decision Point Structure\n\nSituation\n→ Available Options\n→ Opponent Cues\n→ Athlete Choice\n→ Outcome\n→ Evaluation\n\n## Example\n\nAthlete reaches front headlock.\n\nOpponent posts with the near hand.\n\nAvailable options may include:\n\n- Snapdown\n- Go-behind\n- Arm attack\n- Re-shot\n- Disengage\n\nSynochi should evaluate whether the athlete selected an option that matched the opponent’s reaction and the athlete’s existing strengths.\n\n## Cue Recognition\n\nUseful decision points depend on recognising cues such as:\n\n- Weight shift\n- Posture change\n- Hand position\n- Hip direction\n- Base width\n- Head position\n- Frame placement\n- Limb exposure\n- Pressure direction\n- Opponent retreat or forward drive\n\nSynochi should gradually learn which cues reliably predict useful actions.\n\n## Decision Quality\n\n### Strong Decision\n\nThe athlete selects an option that:\n\n- Matches the opponent’s reaction\n- Fits the tactical context\n- Connects to an existing strength\n- Produces a favourable outcome or strong continuation\n\n### Acceptable Decision\n\nThe option is reasonable but not clearly optimal.\n\n### Weak Decision\n\nThe athlete selects an option that:\n\n- Ignores a strong available cue\n- Creates unnecessary risk\n- Breaks connection to their preferred game\n- Repeatedly leads to poor outcomes\n\n## Decision vs Execution\n\nSynochi should separate:\n\n### Good Decision, Poor Execution\n\nThe correct option was selected but technically failed.\n\n### Poor Decision, Good Execution\n\nThe athlete succeeded despite choosing a lower-value option.\n\n### Good Decision, Good Execution\n\nThe choice and execution were both strong.\n\n### Poor Decision, Poor Execution\n\nBoth selection and execution contributed to failure.\n\nThis distinction is important for accurate coaching.\n\n## Repeated Decision Patterns\n\nSynochi should identify whether the athlete repeatedly:\n\n- Chooses the same option regardless of opponent reaction\n- Misses high-value branches\n- Recognises cues late\n- Chooses technically difficult options unnecessarily\n- Selects options that consistently connect to strengths\n\nRepeated decision patterns should become part of the athlete’s combat profile.\n\n## Decision Compression\n\nWhen the same situation repeatedly produces the same choice pattern, Synochi may compress it into a useful model.\n\nExample:\n\n“Against defensive posting from front headlock, athlete consistently attempts snapdowns even when go-behind access is stronger.”\n\nThis should influence future coaching and drill design.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should evolve from recognising actions into understanding tactical choice.\n\nThe goal is to help the athlete make better decisions under resistance, not simply accumulate more techniques.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Opportunity Pattern Identification]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Dilemma Construction.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Dilemma Construction","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand situations where the opponent is forced to choose between two or more responses, each of which exposes a meaningful consequence.\n\nA strong combat system does not only create attacks.\n\nIt creates dilemmas.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA dilemma exists when defending one threat reliably exposes another.\n\nSynochi should identify sequences where:\n\n- The opponent must respond\n- The response solves one problem\n- The same response creates another problem\n- The athlete is prepared to exploit either branch\n\n## Dilemma Structure\n\nThreat A\n→ Opponent Response\n→ Threat B becomes available\n\nOR\n\nOpponent avoids Threat A\n→ Threat A remains available\n\nThis creates a constrained decision environment.\n\n## Example\n\nAthlete threatens a front headlock attack.\n\nOpponent raises posture to defend.\n\nThat reaction may expose:\n\n- Go-behind\n- Re-shot\n- Back access\n\nIf the opponent stays low, the original front headlock threat remains dangerous.\n\nThe value lies in forcing a choice where both branches favour the athlete.\n\n## Dilemma vs Combination\n\nA combination is:\n\nAction A\n→ Action B\n\nA dilemma is:\n\nThreat A\n→ opponent must choose\n→ choice exposes Threat B or preserves Threat A\n\nSynochi should distinguish between pre-planned sequences and reaction-dependent dilemmas.\n\n## Dilemma Quality\n\nA strong dilemma should have:\n\n- Credible first threat\n- Predictable defensive reactions\n- Reliable secondary option\n- Minimal reset between branches\n- Strong connection to athlete strengths\n- Limited opponent escape routes\n\n## False Dilemma\n\nSynochi should not classify a sequence as a dilemma if:\n\n- The first threat is not credible\n- The opponent has several safe alternatives\n- The second option depends on a low-probability reaction\n- The athlete cannot reliably execute the follow-up\n\n## Dilemma Density\n\nSynochi should identify positions where the athlete has multiple connected threats available.\n\nHigher dilemma density can make a position more valuable because the opponent has fewer clean defensive choices.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Submission plus back exposure\n- Sweep plus wrestle-up\n- Pass plus submission threat\n- Takedown plus front headlock\n- Escape denial plus submission isolation\n\n## Dilemma Chains\n\nOne dilemma may lead into another.\n\nThreat\n→ Reaction\n→ Secondary Threat\n→ New Reaction\n→ Tertiary Threat\n\nSynochi should recognise when an athlete can maintain pressure through multiple linked decision points.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Dilemmas\n\nSynochi should learn which dilemmas the athlete already creates naturally.\n\nIt should prioritise building around:\n\n- Existing strengths\n- Repeated opponent reactions\n- Reliable transitions\n- High-confidence positions\n\nThe goal is not to force generic systems onto every athlete.\n\n## Coaching Use\n\nWhen Synochi identifies a weak sequence, it should ask:\n\n- Is the athlete presenting only one threat?\n- Can the opponent defend without consequence?\n- What second threat naturally punishes the common defence?\n- Can the two actions be trained as one decision system?\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help transform isolated attacks into connected threat systems.\n\nThe goal is to create situations where the opponent’s defensive choices increasingly feed the athlete’s next action.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Constraint Creation]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Initiative Control]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Energy Cost of Action.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Energy Cost of Action","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should account for the physical cost of combat actions.\n\nTwo actions may produce similar outcomes while requiring very different amounts of energy.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nCombat efficiency matters.\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n“What does this action cost relative to what it produces?”\n\n## Energy Cost Factors\n\nSynochi should consider:\n\n- Muscular effort\n- Isometric tension\n- Explosive demand\n- Repeated failed attempts\n- Grip fatigue\n- Scramble intensity\n- Positional pressure required\n- Recovery requirement\n- Opponent resistance\n\n## High-Cost Patterns\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Repeated explosive bridging\n- Forcing submissions without isolation\n- Holding inefficient grips\n- Constant failed takedown attempts\n- Maintaining unstable positions through strength\n- Repeated scrambles caused by poor control\n\n## Efficient Action\n\nAn efficient action produces useful advantage with relatively low unnecessary physical cost.\n\nEfficiency may come from:\n\n- Better structure\n- Better timing\n- Better leverage\n- More predictable reaction creation\n- Greater positional control\n- Cleaner decision-making\n\n## Energy Debt\n\nSynochi should recognise when repeated high-cost actions create downstream performance decline.\n\nExample:\n\nRepeated failed shots\n→ fatigue\n→ slower reactions\n→ weaker defence\n→ positional loss\n\nThe original issue may therefore have both technical and energetic consequences.\n\n## Tactical Energy Use\n\nHigh energy expenditure is not always bad.\n\nIt may be justified when:\n\n- Finish probability is high\n- Match time is short\n- Scoring opportunity is significant\n- Opponent is close to breaking\n- Competition context demands urgency\n\n## Athlete-Specific Efficiency\n\nSynochi should learn:\n\n- Which positions drain the athlete\n- Which systems they can sustain\n- Where they recover well\n- Which techniques repeatedly create unnecessary energy cost\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help the athlete produce more useful combat output per unit of effort.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Tempo and Pace Control]]\n\n[[Risk-Reward Evaluation]]\n\n[[Control Before Progression]]\n\n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Escape Route Preservation.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Escape Route Preservation","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should evaluate whether an athlete is preserving viable defensive options while under pressure.\n\nDefence should not be judged only by whether the athlete escapes.\n\nSynochi should also recognise whether the athlete is maintaining the structure and options required to escape later.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA defensive position becomes more dangerous when available escape routes are progressively removed.\n\nSynochi should track not only:\n\n“What escape is available?”\n\nbut also:\n\n“Which escape routes are still alive?”\n\n## Escape Route Definition\n\nAn escape route is a realistic path from a disadvantageous state toward:\n\n- Safer structure\n- Neutral position\n- Guard recovery\n- Stand-up\n- Reversal\n- Scramble\n- Disengagement\n\n## Route Preservation\n\nSynochi should recognise actions that preserve future options, including:\n\n- Maintaining frames\n- Protecting inside space\n- Preventing limb isolation\n- Keeping hips mobile\n- Preserving elbow-knee connection\n- Protecting head position\n- Avoiding flattened posture\n- Maintaining access to underhooks or posts\n- Preventing full upper-body control\n\n## Route Loss\n\nA defensive state becomes more severe when the athlete loses multiple escape routes.\n\nExample:\n\nBottom side control\n→ inside frame removed\n→ near elbow isolated\n→ hips flattened\n→ head controlled\n→ guard recovery options collapse\n\nThe final escape failure may begin much earlier.\n\n## Route Hierarchy\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n### Primary Escape Route\n\nThe athlete’s most reliable or highest-value path out.\n\n### Secondary Escape Route\n\nA viable alternative if the primary route becomes unavailable.\n\n### Emergency Route\n\nA lower-quality but still useful option to prevent further deterioration.\n\n## Route Denial\n\nOpponent actions should be analysed partly by which defensive routes they remove.\n\nExample:\n\nCrossface\n→ reduces head mobility\n\nUnderhook\n→ reduces turning access\n\nHip control\n→ reduces guard recovery\n\nThis allows Synochi to understand control as restriction of options.\n\n## Defensive Priority\n\nWhen under pressure, Synochi should often prioritise:\n\nPreserve Routes\n→ Restore Structure\n→ Create Space\n→ Initiate Escape\n→ Recover State\n\nAttempting an escape after all major routes are already closed may be too late.\n\n## False Escape Attempts\n\nSynochi should identify when an athlete repeatedly attempts an escape that is no longer structurally available.\n\nThis may indicate:\n\n- Late recognition\n- Poor route selection\n- Habitual response\n- Failure to detect route closure\n\n## Athlete-Specific Escape Routes\n\nSynochi should learn which defensive routes are most reliable for the specific athlete.\n\nSome athletes may favour:\n\n- Wrestle-ups\n- Guard recovery\n- Inversions\n- Underhook escapes\n- Explosive bridges\n- Technical stand-ups\n\nA defensive recommendation should account for the athlete’s demonstrated strengths.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand defence as preservation of future options.\n\nThe goal is to recognise when defensive structure is deteriorating before the athlete reaches a point where every escape becomes low probability.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[Threat Hierarchy]]\n\n[[Position-Specific Objective]]\n\n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Fallback Hierarchy.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Fallback Hierarchy","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand what the athlete should fall back to when a preferred option, branch, or system fails.\n\nA strong combat game should not collapse when Plan A is removed.\n\nIt should degrade intelligently.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should identify:\n\n- Primary option\n- Secondary option\n- Tertiary option\n- Safe reset\n\nThe athlete should know what comes next when the preferred route is denied.\n\n## Fallback Structure\n\nPrimary System\n→ Denied\n→ Secondary System\n→ Denied\n→ Tertiary System\n→ Safe Reset\n\n## Example\n\nPreferred route:\n\nSingle leg\n→ opponent sprawls\n\nFallback:\n\nFront headlock\n\nIf front headlock is lost:\n\nGo-behind or re-shot\n\nIf neither is available:\n\nReset to standing\n\nThe key is that each failure still leaves a known next state.\n\n## Fallback Types\n\n### Technical Fallback\n\nA secondary technique inside the same position.\n\n### Branch Fallback\n\nA different branch inside the same system.\n\n### Pillar Fallback\n\nTransfer into another game pillar.\n\n### Positional Fallback\n\nReturn to a stable position.\n\n### Tactical Reset\n\nDisengage and rebuild the exchange.\n\n## Fallback Quality\n\nA strong fallback should:\n\n- Be familiar\n- Preserve structure\n- Avoid major risk\n- Connect to existing strengths\n- Require minimal decision complexity\n- Be realistic under resistance\n\n## Failure Without Fallback\n\nSynochi should identify situations where the athlete:\n\n- Freezes after the first option fails\n- Repeats the same failed attack\n- Forces low-value continuation\n- Gives up position unnecessarily\n- Enters chaotic scrambles with no clear plan\n\nThese may indicate missing fallback structure.\n\n## Fallback Depth\n\nNot every system needs endless alternatives.\n\nSynochi should prefer:\n\nPrimary\n→ Secondary\n→ Safe Reset\n\nover:\n\nLarge numbers of low-confidence options.\n\n## Fallback Reliability\n\nFallbacks should be ranked by:\n\n- Recognition speed\n- Execution reliability\n- Positional safety\n- Connection to strengths\n- Competition evidence\n\n## Athlete-Specific Fallbacks\n\nDifferent athletes should have different fallback priorities.\n\nAn athlete with strong wrestling may prefer:\n\nFailed guard attack\n→ wrestle-up\n\nAnother athlete may prefer:\n\nFailed guard attack\n→ re-guard and reset\n\nSynochi should learn which fallbacks preserve the athlete’s strongest game.\n\n## Strategic Value\n\nFallback hierarchy increases resilience.\n\nIt allows the athlete to remain composed when the preferred sequence breaks.\n\nThe athlete should increasingly experience:\n\n“That option is gone, so I go here.”\n\nrather than:\n\n“That failed, now I improvise.”\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help build combat systems that remain functional even when preferred branches are removed.\n\nThe goal is not perfect execution.\n\nThe goal is robust adaptation.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Game Pillar Architecture]]\n\n[[System Transfer Logic]]\n\n[[System Exit Routes]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Game Pillar Architecture.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Game Pillar Architecture","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should identify and organise the athlete’s highest-value combat systems into a small number of core game pillars.\n\nA game pillar is not a single technique.\n\nIt is a reliable system that repeatedly gives the athlete access to useful positions, reactions, scoring opportunities, or finishes.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should help the athlete build around a small number of highly coherent systems rather than accumulating disconnected techniques.\n\nA strong game should increasingly become:\n\n- Easier for the athlete to navigate\n- Harder for the opponent to disrupt\n- More predictable in structure\n- More adaptable in execution\n\n## Game Pillar Definition\n\nA game pillar is a combat system that:\n\n- Appears frequently\n- Connects naturally to the athlete’s strengths\n- Produces multiple useful branches\n- Holds up under resistance\n- Transfers across opponents\n- Has clear entries\n- Has reliable continuations\n- Can recover when the first option fails\n\n## Example\n\nFront Headlock System\n\nPossible structure:\n\nEntry\n→ Front Headlock Control\n→ Opponent Reaction\n→ Snapdown\n→ Go-Behind\n→ Submission\n→ Re-shot\n→ Back Exposure\n\nThe pillar is not “guillotine.”\n\nThe pillar is the entire connected front-headlock decision system.\n\n## Pillar Components\n\nEach game pillar should contain:\n\n### Entry Routes\n\nHow does the athlete reliably reach the system?\n\n### Control State\n\nWhat position anchors the pillar?\n\n### Primary Threats\n\nWhat actions force meaningful opponent reactions?\n\n### Reaction Branches\n\nWhat does the opponent commonly do?\n\n### Responses\n\nWhat does the athlete do against each reaction?\n\n### Recovery Routes\n\nWhat happens when the first attack fails?\n\n### Exit Routes\n\nHow can the athlete safely leave or transition into another pillar?\n\n## Pillar Strength\n\n### Emerging Pillar\n\nPromising system with repeated success but incomplete branches.\n\n### Functional Pillar\n\nReliable enough to influence training and game planning.\n\n### Core Pillar\n\nOne of the athlete’s highest-confidence competitive systems.\n\n## Pillar Connectivity\n\nSynochi should identify where pillars connect.\n\nExample:\n\nWrestling Pillar\n→ Front Headlock Pillar\n→ Back Attack Pillar\n\nor:\n\nGuard Pillar\n→ Leg Entry Pillar\n→ Top Position\n\nThe strongest athlete models may contain a small number of pillars that feed into one another.\n\n## Pillar Redundancy\n\nDifferent pillars should ideally provide answers to different combat problems.\n\nSynochi should avoid building multiple pillars that all solve the same narrow situation while leaving major phases of combat unsupported.\n\n## Pillar Expansion\n\nA pillar should expand when:\n\n- New reactions repeatedly appear\n- A reliable new branch develops\n- Another athlete strength naturally connects\n- Competition evidence validates the addition\n\nExpansion should remain controlled.\n\n## Pillar Pruning\n\nA branch or entire pillar may be downgraded when:\n\n- It rarely appears\n- It no longer performs reliably\n- Another system consistently outperforms it\n- It creates excessive decision load\n- It conflicts with the athlete’s evolving style\n\n## Pillar Priority\n\nSynochi should give greater development attention to pillars that have:\n\n- High recurrence\n- High consequence\n- Strong athlete fit\n- Strong branch reliability\n- High competition relevance\n- Strong connection to other systems\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should progressively compress the athlete’s combat knowledge into a small number of high-confidence systems.\n\nThe goal is not to know everything.\n\nThe goal is to create a game where the athlete repeatedly reaches familiar situations, recognises the opponent’s reactions, and already has reliable answers.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Strength Pattern Identification]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]\n\n[[Dilemma Construction]]\n\n[[Trilemma Construction]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Initiative Control.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Initiative Control","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand which athlete is currently forcing the other to react.\n\nInitiative is not the same as movement, aggression, or attacking first.\n\nIt is the ability to shape the exchange and constrain the opponent’s choices.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nThe athlete with initiative is usually the athlete creating the questions the opponent must answer.\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- Acting\n- Reacting\n- Forcing reactions\n- Recovering initiative\n- Losing initiative\n\n## Initiative Structure\n\nAction\n→ Opponent Forced Response\n→ Continued Pressure\n→ Reduced Opponent Options\n→ Athlete Maintains Initiative\n\n## Initiative Indicators\n\nSynochi should look for:\n\n- Opponent repeatedly reacting rather than initiating\n- Athlete controlling pace or direction\n- Athlete forcing predictable defensive responses\n- Athlete connecting attacks without allowing reset\n- Opponent abandoning preferred game\n- Athlete deciding where the exchange develops\n- Opponent spending time recovering structure\n\n## False Initiative\n\nActivity does not automatically equal initiative.\n\nAn athlete may appear aggressive while actually reacting to the opponent.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Constantly scrambling from bad positions\n- Throwing attacks without controlling reactions\n- Moving quickly while opponent dictates position\n- Repeatedly defending and countering without establishing their own sequence\n\nSynochi should evaluate who is shaping the exchange.\n\n## Initiative Gain\n\nInitiative may be gained through:\n\n- Strong positional control\n- Effective first contact\n- Successful off-balancing\n- Creating credible threats\n- Forcing predictable reactions\n- Breaking opponent structure\n- Establishing preferred grips or connections\n- Winning a transition\n\n## Initiative Loss\n\nInitiative may be lost when:\n\n- Athlete becomes purely reactive\n- An attack creates poor position\n- Opponent resets the exchange\n- Athlete loses connection\n- Athlete hesitates at a key decision point\n- Defensive pressure forces abandonment of preferred game\n\n## Initiative Recovery\n\nSynochi should identify actions that allow the athlete to regain control of the exchange.\n\nExamples:\n\nDefence\n→ frame\n→ create space\n→ wrestle up\n→ initiate attack\n\nor:\n\nFailed attack\n→ maintain connection\n→ secondary attack\n→ force new reaction\n\n## Initiative Chains\n\nSynochi should recognise repeated sequences where initiative is maintained across multiple actions.\n\nExample:\n\nPressure pass\n→ opponent frames\n→ redirect\n→ opponent turns\n→ back exposure\n→ control\n\nThe important feature is not only technical success.\n\nIt is that the opponent never regains meaningful control of the exchange.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Initiative\n\nDifferent athletes may control initiative differently.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Forward pressure\n- Counter-attacking\n- Grip fighting\n- Pace manipulation\n- Positional control\n- Threat chaining\n- Scramble creation\n\nSynochi should not assume initiative always looks aggressive.\n\n## Tactical Context\n\nInitiative value should account for:\n\n- Score\n- Time remaining\n- Ruleset\n- Position\n- Athlete fatigue\n- Opponent style\n- Competition strategy\n\nSometimes preserving position is more valuable than forcing continued initiative.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand who is shaping the fight.\n\nThe system should help identify:\n\n- Where the athlete reliably takes initiative\n- Where they surrender it\n- How they recover it\n- Which actions allow them to maintain control of the exchange\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Threat Hierarchy]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Offence–Defence Cycle.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Offence–Defence Cycle","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nCombat exchanges should not be understood as isolated techniques.\n\nAn attack creates a defensive response.\n\nThat defensive response changes position, posture, balance, exposure, and available options.\n\nThose changes create the next offensive opportunity.\n\nThe basic cycle is:\n\n**Attack → Defensive Reaction → Positional Change → New Exposure → Re-Attack**\n\nOffence and defence are therefore not separate phases.\n\nThey continuously create one another.\n\n## **Why It Matters**\n\nA technique can fail while still improving the attacker’s position.\n\nFor example:\n\n- a submission attempt may force an opponent to expose their back,\n- a guard pass attempt may force a frame that opens another direction,\n- a takedown attempt may create posture or balance reactions that expose a secondary attack.\n\nThe value of an attack is therefore not only:\n\nDid it finish?\n\nbut also:\n\nWhat reaction did it force?\n\n## **Defensive Reactions as Information**\n\nEvery defensive response reveals something.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- direction of escape,\n- preferred frame,\n- posture change,\n- weight shift,\n- limb exposure,\n- balance recovery,\n- grip commitment,\n- turning tendency.\n\nThese reactions should be treated as information that shapes the next decision.\n\n## **Chaining**\n\nEffective offence often depends on chaining attacks around predictable defensive reactions.\n\nThe structure becomes:\n\n**Threat A → Reaction A → Attack B**\n\nrather than repeatedly forcing Threat A after the opponent has already solved it.\n\nThis creates adaptive offence.\n\n## **Defence Creates Offence**\n\nThe cycle also works in reverse.\n\nA defensive movement may create an offensive transition.\n\nFor example:\n\n- escaping a pin may expose an underhook,\n- recovering guard may create a leg-entry opportunity,\n- defending a takedown may create front-headlock access,\n- defending a submission may reverse positional advantage.\n\nGood defence should therefore not always aim merely to return to neutral.\n\nWhere possible, it should transition directly into offence.\n\n## **Positional Continuity**\n\nThe important unit is not the individual technique.\n\nIt is the **sequence of changing constraints**.\n\nCombat Intelligence should therefore reason about:\n\n- what the current threat forces,\n- what reactions are likely,\n- what those reactions expose,\n- and which next action best exploits the new position.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nWhen diagnosing a failed attack, FRAME should not only ask:\n\nWhy did the technique fail?\n\nIt should also ask:\n\nWhat reaction did the attack create, and was the athlete prepared to exploit it?\n\nA technically correct attack may still be strategically incomplete if there is no follow-up for the expected defence.\n\n## **Video Analysis Implication**\n\nVideo analysis should look beyond isolated success or failure.\n\nA sequence may show:\n\nAthlete attacks → opponent gives predictable reaction → athlete resets instead of exploiting exposure.\n\nThe useful observation may therefore be:\n\nThe primary issue is not attack selection, but failure to recognise and connect the defensive reaction to the next action.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Every attack changes the problem. Every defence creates new information.**\n\nCombat should be understood as a continuous cycle of:\n\n**threat → reaction → adaptation → new threat**\n\nrather than a collection of disconnected techniques.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Opponent Adaptation Detection.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Opponent Adaptation Detection","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should recognise when an opponent changes their behaviour in response to the athlete’s successful actions.\n\nA strong opponent does not remain static.\n\nSynochi should detect when previously reliable reactions begin to change.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA previously successful pattern may lose value once the opponent adapts.\n\nSynochi should therefore track:\n\nInitial Pattern\n→ Athlete Exploitation\n→ Opponent Adjustment\n→ New Behaviour\n→ Tactical Reassessment\n\n## Adaptation Signals\n\nSynochi should look for changes such as:\n\n- Opponent stops giving the same reaction\n- Opponent changes stance or posture\n- Opponent switches defensive direction\n- Opponent begins pre-empting a known attack\n- Opponent changes grip strategy\n- Opponent disengages earlier\n- Opponent alters pace\n- Opponent begins countering a repeated entry\n- Opponent sacrifices one position to protect another\n\n## Adaptation vs Random Variation\n\nNot every change is meaningful.\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n### Noise\n\nOne unusual response with no recurrence.\n\n### Emerging Adaptation\n\nA new response begins appearing after the athlete repeatedly exploits the old one.\n\n### Confirmed Adaptation\n\nThe opponent consistently changes behaviour in the same context.\n\n## Adaptation Trigger\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n“What caused the opponent to change?”\n\nPossible causes include:\n\n- Repeated punishment\n- Better recognition of the athlete’s setup\n- Score or time pressure\n- Fatigue\n- Coaching instruction\n- Tactical reset\n- Increased risk awareness\n\n## Tactical Response\n\nWhen adaptation is detected, Synochi should evaluate:\n\n- Is the original option still viable?\n- Has a new branch opened?\n- Can the athlete disguise the same attack differently?\n- Should the athlete transfer to another system?\n- Is the opponent now overcorrecting?\n\n## Overcorrection\n\nAdaptation can create new weaknesses.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent repeatedly gets caught turning away.\n\nThey begin aggressively turning inward instead.\n\nThat adjustment may now expose:\n\n- Underhooks\n- Front headlock\n- Sweep direction\n- Submission access\n\nSynochi should look for these secondary opportunities.\n\n## Adaptation Loop\n\nAthlete Threat\n→ Opponent Reaction\n→ Athlete Exploits\n→ Opponent Adapts\n→ New Reaction\n→ Athlete Re-adapts\n\nThis loop should be treated as a core part of combat intelligence.\n\n## Adaptation Speed\n\nSynochi should also track how quickly an opponent adapts.\n\n### Slow Adapter\n\nContinues repeating the same response after being punished.\n\n### Moderate Adapter\n\nChanges after several repetitions.\n\n### Fast Adapter\n\nAdjusts after one or two clear exposures.\n\nThis can influence how aggressively the athlete should repeat successful patterns.\n\n## Athlete Adaptation\n\nSynochi should also monitor whether the athlete themselves adapts when the opponent changes.\n\nRepeatedly forcing an old branch after the opponent has solved it may indicate tactical rigidity.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand combat as an evolving interaction.\n\nThe goal is not only to detect patterns.\n\nIt is to detect when those patterns stop being reliable and help the athlete adapt faster than the opponent.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Opponent Response Profiling]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]\n\n[[System Transfer Logic]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Opponent Counterfactual Modeling.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Opponent Counterfactual Modeling","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should reason about alternative opponent responses that did not occur but were realistically available.\n\nThe goal is to avoid overfitting the athlete’s game to only the reactions already observed.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n“What else could a competent opponent have done here?”\n\nCombat Intelligence should model not only actual reactions, but also plausible defensive alternatives.\n\n## Counterfactual Structure\n\nObserved Situation\n→ Actual Opponent Reaction\n→ Athlete Response\n→ Alternative Plausible Reactions\n→ Risk Assessment\n→ System Robustness\n\n## Why Counterfactuals Matter\n\nA sequence may appear highly successful because the opponent selected one favourable reaction.\n\nSynochi should test whether the system remains strong if the opponent instead:\n\n- Turns the other direction\n- Refuses to engage\n- Changes base\n- Posts differently\n- Stalls\n- Scrambles\n- Counters immediately\n- Breaks connection earlier\n- Accepts a lesser positional loss to avoid a larger threat\n\n## Actual vs Plausible\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n### Observed Reaction\n\nWhat the opponent actually did.\n\n### Plausible Alternative\n\nA realistic response available from the same position.\n\n### Low-Probability Alternative\n\nTechnically possible but unlikely under normal resistance.\n\nCounterfactual reasoning should remain realistic.\n\n## System Robustness\n\nA robust system should remain useful across several realistic opponent responses.\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n- Does the current branch only work against one reaction?\n- Is the first threat still credible if the opponent reacts differently?\n- Does another game pillar become available?\n- Does the athlete have a safe fallback?\n- Does the system collapse if the opponent refuses the expected branch?\n\n## Example\n\nAthlete repeatedly attacks from front headlock.\n\nObserved opponent reaction:\n\nBuild posture\n→ athlete goes behind.\n\nCounterfactual alternatives may include:\n\n- Opponent stays compact\n- Opponent drives forward\n- Opponent sits through\n- Opponent disengages early\n\nSynochi should evaluate whether the broader system has useful responses to these alternatives.\n\n## Anti-Overfitting Rule\n\nSynochi should not assume:\n\n“Opponent did X three times, therefore X is the only meaningful reaction.”\n\nRepeated evidence increases confidence in a tendency but should not erase other realistic possibilities.\n\n## Counterfactual Priority\n\nAlternative reactions deserve more attention when:\n\n- They are common at higher levels\n- They expose a weakness in the current system\n- They would create major consequences\n- The athlete has no clear answer\n- The same alternative appears in previous matches or sessions\n\n## Training Use\n\nCounterfactual modeling can inform:\n\n- Reaction drills\n- Positional sparring\n- Branch testing\n- Competition preparation\n- Scenario training\n- System stress-testing\n\nThe goal is to expose the athlete to credible alternative reactions before competition does.\n\n## Opponent-Specific Use\n\nSynochi should combine:\n\nGeneral plausible reactions\n+\nObserved opponent tendencies\n\nThis allows the system to remain robust without ignoring opponent-specific evidence.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help the athlete build systems that survive intelligent resistance.\n\nThe goal is not to predict one reaction perfectly.\n\nThe goal is to remain functional across the most realistic reaction space.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Opponent Response Profiling]]\n\n[[Opponent Adaptation Detection]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]\n\n[[Fallback Hierarchy]]\n\n[[System Transfer Logic]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Opponent Response Profiling.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Opponent Response Profiling","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should build a temporary working model of an opponent based on repeated reactions during a match, round, or observed sequence.\n\nThe goal is not to label the opponent permanently.\n\nThe goal is to identify what they are currently showing and adapt accordingly.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n“What does this opponent repeatedly do when presented with the same problem?”\n\nOpponent behaviour becomes useful when reactions begin to repeat.\n\n## Profiling Structure\n\nAthlete Action\n→ Opponent Reaction\n→ Repetition\n→ Response Pattern\n→ Tactical Adjustment\n\n## Observable Response Patterns\n\nSynochi should look for repeated tendencies such as:\n\n- Always posting with the same hand\n- Turning consistently in one direction\n- Retreating under forward pressure\n- Driving forward when threatened\n- Giving underhooks\n- Overcommitting to frames\n- Standing quickly from guard\n- Protecting one limb before another\n- Repeatedly conceding the same angle\n- Reacting late to secondary attacks\n\n## Temporary Model\n\nOpponent models should remain context-specific.\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- Strong repeated pattern\n- Emerging tendency\n- Single reaction\n- Unknown behaviour\n\nA temporary match-level model should not automatically become a permanent truth about the opponent.\n\n## Pattern Confidence\n\n### Low Confidence\n\nObserved once or under unclear conditions.\n\n### Moderate Confidence\n\nRepeated in similar situations.\n\n### High Confidence\n\nAppears consistently across several comparable exchanges.\n\nHigher confidence should justify stronger tactical adaptation.\n\n## Reaction Prediction\n\nWhen confidence increases, Synochi may begin predicting likely opponent responses.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete applies passing pressure.\n\nOpponent has repeatedly turned away.\n\nPrediction:\n\nBack exposure is increasingly likely when pressure reaches the same threshold.\n\nSynochi can then prioritise preparation for that branch.\n\n## Exploitation Rule\n\nA repeated tendency should only be exploited when:\n\n- The pattern is sufficiently reliable\n- The athlete has a strong response available\n- The adjustment does not create excessive risk\n- The opportunity fits the current tactical context\n\n## Adaptation\n\nSynochi should update the opponent model when behaviour changes.\n\nAn opponent may alter strategy after being punished for a repeated reaction.\n\nThe system should therefore monitor:\n\nPrevious Pattern\n→ Opponent Adjustment\n→ New Evidence\n→ Model Update\n\n## False Pattern Warning\n\nSynochi should avoid overfitting to:\n\n- One unusual exchange\n- Fatigue-specific behaviour\n- Injury-related behaviour\n- Boundary effects\n- Score-driven desperation\n- Single technical mistake\n\nContext matters.\n\n## Athlete Interaction\n\nOpponent responses may depend on the athlete’s specific threats.\n\nThe same opponent may react differently against different styles.\n\nSynochi should therefore model:\n\nThis opponent\nagainst\nthis athlete\nin\nthis context\n\nrather than assuming universal behaviour.\n\n## Competition Use\n\nOpponent response profiling may influence:\n\n- Branch selection\n- Tempo changes\n- Entry choice\n- Threat sequencing\n- Dilemma construction\n- Tactical risk\n- Game-plan adjustment\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should learn opponent behaviour quickly enough to improve decision quality within the same competitive context.\n\nThe goal is adaptive tactical intelligence.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Dilemma Construction]]\n\n[[Trilemma Construction]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Position-Specific Objective.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Position-Specific Objective","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should identify the primary objective of the athlete in a given combat position.\n\nThe same action can be good or bad depending on what the athlete is actually trying to achieve from that state.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nEvery position should have a hierarchy of useful objectives.\n\nSynochi should evaluate actions against the demands of the current position rather than against generic technical ideals.\n\n## Position Objective Structure\n\nPosition\n→ Immediate Requirement\n→ Primary Objective\n→ Secondary Objective\n→ Available Progression\n\n## Example\n\n### Bottom Side Control\n\nImmediate requirement:\n\nPrevent submission and further positional deterioration.\n\nPrimary objective:\n\nRecover structure and create space.\n\nSecondary objective:\n\nRebuild frames and establish an escape route.\n\nProgression:\n\nRecover guard, wrestle up, or return to neutral.\n\nA submission attempt from here may be technically possible but strategically low priority.\n\n## Example\n\n### Top Half Guard\n\nImmediate requirement:\n\nMaintain base and prevent underhook-based reversal.\n\nPrimary objective:\n\nControl upper body and hips.\n\nSecondary objective:\n\nFlatten opponent or win inside position.\n\nProgression:\n\nPass, mount, or create submission access.\n\n## Position Objective Categories\n\nSynochi should consider:\n\n- Survival\n- Structure recovery\n- Stabilisation\n- Control\n- Escape\n- Reversal\n- Scoring\n- Progression\n- Isolation\n- Submission\n- Disengagement\n- Tactical reset\n\n## Objective Conflict\n\nSometimes multiple objectives compete.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete in mount may have:\n\n- Strong submission access\n- Weak positional stability\n- Limited time remaining\n\nSynochi should determine which objective has the highest value in context.\n\n## Objective Misalignment\n\nA decision may be technically valid but positionally misaligned.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete attacks aggressively while their base is unstable.\n\nThe issue is not necessarily the attack itself.\n\nThe issue may be that the attack conflicts with the current positional priority.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Objective\n\nPosition objectives should adapt to the athlete’s game.\n\nAn athlete with a strong leg-lock system may value certain transitional positions differently from an athlete focused on passing and top control.\n\nSynochi should learn which positions function as:\n\n- Destination states\n- Transitional states\n- Offensive hubs\n- Defensive recovery states\n- Low-value states\n\nfor the specific athlete.\n\n## Ruleset Influence\n\nPosition objective should also consider:\n\n- Points\n- Advantages\n- Submission-only rules\n- Time remaining\n- Overtime structure\n- Penalties\n- Boundary position\n\nA good positional objective may change depending on competition context.\n\n## Objective Evaluation\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n- What does the athlete need first?\n- What would improve the state most?\n- What risks exist if they progress now?\n- What opportunities are available?\n- Which objective best matches the athlete’s game?\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand what the athlete is trying to accomplish from each position.\n\nThis allows coaching to become more contextual, tactical, and athlete-specific rather than technique-driven.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[Threat Hierarchy]]\n\n[[Control Before Progression]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Positional State Transition.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Positional State Transition","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand combat as movement between states rather than as isolated techniques.\n\nA position is not static.\n\nEvery exchange contains transitions that improve, worsen, or preserve the athlete’s situation.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should evaluate not only:\n\n“What technique happened?”\n\nbut also:\n\n“What state did the athlete move from, and what state did they move into?”\n\n## State Structure\n\nCurrent State\n→ Action\n→ Opponent Reaction\n→ Transition\n→ New State\n\n## State Categories\n\nA combat state may be:\n\n### Advantageous\n\nThe athlete has meaningful positional, tactical, or finishing advantage.\n\n### Neutral\n\nNeither athlete has a clear advantage.\n\n### Disadvantageous\n\nThe opponent has meaningful control, scoring access, or finishing potential.\n\n### Transitional\n\nThe exchange is unstable and both athletes still have meaningful access to improvement.\n\n## State Improvement\n\nSynochi should recognise when an action improves the athlete’s state.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Neutral standing\n→ successful entry\n→ connected takedown position\n\n- Open guard\n→ off-balance\n→ top position\n\n- Side control\n→ isolated arm\n→ submission exposure\n\n## State Degradation\n\nSynochi should recognise when the athlete’s position becomes progressively worse.\n\nExample:\n\nGuard\n→ inside position lost\n→ opponent controls hips\n→ pass initiated\n→ side control\n\nThe final state may be side control, but Synochi should understand the entire degradation process.\n\n## Transitional Windows\n\nTransitions often contain the highest-value opportunities.\n\nSynochi should pay particular attention to:\n\n- Scrambles\n- Guard-pass transitions\n- Takedown finishes\n- Submission escapes\n- Stand-up transitions\n- Back exposure\n- Mount recovery\n- Opponent posture changes\n\nThese are moments where state can change rapidly.\n\n## Stable vs Unstable States\n\n### Stable State\n\nThe athlete has enough control that the position is relatively resistant to immediate change.\n\n### Unstable State\n\nSmall actions can significantly change the outcome.\n\nSynochi should distinguish technical mistakes made in stable positions from decisions made during unstable transitions.\n\n## State Preservation\n\nSometimes the correct action is not progression.\n\nThe athlete may need to:\n\n- Consolidate\n- Stabilise\n- Recover structure\n- Prevent reversal\n- Reduce immediate risk\n\nSynochi should not reward constant progression when preservation is tactically superior.\n\n## State Value\n\nState value should consider:\n\n- Positional control\n- Scoring potential\n- Submission potential\n- Exposure to danger\n- Ability to initiate\n- Ability to disengage\n- Energy cost\n- Ruleset\n- Time remaining\n- Athlete’s preferred game\n\n## Athlete-Specific State Value\n\nThe same position may have different value for different athletes.\n\nExample:\n\nA front headlock may be extremely valuable for an athlete with a strong front-headlock system but less valuable for an athlete who rarely attacks from there.\n\nSynochi should therefore combine general positional value with athlete-specific competence.\n\n## Transition Pattern\n\nRepeated transitions should be tracked.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete repeatedly moves:\n\nHalf guard top\n→ knee cut attempt\n→ opponent underhook\n→ dogfight\n→ loss of top position\n\nThis should become a meaningful transition pattern rather than four isolated events.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand how the athlete moves through combat states.\n\nThe system should progressively learn:\n\n- Which states the athlete creates well\n- Which states they struggle to maintain\n- Which transitions they exploit\n- Which transitions repeatedly cause breakdowns\n- Which states best connect to their preferred game\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Range and Distance State.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Range and Distance State","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand physical distance between athletes and how that distance changes which actions are available.\n\nThis becomes especially important as Combat Intelligence expands into wrestling, MMA, boxing, Muay Thai, and other combat sports.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nTechnique availability is constrained by range.\n\nSynochi should first understand:\n\n“Where are the athletes relative to each other?”\n\nbefore evaluating what action makes sense.\n\n## General Range States\n\n### Outside Range\n\nMinimal immediate contact.\n\nCommon priorities:\n\n- Distance management\n- Entry preparation\n- Feints\n- Angle creation\n\n### Entry Range\n\nAthletes can initiate meaningful contact.\n\nCommon priorities:\n\n- Timing\n- First contact\n- Level change\n- Grip or strike entry\n- Defensive readiness\n\n### Connected Range\n\nPhysical connection is established.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Clinch\n- Tie-up\n- Grip fighting\n- Body lock\n- Hand fighting\n\n### Ground Engagement\n\nRange becomes positional rather than purely spatial.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Open guard distance\n- Half guard\n- Chest-to-chest control\n- Submission range\n\n## Range Transition\n\nOutside\n→ Entry\n→ Connection\n→ Control\n\nor:\n\nControl\n→ Separation\n→ Reset\n\nSynochi should recognise the transition rather than treating each state independently.\n\n## Range Mismatch\n\nSynochi should identify when the athlete attempts an action from an unsuitable range.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Shooting from too far away\n- Striking while overextended\n- Attempting control without sufficient connection\n- Passing without managing guard distance\n\n## Athlete-Specific Range\n\nDifferent athletes may perform best at different ranges.\n\nSynochi should learn:\n\n- Preferred distance\n- Strongest entry range\n- Strongest connected positions\n- Weakest transition range\n- Opponent styles that disrupt preferred distance\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand range as a state variable that determines what actions are realistically available.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[System Entry Routes]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Tempo and Pace Control]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Reaction Chain Mapping.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Reaction Chain Mapping","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should model combat as a sequence of actions and reactions rather than isolated techniques.\n\nA technique gains more value when Synochi understands what it tends to make the opponent do next.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nCombat is interactive.\n\nAn athlete acts.\n\nThe opponent reacts.\n\nThat reaction creates a new decision point.\n\nSynochi should therefore model:\n\nAction\n→ Opponent Reaction\n→ Available Response\n→ New Reaction\n→ Continuation\n\n## Reaction Chain\n\nA reaction chain is a repeatable sequence where one action predictably creates another response.\n\nExample:\n\nArm drag\n→ opponent squares up\n→ athlete switches to single leg\n\nOr:\n\nKnee cut pressure\n→ opponent frames\n→ athlete redirects into leg drag\n\nThe value is not only in recognising each technique.\n\nThe value is in understanding the connection between them.\n\n## Reaction Categories\n\nSynochi should identify common opponent reactions such as:\n\n- Post\n- Frame\n- Turn away\n- Turn in\n- Square posture\n- Retreat\n- Drive forward\n- Pull limb free\n- Extend\n- Contract\n- Base wider\n- Base narrower\n- Stand\n- Sit\n- Underhook\n- Overhook\n- Hip escape\n- Wrestle up\n- Protect neck\n- Protect limb\n\n## Forced vs Voluntary Reaction\n\n### Forced Reaction\n\nA response the opponent is strongly compelled to make.\n\nThese reactions are especially valuable because they create predictable follow-up opportunities.\n\n### Voluntary Reaction\n\nOne of several reasonable choices available to the opponent.\n\nThese should be modelled with lower certainty.\n\n## Branching\n\nSynochi should avoid assuming every opponent reacts the same way.\n\nA useful reaction model may contain branches.\n\nExample:\n\nFront headlock pressure\n→ opponent posts hand\n→ attack arm\n\nOR\n\nFront headlock pressure\n→ opponent circles away\n→ go behind\n\nOR\n\nFront headlock pressure\n→ opponent drives forward\n→ snapdown or re-attack\n\n## Athlete-Specific Reaction Chains\n\nSynochi should learn which reactions this athlete reliably produces.\n\nThis matters because two athletes may use the same technique but create different opponent behaviour based on:\n\n- Pressure\n- Timing\n- Body type\n- Speed\n- Grip preference\n- Threat profile\n- Technical style\n\n## Reaction Reliability\n\nSynochi should track:\n\n### Low Reliability\n\nReaction appears occasionally.\n\n### Moderate Reliability\n\nReaction appears repeatedly in similar contexts.\n\n### High Reliability\n\nThe athlete can consistently create the reaction against resisting opponents.\n\nHigh-reliability reactions can become foundations for game planning.\n\n## Reaction Exploitation\n\nOnce Synochi identifies a predictable reaction, it should ask:\n\n- Does the athlete recognise it?\n- Do they have a reliable response?\n- Does that response connect to an existing strength?\n- Is there a stronger continuation available?\n- Can the reaction be deliberately created?\n\n## Game Development Principle\n\nDo not only teach more techniques.\n\nBuild stronger connections between:\n\nThreat\n→ Reaction\n→ Answer\n\nA coherent game becomes increasingly predictable for the athlete and increasingly difficult for the opponent.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should learn not only what the athlete does, but what their actions cause opponents to do.\n\nThis allows Combat Intelligence to evolve from technique recognition into interactive combat reasoning.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Strength Pattern Identification]]\n\n[[Opportunity Pattern Identification]]\n\n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Reaction Mapping.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Reaction Mapping","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nReaction Mapping is the process of identifying how an opponent responds to a threat and using that response to predict the next available action.\n\nThe central idea is:\n\n**Threats create reactions. Reactions create openings.**\n\nA technique should therefore not be understood only by its direct objective.\n\nIt should also be understood by the reactions it reliably produces.\n\n## **Reaction Structure**\n\nFor any meaningful attack or positional threat, Combat Intelligence should ask:\n\n- What reaction is this likely to create?\n- What direction does the opponent move?\n- What posture changes?\n- What frames or grips appear?\n- What weight shifts?\n- What limbs become exposed?\n- What new positions become available?\n\nThis creates a map from:\n\n**Threat → Reaction → Opportunity**\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete begins passing to one side.\n\nThe opponent reacts by:\n\n- turning their hips,\n- inserting a frame,\n- exposing an underhook,\n- or shifting weight away from the passing direction.\n\nThe correct next action may depend less on forcing the original pass and more on recognising which reaction occurred.\n\nThe reaction determines the next branch.\n\n## **Preferred Reactions**\n\nAthletes often defend in recurring ways.\n\nOne opponent may consistently:\n\n- turn away,\n- frame hard,\n- turtle,\n- square back up,\n- retreat,\n- overcommit an underhook,\n- or expose the legs.\n\nThese tendencies become useful tactical information.\n\nIf a reaction repeats, the athlete can begin attacking the reaction rather than merely attacking the position.\n\n## **Reaction Chains**\n\nReactions can also be chained.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Threat A → Reaction A → Threat B → Reaction B → Finish**\n\nThe athlete is no longer hoping one technique works.\n\nThey are guiding the opponent through a sequence of increasingly constrained choices.\n\n## **Forced vs Optional Reactions**\n\nNot all reactions are equal.\n\nSome are:\n\n**Forced reactions**  \nThe opponent has very few realistic alternatives.\n\nOthers are:\n\n**Preferred reactions**  \nThe opponent has options but repeatedly chooses one.\n\nOthers are:\n\n**Unpredictable reactions**  \nThe situation remains too open to anticipate reliably.\n\nStrong systems tend to create more forced or highly probable reactions.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nReaction Mapping is especially useful for video analysis.\n\nFRAME should look for moments where:\n\n- an athlete successfully creates a reaction,\n- the reaction exposes something useful,\n- but the athlete fails to recognise or exploit it.\n\nThis may indicate that the athlete’s problem is not technical execution.\n\nIt may be:\n\npoor reaction recognition.\n\nOr:\n\ninsufficient connection between one attack and the next.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of teaching:\n\n“Do this technique.”\n\nCoaching can become:\n\n“Use this threat to create this reaction. When they give you that reaction, move to the next action.”\n\nThis creates more adaptive and transferable skill.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**The opponent’s reaction is part of the technique.**\n\nA complete attack should include not only:\n\n**what I do**\n\nbut also:\n\n**what they are likely to do because of it**\n\nand:\n\n**what I do next.**\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Offence–Defence Cycle]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Recovery State Logic.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Recovery State Logic","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand what happens after an athlete loses advantage, misses an attack, or enters a compromised position.\n\nRecovery is not simply “escaping.”\n\nIt is the process of restoring enough structure, control, or initiative to re-enter a useful part of the athlete’s game.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- Immediate survival\n- Structural recovery\n- Positional recovery\n- Tactical recovery\n- Re-entry into offence\n\nA good recovery sequence should move the athlete from instability toward a familiar and usable state.\n\n## Recovery Structure\n\nThreat or Failure\n→ Stabilise\n→ Restore Structure\n→ Recover Position\n→ Rebuild Initiative\n→ Re-enter Preferred System\n\n## Recovery State Categories\n\n### Emergency State\n\nImmediate submission, scoring, or positional danger.\n\nPrimary goal:\n\nSurvive and prevent further deterioration.\n\n### Structural Recovery State\n\nAthlete is still disadvantaged but can rebuild:\n\n- Frames\n- Base\n- Posture\n- Alignment\n- Inside position\n- Hip mobility\n\n### Positional Recovery State\n\nAthlete has restored enough structure to improve position.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Side control\n→ half guard\n\n- Mount\n→ elbow-knee escape\n→ guard\n\n- Failed shot\n→ front headlock defence\n→ neutral standing\n\n### Tactical Recovery State\n\nAthlete is no longer in immediate danger but has not yet regained initiative.\n\nThe goal becomes:\n\n- Reset\n- Reconnect\n- Establish preferred grips\n- Rebuild pressure\n- Create new entry\n\n### Offensive Re-entry\n\nRecovery becomes complete when the athlete can return to a familiar offensive system.\n\n## Recovery Quality\n\nSynochi should assess:\n\n- How quickly structure is restored\n- Whether unnecessary risk is avoided\n- Whether the athlete chooses an appropriate recovery route\n- Whether the recovery connects to an existing game pillar\n- Whether initiative can be regained\n- Energy cost of the recovery\n\n## Recovery Failure\n\nSynochi should identify repeated patterns such as:\n\n- Panicked movement\n- Attempting offence before structure is restored\n- Escaping into another bad position\n- Repeatedly choosing low-value scramble routes\n- Failing to recognise when neutralisation has already been achieved\n\n## Recovery vs Reset\n\nA reset returns the exchange to a neutral or familiar starting state.\n\nA recovery may end before full reset if the athlete reaches a sufficiently strong state.\n\nExample:\n\nBad scramble\n→ half guard top\n\nThe athlete may not have returned to the original system, but the recovery is successful because a strong usable state has been restored.\n\n## Recovery Efficiency\n\nHigh-quality recovery should minimise:\n\n- Positional loss\n- Energy waste\n- Unnecessary exposure\n- Decision chaos\n- Time spent in low-value states\n\n## Athlete-Specific Recovery\n\nSynochi should learn the athlete’s most reliable recovery routes.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Wrestle-up recovery\n- Guard recovery\n- Scramble recovery\n- Technical stand-up\n- Front headlock re-entry\n- Underhook-based reversal\n\nThese should influence future coaching and drill prescriptions.\n\n## Recovery Chain\n\nFailure\n→ Preserve Escape Route\n→ Restore Structure\n→ Improve State\n→ Recover Initiative\n→ Transfer into Game Pillar\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand recovery as a structured process rather than an isolated escape.\n\nThe goal is to help the athlete recover intelligently, minimise damage, and return to familiar systems as efficiently as possible.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Escape Route Preservation]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[Fallback Hierarchy]]\n\n[[System Transfer Logic]]\n\n[[Initiative Control]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Risk-Reward Evaluation.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Risk-Reward Evaluation","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should evaluate whether a combat action is worth attempting given its potential upside, downside, probability of success, and tactical context.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nThe best technical option is not always the best tactical option.\n\nSynochi should consider:\n\nPotential Reward\n→ Probability of Success\n→ Failure Cost\n→ Positional Risk\n→ Tactical Context\n\n## Reward Categories\n\nPotential reward may include:\n\n- Submission\n- Score\n- Positional advancement\n- Back exposure\n- Opponent fatigue\n- Initiative gain\n- Entry into a strong game pillar\n- Forced reaction\n- Time advantage\n\n## Risk Categories\n\nPotential risk may include:\n\n- Submission exposure\n- Positional loss\n- Reversal\n- Score concession\n- Energy waste\n- Loss of initiative\n- Entry into opponent strength\n- Boundary disadvantage\n- Time loss\n\n## Risk-Reward Profile\n\n### High Reward / Low Risk\n\nGenerally strong option.\n\n### High Reward / High Risk\n\nContext-dependent.\n\nMay be appropriate when urgency is high or athlete has strong confidence in the sequence.\n\n### Low Reward / Low Risk\n\nUseful for consolidation, control, or tactical management.\n\n### Low Reward / High Risk\n\nUsually poor unless unusual context justifies it.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Risk\n\nThe same action may carry different risk for different athletes.\n\nA leg-entry transition may be low risk for an athlete with a strong leg-lock system and high risk for an athlete with poor leg-position awareness.\n\nSynochi should therefore combine general risk with athlete-specific reliability.\n\n## Context Factors\n\nRisk tolerance should change based on:\n\n- Score\n- Time remaining\n- Ruleset\n- Competition round\n- Athlete fatigue\n- Opponent strength\n- Current position\n- Available fallback\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help the athlete choose actions with strong expected value rather than simply technically available actions.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Threat Hierarchy]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]\n\n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Score-Time-Ruleset Context.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Score-Time-Ruleset Context","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should modify combat recommendations based on score, time remaining, and competition rules.\n\nA technically good action may become tactically poor when competition context changes.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nCombat decisions should never be evaluated independently of the rules environment.\n\n## Context Inputs\n\nSynochi should consider:\n\n- Current score\n- Score differential\n- Time remaining\n- Advantages\n- Penalties\n- Submission-only format\n- Overtime rules\n- Boundary rules\n- Legal and illegal techniques\n- Stalling criteria\n\n## Score Context\n\n### Athlete Ahead\n\nPriority may shift toward:\n\n- Control\n- Risk reduction\n- Positional stability\n- Safe progression\n- Denying opponent initiative\n\n### Athlete Behind\n\nPriority may shift toward:\n\n- Higher-value scoring routes\n- Submission threats\n- Increased tempo\n- Greater tactical risk\n- Reduced emphasis on low-value control\n\n## Time Context\n\n### Early Match\n\nGreater opportunity for patient system development.\n\n### Mid Match\n\nTactical direction becomes clearer.\n\n### Late Match\n\nScore and urgency can dramatically alter optimal decisions.\n\n## Ruleset Influence\n\nThe same position may have different value under different rulesets.\n\nExample:\n\nA position that produces control but no scoring may be valuable in submission-only but tactically weaker in a points format when the athlete is behind.\n\n## Dynamic Priority\n\nSynochi should continuously update:\n\nPosition Value\n+\nScore\n+\nTime\n+\nRuleset\n=\nCurrent Tactical Priority\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand that combat intelligence is contextual.\n\nThe best decision is the best decision for this moment, not in isolation.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Threat Hierarchy]]\n\n[[Risk-Reward Evaluation]]\n\n[[Position-Specific Objective]]\n\n[[Tempo and Pace Control]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/System Entry Routes.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"System Entry Routes","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand the ways an athlete reliably enters their strongest combat systems.\n\nA powerful system is only useful if the athlete can actually reach it.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- Knowing a strong system\n- Reaching that system reliably\n\nEntry quality determines whether a game pillar is practically usable under resistance.\n\n## Entry Route Definition\n\nAn entry route is a repeatable pathway from one combat state into a preferred system.\n\nExamples:\n\nStanding\n→ collar tie\n→ snapdown\n→ front headlock\n\nOpen guard\n→ off-balance\n→ wrestle-up\n→ single leg\n\nTop half guard\n→ crossface\n→ knee cut\n→ dominant passing sequence\n\n## Entry Route Categories\n\n### Direct Entry\n\nThe athlete reaches the preferred system immediately.\n\n### Reaction Entry\n\nThe athlete creates a reaction that exposes the entry.\n\n### Transitional Entry\n\nThe system appears during a scramble or positional transition.\n\n### Counter Entry\n\nThe opponent initiates and the athlete redirects into a preferred system.\n\n### Recovery Entry\n\nThe athlete moves from a defensive or neutral position into a preferred offensive state.\n\n## Entry Reliability\n\nSynochi should evaluate:\n\n- How often the route appears\n- Whether it works against resistance\n- Whether it depends on a specific opponent behaviour\n- Whether the athlete recognises the opening\n- Whether the route maintains structural integrity\n- Whether it leads directly into a strong game pillar\n\n## Primary Entry Routes\n\nA primary entry route should be:\n\n- Frequent\n- Reliable\n- Low unnecessary complexity\n- Strongly connected to an existing game pillar\n- Usable across multiple opponent types\n\n## Secondary Entry Routes\n\nSecondary routes may be less frequent but still valuable when:\n\n- The primary route is denied\n- A different ruleset changes the exchange\n- The opponent’s style blocks the preferred entry\n- The athlete is forced into an unfamiliar position\n\n## Entry Failure\n\nSynochi should identify why entry attempts fail.\n\nPossible causes include:\n\n- Poor distance\n- Poor timing\n- Weak connection\n- Incorrect cue recognition\n- Predictable setup\n- Technical execution failure\n- Strong opponent denial\n- Entry chosen from the wrong state\n\n## Entry Chains\n\nA failed entry may still create another useful route.\n\nExample:\n\nSingle-leg attempt\n→ opponent sprawls\n→ front headlock access\n\nThis should not automatically be treated as failure if the secondary state connects to another strong pillar.\n\n## Entry Funnel\n\nSynochi should identify whether several different situations can funnel into the same preferred system.\n\nExample:\n\nSnapdown\n→ front headlock\n\nFailed shot\n→ front headlock\n\nOpponent wrestle-up\n→ front headlock\n\nScramble\n→ front headlock\n\nA system with multiple reliable entry routes becomes more strategically valuable.\n\n## Entry Denial\n\nSynochi should also learn how opponents prevent access to the athlete’s preferred systems.\n\nThis may include:\n\n- Distance management\n- Grip denial\n- Posture control\n- Angle change\n- Early disengagement\n- Hand fighting\n- Base adjustment\n\nRepeated entry denial should influence future drill and game-planning recommendations.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should learn how the athlete reliably reaches their strongest systems.\n\nThe goal is not only to identify game pillars.\n\nIt is to make those pillars increasingly accessible from real combat situations.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Game Pillar Architecture]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/System Exit Routes.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"System Exit Routes","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand the ways an athlete safely leaves, resets, or transfers out of a combat system when continuation is no longer high value.\n\nA good system should not only have entries and attacks.\n\nIt should also have clean exits.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- Productive continuation\n- Forced continuation\n- Tactical reset\n- Safe disengagement\n- Transfer into another game pillar\n\nStaying in a system too long can become as inefficient as abandoning it too early.\n\n## Exit Route Definition\n\nAn exit route is a repeatable pathway from a current system into:\n\n- Neutral position\n- Safer structure\n- Another strong game pillar\n- Tactical reset\n- Controlled disengagement\n\n## Exit Route Categories\n\n### Neutral Exit\n\nReturn to a stable neutral state.\n\n### Defensive Exit\n\nLeave the current exchange to reduce risk.\n\n### Transfer Exit\n\nMove directly into another game pillar.\n\n### Reset Exit\n\nBreak the exchange and rebuild from a preferred starting condition.\n\n### Scoring Exit\n\nSecure the available score or positional gain rather than forcing further attack.\n\n## Exit Triggers\n\nSynochi should recognise when an exit may be preferable because:\n\n- Branch reliability drops\n- Opponent has fully stabilised\n- Position is becoming increasingly risky\n- Athlete is losing structural control\n- Energy cost is rising without useful progress\n- Time or score changes the tactical objective\n- Another game pillar becomes more accessible\n- The current system has reached a dead end\n\n## Example\n\nAthlete attacks from front headlock.\n\nOpponent successfully clears the immediate submission threat and begins regaining posture.\n\nPossible options:\n\n- Force another low-percentage attack\n- Disengage\n- Re-shot\n- Go behind\n- Transition into another control system\n\nSynochi should evaluate whether continuing the original system still has positive expected value.\n\n## Exit Quality\n\nA strong exit should:\n\n- Preserve position where possible\n- Avoid unnecessary exposure\n- Maintain initiative when possible\n- Connect to another familiar state\n- Reduce decision chaos\n- Avoid excessive energy expenditure\n\n## Bad Exit\n\nAn exit may be poor when the athlete:\n\n- Gives up dominant position unnecessarily\n- Disengages while strong continuation remains\n- Creates a scramble that favours the opponent\n- Abandons a system because of hesitation rather than tactical need\n- Exits into an unfamiliar or low-value state\n\n## Exit vs Failure\n\nLeaving a system does not automatically mean the system failed.\n\nA successful sequence may end with:\n\nEntry\n→ Threat\n→ Opponent forced to defend\n→ Positional improvement\n→ Tactical exit\n\nThe athlete may have gained value even without completing the original attack.\n\n## Pillar Transfer\n\nHigh-level game architecture should allow movement between pillars.\n\nExample:\n\nLeg Entry System\n→ opponent clears knee line\n→ wrestle-up\n→ Wrestling Pillar\n\nor:\n\nPassing System\n→ opponent turns away\n→ Back Attack Pillar\n\nSynochi should identify these transfers as successful exits rather than broken sequences.\n\n## Exit Reliability\n\nSynochi should track whether the athlete can consistently:\n\n- Recognise when the current system is exhausted\n- Leave without conceding major advantage\n- Transfer into another strong position\n- Reset without unnecessary risk\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand when to stop forcing a sequence.\n\nThe goal is to create systems with reliable entries, strong internal branches, and intelligent exits.\n\nA mature game should allow the athlete to move between systems without losing coherence.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Game Pillar Architecture]]\n\n[[System Entry Routes]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]\n\n[[Initiative Control]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/System Robustness.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"System Robustness","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should judge whether a combat system continues to function when the opponent resists intelligently, adapts, denies preferred branches, or creates unfamiliar reactions.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA strong system should not depend on perfect conditions.\n\nIt should remain useful under disruption.\n\n## Robustness Questions\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n- Does the system work against multiple opponent styles?\n- Does it survive common defensive reactions?\n- Does it contain reliable fallbacks?\n- Can the athlete transfer when a branch dies?\n- Does it collapse if the primary entry is denied?\n- Can the athlete recover after failure?\n- Does it still function under fatigue or competition pressure?\n\n## Robustness Components\n\n### Entry Robustness\n\nCan the athlete access the system through more than one route?\n\n### Branch Robustness\n\nCan the system handle common opponent reactions?\n\n### Recovery Robustness\n\nCan the athlete recover when control breaks down?\n\n### Transfer Robustness\n\nCan the athlete move into another pillar rather than reset completely?\n\n### Adaptation Robustness\n\nCan the system evolve when the opponent changes behaviour?\n\n## Fragile System\n\nA fragile system may:\n\n- Depend on one specific reaction\n- Require perfect timing\n- Collapse after first defence\n- Have no fallback\n- Create excessive decision load\n- Fail against unfamiliar resistance\n\n## Robust System\n\nA robust system usually has:\n\n- Reliable entry\n- Clear primary objective\n- Limited high-quality branches\n- Strong fallback\n- Safe exit\n- Transfer route\n- Recovery structure\n\n## Stress Testing\n\nSynochi should stress-test systems through:\n\n- Alternative opponent reactions\n- Stronger resistance\n- Different body types\n- Different rulesets\n- Fatigue\n- Competition footage\n- Counterfactual analysis\n\n## System Maturity\n\n### Experimental\n\nTechnically plausible but weakly validated.\n\n### Functional\n\nWorks reliably in familiar contexts.\n\n### Robust\n\nWorks against varied resistance and adapts to disruption.\n\n### Core Competitive System\n\nHighly reliable under realistic competition conditions.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should favour combat systems that remain functional when reality becomes messy.\n\nThe goal is not a perfect flowchart.\n\nThe goal is a game that survives intelligent opposition.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Game Pillar Architecture]]\n\n[[Branch Reliability]]\n\n[[Fallback Hierarchy]]\n\n[[System Transfer Logic]]\n\n[[Opponent Counterfactual Modeling]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/System Transfer Logic.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"System Transfer Logic","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand movement from one established combat system into another.\n\nA mature game should not behave like a collection of isolated systems.\n\nIt should allow the athlete to transfer between game pillars as the opponent changes the exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nWhen one system loses value, Synochi should ask:\n\n“What strong system becomes available next?”\n\nThe goal is not to force continuation inside a dying sequence.\n\nThe goal is to preserve coherence by moving into another familiar system.\n\n## Transfer Structure\n\nCurrent System\n→ Opponent Reaction\n→ Transfer Cue\n→ New System\n→ Continued Initiative\n\n## Example\n\nWrestling attack\n→ opponent sprawls\n→ front headlock established\n→ Front Headlock Pillar\n\nThe original takedown did not succeed.\n\nBut the athlete successfully transferred into another strong system.\n\n## Another Example\n\nPassing pressure\n→ opponent turns away\n→ back exposure\n→ Back Attack Pillar\n\nThe pass itself may no longer be the highest-value objective.\n\nThe opponent’s reaction creates a transfer.\n\n## Transfer Types\n\n### Reaction Transfer\n\nThe opponent’s defence exposes another system.\n\n### Positional Transfer\n\nThe exchange naturally moves into another established position.\n\n### Counter Transfer\n\nThe opponent’s attack creates access to one of the athlete’s preferred systems.\n\n### Recovery Transfer\n\nThe athlete escapes or recovers directly into an offensive pillar.\n\n### Failure Transfer\n\nAn unsuccessful attack still creates useful access to another system.\n\n## Transfer Cue\n\nA transfer should be triggered by meaningful information.\n\nPossible cues include:\n\n- Opponent posture change\n- Back exposure\n- Limb exposure\n- Weight shift\n- Lost connection\n- Defensive frame\n- Sprawl\n- Turn away\n- Wrestle-up\n- Guard recovery\n- Opponent overcommitment\n\nSynochi should learn which cues reliably signal a transfer opportunity.\n\n## Transfer Quality\n\nA strong transfer should:\n\n- Preserve initiative where possible\n- Move toward an established strength\n- Require minimal unnecessary reset\n- Match the opponent’s reaction\n- Reduce decision complexity\n- Maintain positional or tactical value\n\n## Forced Transfer\n\nSynochi should recognise when the athlete stays inside a system after its useful branches have disappeared.\n\nThis may result in:\n\n- Low-percentage attacks\n- Energy waste\n- Position loss\n- Missed opportunities\n- Loss of initiative\n\nThe better response may be to transfer earlier.\n\n## Premature Transfer\n\nLeaving too early can also be inefficient.\n\nSynochi should avoid recommending transfer when:\n\n- Strong branches remain available\n- Control is still improving\n- The opponent has not solved the original system\n- The transfer sacrifices a clearly superior state\n\n## Pillar Network\n\nSynochi should progressively map relationships between the athlete’s game pillars.\n\nExample:\n\nWrestling Pillar\n→ Front Headlock Pillar\n→ Back Attack Pillar\n→ Mount System\n→ Submission System\n\nThe athlete’s game should increasingly resemble a connected network rather than separate technical categories.\n\n## Transfer Reliability\n\nSynochi should track:\n\n- How often the transfer opportunity appears\n- Whether the athlete recognises it\n- Whether the athlete executes it successfully\n- Whether the new pillar remains stable\n- Whether the transfer holds up under competition resistance\n\n## Game Coherence\n\nThe strongest transfer routes should make the athlete’s game feel continuous.\n\nInstead of:\n\n“Technique failed, start again.”\n\nSynochi should favour:\n\n“System changed, continue from here.”\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help create a combat game where failure of one branch does not end the athlete’s offensive structure.\n\nThe goal is continuous navigation between reliable systems.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Game Pillar Architecture]]\n\n[[System Entry Routes]]\n\n[[System Exit Routes]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Initiative Control]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Tempo and Pace Control.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Tempo and Pace Control","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand the speed, rhythm, and timing of an exchange.\n\nCombat performance is not only determined by technical choice.\n\nThe athlete can also influence when exchanges accelerate, slow down, reset, or become chaotic.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- Moving fast\n- Acting at the right time\n- Forcing the opponent to match pace\n- Slowing the exchange to regain control\n- Changing tempo to create mistakes\n\nTempo is a tactical variable.\n\n## Tempo Structure\n\nCurrent Rhythm\n→ Tempo Change\n→ Opponent Adjustment\n→ Reaction Window\n→ Exploitation\n\n## Pace Control\n\nPace control is the ability to influence how quickly exchanges develop.\n\nAn athlete may use:\n\n- Pressure\n- Grip fighting\n- Positional control\n- Rapid transitions\n- Pauses\n- Threats\n- Resets\n- Scramble initiation\n\nto change the opponent’s decision environment.\n\n## Tempo Change\n\nA tempo change occurs when the athlete deliberately alters the speed or rhythm of the exchange.\n\nExamples:\n\nSlow pressure\n→ opponent relaxes structure\n→ sudden transition\n\nFast scramble\n→ opponent overreacts\n→ athlete stabilises position\n\nStatic control\n→ opponent forced to move\n→ reaction becomes predictable\n\n## High Tempo\n\nHigh tempo may be useful when:\n\n- Opponent needs time to build structure\n- Athlete has superior reaction speed\n- Successive attacks create cumulative pressure\n- Opponent struggles during transitions\n- Time or score creates urgency\n\n## Low Tempo\n\nLower tempo may be useful when:\n\n- Athlete needs to consolidate control\n- Opponent thrives in scrambles\n- Energy conservation matters\n- Position is already advantageous\n- Athlete needs time to read reactions\n\n## False Pace\n\nHigh activity does not automatically mean useful pace.\n\nSynochi should identify when speed creates:\n\n- Poor decisions\n- Loss of control\n- Energy waste\n- Missed cues\n- Technical breakdown\n- Unnecessary scrambling\n\n## Tempo Mismatch\n\nSynochi should identify whether the athlete performs worse when forced outside their preferred rhythm.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Pressure athlete forced into repeated fast scrambles\n- Scramble-heavy athlete trapped in static control\n- Counter-fighter forced to initiate continuously\n\nRepeated tempo mismatch may become a meaningful tactical pattern.\n\n## Rhythm Recognition\n\nSynochi should look for recurring rhythm patterns.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent consistently pauses after defending the first attack.\n\nThat pause may become a predictable timing window for a secondary attack.\n\n## Tempo as Constraint\n\nTempo can constrain the opponent.\n\nHigh pace may reduce thinking time.\n\nSlow positional pressure may reduce movement options.\n\nThe correct tempo depends on what reaction the athlete is trying to create.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Tempo\n\nSynochi should learn:\n\n- Preferred pace\n- Best-performing tempo\n- Fatigue response\n- Ability to accelerate\n- Ability to slow exchanges\n- Positions where tempo control is strongest\n- Opponent styles that disrupt rhythm\n\n## Competition Context\n\nTempo recommendations should consider:\n\n- Time remaining\n- Score\n- Ruleset\n- Athlete conditioning\n- Opponent fatigue\n- Match phase\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand not only what the athlete does, but when and at what pace they do it.\n\nThe goal is to help the athlete control the rhythm of exchanges rather than simply react to the tempo imposed by the opponent.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Initiative Control]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Threat Hierarchy.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Threat Hierarchy","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should rank threats, opportunities, and priorities inside a live combat exchange.\n\nNot every available action deserves equal attention.\n\nSynochi should identify what matters most in the current moment.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nCombat decisions should be shaped by hierarchy.\n\nBefore progressing, the athlete may need to:\n\n- Survive\n- Prevent score\n- Recover structure\n- Stabilise position\n- Remove immediate threat\n- Create control\n- Attack\n- Progress\n\nThe correct priority depends on the state of the exchange.\n\n## Basic Threat Hierarchy\n\nA general hierarchy may be:\n\nImmediate Submission Threat\n→ Major Positional Loss\n→ Scoring Exposure\n→ Structural Breakdown\n→ Loss of Initiative\n→ Missed Opportunity\n→ Technical Efficiency\n\nHigher-order threats should normally override lower-order opportunities.\n\n## Example\n\nAthlete is in top half guard with a submission opportunity but their base is compromised.\n\nSynochi should not prioritise the submission if the athlete is about to be reversed.\n\nThe more important priority may be:\n\nRestore base\n→ stabilise\n→ re-establish control\n→ return to attack\n\n## Survival Before Progression\n\nWhen the athlete is under immediate threat, Synochi should prioritise:\n\n1. Remove danger\n2. Restore structure\n3. Recover control\n4. Re-enter preferred game\n\nProgression should not be recommended before stability exists.\n\n## Offensive Hierarchy\n\nWhen the athlete is in control, Synochi may prioritise:\n\nControl\n→ Reaction Creation\n→ Isolation\n→ Scoring or Positional Advancement\n→ Submission Access\n→ Finish\n\nThe exact hierarchy should adapt to the athlete’s style and ruleset.\n\n## Threat Types\n\n### Submission Threat\n\nImmediate risk of being finished.\n\n### Positional Threat\n\nRisk of losing a dominant or neutral position.\n\n### Scoring Threat\n\nRisk of conceding points or meaningful competitive advantage.\n\n### Structural Threat\n\nLoss of posture, alignment, inside position, frames, base, or connection.\n\n### Tactical Threat\n\nOpponent begins imposing a preferred sequence or forcing the athlete away from their game.\n\n### Time Threat\n\nClock, score, or ruleset creates urgency.\n\n## Opportunity Competition\n\nSometimes several good options exist.\n\nSynochi should rank them based on:\n\n- Risk\n- Reward\n- Athlete strength\n- Opponent reaction\n- Time\n- Score\n- Position\n- Ruleset\n- Probability of continuation\n\n## Athlete-Specific Hierarchy\n\nThe same situation may produce different priorities for different athletes.\n\nAn athlete with a strong front headlock system may treat front headlock access as high-value.\n\nAnother athlete may be better served by using the same reaction to secure top position.\n\nSynochi should therefore combine general combat hierarchy with athlete-specific game knowledge.\n\n## Dynamic Priority\n\nThreat hierarchy is not static.\n\nA sequence may move through:\n\nAttack\n→ opponent counter\n→ defensive threat\n→ recovery\n→ new opportunity\n\nSynochi should update priority as the state changes.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help determine:\n\n“What matters most right now?”\n\nThe system should reduce poor decision-making caused by chasing low-value opportunities while ignoring higher-order threats.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Transition Window Exploitation.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Transition Window Exploitation","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand the brief moments during positional change when an opponent is temporarily less stable, less structured, or more exposed.\n\nTransitions often create better opportunities than static positions.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA transition window exists when the opponent is moving between states and has not yet fully rebuilt structure.\n\nSynochi should learn to recognise:\n\n- When the window opens\n- What cue created it\n- How long it remains available\n- Which action best exploits it\n- Whether the athlete consistently reacts in time\n\n## Transition Window Structure\n\nStable State\n→ Movement or Reaction\n→ Temporary Exposure\n→ Recognition\n→ Exploitation\n→ New State\n\n## Common Transition Windows\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Opponent turning during a pass\n- Opponent standing from guard\n- Opponent posting during a sweep\n- Opponent retracting a limb after defending\n- Opponent changing base\n- Opponent recovering posture\n- Opponent scrambling after a failed attack\n- Opponent rotating during an escape\n- Opponent switching grips or frames\n\n## Why Windows Matter\n\nStatic positions often contain strong defensive structure.\n\nMovement temporarily disrupts that structure.\n\nSynochi should therefore distinguish between:\n\n“Technique is available”\n\nand\n\n“Technique is available now because the transition created the correct conditions.”\n\n## Window Quality\n\nA transition window becomes higher value when:\n\n- Opponent structure is compromised\n- Reaction is predictable\n- Athlete maintains connection\n- Opportunity aligns with an existing strength\n- Opponent has little time to reorganise\n- Successful action leads into a strong game pillar\n\n## Recognition Failure\n\nSynochi should identify when the athlete repeatedly sees opportunities too late.\n\nPossible causes include:\n\n- Slow cue recognition\n- Over-focus on the original attack\n- Poor anticipation\n- Excessive technical decision load\n- Failure to recognise opponent state change\n\n## Premature Exploitation\n\nThe athlete may also act before the window fully develops.\n\nThis can lead to:\n\n- Forced attacks\n- Weak connection\n- Poor timing\n- Loss of position\n- Missed stronger opportunities\n\nSynochi should distinguish late action from premature action.\n\n## Window Closure\n\nA transition window closes when the opponent:\n\n- Rebuilds base\n- Re-establishes frames\n- Recovers alignment\n- Creates distance\n- Regains posture\n- Removes exposure\n- Stabilises position\n\nOnce the window closes, Synochi should avoid recommending the same action as though the conditions remain unchanged.\n\n## Window Chaining\n\nOne exploited transition may create another.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent turns during pass\n→ back exposure\n→ opponent posts\n→ arm isolation\n→ submission opportunity\n\nSynochi should recognise these as connected windows rather than isolated techniques.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Windows\n\nSynochi should learn which transitional moments the athlete exploits best.\n\nSome athletes may excel during:\n\n- Scrambles\n- Guard recovery\n- Takedown transitions\n- Back exposure\n- Submission escapes\n- Passing reactions\n\nThese should become part of the athlete’s combat profile.\n\n## Training Implication\n\nTransition windows can be trained through:\n\n- Reaction drills\n- Positional sparring\n- Constraint-led rounds\n- Cue recognition\n- Delayed resistance\n- Transition-specific repetition\n\nThe goal is not only technical execution.\n\nIt is recognition speed.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand that combat opportunity is often temporary.\n\nThe system should help the athlete recognise and exploit high-value moments before the opponent has time to rebuild structure.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Tempo and Pace Control]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Combat Models/Trilemma Construction.md","folder":"Combat Models","title":"Trilemma Construction","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should understand situations where the opponent is forced into three meaningful defensive branches and each branch exposes a different consequence.\n\nA trilemma increases complexity for the opponent while preserving clarity for the athlete.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA trilemma exists when one offensive structure creates three credible responses and the athlete has a prepared answer for each.\n\nThe goal is not simply to have three techniques available.\n\nThe goal is to create a position where the opponent’s realistic choices are limited and each choice leads into a known continuation.\n\n## Trilemma Structure\n\nPrimary Threat\n→ Reaction A\n→ Response A\n\nOR\n\nPrimary Threat\n→ Reaction B\n→ Response B\n\nOR\n\nPrimary Threat\n→ Reaction C\n→ Response C\n\nThe athlete should be able to recognise which branch appears and transition accordingly.\n\n## Example\n\nAthlete establishes front headlock control.\n\nOpponent may:\n\n### Reaction A\nStay low and protect posture.\n\nPossible continuation:\n\nFront headlock submission attack.\n\n### Reaction B\nPost and begin building posture.\n\nPossible continuation:\n\nGo-behind or back exposure.\n\n### Reaction C\nDrive forward or re-enter aggressively.\n\nPossible continuation:\n\nRe-shot, snapdown, or angle change.\n\nThe value of the system comes from the athlete being prepared for all three major reactions.\n\n## Trilemma vs Dilemma\n\nA dilemma creates two meaningful branches.\n\nA trilemma creates three.\n\nDilemma:\n\nThreat\n→ Reaction A or B\n→ prepared answer\n\nTrilemma:\n\nThreat\n→ Reaction A, B, or C\n→ prepared answer for each branch\n\nThe increase in branches should only be considered useful if all three are genuinely credible and trainable.\n\n## True Trilemma Conditions\n\nSynochi should classify a sequence as a true trilemma when:\n\n- The initial threat is credible\n- Three common responses are repeatedly observed\n- Each response creates a distinct useful continuation\n- The athlete can recognise the difference between the reactions\n- The branches remain connected to the same system\n- The opponent has limited clean alternatives outside the three branches\n\n## False Trilemma\n\nSynochi should avoid forcing three branches where only two meaningful reactions exist.\n\nA false trilemma may occur when:\n\n- The third branch is extremely rare\n- The third option is not meaningfully different\n- One branch depends on unrealistic opponent behaviour\n- The athlete lacks a reliable continuation for one reaction\n\nSystem complexity should be earned by evidence.\n\n## Branch Recognition\n\nThe athlete must recognise which reaction is occurring.\n\nUseful cues may include:\n\n- Posture\n- Direction of pressure\n- Hand placement\n- Hip movement\n- Weight shift\n- Head position\n- Base width\n- Direction of escape\n\nThe quality of the trilemma depends on recognition speed as much as technical knowledge.\n\n## Branch Compression\n\nOnce the athlete repeatedly recognises and executes the correct branches, Synochi should begin treating the structure as one integrated decision system rather than three separate techniques.\n\nExample:\n\nFront Headlock Trilemma\n→ stay low = attack neck\n→ posture = go behind\n→ drive forward = re-attack\n\n## Trilemma Depth\n\nA branch of a trilemma may itself create another dilemma or trilemma.\n\nThis can produce deeper decision trees.\n\nHowever, Synochi should avoid unnecessary complexity.\n\nPrefer:\n\nSmall number of highly reliable branches\n\nover:\n\nLarge number of poorly connected options.\n\n## Athlete-Specific Trilemmas\n\nSynochi should identify which three-way decision structures naturally fit the athlete’s game.\n\nStrong candidates should usually emerge from:\n\n- Existing strengths\n- High-frequency positions\n- Predictable opponent reactions\n- Strong transition chains\n- Reliable threat creation\n\n## Coaching Use\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n- What are the three most common reactions from this position?\n- Can the athlete distinguish them quickly?\n- Is there a reliable answer to each?\n- Can those responses remain inside the athlete’s preferred game?\n- Can the entire structure be trained as one reaction-based system?\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should help athletes build compact systems where a small number of threats cover a large proportion of realistic opponent responses.\n\nThe goal is not technical complexity.\n\nThe goal is decision simplicity for the athlete and decision difficulty for the opponent.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Dilemma Construction]]\n\n[[Constraint Creation]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Decision Point Recognition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Backward Failure Tracing.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Backward Failure Tracing","body":"# Backward Failure Tracing\n\n**Category:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA diagnostic method that starts from an observed combat failure and traces backwards through preceding positions, decisions and opponent responses to identify earlier contributors and potential intervention points.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nIdentify where a recurring failure begins to develop, including decisions several steps before the visible loss.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Define the outcome.** Specify the observable failure: mount lost, guard passed, takedown conceded, submission escaped.\n2. **Reconstruct the path.** Trace the preceding states, athlete decisions and opponent responses backwards. Mark missing information explicitly.\n3. **Compare repeated instances.** Identify earlier decisions or conditions shared across failures, even when intermediate actions differ.\n4. **Compare successful instances.** Check whether the same earlier decisions also occur without failure, and identify where the paths diverge.\n5. **Locate candidate contributors.** Identify choices that repeatedly create vulnerability, remove useful options or make recovery more difficult.\n6. **Test an intervention.** Change one candidate contributor where practical and reassess the outcome under comparable conditions.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- The final mistake may be the consequence of an earlier decision.\n- Different sequences can converge on the same failure.\n- Repeated upstream decisions deserve investigation, but recurrence alone does not establish causation.\n- The earliest decision is not automatically the best intervention point.\n- Include opponent actions and positional constraints; failure does not always originate in the athlete’s decision.\n- Separate observed events from inferred explanations.\n- Describe failure as more likely or difficult to recover from unless inevitability is supported.\n- Revise the explanation when new evidence contradicts it.\n\n## **Diagnostic Output**\n\n- Observed failure.\n- Reconstructed sequence and evidence gaps.\n- Recurring earlier contributors.\n- Alternative explanations and successful counterexamples.\n- Candidate intervention point and rationale.\n- Confidence in the explanation.\n- Practice or observation needed to test it.\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete repeatedly loses mount after committing both hands to an attack. The opponent’s intermediate escape actions vary, but the sequences converge on a compromised base.\n\nBackward tracing investigates whether the earlier hand commitment created the vulnerability, whether successful attacks used different positioning or timing, and whether changing that commitment reduces mount losses.\n\n## **Scope**\n\nBackward Failure Tracing identifies and evaluates upstream contributors. Its findings inform the choice of coaching intervention; it does not automatically prioritise the earliest event.\n\n[[Backward Causal Reasoning]]\n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]\n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Competing Diagnostic Hypotheses.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Competing Diagnostic Hypotheses","body":"# **Competing Diagnostic Hypotheses**\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA diagnostic method that maintains and compares multiple plausible explanations for an observed combat performance pattern until evidence supports narrowing, combining or rejecting them.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nPrevent premature commitment to the first convincing explanation and identify what evidence would distinguish the alternatives.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Define the observation.** Describe what happened without embedding an assumed cause.\n2. **Generate plausible explanations.** Consider relevant technical, decision, opponent, constraint and athlete-state factors.\n3. **Specify distinguishing predictions.** State what should be observable if each explanation contributes.\n4. **Compare existing evidence.** Identify support, contradictions and missing information for each hypothesis.\n5. **Select a useful check.** Choose a question, observation or representative practice task that separates the leading explanations.\n6. **Update confidence.** Strengthen, weaken, combine or reject hypotheses according to the results.\n7. **Choose a proportionate response.** Act on the best-supported explanation while keeping unresolved uncertainty visible.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- Plausibility is not confirmation.\n- Competing hypotheses need not be mutually exclusive; several contributors may interact.\n- Consider alternatives that materially change the coaching response, rather than listing every imaginable cause.\n- Missing evidence is different from contradictory evidence.\n- Successful counterexamples can challenge an explanation or reveal its limits.\n- Prefer checks that produce different predictions across hypotheses.\n- An intervention improving performance does not necessarily prove the proposed cause.\n- Retain uncertainty when available evidence cannot distinguish the explanations.\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete repeatedly fails to finish a single-leg takedown.\n\nPossible explanations include insecure initial control, difficulty recognising the opponent’s defensive reaction, or loss of position during the finish.\n\nCompare where successful and unsuccessful attempts first diverge. Use representative practice to check whether secure initial control changes the result and whether the athlete can respond when the defensive reaction varies.\n\nThe findings may support one explanation or reveal interacting problems.\n\n## **Diagnostic Output**\n\n- Clearly defined observation.\n- Leading hypotheses.\n- Distinguishing predictions.\n- Supporting and contradicting evidence.\n- Unresolved information.\n- A discriminating question or practice task.\n- Updated confidence and proposed response.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nCompeting Diagnostic Hypotheses governs how explanations are compared. The other diagnostic nodes supply candidate explanations; Intervention Priority determines which supported issue to address first.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Contextual Diagnosis]]  \n[[Backward Causal Reasoning]]  \n[[Backward Failure Tracing]]  \n[[Opponent-Induced Failure]]  \n[[Constraint-Dependent Performance]]  \n[[MECHANISMS/State-Dependent Performance]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Constraint-Dependent Performance.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Constraint-Dependent Performance","body":"# Constraint-Dependent Performance\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA diagnostic method that examines how performance changes with task rules, starting conditions, permitted actions, resistance and environmental limits.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nIdentify the conditions under which a skill or decision remains effective, and explain gaps between practice success and performance in a different setting.\n\n## **Relevant Constraints**\n\n- Starting position, grips and existing control.\n- Permitted attacks, defences and transitions.\n- Partner resistance and freedom to respond.\n- Scoring, time limits and victory conditions.\n- Available space, boundaries, surface and equipment.\n- Instructions that make an action predictable or restrict available choices.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Define the performance difference.** Specify what succeeds in one setting and fails in another.\n2. **Compare the constraints.** Identify relevant differences between those settings.\n3. **Locate the changed demand.** Determine whether the athlete now needs additional recognition, timing, control, execution or decision skills.\n4. **Generate competing explanations.** Consider constraint changes alongside opponent differences and athlete state.\n5. **Test a relevant constraint.** Where practical, change one condition at a time while preserving the interaction being investigated.\n6. **Progress towards the target setting.** Introduce the freedom and demands needed for the intended application.\n7. **Reassess transfer.** Check whether improvement persists when the original practice restrictions are removed.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- Success in restricted practice establishes ability under those conditions.\n- Removing restrictions can expose demands that the original task did not test.\n- Constraints can conceal weaknesses or make existing strengths difficult to express.\n- A change in outcome may reflect a changed objective or opportunity, rather than reduced skill.\n- Greater resistance and greater decision complexity are different demands.\n- Altering several constraints together makes the cause of a performance change harder to identify.\n- Restricted practice remains useful when its purpose and limits are understood.\n- Evaluate transfer in the setting where the athlete needs the skill.\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete completes a knee cut from a fixed starting position, but struggles during open sparring.\n\nThe fixed task begins with inside position and limits the partner’s responses. Open sparring requires the athlete to obtain that position, recognise the opening and respond to alternative defences.\n\nDiagnosis identifies which added demand causes the breakdown, then adjusts practice to include it.\n\n## **Diagnostic Output**\n\n- Performance difference between settings.\n- Relevant constraint changes.\n- Additional demands introduced.\n- Candidate explanations and evidence gaps.\n- A targeted practice adjustment.\n- A check for transfer to the intended setting.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nConstraint-Dependent Performance focuses on task and environmental conditions. Opponent actions and temporary athlete states may interact with those constraints and require separate examination.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Contextual Diagnosis]]  \n[[Opponent-Induced Failure]]  \n[[Backward Failure Tracing]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Contextual Diagnosis.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Contextual Diagnosis","body":"# **Contextual Diagnosis**\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA diagnostic method that examines the conditions surrounding a combat performance pattern to determine how those conditions change its interpretation and the appropriate coaching response.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nDistinguish a persistent skill or decision problem from an outcome that depends on the opponent, athlete state, environment, rules or task objectives.\n\n## **Context Dimensions**\n\n- **Opponent:** Actions, reactions, skill, physical attributes and familiarity.\n- **Athlete state:** Fatigue, readiness, attention and reported psychological state.\n- **Task:** Intended objective, permitted actions, starting position and resistance.\n- **Rules and incentives:** Scoring, time remaining and consequences of taking risks.\n- **Environment:** Available space, surface, equipment and relevant distractions.\n\nThese are factors to investigate, not assumed explanations.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Define the pattern.** Describe the observable behaviour or outcome.\n2. **Establish the objective.** Identify what the athlete was attempting to achieve.\n3. **Record relevant conditions.** Separate observed context, athlete reports and unknowns.\n4. **Compare contrasting cases.** Examine when the pattern appears, disappears or changes.\n5. **Generate explanations.** Identify which conditions may contribute and how.\n6. **Test the distinction.** Where practical, vary a relevant condition while keeping others comparable.\n7. **Select a response.** Determine whether to develop a skill, adjust a decision, modify practice conditions or gather more evidence.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- The same outcome can have different explanations in different contexts.\n- Judge a decision against the athlete’s objective and available options.\n- Context can contribute to a failure without fully explaining it.\n- A weakness appearing only under particular conditions can still be a meaningful development target.\n- External constraints and athlete decisions can interact.\n- Psychological explanations require supporting evidence; behaviour alone does not establish an internal state.\n- One context-specific result does not justify a general conclusion about ability.\n- Consider successful performances under similar conditions.\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete retains mount during early rounds but repeatedly loses it later.\n\nPossible contributors include fatigue, different opponents, altered attack choices or increased resistance. Compare similar exchanges, ask about the athlete’s experience and examine where execution changes before selecting an intervention.\n\n## **Diagnostic Output**\n\n- Observed pattern and intended objective.\n- Relevant conditions and evidence sources.\n- Conditions associated with improvement or deterioration.\n- Competing explanations.\n- Missing information and confidence.\n- A context-appropriate intervention or next observation.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nContextual Diagnosis establishes which surrounding conditions matter. Detailed analysis of a particular factor can follow. It does not treat context as proof of causation or automatically attribute failure to the athlete.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Backward Causal Reasoning]]  \n[[Backward Failure Tracing]]  \n[[Decision Loop Detection]]  \n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Decision Loop Detection.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Decision Loop Detection","body":"# Decision Loop Detection\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nIdentifies when an athlete repeatedly returns to a decision or sequence of decisions that recreates the same vulnerability, despite opportunities to choose a different response.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nFind recurring choices that sustain a problem across exchanges or rounds, and identify where the athlete can interrupt that repetition.\n\n## **Recognition Criteria**\n\n- A similar situation repeatedly triggers the same choice.\n- That choice repeatedly leads towards a similar vulnerability.\n- Relevant alternatives exist but are overlooked, unavailable to the athlete’s current skill, or abandoned.\n- The pattern persists across attempts.\n\nRepetition alone is insufficient: a reliable, effective decision can also be repeated.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Identify the trigger.** What position, opponent response or perceived opening precedes the choice?\n2. **Identify the repeated decision.** What does the athlete consistently do?\n3. **Trace the consequence.** How does that choice contribute to the recurring vulnerability?\n4. **Compare exceptions.** When does the same choice succeed, and what differs?\n5. **Investigate persistence.** Does the athlete fail to recognise alternatives, lack the skill to execute them, or deliberately accept the tradeoff? Treat explanations as hypotheses.\n6. **Select an interruption point.** Define a recognisable cue and a feasible alternative response.\n7. **Reassess under resistance.** Check whether the athlete changes the decision and whether the outcome improves.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- Distinguish repeated choices across exchanges from a sequence that returns to the same state within one exchange.\n- Evaluate the decision in context, including opponent responses and available options.\n- Do not infer stubbornness, fear or habit solely from repeated behaviour.\n- A technically sound choice may become ineffective when used indiscriminately.\n- Breaking a loop requires an alternative the athlete can recognise and execute.\n- Assess both decision change and outcome change.\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete repeatedly attacks from mount when the opponent brings an elbow across the body. They commit both hands before securing sufficient control and repeatedly lose balance.\n\nInvestigate whether the opening is being misread, the attack is mistimed, or the athlete lacks a stabilising response. Practise recognising the relevant cue and choosing an appropriate action, then check whether mount retention improves.\n\n## **Diagnostic Output**\n\n- Recurring trigger.\n- Repeated decision or sequence.\n- Resulting vulnerability.\n- Successful exceptions.\n- Possible reasons for persistence.\n- Interruption cue and alternative response.\n- Evidence needed to assess improvement.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nDecision Loop Detection identifies recurring choice patterns. Backward Failure Tracing reconstructs earlier contributors to an outcome; Intervention Priority determines which identified issue deserves attention first.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Backward Failure Tracing]]  \n[[Backward Causal Reasoning]]  \n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Diagnostic Bottleneck Identification.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Diagnostic Bottleneck Identification","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should identify the earliest meaningful failure point in a combat sequence.\n\nThe visible failure is not always the true problem.\n\nSynochi should look upstream for the factor that caused the rest of the sequence to break down.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nDo not automatically coach the final failure.\n\nFind the earliest controllable bottleneck.\n\nA submission failure may begin with poor positional control.\n\nA failed pass may begin with lost inside position.\n\nA takedown failure may begin with poor entry timing rather than poor finishing mechanics.\n\n## Bottleneck Definition\n\nA bottleneck is the earliest high-impact factor that restricts the success of the sequence that follows.\n\nIt should be:\n\n- Causally relevant\n- Repeatable\n- Trainable\n- High leverage\n- Supported by evidence\n\n## Diagnostic Sequence\n\nSynochi should analyse backwards from the visible outcome.\n\nOutcome\n→ Previous transition\n→ Positional condition\n→ Technical decision\n→ Earlier setup\n→ Earliest meaningful failure point\n\n## Example\n\nVisible problem:\n\nAthlete repeatedly loses mount.\n\nSurface diagnosis:\n\nPoor mount retention.\n\nDeeper sequence:\n\nOpponent establishes elbow frame\n→ creates hip space\n→ recovers knee line\n→ athlete loses mount\n\nPossible bottleneck:\n\nFailure to control the opponent’s upper-body frame early.\n\nThe intervention should target the earliest meaningful cause rather than only the final positional loss.\n\n## Another Example\n\nVisible problem:\n\nAthlete cannot finish a single-leg takedown.\n\nPossible sequence:\n\nPoor distance\n→ weak penetration\n→ poor connection to the hips\n→ opponent establishes defensive angle\n→ finish fails\n\nIf poor connection is the first repeated breakdown, Synochi should prioritise that rather than prescribing additional finishing variations.\n\n## Bottleneck Types\n\n### Technical Bottleneck\n\nA missing or unreliable technical action.\n\n### Positional Bottleneck\n\nFailure to establish or maintain a necessary position.\n\n### Tactical Bottleneck\n\nPoor decision-making or incorrect sequence selection.\n\n### Timing Bottleneck\n\nThe correct action occurs too early or too late.\n\n### Information Bottleneck\n\nThe athlete does not recognise the relevant cue or opponent reaction.\n\n### Physical Bottleneck\n\nFatigue, mobility, strength, speed, or other physical constraints materially affect execution.\n\nSynochi should avoid assuming a physical bottleneck when technical or tactical explanations remain more likely.\n\n## Competing Bottlenecks\n\nMore than one plausible bottleneck may exist.\n\nSynochi should rank them by:\n\n- Evidence strength\n- Recurrence\n- Consequence\n- Position in the causal chain\n- Trainability\n- Expected transfer\n\nWhen confidence is low, Synochi should preserve multiple hypotheses rather than force a single explanation.\n\n## Earliest Useful Cause\n\nThe earliest cause is not automatically the best intervention.\n\nSynochi should identify the earliest cause that is both:\n\n1. Meaningfully connected to the outcome\n2. Actionable through coaching or training\n\n## Bottleneck Rule\n\nCorrect causes before symptoms where practical.\n\nPrefer:\n\nRoot constraint\n→ downstream improvement\n\nOver:\n\nMultiple isolated corrections\n→ fragmented improvement\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should simplify complex performance problems into the smallest number of high-leverage causes.\n\nThe goal is not to identify every technical imperfection.\n\nThe goal is to find the constraint that, if improved, changes the greatest amount of the athlete’s game.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]\n\n[[Pattern Consequence Weighting]]\n\n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Diagnostic Learning Retrieval.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Diagnostic Learning Retrieval","body":"# Diagnostic Learning Retrieval\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA process that retrieves relevant previous diagnoses, interventions and outcomes before forming or repeating a coaching recommendation.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nApply accumulated feedback to new situations, preserve effective approaches and prevent unsupported repetition of interventions previously flagged as ineffective or adverse.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Define the current case.** Identify the observed pattern, athlete objective and relevant conditions.\n2. **Retrieve related records.** Find previous cases with comparable decisions, opponent actions, constraints or athlete states.\n3. **Assess applicability.** Identify meaningful similarities and differences; do not rely on matching outcome labels alone.\n4. **Review learning flags.** Examine intervention results, implementation quality, confidence and unresolved questions.\n5. **Adjust the recommendation.** Reuse, adapt, reconsider or avoid the previous approach according to its evidence and current relevance.\n6. **Record the rationale.** State what prior learning influenced the recommendation and why.\n7. **Return new feedback.** Update the intervention history after reassessment.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- Similar outcomes can arise from different causes.\n- Previous success supports reuse under comparable conditions, not universal effectiveness.\n- Previous failure should inform the next recommendation without permanently excluding an approach.\n- An adverse flag requires review before reuse.\n- Repeating an unsupported intervention requires an explicit reason: changed conditions, improved implementation, revised delivery or new evidence.\n- Multiple comparable observations carry more weight than one isolated result.\n- Conflicting records should remain visible and prompt further investigation.\n- Athlete-specific learning must not automatically become a general rule.\n- When no relevant history is available, proceed with a provisional explanation and a clear test.\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete again reports losing mount.\n\nA previous record shows that delaying an attack improved retention but reduced attacking activity. Synochi retrieves the mixed result and checks whether the present loss follows the same sequence.\n\nIf it does, the next recommendation refines the attack cue to preserve both control and initiative. If the sequence differs, the previous intervention remains background evidence rather than the default answer.\n\n## **Output**\n\n- Relevant previous cases.\n- Context similarities and differences.\n- Applicable learning flags.\n- Decision to reuse, adapt or reconsider an intervention.\n- Reason for any repeated flagged recommendation.\n- Remaining uncertainty and reassessment criteria.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nDiagnostic Learning Retrieval applies recorded learning. Intervention Feedback Assimilation evaluates and updates that learning. Both require a retrievable history; the notes alone do not implement persistent memory.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Intervention Feedback Assimilation]]  \n[[Contextual Diagnosis]]  \n[[Competing Diagnostic Hypotheses]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]  \n[[Decision Loop Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Failure Chain Analysis.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Failure Chain Analysis","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should analyse sequences where one mistake creates another and the visible failure is only the end of a larger chain.\n\nCombat Intelligence should understand breakdowns as connected sequences rather than isolated errors.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nCombat failures often happen in chains.\n\nA small early error can create:\n\n- Worse positioning\n- Reduced options\n- Defensive reactions\n- Forced decisions\n- Energy loss\n- Eventual positional or submission failure\n\nSynochi should identify how the sequence developed, not only how it ended.\n\n## Failure Chain Structure\n\nA typical failure chain may look like:\n\nInitial Condition\n→ Early Error\n→ Opponent Advantage\n→ Reduced Athlete Options\n→ Secondary Error\n→ Major Consequence\n\n## Example\n\nAthlete loses side control.\n\nSurface observation:\n\nOpponent escapes.\n\nFailure chain:\n\nAthlete allows inside frame\n→ opponent creates elbow-knee connection\n→ hip space opens\n→ athlete reacts late\n→ knee recovers\n→ guard is restored\n\nThe final failure is guard recovery.\n\nThe meaningful earlier failure may be allowing the inside frame.\n\n## Chain Analysis Questions\n\nSynochi should ask:\n\n- What was the first meaningful loss?\n- What changed immediately after it?\n- Which later problems were consequences rather than independent errors?\n- At what point did the athlete still have realistic recovery options?\n- Which part of the chain is most trainable?\n- Which correction would remove the greatest number of downstream problems?\n\n## Primary Error vs Secondary Error\n\n### Primary Error\n\nThe earliest meaningful mistake that materially changes the exchange.\n\n### Secondary Error\n\nA later mistake created or made more likely by the earlier problem.\n\nSynochi should avoid treating every secondary error as a separate coaching target.\n\n## Recovery Window\n\nFailure chains should also identify whether the athlete had a realistic recovery opportunity after the initial error.\n\nExample:\n\nLost underhook\n→ opponent begins body lock\n→ athlete still has framing opportunity\n→ frame is missed\n→ pass occurs\n\nSynochi may identify both:\n\n- Primary prevention point\n- Best recovery point\n\nThis allows coaching to include both prevention and damage control.\n\n## Repeated Failure Chains\n\nWhen similar chains recur, Synochi should begin recognising the full sequence as a diagnostic pattern.\n\nExample:\n\nPoor distance management\n→ forced shot\n→ extended posture\n→ front headlock exposure\n\nIf this sequence repeats, the problem should not be stored as several unrelated mistakes.\n\nIt should be understood as one recurring failure architecture.\n\n## Chain Compression\n\nSynochi should compress repeated sequences into useful internal representations.\n\nInstead of storing:\n\n- Lost inside position\n- Poor frame\n- Late hip movement\n- Guard pass\n\nit may identify:\n\n“Inside-position loss repeatedly initiates the athlete’s guard-retention failure chain.”\n\nThis produces cleaner coaching.\n\n## Intervention Rule\n\nPrefer correcting:\n\nThe earliest high-confidence, high-leverage point in the failure chain\n\nrather than:\n\nThe largest number of visible downstream errors.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should understand how mistakes propagate.\n\nThe goal is to move from isolated technical correction toward causal sequence understanding.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Diagnostic Bottleneck Identification]]\n\n[[Intervention Priority]]\n\n[[Pattern Consequence Weighting]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Intervention Feedback Assimilation.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Intervention Feedback Assimilation","body":"# Intervention Feedback Assimilation\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA feedback process that evaluates the response to a coaching intervention, revises the underlying diagnosis and records context-specific learning for future recommendations.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nHelp Synochi learn from effective, ineffective and harmful interventions instead of repeatedly recommending an approach without considering its previous results.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Record the starting hypothesis.** Specify the observed problem, suspected contributors and confidence.\n2. **Define the intervention and test.** Record what will change, the expected response and what evidence would support or challenge the explanation.\n3. **Check implementation.** Establish whether the athlete understood, attempted and sufficiently practised the intervention.\n4. **Assess the response.** Compare the targeted behaviour and outcome with the baseline, accounting for opponent, constraints and athlete state.\n5. **Revise the diagnosis.** Strengthen, weaken or reopen the explanation according to the evidence.\n6. **Assign a learning flag.** Mark the intervention’s current status and the conditions under which that status applies.\n7. **Assimilate the lesson.** Use the record to inform future recommendations and schedule reassessment where uncertainty remains.\n\n## **Learning Flags**\n\n- **Supported:** The intended behaviour and outcome improved under relevant conditions. Retain the intervention with its scope and confidence.\n- **Mixed:** Benefits were inconsistent or accompanied by tradeoffs. Refine the intervention or identify the conditions governing its usefulness.\n- **Unresolved:** Evidence or implementation was insufficient. Gather more information before judging effectiveness.\n- **Unsupported:** An adequately tested intervention did not produce the expected response. Reconsider the explanation or approach.\n- **Adverse:** The intervention worsened the target outcome or created a meaningful new problem. Flag for review before reuse.\n\nFlags are revisable evidence summaries, not permanent verdicts.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- Separate intervention failure from incomplete implementation.\n- Assess both the behaviour being changed and the eventual outcome.\n- Improvement supports usefulness but does not automatically prove the proposed cause.\n- No improvement does not automatically disprove the diagnosis.\n- Record effective interventions as well as unsuccessful ones.\n- Attach learning to the athlete, task and relevant conditions before generalising.\n- Consult previous feedback before repeating a recommendation.\n- Repeating a flagged intervention requires a reason, such as changed conditions, revised execution or new evidence.\n- Update recommendation confidence without automatically rewriting established knowledge from a single case.\n\n## **Example**\n\nSynochi recommends delaying a mount attack until the athlete establishes more secure control.\n\nIf mount retention improves, record the benefit and check whether attacking opportunities remain available. If retention improves but attacks become excessively passive, mark the response as mixed and refine the timing cue.\n\nIf the athlete continues losing mount despite applying the change, revisit the suspected contributor rather than repeating the same instruction unchanged.\n\n## **Feedback Record**\n\n- Original observation and diagnostic hypothesis.\n- Intervention and expected response.\n- Implementation evidence.\n- Behavioural and outcome changes.\n- Relevant context and alternative explanations.\n- Learning flag and confidence.\n- Updated recommendation and reassessment trigger.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nThis node specifies how feedback should inform future reasoning. Reliable use requires a retrievable intervention history and a process that consults it; creating the note alone does not implement automatic learning.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Competing Diagnostic Hypotheses]]  \n[[Contextual Diagnosis]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]  \n[[Decision Loop Detection]]  \n[[Backward Failure Tracing]]  \n[[MECHANISMS/State-Dependent Performance]]  \n[[Constraint-Dependent Performance]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Intervention Priority.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Intervention Priority","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should decide which combat problem, pattern, or opportunity deserves attention first.\n\nThe highest-priority intervention is not necessarily the most obvious technical flaw.\n\nIt is the change most likely to produce meaningful improvement in the athlete’s performance.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should prioritise interventions based on expected value.\n\nA useful intervention should consider:\n\n- Pattern recurrence\n- Consequence severity\n- Diagnostic confidence\n- Relevance to the athlete’s preferred game\n- Competition relevance\n- Trainability\n- Transfer across positions or exchanges\n- Cost of ignoring the issue\n- Potential upside if improved\n\n## Priority Factors\n\n### Recurrence\n\nHow often does the pattern appear?\n\nRepeated issues generally deserve more attention than isolated ones.\n\n### Consequence\n\nWhat happens when the pattern occurs?\n\nPatterns that lead to scoring loss, submission exposure, positional failure, or breakdown of the athlete’s main game should usually rank higher.\n\n### Confidence\n\nHow certain is Synochi that the diagnosis is correct?\n\nLow-confidence diagnoses should rarely produce highly prescriptive interventions.\n\n### Game Relevance\n\nDoes the issue interfere with the athlete’s preferred system?\n\nA weakness inside a central part of the athlete’s game often deserves more attention than a weakness in a rarely used position.\n\n### Competition Relevance\n\nIs the issue likely to matter under the athlete’s current ruleset, opponent profile, or upcoming competition demands?\n\n### Trainability\n\nCan the issue be meaningfully improved through focused practice?\n\nSome problems can be changed quickly through a cue or constraint.\n\nOthers require longer-term technical development.\n\n### Transfer\n\nWill improving this issue positively affect multiple areas of the athlete’s game?\n\nHigh-transfer interventions should generally receive greater priority.\n\n## Intervention Categories\n\nSynochi may classify interventions as:\n\n### Immediate\n\nHigh consequence, high confidence, and directly relevant.\n\nShould influence current training or competition preparation.\n\n### Near-Term\n\nMeaningful but not urgent.\n\nShould enter the athlete’s upcoming development priorities.\n\n### Developmental\n\nImportant for long-term progression but unlikely to produce immediate performance change.\n\n### Monitor\n\nEvidence exists, but intervention is not yet justified.\n\nContinue collecting evidence.\n\n### Ignore For Now\n\nLow consequence, low relevance, or insufficient evidence.\n\nNot every imperfection requires correction.\n\n## Leverage Principle\n\nSynochi should search for high-leverage interventions.\n\nA high-leverage intervention solves or improves several downstream problems at once.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete repeatedly loses passing exchanges because they fail to control inside position.\n\nImproving inside-position control may also improve:\n\n- Passing stability\n- Pressure\n- Transition control\n- Submission access\n- Energy efficiency\n\nThis should generally rank above correcting several smaller downstream symptoms individually.\n\n## Bottleneck Principle\n\nWhen several weaknesses appear connected, Synochi should identify the earliest limiting factor in the chain.\n\nExample:\n\nPoor mount finishing may appear to be a submission problem.\n\nFurther analysis may show:\n\nPoor upper-body control\n→ opponent creates frames\n→ athlete loses isolation\n→ submission attempts fail\n\nThe intervention should target the bottleneck rather than the final visible failure.\n\n## Priority Flow\n\nObservation\n→ Pattern\n→ Consequence\n→ Diagnosis\n→ Bottleneck\n→ Expected Value\n→ Intervention Priority\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should reduce coaching noise.\n\nThe athlete should not receive ten corrections when one high-leverage intervention would create greater improvement.\n\nThe system should identify what matters most now while preserving lower-priority patterns for future reassessment.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]\n\n[[Pattern Recurrence Threshold]]\n\n[[Pattern Consequence Weighting]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Observation vs Inference.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Observation vs Inference","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines the distinction between what Synochi can directly observe and what it can only infer.\n\nThis distinction is critical for Combat Intelligence because technical analysis can easily become overconfident when limited evidence is treated as certainty.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nObservation and inference are not the same thing.\n\nSynochi should clearly separate:\n\n- What was directly observed\n- What is strongly supported by repeated evidence\n- What is a likely interpretation\n- What is only a low-confidence hypothesis\n\n## Observation\n\nAn observation is something directly visible, reported, measured, or explicitly confirmed.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Athlete repeatedly loses inside position\n- Opponent clears the knee line before the submission attempt\n- Athlete reports difficulty finishing from mount\n- Coach confirms the athlete regularly gives up underhooks\n- Video shows the athlete reaching with both arms during passing\n\nObservations should be described neutrally before explanation is added.\n\n## Inference\n\nAn inference is an interpretation of why an observation occurred.\n\nExamples:\n\nObservation:\nAthlete repeatedly loses inside position.\n\nPossible inference:\nThe athlete may be prioritising upper-body control before establishing lower-body alignment.\n\nObservation:\nAthlete struggles to finish from mount.\n\nPossible inference:\nThe athlete may lack a reliable system for isolating defensive frames.\n\nInference should remain proportional to the available evidence.\n\n## Confidence Levels\n\n### High Confidence\n\nUsed when:\n\n- The pattern is repeatedly observed\n- Multiple sources agree\n- The mechanism is clear\n- Contradictory evidence is minimal\n\n### Moderate Confidence\n\nUsed when:\n\n- There is some repeated evidence\n- The explanation is plausible\n- Alternative explanations still exist\n\n### Low Confidence\n\nUsed when:\n\n- Evidence is limited\n- Observation quality is poor\n- The interpretation depends heavily on assumptions\n- Multiple explanations are equally plausible\n\n## Diagnostic Rule\n\nSynochi should not convert a single event into a stable athlete trait without sufficient evidence.\n\nOne failed takedown does not mean poor wrestling.\n\nOne successful submission does not mean the submission is a reliable strength.\n\nOne visible technical error does not automatically identify its underlying cause.\n\n## Response Behaviour\n\nWhere uncertainty matters, Synochi should communicate it.\n\nPreferred language:\n\n- “This may indicate...”\n- “A likely factor is...”\n- “One possible cause is...”\n- “There is not enough evidence yet to distinguish between...”\n- “This becomes more significant if it repeats across sessions.”\n\nAvoid:\n\n- “You always...”\n- “Your problem is...”\n- “This proves...”\n\nunless the evidence genuinely supports that level of certainty.\n\n## Combat Intelligence Flow\n\nObservation\n→ Evidence Weight\n→ Pattern Detection\n→ Inference\n→ Confidence\n→ Diagnostic Hypothesis\n→ Intervention\n\n## Design Objective\n\nCombat Intelligence should become more useful as evidence accumulates without becoming falsely certain.\n\nThe system should prefer accurate uncertainty over confident misdiagnosis.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Combat Intelligence — Operating Definition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Opponent-Induced Failure.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Opponent-Induced Failure","body":"# **Opponent-Induced Failure**\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA diagnostic method that investigates how an opponent’s actions, capabilities and adaptations contribute to an athlete’s failure.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nIdentify what the opponent creates, removes or forces, and distinguish that contribution from errors the athlete makes independently. Both can contribute to the same outcome.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Define the failure.** Describe the observable outcome and the athlete’s intended objective.\n2. **Identify the opponent’s contribution.** Record relevant pressure, positioning, timing, deception, control or reactions.\n3. **Trace the interaction.** Examine how the opponent’s action changes the athlete’s available options and subsequent responses.\n4. **Compare cases.** Check whether the problem occurs against other opponents using similar actions, and whether the athlete sometimes handles those actions successfully.\n5. **Generate competing explanations.** Consider technical gaps, recognition delays, forced tradeoffs, capability differences and opponent adaptation.\n6. **Recreate the relevant challenge.** Use representative practice to test the suspected interaction where practical.\n7. **Reassess.** Check whether the athlete recognises the action, responds effectively and improves the outcome.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- An opponent actively shapes the conditions in which decisions are made.\n- Failure can emerge from an interaction rather than a single isolated mistake.\n- Distinguish an unforced error from a response made under constrained options.\n- A sound decision can still be defeated by effective opposition.\n- Failure against one opponent does not establish a general weakness.\n- Repeated failure against the same opponent action may reveal a specific development need.\n- Opponent intent remains an inference unless supported; describe observable actions first.\n- Physical or skill differences are relevant context, but do not fully explain the sequence by themselves.\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete repeatedly loses mount against an opponent who combines bridging with elbow–knee recovery.\n\nInvestigate whether the opponent first provokes a hand post, then uses that response to recover inside position. Compare successful mount retention against the same sequence and practise recognising and responding to the linked actions.\n\n## **Diagnostic Output**\n\n- Observed failure.\n- Relevant opponent actions and capabilities.\n- Changes to the athlete’s options.\n- Athlete responses and candidate breakdowns.\n- Successful counterexamples and alternative explanations.\n- A representative practice task.\n- Evidence needed to assess improvement.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nOpponent-Induced Failure examines the opponent’s contribution to an outcome. It does not assume the failure was unavoidable or remove the need to evaluate the athlete’s decisions.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Contextual Diagnosis]]  \n[[Backward Failure Tracing]]  \n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]  \n[[Decision Loop Detection]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Opportunity Pattern Identification.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Opportunity Pattern Identification","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should identify recurring situations where the athlete is close to creating an advantage but does not consistently capitalise on it.\n\nNot every useful diagnostic is a weakness.\n\nSometimes the athlete is already creating the right conditions but fails to recognise, connect, or exploit the opportunity.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should look for repeated moments where the athlete:\n\n- Creates a useful reaction\n- Reaches an advantageous position\n- Forces predictable defensive behaviour\n- Exposes a transition\n- Produces a scoring or submission opportunity\n- Creates the correct setup but fails to complete the sequence\n\nThese moments should be treated differently from fundamental technical failures.\n\n## Opportunity Definition\n\nAn opportunity pattern exists when:\n\n1. The athlete repeatedly creates favourable conditions\n2. A useful next action is available\n3. The athlete does not consistently recognise or exploit it\n4. The opportunity is relevant to their existing game\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1\n\nAthlete repeatedly forces opponents to post during a sweep attempt.\n\nThe sweep fails, but the post creates:\n\n- Arm exposure\n- Triangle access\n- Wrestle-up opportunity\n\nThe problem may not be the sweep itself.\n\nThe opportunity may be the predictable reaction it creates.\n\n### Example 2\n\nAthlete reaches front headlock repeatedly but disengages when the opponent builds posture.\n\nSynochi may identify recurring access to:\n\n- Snapdowns\n- Go-behinds\n- Back exposure\n- Secondary takedowns\n\n### Example 3\n\nAthlete passes into half guard repeatedly but immediately attempts to force the pass.\n\nThe position may already provide:\n\n- Crossface control\n- Underhook access\n- Upper-body isolation\n- Submission threats\n\nThe missed opportunity is not necessarily technical inability.\n\nIt may be failure to recognise available progression.\n\n## Opportunity Types\n\n### Technical Opportunity\n\nA specific technique or transition repeatedly becomes available.\n\n### Tactical Opportunity\n\nThe opponent repeatedly reacts in a predictable way that can be exploited.\n\n### Positional Opportunity\n\nThe athlete consistently reaches a useful position but does not consolidate or progress.\n\n### Submission Opportunity\n\nSubmission exposure appears repeatedly without recognition or correct sequencing.\n\n### Reaction Opportunity\n\nOne attack reliably creates a defensive response that opens another route.\n\n## Recognition vs Execution\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n### Recognition Failure\n\nThe athlete does not appear to notice the opportunity.\n\n### Decision Failure\n\nThe athlete notices it but chooses an inferior option.\n\n### Execution Failure\n\nThe correct option is attempted but technically fails.\n\n### Timing Failure\n\nThe correct action is selected but the window has already closed.\n\nThese should produce different coaching responses.\n\n## Opportunity Value\n\nSynochi should increase the priority of an opportunity when:\n\n- It appears frequently\n- It connects naturally to an existing strength\n- It requires relatively little additional skill\n- It creates major scoring or finishing potential\n- Opponents repeatedly produce the same reaction\n- It can connect multiple parts of the athlete’s game\n\n## Conversion Principle\n\nCombat Intelligence should ask:\n\n“What is the athlete already creating that could be converted into more value?”\n\nThis can often produce faster improvement than introducing an entirely new technique.\n\n## Opportunity Flow\n\nExisting Strength\n→ Predictable Reaction\n→ Opportunity\n→ Recognition\n→ Correct Decision\n→ Execution\n→ Advantage\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should identify hidden upside within the athlete’s existing game.\n\nThe goal is not only to repair what fails.\n\nIt is also to recognise where the athlete is already close to greater performance and help convert those moments into reliable advantages.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]\n\n[[Strength Pattern Identification]]\n\n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Pattern Consequence Weighting.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Pattern Consequence Weighting","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should judge the importance of a recurring combat pattern.\n\nNot every repeated behaviour deserves equal coaching attention.\n\nA pattern becomes more important when it consistently creates meaningful consequences.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nFrequency tells Synochi that something happens.\n\nConsequence tells Synochi how much it matters.\n\nA highly frequent pattern with little impact may be lower priority than a less frequent pattern that repeatedly leads to position loss, scoring, submission exposure, or failed attack sequences.\n\n## Consequence Categories\n\nSynochi should assess whether a pattern affects:\n\n- Positional control\n- Positional loss\n- Scoring\n- Submission creation\n- Submission exposure\n- Transition success\n- Defensive survival\n- Energy expenditure\n- Tactical initiative\n- Decision quality\n- Ability to impose preferred game\n- Opponent ability to impose their game\n\n## Consequence Severity\n\n### Low\n\nThe pattern has limited effect on the overall exchange.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Small efficiency loss\n- Minor grip or stance issue\n- Slight delay in progression\n- Technical imperfection that does not alter the outcome\n\n### Moderate\n\nThe pattern meaningfully affects position or tactical flow.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Regularly conceding an underhook\n- Losing preferred alignment\n- Giving an opponent repeated access to a transition\n- Wasting significant energy\n- Failing to consolidate advantageous positions\n\n### High\n\nThe pattern repeatedly creates major competitive consequences.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Directly causing takedown loss\n- Repeatedly leading to guard passes\n- Exposing submissions\n- Preventing reliable finishing sequences\n- Causing repeated scoring concessions\n- Breaking down the athlete’s primary game\n\n## Priority Interaction\n\nPattern priority should consider:\n\nRecurrence\n×\nConsequence\n×\nConfidence\n×\nContext Relevance\n\nA pattern should not become a major coaching priority purely because it is frequent.\n\nLikewise, a severe consequence should not be over-weighted if the evidence is weak or highly context-specific.\n\n## Example\n\nObservation:\n\nAthlete repeatedly reaches with the near-side arm while passing.\n\nPossible consequence:\n\nOpponent gains access to underhooks and wrestle-ups.\n\nIf this occurs repeatedly and consistently causes positional loss, the pattern should carry higher coaching priority.\n\nIf the same reaching behaviour occurs without meaningful consequence, its priority should remain lower.\n\n## Tactical Relevance\n\nSynochi should increase consequence weighting when a pattern interferes with:\n\n- The athlete’s strongest positions\n- Their preferred tactical route\n- Their competition strategy\n- High-frequency match situations\n- Known opponent tendencies\n- Current training objectives\n\n## Priority Rule\n\nA useful diagnostic priority is not:\n\n“What happens most?”\n\nIt is:\n\n“What repeatedly matters most?”\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should prioritise interventions that create the greatest improvement in actual combat performance.\n\nThe system should avoid spending excessive coaching attention on technically imperfect behaviours that have little practical consequence.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Pattern Recurrence Threshold]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/Pattern Recurrence Threshold.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"Pattern Recurrence Threshold","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines when an observed combat behaviour should begin to be treated as a meaningful pattern rather than an isolated event.\n\nSynochi should not overreact to single moments.\n\nA pattern becomes useful when recurrence, context, and consequence begin to align.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nRepetition increases confidence, but repetition alone is not enough.\n\nSynochi should consider:\n\n- How often the behaviour occurs\n- Whether it appears across multiple sessions\n- Whether it appears against different opponents\n- Whether it appears in similar positions or contexts\n- Whether it leads to meaningful positive or negative outcomes\n- Whether the athlete or coach independently reports the same issue\n\n## Isolated Event\n\nAn isolated event should usually remain an observation.\n\nExamples:\n\n- One failed takedown\n- One missed submission\n- One positional escape against an unusually strong opponent\n- One technical error during fatigue\n\nDo not convert isolated events into stable athlete traits.\n\n## Emerging Pattern\n\nA behaviour becomes an emerging pattern when:\n\n- It appears more than once\n- It occurs in comparable contexts\n- It produces a similar outcome\n- The explanation remains plausible across examples\n\nEmerging patterns should be tracked but not overstated.\n\n## Established Pattern\n\nA behaviour becomes an established pattern when:\n\n- It repeats across multiple sessions or matches\n- It appears against different opponents\n- It has consistent tactical or technical consequences\n- Multiple evidence sources support it\n- Competing explanations have weakened\n\nEstablished patterns can influence coaching priorities and athlete modelling.\n\n## Pattern Strength\n\n### Weak\n\nLimited recurrence or narrow context.\n\n### Moderate\n\nRepeated enough to influence analysis, but still context-dependent.\n\n### Strong\n\nRepeated across contexts with consistent consequences.\n\n## Context Sensitivity\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- Global pattern\n- Position-specific pattern\n- Opponent-specific pattern\n- Ruleset-specific pattern\n- Fatigue-related pattern\n- Competition-only pattern\n- Training-only pattern\n\nA pattern should only generalise as far as the evidence supports.\n\n## Promotion Rule\n\nObservation\n→ Repeated Observation\n→ Emerging Pattern\n→ Established Pattern\n→ Athlete Model Update\n\nPattern promotion should be gradual.\n\n## Demotion Rule\n\nPatterns should weaken when:\n\n- New evidence contradicts them\n- The athlete successfully corrects the behaviour\n- The behaviour stops recurring\n- The original interpretation becomes less likely\n\nCombat Intelligence should allow patterns to decay rather than treating them as permanent truths.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should learn an athlete’s game through accumulated evidence while remaining capable of changing its mind.\n\nThe goal is adaptive pattern recognition, not static labelling.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Diagnostic Patterns/State-Dependent Performance.md","folder":"Diagnostic Patterns","title":"State-Dependent Performance","body":"# State-Dependent Performance\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Diagnostic Patterns\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA diagnostic method that investigates whether changes in an athlete’s temporary physical, attentional or psychological state contribute to changes in combat performance.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nDistinguish a persistent skill gap from difficulty accessing or executing an existing skill under particular states, while recognising that both can interact.\n\n## **Relevant State Factors**\n\n- Perceived fatigue and readiness.\n- Attention, distraction and mental workload.\n- Reported arousal, confidence, frustration or hesitation.\n- Perceived discomfort and movement restriction.\n- Changes within a round, between rounds or across sessions.\n\nThese are factors to investigate, not explanations to assume.\n\n## **Operating Sequence**\n\n1. **Define the performance change.** Specify which observable decisions or actions deteriorate, improve or become inconsistent.\n2. **Establish the comparison.** Identify instances where the athlete performs differently under otherwise comparable conditions.\n3. **Record state evidence.** Separate athlete reports, observable behaviour and inferred explanations.\n4. **Check competing factors.** Examine opponent actions, task constraints and tactical objectives.\n5. **Locate the affected function.** Determine whether the change concerns recognition, choice, timing, execution or sustained effort.\n6. **Test a practical adjustment.** Where appropriate, adjust pacing, recovery, task complexity or preparation.\n7. **Reassess.** Check whether the targeted behaviour and outcome change across repeated instances.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- Temporary state and underlying skill can jointly influence performance.\n- A decline late in a session does not establish fatigue as its cause.\n- Hesitation, passivity or rushed action does not by itself establish fear, low confidence or excessive arousal.\n- Athlete reports provide relevant evidence but may not fully explain the sequence.\n- A state can affect different skills differently.\n- State-linked improvement or deterioration should be assessed across comparable cases.\n- Difficulty performing under a recurring state can become a training target.\n- Do not reduce a technical or tactical problem to “mindset” without supporting evidence.\n\n## **Example**\n\nAn athlete executes takedown entries early in a session but later begins reaching without moving their feet.\n\nInvestigate whether this coincides with reported fatigue, reduced attention, different opponent pressure or a deliberate change in approach. Identify the specific execution change before choosing a practice or pacing adjustment.\n\n## **Diagnostic Output**\n\n- Observable performance change.\n- Reported and observed state indicators.\n- Comparable successful and unsuccessful instances.\n- Affected decision or execution function.\n- Competing explanations and evidence gaps.\n- A practical adjustment or further observation.\n- Criteria for reassessment.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nState-Dependent Performance examines temporary conditions affecting performance. It does not diagnose psychological or medical conditions, infer stable personality traits, or establish state as the cause from behaviour alone.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Contextual Diagnosis]]  \n[[Constraint-Dependent Performance]]  \n[[Opponent-Induced Failure]]  \n[[Decision Loop Detection]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Boundary Management Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Boundary Management Drill","body":"# Boundary Management Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop the ability to manage restricted space while preserving useful attacking, defensive and movement options.\n\n## Training Target\nBoundary recognition, containment, turning, exit selection and tactical positioning under environmental limits.\n\nA mat edge, ring boundary and wall create different problems. Specify physical restrictions and rules consequences separately.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- Sport, format and applicable ruleset.\n- Boundary type: marked edge, ring ropes or suitable wall or cage surface.\n- Exit, penalty, restart and continuation rules.\n- Starting distance, orientation, stance, grips and contact.\n- Each athlete's objective and permitted actions.\n- Whether boundary support or control is permitted.\n- Surface, equipment and intensity.\n- Success, reset, work and rest conditions.\n\nUse the target environment or record the limitations of a safe substitute. A floor marker does not reproduce wall contact.\n\n## Task\nOne athlete contains, turns or pressures the opponent while preserving viable action.\n\nThe other seeks useful space, reverses the relationship or acts effectively within the restriction.\n\nObjectives must fit tactical context and rules. Leaving the boundary is not automatically best.\n\nContinue far enough to test the resulting position. Reset at the agreed milestone, applicable stoppage or loss of safe conditions.\n\n## Information and Resistance\nUse available space, remaining exits, relative orientation, pressure direction, support changes, opponent commitment and boundary contact.\n\nThe containing athlete actively closes routes rather than receiving a stationary partner. The constrained athlete reads available routes rather than following a predetermined escape.\n\n## Constraints\nAdjust proximity, orientation, movement area, exits, grips, attacks, contact, boundary use and available time.\n\nSpecify whether turning, disengagement or reversal is allowed. Separate opponent pressure from environmental restriction.\n\n## Dosage\nUse short contested exchanges with frequent starts near the relevant boundary.\n\nSpecify exchange count or duration, sets, intensity and rest. Rotate roles to practise containment and response.\n\nEnd or simplify when attention or partner control deteriorates. Longer bouts should serve an explicit sustained-management target.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- The athlete recognises changed options near the boundary.\n- Containment closes meaningful routes while retaining useful action.\n- Repositioning produces usable space, orientation or reversal.\n- Balance and defensive readiness survive the interaction.\n- The next action fits tactical context and rules.\n- Required stops, exits and resets are respected.\n\nRecord legal outcomes separately from physical control. Boundary contact alone is not successful containment.\n\n## Progression\n1. Introduce one relationship at controlled intensity.\n2. Allow active containment and viable response options.\n3. Expand turning, exit and attacking options.\n4. Vary orientation, contact and proximity.\n5. Add relevant score or time scenarios.\n6. Assess in representative exchanges using the target boundary.\n\nIncrease freedom without introducing uncontrolled collisions.\n\n## Regression\nReduce pressure, contact, proximity or available actions. Provide more space or isolate a turn or repositioning component.\n\nPreserve the relevant boundary consequence when simplifying.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish missed space restrictions, linear retreat that removes exits, incomplete containment, poor turning mechanics, unusable resulting positions and misunderstood rules.\n\nCheck whether a boundary position is tactically preferred rather than a failed escape. Do not infer a general movement deficit from one boundary-specific failure.\n\n## Sport Adaptation\nComplete these sport-specific prescription fields:\n- Format, boundary type and applicable rules.\n- Exit, penalty, restart and continuation conditions.\n- Starting configuration and each athlete's tactical objective.\n- Legal attacks, grips, contact and boundary use.\n- Resistance, equipment and safe movement limits.\n- Exchange count or duration, sets, rest and quality threshold.\n- Success, reset, stopping conditions and transfer criteria.\n\n| Sport | Example boundary problem | Fields requiring particular attention |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| BJJ | Maintain engagement or recover useful space near the mat edge. | Out-of-bounds handling and positional restart; edge is not a wall. |\n| Judo | Manage grips and orientation near the contest-area boundary. | Selected format's attack, boundary, penalty and stoppage rules. |\n| Karate | Pressure or reposition near the competition-area edge. | Format, exit consequences, contact and scoring continuation. |\n| Kickboxing | Cut off or regain space near ropes or the contest boundary. | Ring or mat format, legal clinch actions and boundary use. |\n| MMA | Contain, turn, exit or act during cage or wall contact. | Legal strikes, takedowns, phase transitions and surface use. |\n| Muay Thai | Manage striking or clinch position near ropes or a corner. | Clinch actions, contact, rope interaction and breaks. |\n| Sambo | Maintain or reverse a useful relationship near the mat edge. | Sport or combat format, stoppages and legal continuation. |\n| Taekwondo | Deny or regain kicking space near the contest boundary. | Format, exit consequences, targets and scoring continuation. |\n\nCheck rules for the selected format. Do not transfer boundary assumptions between sports.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\nSelect when restricted space repeatedly alters decisions or execution, or containment is a supported target.\n\nCompare performance away from the boundary to identify added demands. Use range work for general positioning and movement work for isolated components.\n\nAssess transfer in the intended environment and record substitute-boundary limitations.\n\n## Safety\nUse suitable surfaces and agree permitted boundary contact and stop signals.\n\nControl impact and protect landing space. Do not drive partners into hard walls, ring hardware or unprotected edges.\n\nReset before controlled movement or safe defence becomes unavailable. Honour taps and stoppages immediately.\n\n## Links\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]] — connect to the specialist layer.\n- [[Space Creation and Space Denial]] — understand space and exits.\n- [[Footwork and Pivoting]] — reorganise under restriction.\n- [[Score-Time-Ruleset Context]] — establish legal and tactical consequences.\n- [[Constraint-Dependent Performance]] — investigate environmental effects.\n- [[Movement-Pattern Drill]] — develop limiting repositioning components.\n- [[Range and Angle Drill]] — establish useful relative positioning.\n- [[Escape Recovery Drill]] — recover from established disadvantage.\n- [[Individualised Constrained Live Rounds]] — assess transfer."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Constraint Based Positional Sparring.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Constraint Based Positional Sparring","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\nType:\nDrill Template\n\nRuleset:\nBoth\n\nPurpose:\n\nConstraint Based Positional Sparring is a live training format where the athletes begin in a specific position and are given narrow objectives or restrictions that force repeated exposure to a particular problem.\n\nThe goal is not simply to win the round.\n\nThe goal is to increase the number of meaningful decision repetitions around a selected position, mechanic, or tactical problem.\n\nTemplate Structure:\n\nStart Position:\nDefine the exact position or configuration.\n\nAttacker Objective:\nDefine what counts as meaningful attacking success.\n\nDefender Objective:\nDefine what counts as meaningful defensive success.\n\nConstraints:\nLimit available options where necessary to increase repetition density.\n\nReset Condition:\nDefine exactly when the position resets.\n\nRound Duration:\nUsually short enough to maintain high repetition quality.\n\nExample:\n\nStart Position:\nBottom athlete in Half Guard with knee shield.\n\nTop Objective:\nFlatten the bottom athlete and clear the knee line.\n\nBottom Objective:\nMaintain structure and achieve an underhook, wrestle up, or guard transition.\n\nConstraints:\nTop athlete cannot disengage completely.\nBottom athlete cannot immediately switch to Closed Guard.\n\nReset:\nReset when the top player establishes stable side control or the bottom player achieves a meaningful reversal or transition.\n\nWhy It Works:\n\nThe drill compresses training around a specific decision environment.\n\nInstead of waiting for the position to appear naturally during sparring, the athlete repeatedly encounters:\n\n- the same positional cues\n- common opponent reactions\n- timing windows\n- successful and unsuccessful decisions\n- transitions that emerge from the position\n\nProgression Variables:\n\nThe drill can be made easier or harder by changing:\n\n- starting advantage\n- permitted techniques\n- resistance level\n- number of available reactions\n- reset conditions\n- round duration\n- scoring incentives\n\nDiagnostic Use:\n\nSynochi should use this template when an athlete:\n\n- repeatedly fails in the same position\n- understands a technique but cannot apply it live\n- needs better timing or recognition\n- struggles to connect multiple options\n- needs higher repetition density around a specific problem\n\nPrescription Logic:\n\nIdentify the athlete’s recurring positional problem.\n\nSelect the smallest representative starting position.\n\nDefine one clear objective for each athlete.\n\nRestrict irrelevant options.\n\nCreate frequent resets.\n\nIncrease complexity as success becomes consistent.\n\nSynochi should treat Constraint Based Positional Sparring as a general drill framework that can be instantiated around any position, transition, escape, pass, or submission system."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Counter and Re-Counter Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Counter and Re-Counter Drill","body":"# Counter and Re-Counter Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop the ability to respond to an opponent’s action and continue effectively when that response is itself challenged.\n\n## Training Target\nThreat recognition, defensive timing, counter opportunities, initiative and adaptive continuation.\n\nA counter exploits or interrupts an opponent’s action. A re-counter responds to the counter that follows an initial action.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- The sport and applicable ruleset.\n- The initial action or attack family.\n- Starting posture, distance, contact and boundaries.\n- Each athlete’s permitted responses.\n- How far the exchange may continue.\n- Success conditions and reset points.\n- Equipment and contact intensity.\n\nIdentify whether the training target is the first counter, the re-counter or the connection between them.\n\n## Task\nOne athlete initiates a credible action.\n\nThe other athlete manages the threat and attempts an appropriate counter.\n\nThe initiating athlete recognises the counter and attempts a suitable continuation, recovery or re-counter.\n\nRoles may remain fixed during introductory practice or change as initiative shifts.\n\n## Information and Resistance\nUse cues generated by the opponent’s action:\n- Changes in balance and posture.\n- Direction and degree of commitment.\n- Distance and angle.\n- Grip, contact and pressure.\n- Openings created during attack or recovery.\n\nThe initial action must remain credible. Avoid attacks performed only to feed a predetermined counter.\n\n## Constraints\nAdjust:\n- Available initial attacks.\n- Permitted counters and continuations.\n- Predictability of the opening action.\n- Speed, resistance and contact intensity.\n- Starting conditions.\n- Length of the exchange.\n- Conditions for disengagement or reset.\n\nPermit defence without countering when no viable counter opportunity exists.\n\n## Dosage\nUse repetitions to establish an unfamiliar response, then short contested exchanges to develop timing and adaptation.\n\nReset before the exchange loses its intended focus.\n\nAllow enough rest to preserve recognition and controlled execution.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- The initial threat is managed.\n- The counter fits the opportunity and ruleset.\n- Timing and positioning make the response viable.\n- The athlete recognises when the counter is challenged.\n- Continuation adapts to the opponent’s actual response.\n- Balance and defensive responsibility are preserved.\n- The athlete can disengage or recover when further attack is unsuitable.\n\nAssess each stage separately rather than scoring only the final winner.\n\n## Progression\n1. Introduce a defined attack and counter at controlled intensity.\n2. Allow the initiating athlete to resist the counter.\n3. Introduce a limited choice of counters.\n4. Allow alternative continuations and re-counters.\n5. Vary the initial attack and starting conditions.\n6. Assess the skill within representative live exchanges.\n\nExpand freedom gradually. Do not require increasingly long fixed sequences as the main measure of progress.\n\n## Regression\nReduce speed, resistance, action options or exchange length.\n\nIsolate the stage where recognition, positioning or execution breaks down.\n\nRetain a credible initial action even when simplifying the task.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish between:\n- Failure to manage the initial threat.\n- Failure to recognise a counter opportunity.\n- Countering before suitable conditions exist.\n- Appropriate counter selection followed by poor execution.\n- Overcommitment that exposes a re-counter.\n- Failure to recognise the opponent’s continuation.\n- Persisting when recovery or disengagement is preferable.\n\nDetermine whether the limitation concerns a specific response or adaptation across the exchange.\n\n## Sport Adaptation\nPreserve the shared function while adapting the actions:\n- Grappling: respond to entries, throws, takedowns, passing actions or submission attempts.\n- Striking: respond to attacks while managing distance, defensive exposure and return attacks.\n- Mixed combat: counter within a phase or through a permitted transition between striking, clinch and ground.\n\nApply the selected ruleset to legal techniques, scoring, contact and continuation.\n\nDistinguish counters that stop an exchange under the rules from counters that require continued defensive readiness.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\nSelect when an athlete defends without exploiting available opportunities, repeatedly loses initiative after attacking or struggles when an opponent counters their preferred action.\n\nUse this template when the specific training problem is reciprocal opposition. Use broader recognition or transition work when countering is not the central objective.\n\nAssess transfer against varied, credible attacks rather than cooperative sequences alone.\n\n## Safety\nDefine contact intensity, prohibited actions and stopping conditions.\n\nControl impact, throwing force and submission pressure as uncertainty increases.\n\nHonour taps and stop signals immediately. Do not require a re-counter once safe defensive action is no longer available.\n\n## Links\n- [[Recognition Decision Drill]]identify threats and viable counter opportunities.\n- [[Entry Setup Drill]]— establish credible initiating actions.\n- [[Escape Recovery Drill]]— recover when countering or continuing is unsuitable.\n- [[Transition Drill]] — connect responses as initiative changes.\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]]— scale opposition and response freedom.\n- [[Individualised Constrained Live Rounds]] — assess counters against the athlete’s recurring live problem."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Entry Setup Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Entry Setup Drill","body":"# Entry / Setup Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop the ability to create the conditions needed to initiate a technique against a partner.\n\n## Training Target\nAccess, positioning, grip acquisition, alignment, off-balancing and timing before commitment to an attack.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- The intended attack or attack family.\n- The starting position and initial contact.\n- The conditions required for a viable entry.\n- The partner’s permitted defensive actions.\n- The success condition and reset point.\n\n## Task\nThe attacking athlete creates an opening and establishes a viable entry.\n\nThe defending athlete denies access through the permitted defensive actions.\n\nSuccess requires an entry that supports continuation, rather than contact with a target alone.\n\n## Constraints\nSelect constraints that expose the specific setup problem:\n- Starting distance or contact.\n- Permitted grips and defensive responses.\n- Available attack routes.\n- Movement boundaries.\n- Time available to establish the entry.\n\nPreserve enough defensive freedom that the attacker must recognise an opening rather than receive it automatically.\n\n## Dosage\nUse repetitions for a predictable setup or short timed exchanges for contested access.\n\nSelect work and rest according to the objective. Preserve attention and execution quality when learning; introduce fatigue only when maintaining entries under fatigue is the intended target.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- Required entry conditions are established.\n- Balance and useful positioning are maintained.\n- The athlete recognises when the opening is available.\n- The entry permits a credible next action.\n- Failed attempts can be abandoned without uncontrolled commitment.\n\n## Progression\n1. Establish the entry against a cooperative partner.\n2. Introduce a defined defensive response.\n3. Allow several defensive responses.\n4. Increase resistance and vary starting conditions.\n5. Continue from the entry into the attack or subsequent exchange.\n\nProgress according to demonstrated competence rather than a fixed schedule.\n\n## Regression\nReduce defensive options, resistance, starting distance or setup complexity. Isolate the condition the athlete cannot yet establish.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish between:\n- Failure to create an opening.\n- Failure to recognise an opening.\n- Failure to enter when an opening exists.\n- Failure after a successful entry.\n\nIf the entry succeeds but the technique fails later, move the training target to the relevant execution, transition or finishing template.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\nSelect when an athlete understands a technique but repeatedly cannot access it during live exchanges.\n\nIndividualise the setup around the athlete’s intended game, observed obstacle, partner availability and current capability.\n\n## Links\n- [[Repetition Drill]] — practise a defined setup with consistent starting conditions.\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]] — increase opposition as entry competence improves.\n- [[Transition Drill]] — connect the established entry to the next action.\n- [[Constraint Based Positional Sparring]] — contest entries within a bounded position.\n- [[Individualised Constrained Live Rounds]] — assess whether the entry transfers into live exchanges."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Escape Recovery Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Escape Recovery Drill","body":"# Escape / Recovery Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop the ability to resolve a disadvantage and regain a position from which effective action is possible.\n\n## Training Target\nThreat recognition, defensive structure, timing, space creation and connection between recovery actions.\n\nEscape removes or reduces an opponent’s immediate control or threat. Recovery restores usable posture, balance, distance, angle or position.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- The sport and applicable ruleset.\n- The starting disadvantage.\n- Distance, contact, posture and environmental boundaries.\n- Any immediate threats.\n- Each participant’s permitted actions.\n- The recovery target, success condition and reset point.\n- Equipment and contact intensity.\n\nDistinguish early defensive intervention from recovery against an established disadvantage.\n\n## Task\nThe recovering athlete manages the immediate threat, disrupts the opponent’s advantage and restores a usable state.\n\nThe opposing athlete maintains pressure, control or attacking opportunity within the permitted actions.\n\nContinue until recovery is demonstrated, the opponent achieves their objective or a reset condition is reached.\n\n## Information and Resistance\nIdentify the cues the athlete must recognise, such as changes in pressure, grip, balance, distance, angle or attacking commitment.\n\nBegin with predictable opposition where needed. Introduce uncertainty and additional responses as competence improves.\n\nResistance must challenge the target skill without making the recovery task inaccessible.\n\n## Constraints\nSelect constraints around the observed problem:\n- Available recovery routes.\n- Permitted attacks, grips, frames and defensive actions.\n- Opponent movement and continuation options.\n- Starting distance or depth of control.\n- Space, boundary or equipment restrictions.\n- Time available to recover.\n- Actions required after recovery.\n\nEnsure the success condition does not reward escaping one disadvantage while immediately entering another.\n\n## Dosage\nUse repetitions to develop a defined component and short contested exchanges to practise recognition and timing.\n\nSet work, rest and intensity according to the learning objective, contact demands and athlete capability.\n\nIntroduce fatigue only when recovery under fatigue is the explicit training target.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- The immediate threat is recognised and managed.\n- The opponent’s advantage is meaningfully reduced.\n- Useful posture, balance, distance, angle or position is restored.\n- Recovery actions adapt when the first option is blocked.\n- The athlete can defend or continue effectively after recovery.\n\nInclude a brief continuation where appropriate to distinguish a temporary opening from a functional recovery.\n\n## Progression\n1. Practise against a predictable, manageable disadvantage.\n2. Increase resistance or pressure.\n3. Introduce a response that blocks the initial recovery.\n4. Vary starting conditions and available threats.\n5. Require recovery followed by a useful next action.\n6. Assess transfer within a representative live task.\n\nIncrease one main difficulty at a time so the source of failure remains identifiable.\n\n## Regression\nReduce pressure, contact intensity, starting disadvantage or opponent options.\n\nProvide a more accessible recovery target or isolate the missing component before rebuilding the exchange.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish between:\n- Failure to recognise the threat.\n- Failure to establish defensive structure.\n- Failure to disrupt the opponent’s advantage.\n- Failure to select or connect recovery actions.\n- Failure to maintain the recovered state.\n- Failure to continue effectively after recovery.\n\nRecord where the recovery breaks down rather than treating every unsuccessful exchange as the same problem.\n\n## Sport Adaptation\nPreserve the shared training function while adapting the task to the discipline:\n- Grappling: recover posture, escape control or regain a usable position.\n- Striking: manage attacking pressure and restore defensive structure, distance or angle.\n- Mixed combat: recover within or between striking, clinch and ground exchanges.\n\nUse the selected ruleset to determine legal actions, scoring implications, permitted contact and continuation.\n\nAccount for the discipline’s tactical priorities. Do not assume the same recovery target is appropriate across judo, karate, kickboxing, MMA, Muay Thai, sambo or taekwondo.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\n\nSelect when an athlete repeatedly remains disadvantaged, expends effort without improving their situation or recovers briefly before losing it again.\n\nIndividualise the starting conditions and recovery target around the observed failure, sport, ruleset, experience, partner suitability and movement limitations.\n\nAssess improvement in a representative sport task before concluding that drill success has transferred.\n\n## Safety\nDefine contact intensity, prohibited actions and stopping conditions before starting.\n\nUse suitable protective equipment and partner control. Honour taps and stop signals immediately.\n\nReset when safe defensive action is no longer available. Do not require movement through painful ranges or uncontrolled impact to complete the task.\n\n## Links\n- [[Movement-Pattern Drill]] — develop a movement component needed for recovery.\n- [[Repetition Drill]] — practise a defined recovery component.\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]] — increase opposition as recovery competence improves.\n- [[Transition Drill]] — connect recovery to the next useful action.\n- [[Position Specific Sparring]] — assess recovery where a defined position is relevant.\n- [[Individualised Constrained Live Rounds]] — test transfer against the athlete’s recurring disadvantage."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Finishing-Isolation Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Finishing-Isolation Drill","body":"# Finishing-Isolation Drill\n\n## Definition\nA Finishing-Isolation Drill isolates the final phase of a submission or scoring sequence so the athlete can repeatedly refine control, finishing mechanics, and resistance management.\n\n## Purpose\nUse when the athlete can reliably reach the finishing position but completion quality is inconsistent.\n\nBest suited for:\n- submission finishing mechanics\n- grip configuration\n- angle refinement\n- control before finish\n- breaking defensive structure\n- maintaining position during finishing attempts\n- improving efficiency under resistance\n\n## Starting Position\nBegin from the finishing position or immediately before the decisive finishing mechanics.\n\nThe setup should remove unnecessary earlier phases so most repetitions occur around the actual completion problem.\n\n## Attacker Objective\nComplete the finish while preserving:\n- positional control\n- mechanical alignment\n- appropriate pressure\n- efficient force application\n- awareness of defensive reactions\n\nThe athlete should prioritise control and mechanics over simply forcing the submission.\n\n## Defender Objective\nProvide realistic defensive structure within the agreed limits.\n\nThe defender may:\n- protect grips\n- adjust posture\n- create space\n- rotate\n- frame\n- initiate agreed escape mechanics\n\nThe defender should not immediately abandon the isolated finishing problem unless progression requires it.\n\n## Allowed Actions\nActions should remain inside the finishing sequence being trained.\n\nRelated grip adjustments, positional corrections, and finishing variations may be allowed.\n\nUnrelated positional transitions should initially be restricted.\n\n## Constraints\nPossible constraints include:\n- fixed finishing position\n- limited defensive options\n- no positional escape beyond a defined boundary\n- attacker cannot switch submission family\n- control must be maintained before force is increased\n- finish must be achieved through correct mechanics rather than explosive force\n\n## Reset Condition\nReset when:\n- the submission is completed\n- the defender fully escapes the isolated finishing structure\n- the attacker loses positional control\n- mechanics deteriorate significantly\n- the exchange leaves the intended finishing problem\n\n## Duration / Reps\nCan be trained through:\n- fixed repetitions\n- timed finishing rounds\n- alternating attack/defence sets\n- successful completion thresholds\n- progressive-resistance rounds\n\n## Success Metric\nSuccess may be measured through:\n- finish rate\n- control retained before completion\n- reduced use of unnecessary force\n- mechanical consistency\n- ability to adjust to defence\n- time to completion\n- reduced escape opportunities\n\n## Common Errors\n- rushing the finish before control is secure\n- relying on strength rather than mechanics\n- losing positional control while chasing the submission\n- repeating incorrect finishing angles\n- ignoring defensive reactions\n- training only compliant finishes\n\n## Progression\nProgress by:\n- increasing defensive resistance\n- allowing more escape options\n- adding grip-fighting phases\n- varying starting alignment\n- introducing reaction chains\n- transitioning into positional sparring\n\n## Regression\nSimplify by:\n- reducing resistance\n- fixing the defensive reaction\n- isolating one finishing mechanic\n- removing grip complexity\n- starting deeper in the finishing position\n\n## Athlete Model Signal\nThe drill can reveal:\n- finishing efficiency\n- force dependence\n- control quality\n- defensive recognition\n- mechanical weak points\n- finishing hesitation\n- loss of position under submission pressure\n- preferred finishing adjustments\n\n## Core Rule\nIsolate the finish so completion mechanics can be refined without the rest of the sequence obscuring the problem.\n\n## Backlinks\n- [[Repetition Drill]]\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]]\n- [[Reaction-Chain Drill]]\n- [[Transition Drill]]\n- [[Constraint Based Positional Sparring]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Individualised Constrained Live Rounds.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Individualised Constrained Live Rounds","body":"# Individualised Constrained Live Rounds\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Drill Templates\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA live sparring template that uses athlete-specific objectives or restrictions to develop an identified skill or decision pattern while allowing exchanges across positions.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nConnect focused development work to open sparring, preserving realistic opponent responses and opportunities to recognise when the target action is appropriate.\n\n## **Required Inputs**\n\n- Athlete’s development need and supporting observations.\n- Target behaviour or decision.\n- Starting conditions and applicable rules.\n- Athlete-specific objective or constraint.\n- Partner instructions.\n- Round duration, intensity and recovery.\n- Observable success criteria.\n\n## **Construction Sequence**\n\n1. **Select the development target.** Identify a supported weakness, missed opportunity or strength to extend.\n2. **Choose a relevant constraint.** Use an objective, scoring incentive or action restriction that encourages the target behaviour.\n3. **Preserve opponent freedom.** Allow realistic responses wherever they do not undermine the task.\n4. **Define appropriate opportunities.** Clarify when the target action makes sense; avoid requiring it regardless of context.\n5. **Run the round.** Allow exchanges to develop across positions.\n6. **Review decisions and outcomes.** Assess opportunity recognition, execution and unintended tradeoffs.\n7. **Relax the constraint.** Check whether learning persists in ordinary live rounds.\n\n## **Constraint Options**\n\n- **Objective:** Seek a particular control, entry or transition when available.\n- **Scoring incentive:** Reward the target behaviour or a meaningful milestone.\n- **Action restriction:** Temporarily limit a familiar option to develop alternatives.\n- **Decision rule:** Require a relevant cue before committing to an action.\n- **Role assignment:** Ask the partner to present a particular challenge while retaining freedom to adapt.\n\nUse the minimum constraint needed to produce useful practice.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- Constraints should follow the athlete’s development need.\n- Do not reward forcing an unsuitable technique.\n- Both partners must understand their roles.\n- Distinguish an absent opportunity from a missed opportunity.\n- Assess decision quality alongside completion rate.\n- Monitor whether the constraint creates predictable or exploitable behaviour.\n- Constraints are temporary practice tools; test transfer after removing them.\n- Adjust difficulty when the task produces too few relevant exchanges.\n\n## **Example**\n\n**Development need:** The athlete repeatedly attacks from mount before establishing sufficient control.\n\n**Constraint:** Submission attempts must follow an observable control milestone agreed with the coach. Transitions and defensive adjustments remain available.\n\n**Partner role:** Defend and escape realistically.\n\n**Observe:** Whether the athlete establishes control, recognises suitable attacking opportunities and avoids becoming excessively passive.\n\n**Transfer check:** Remove the rule and assess whether improved attack selection persists.\n\n## **Diagnostic Output**\n\n- Relevant opportunities encountered.\n- Target decisions attempted and omitted.\n- Execution and outcomes.\n- Unintended effects of the constraint.\n- Adjustment needed for the next round.\n- Performance after the constraint is removed.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nIndividualised Constrained Live Rounds allow practice across positions. Position-Specific Sparring repeatedly starts from a defined position. Either can use athlete-specific constraints.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Position Specific Sparring]]  \n[[Decision Loop Detection]]  \n[[Constraint-Dependent Performance]]  \n[[Intervention Priority]]  \n[[Intervention Feedback Assimilation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Isometric and Dynamic Resistance Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Isometric and Dynamic Resistance Drill","body":"# Isometric and Dynamic Resistance Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop the ability to maintain or change a relevant body position against resistance.\n\n## Training Target\nForce production, force absorption, positional strength and controlled movement under load.\n\nIsometric work produces force without substantial movement at the targeted joint or position. Dynamic work produces or controls movement against resistance.\n\nA drill may combine both when the task requires holding, moving and stabilising.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- The sport and physical capability being targeted.\n- The position, movement or force direction.\n- Whether the task is isometric, dynamic or combined.\n- The resistance source and how it will be controlled.\n- Relevant joint positions and movement range.\n- Work, rest and stopping conditions.\n- Equipment and partner requirements.\n\nResistance may come from body weight, a partner or suitable equipment.\n\nDistinguish physical capacity training from technical practice. Similarity to a sporting movement does not establish transfer by itself.\n\n## Task\nThe athlete maintains a defined position, moves against resistance or controls an externally imposed movement.\n\nA partner, where used, applies resistance within agreed directions and limits.\n\nFor combined tasks, define when the athlete holds, moves and stabilises without requiring an arbitrary fixed sequence.\n\n## Information and Resistance\nSpecify:\n- Resistance direction and magnitude.\n- Whether resistance is steady or variable.\n- Whether its timing is predictable.\n- Permitted adjustments.\n- How resistance will be reduced or released.\n\nUse predictable loading when physical capacity is the primary objective. Introduce responsive partner opposition when adjusting force to changing information is part of the task.\n\n## Constraints\nAdjust:\n- Joint position and movement range.\n- Base of support.\n- Available grips or contact points.\n- Resistance direction.\n- Hold duration or movement tempo.\n- Permitted compensation.\n- Partner movement.\n\nConstraints should expose the intended physical demand without forcing painful positions or unnecessary rigidity.\n\n## Dosage\nSet hold duration, repetitions, sets, resistance and rest according to the target capability and athlete readiness.\n\nFor strength-focused work, preserve controlled execution and allow adequate recovery.\n\nFor endurance-focused work, use repeatable efforts and monitor deterioration in position or movement.\n\nCount the drill within the athlete’s total training load.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- Force is applied or absorbed in the intended direction.\n- The target position or movement remains controlled.\n- Compensations do not defeat the training objective.\n- Effort matches the prescribed task.\n- Resistance can be released safely.\n- Performance remains within the defined quality threshold.\n\nRecord resistance, duration or repetitions alongside execution quality.\n\n## Progression\n1. Establish control with manageable, predictable resistance.\n2. Increase resistance, duration or repetitions according to the objective.\n3. Expand movement range or relevant positional demands.\n4. Introduce controlled changes in resistance direction.\n5. Combine holding and movement where appropriate.\n6. Assess the capability within a representative sport task.\n\nChange one main loading variable at a time. Greater instability or complexity is not automatically a useful progression.\n\n## Regression\nReduce resistance, duration, repetitions or movement range.\n\nProvide a more stable base, simplify the force direction or replace variable partner resistance with a predictable load.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish between:\n- Insufficient force production.\n- Insufficient endurance.\n- Poor alignment or force direction.\n- Difficulty controlling movement under load.\n- Excessive tension or unnecessary effort.\n- Technical failure despite adequate physical capacity.\n\nDo not infer a strength deficit from every lost sporting exchange. Timing, positioning and decision-making may be the limiting factors.\n\n## Sport Adaptation\nPreserve the shared physical function while adapting the task:\n- Grappling: develop relevant grip, posture, pulling, pushing or positional force.\n- Striking: develop force capacities supporting stance, movement, braking and contact where relevant.\n- Mixed combat: address physical demands across striking, clinch and ground phases.\n\nSelect loading positions according to the athlete’s actual needs. Do not force every exercise to visually imitate a technique.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\nSelect when assessment suggests that limited force production, endurance or control under load obstructs a sport task.\n\nIdentify whether isometric, dynamic or combined work addresses the limitation.\n\nReassess transfer through the relevant task. Increased drill capacity should not automatically be recorded as improved technical skill.\n\n## Safety\nUse controllable resistance and agree release signals before partner work.\n\nAvoid sudden maximal loading, uncontrolled joint pressure and prolonged maximal breath-holding.\n\nStop for pain, dizziness or loss of control. Reduce loading when fatigue prevents safe execution.\n\n## Links\n- [[Movement-Pattern Drill]] — establish controlled movement before adding resistance.\n- [[Retention Stabilisation Drill]] — apply physical capacity to maintaining a useful state.\n- [[Escape Recovery Drill]] — apply force within a recovery task.\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]] — scale opposition during skill practice; distinguish this from physical loading progression.\n- [[Individualised Constrained Live Rounds]] — assess whether increased capacity supports live performance."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Movement-Pattern Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Movement-Pattern Drill","body":"# Movement-Pattern Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop a movement component that enables an effective combat action before adding the demands of a complete technique or live exchange.\n\n## Training Target\nCoordination, base reorganisation, weight transfer, rotation, braking and controlled repositioning.\n\nSelect the component because it limits a relevant sport task. Judge movement by the action it enables rather than resemblance to a fixed shape.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- Sport, format and applicable ruleset.\n- Observed movement limitation and supporting evidence.\n- Starting posture, orientation, contact and support points.\n- Movement component, permitted adjustments and useful endpoint.\n- Solo, equipment or partner format.\n- Resistance source, direction and release conditions.\n- Work, rest, quality threshold and equipment.\n- Success, reset and stopping conditions.\n\nSeparate movement learning from strength, flexibility and conditioning targets.\n\n## Task\nMove from the defined start to a usable endpoint while managing balance, alignment and support.\n\nA partner provides agreed contact or disruption without supplying the movement for the athlete.\n\nRequire a simple next action where useful to show that the endpoint supports attack, defence or further movement.\n\nReset after completion, repeated loss of the quality threshold or departure from the intended problem.\n\n## Information and Resistance\nBegin with predictable demands when coordination is the limiting factor.\n\nIntroduce changes in support, pressure direction, available space or partner movement as competence improves.\n\nAdd resistance when it exposes the intended demand. Solo success does not establish responsiveness to an opponent.\n\n## Constraints\nAdjust starting configuration, direction, movement range, available posts, grips, tempo and required continuation.\n\nIdentify compensations that bypass the target component. Permit useful adjustments even when they differ from the introductory demonstration.\n\n## Dosage\nUse short sets of controlled repetitions with rest sufficient to preserve attention and movement quality.\n\nPrescribe repetitions or duration, sets, tempo, resistance, rest and the quality threshold explicitly. End a set when quality is repeatedly lost.\n\nIntroduce speed, uncertainty or fatigue only when it serves the selected function. Record these demands separately from volume.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- The component occurs with usable balance and alignment.\n- Support and weight are reorganised for the task.\n- Compensation does not bypass the target.\n- The endpoint permits the required next action.\n- The athlete can stop or redirect within the agreed task.\n- Improvement appears in the associated sport action.\n\nAssess component quality and transfer separately.\n\n## Progression\n1. Establish the component from a consistent start.\n2. Connect it to a useful next action.\n3. Vary direction, orientation or starting conditions.\n4. Introduce controlled contact or resistance.\n5. Require adjustment to readable partner information.\n6. Assess within a representative entry, recovery or live exchange.\n\nChange one main demand at a time. Complexity alone is not progress.\n\n## Regression\nReduce distance, range, speed or resistance. Provide a more stable start or isolate the support change that breaks down.\n\nRetain the functional endpoint when simplifying.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish coordination difficulty, poor base or weight transfer, insufficient usable range, loading limitations, missed cues and failure to integrate the component into the full action.\n\nDo not infer a mobility or strength deficit from every movement failure.\n\n## Sport Adaptation\nComplete these sport-specific prescription fields:\n- Format, legal actions and contact limits.\n- Representative component and starting configuration.\n- Relevant cue, support points and required endpoint.\n- Partner task and permitted disruption.\n- Resistance, speed, range and equipment.\n- Repetitions or duration, sets, rest and quality threshold.\n- Reset, stopping conditions and sport task used to assess transfer.\n\n| Sport | Example component and endpoint | Fields requiring particular attention |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| BJJ | Hip repositioning or base change supporting guard, frames or passing stance. | Ground configuration, posts, grips and permitted continuation. |\n| Judo | Stepping, pivoting or weight transfer supporting entry or defence. | Grips, moving partner and whether throwing is included. |\n| Karate | Stance change, braking or pivot supporting legal attack or exit. | Competition format, contact and continuation after scoring. |\n| Kickboxing | Step, pivot or stance recovery preserving striking readiness. | Legal attack families, defensive continuation and contact. |\n| MMA | Level change or ground-to-standing movement supporting the next phase. | Permitted strikes, phase transitions and wall involvement. |\n| Muay Thai | Turning or support-leg repositioning preserving balance for continuation. | Contact, balance demands and permitted clinch actions. |\n| Sambo | Stepping, turning or support changes enabling entry or recovery. | Sport or combat format, grips and ground continuation. |\n| Taekwondo | Support-leg pivot, landing or stance recovery supporting the next action. | Format, legal targets and controlled kicking contact. |\n\nExamples identify functions, not mandatory techniques. Individualise around the observed limitation.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\nSelect when a repeatable component obstructs an otherwise appropriate action.\n\nUse repetition work for a component already available but inconsistent; use resistance work when loading capacity is the supported limitation.\n\nReassess the full sport task before recording technical improvement.\n\n## Safety\nAgree contact, resistance and release signals. Use controlled ranges and suitable surfaces.\n\nStop for pain or loss of control. Do not force joint range or introduce uncontrolled impact to obtain the endpoint.\n\n## Links\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]] — connect to the specialist layer.\n- [[Footwork and Pivoting]] — organise repositioning and orientation.\n- [[Base]] — manage support.\n- [[Weight Transfer]] — reorganise load.\n- [[Repetition Drill]] — consolidate a defined component.\n- [[Isometric and Dynamic Resistance Drill]] — address loading limitations.\n- [[Escape Recovery Drill]] — apply movement to recovery.\n- [[Transition Drill]] — connect useful states."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Position Specific Sparring.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Position Specific Sparring","body":"# **Position-Specific Sparring**\n\n**Placement:** Combat Intelligence → Drill Templates\n\n## **Definition**\n\nA live practice template that starts from a defined combat position and gives each athlete clear objectives, with realistic opposition and explicit continuation or reset rules.\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nDevelop position-specific recognition, decisions and execution through repeated exposure to relevant attacking and defensive exchanges.\n\n## **Required Inputs**\n\n- Target position and precise starting configuration.\n- Athlete’s development need.\n- Objectives for both partners.\n- Permitted actions and applicable rules.\n- Resistance and partner suitability.\n- Round duration or exchange count.\n- Success criteria and reset conditions.\n\n## **Construction Sequence**\n\n1. **Select the problem.** Identify what the athlete needs to retain, recognise, create, defend or escape.\n2. **Define the start.** Specify orientation, grips, limb placement and existing control.\n3. **Assign complementary objectives.** Give both partners meaningful tasks.\n4. **Set the practice boundary.** Decide whether exchanges reset at a milestone or continue into transitions.\n5. **Set resistance and duration.** Match the demands to the objective and athlete’s current ability.\n6. **Observe the target behaviour.** Track relevant choices and execution alongside outcomes.\n7. **Adjust and reassess.** Change the starting difficulty, available actions or continuation rules as needed.\n\n## **Position Applications**\n\n|**Position**|**Controlling athlete**|**Defending athlete**|\n|---|---|---|\n|Turtle|Establish control, turn the opponent or secure the back.|Protect against attacks and recover guard, stand or reverse.|\n|Back|Maintain control, manage defensive reactions and create submission opportunities.|Defend submissions, remove control and escape.|\n|Mount|Retain position, transition or attack while managing escape attempts.|Disrupt control and recover guard, reverse or escape.|\n|Side control|Maintain control, advance position or attack.|Create usable space and recover guard, reverse or escape.|\n|Half guard|Top: clear defensive controls, pass and stabilise.|Bottom: retain useful structure, sweep, wrestle up or recover guard.|\n\nObjectives must be adapted to the specific configuration and ruleset.\n\n## **Progression Options**\n\n- Start with an advantage, then reduce it.\n- Begin with a limited objective, then introduce additional options.\n- Increase realistic resistance without making every exchange maximal.\n- Allow continuation beyond the initial position to test transitions.\n- Rotate partners to assess whether learning transfers across responses.\n- Alternate roles where useful for understanding the interaction.\n\n## **Core Principles**\n\n- A named position is insufficient; starting details materially change the task.\n- Both partners need objectives that produce the intended interaction.\n- Distinguish achieving a milestone from stabilising it.\n- Reset rules should serve the learning objective.\n- Restricting practice to one position can hide entry or transition problems.\n- Success rate alone does not show whether the target behaviour improved.\n- Progress towards representative exchanges as competence develops.\n\n## **Example**\n\n**Objective:** Retain mount while responding to escape attempts.\n\n**Start:** Mount with agreed hand and leg placement; no submission already secured.\n\n**Roles:** Top retains mount and may transition when appropriate. Bottom attempts legal escapes.\n\n**Reset:** Reset after a completed escape or agreed positional milestone. In a later progression, continue through transitions.\n\n**Observe:** Whether top recognises the escape preparation, adjusts base and maintains useful control.\n\n## **Boundary**\n\nPosition-Specific Sparring organises practice around a starting position. Constraint-Based Positional Sparring deliberately manipulates available actions or task conditions to elicit a particular behaviour. The templates can be combined.\n\n## **Related Nodes**\n\n[[Contextual Diagnosis]]  \n[[Constraint-Dependent Performance]]  \n[[Opponent-Induced Failure]]  \n[[Intervention Feedback Assimilation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Progressive-Resistance Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Progressive-Resistance Drill","body":"# Progressive-Resistance Drill\n\n## Definition\nA Progressive-Resistance Drill increases partner resistance gradually as the athlete demonstrates stable execution.\n\n## Purpose\nUse when a technique or movement is understood mechanically but needs to become reliable against increasing realism.\n\nBest suited for:\n- bridging drilling into live application\n- testing whether mechanics survive resistance\n- identifying the point at which execution breaks down\n- improving timing and adaptation\n- preventing the jump from passive drilling straight into full sparring\n\n## Starting Position\nBegin from the exact position where the target technique or sequence is intended to operate.\n\nThe starting position should remain consistent initially so resistance is the main changing variable.\n\n## Attacker Objective\nComplete the target action while preserving:\n- structure\n- timing\n- positional control\n- mechanical efficiency\n\nThe goal is not simply to force completion.\n\n## Defender Objective\nApply resistance at the prescribed level without exceeding it.\n\nResistance should challenge execution while still allowing the athlete to work on the intended mechanism.\n\n## Allowed Actions\nDefensive actions should initially be limited to those relevant to the target technique.\n\nAs the drill progresses, more realistic reactions can be introduced.\n\n## Constraints\nResistance may progress through stages such as:\n- passive\n- structured posture\n- light resistance\n- moderate resistance\n- strong predictable resistance\n- variable resistance\n- near-live resistance\n\nOnly increase resistance when execution remains stable.\n\n## Reset Condition\nReset when:\n- the attacker completes the target action\n- the defender fully escapes the intended pathway\n- mechanics collapse\n- the drill moves outside the intended training problem\n\n## Duration / Reps\nCan be performed through:\n- fixed repetitions at each resistance level\n- timed rounds\n- successful-completion thresholds\n- progression only after repeated clean execution\n\n## Success Metric\nSuccess is not simply completion.\n\nTrack:\n- technical integrity\n- completion rate\n- point of breakdown\n- unnecessary effort\n- ability to maintain structure as resistance rises\n- whether timing adapts appropriately\n\n## Common Errors\n- increasing resistance too quickly\n- defender turning the drill into sparring\n- attacker replacing mechanics with force\n- remaining at low resistance after stability is established\n- progressing despite repeated technical breakdown\n- changing too many variables at once\n\n## Progression\nProgress by:\n- increasing resistance\n- introducing additional defensive reactions\n- varying timing\n- changing starting orientation\n- allowing branching responses\n- transitioning into positional sparring\n\n## Regression\nRegress by:\n- reducing resistance\n- limiting defensive options\n- returning to a fixed reaction\n- isolating a smaller movement\n- slowing the exchange\n- returning temporarily to repetition drilling\n\n## Athlete Model Signal\nThe drill can reveal:\n- resistance threshold\n- whether technique depends excessively on compliance\n- force substitution\n- timing breakdown\n- poor adaptation to defensive reactions\n- structural weakness\n- confidence loss as resistance increases\n\n## Core Rule\nIncrease realism only as fast as technical stability can survive it.\n\n## Backlinks\n- [[Repetition Drill]]\n- [[Reaction-Chain Drill]]\n- [[Constraint Based Positional Sparring]]\n- [[Transition Drill]]\n- [[Finishing-Isolation Drill]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Range and Angle Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Range and Angle Drill","body":"# Range and Angle Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop the ability to create, preserve or deny useful action opportunities through relative distance and orientation.\n\n## Training Target\nRange recognition, angle creation, repositioning, access denial and readiness to act after movement.\n\nThe target is a useful relationship between athletes. The eventual attack need not be fixed in advance.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- Sport, format and applicable ruleset.\n- Starting distance, orientation, stance and contact.\n- The relationship each athlete seeks or denies.\n- Available attacks, defences and movement options.\n- Space, equipment and contact intensity.\n- Follow-up demonstrating tactical usefulness.\n- Success, reset, work and rest conditions.\n\nDefine functional range through available actions. One fixed distance will not suit every athlete or technique.\n\n## Task\nOne athlete establishes a relationship from which a permitted action is viable.\n\nThe other denies that relationship or establishes their own useful range and angle.\n\nRequire a controlled follow-up or brief continuation to show that positioning creates a usable opportunity.\n\nReset after the agreed milestone, decisive denial or departure from the training problem. Alternate roles where appropriate.\n\n## Information and Resistance\nUse opponent-generated information: stance, orientation, movement direction, commitment, contact availability and accessible targets or control points.\n\nBegin with limited movement options where needed. Increase resistance through timing and route freedom as well as speed or physical opposition.\n\n## Constraints\nAdjust separation, orientation, grips, attack families, defensive options, movement area, exit routes, available time and continuation length.\n\nAvoid rewarding an angle that creates equal or greater exposure elsewhere.\n\n## Dosage\nUse brief contested exchanges with frequent relevant opportunities and resets.\n\nSpecify exchange count or duration, sets, intensity and rest. Preserve recognition and controlled movement during learning.\n\nUse longer bouts when sustained range management is the target. Record opportunities encountered alongside successful actions.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- Range and angle support a permitted action.\n- The athlete recognises changing opportunities.\n- Movement preserves balance and defensive readiness.\n- Follow-up occurs while the relationship remains usable.\n- Denial prevents intended access without unnecessary exposure.\n- Unsuitable routes can be abandoned or changed.\n\nAssess relationship creation, selection and execution separately.\n\n## Progression\n1. Establish useful relationships against predictable movement.\n2. Allow the partner to deny one route.\n3. Introduce alternative routes and follow-ups.\n4. Vary stance, contact and orientation.\n5. Allow reciprocal attacks and initiative changes.\n6. Assess in representative live exchanges.\n\nPreserve readable information while expanding opponent freedom.\n\n## Regression\nReduce speed, contact, route options or exchange length. Use a clearer starting relationship or simpler follow-up.\n\nRetain an active partner task rather than making access automatic.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish unsuitable range, ineffective orientation changes, unreachable opportunities, positioning lost before action and poor execution after adequate positioning.\n\nCheck whether denial sacrifices balance or defensive responsibility. Identify whether positioning, recognition, timing or execution is the main limitation.\n\n## Sport Adaptation\nComplete these sport-specific prescription fields:\n- Format, legal targets, scoring and continuation rules.\n- Starting range, orientation, stance and contact.\n- Useful relationship and action demonstrating it.\n- Each partner's movement, attack and denial options.\n- Resistance, intensity, equipment and space.\n- Exchange count or duration, sets, rest and quality threshold.\n- Success, reset, stopping conditions and transfer criteria.\n\n| Sport | Example relationship to contest | Fields requiring particular attention |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| BJJ | Guard engagement or passing angle supporting attack or defence. | Seated, standing or ground start; grips and positional continuation. |\n| Judo | Gripping distance and orientation supporting entry and access denial. | Grips, stance and whether a throw completes the exchange. |\n| Karate | Scoring distance and attack line with defensive readiness. | Format, legal contact and whether scoring stops the exchange. |\n| Kickboxing | Striking range and angle without uncontrolled overreach. | Legal attacks, defence and continuation after contact. |\n| MMA | Range for striking, connection or phase transition. | Threat families, legal phase changes and wall involvement. |\n| Muay Thai | Striking or clinch relationship supporting legal action with balance. | Contact, clinch continuation and defensive responsibility. |\n| Sambo | Grip and entry relationship or ground separation supporting action. | Sport or combat format and permitted phase transitions. |\n| Taekwondo | Kicking distance and orientation supporting attack and recovery. | Format, targets, contact and scoring continuation. |\n\nAdapt to both athletes. No range or angle is universally advantageous.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\nSelect when athletes act from unsuitable distance, lose access through orientation or cannot deny preferred opponent range.\n\nUse entry work when access to one intended attack is central. Use boundary work when environmental limits are decisive.\n\nConfirm transfer after removing introductory restrictions.\n\n## Safety\nAgree legal actions, contact intensity and stop signals.\n\nControl collisions, impact and throwing force as freedom increases. Reset when safe defence is no longer available.\n\n## Links\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]] — connect to the specialist layer.\n- [[Range and Distance State]] — identify available actions.\n- [[Footwork and Pivoting]] — change distance and orientation.\n- [[Space Creation and Space Denial]] — create or restrict useful space.\n- [[Movement-Pattern Drill]] — develop limiting movement components.\n- [[Recognition Decision Drill]] — select from changing information.\n- [[Entry Setup Drill]] — access an intended attack.\n- [[Boundary Management Drill]] — contest positioning under environmental limits.\n- [[Individualised Constrained Live Rounds]] — assess transfer."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Reaction-Chain Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Reaction-Chain Drill","body":"# Reaction-Chain Drill\n\n## Definition\nA Reaction-Chain Drill trains the athlete to respond to a partner’s changing reactions rather than execute a fixed memorised sequence.\n\n## Purpose\nUse when the athlete understands individual techniques but needs to connect them dynamically based on resistance, positioning, and opponent behaviour.\n\nBest suited for:\n- linking attacks together\n- recognising defensive reactions\n- improving transition timing\n- reducing rigid sequencing\n- building decision speed\n- developing adaptive technical chains\n\n## Starting Position\nBegin from a position where the athlete has a clear primary objective and the partner has several realistic defensive responses available.\n\nThe starting position should create a meaningful decision tree without becoming completely open sparring.\n\n## Attacker Objective\nMaintain progression toward the positional or submission goal by selecting the appropriate response to the defender’s reaction.\n\nThe athlete should not force the original technique when the reaction makes another option more appropriate.\n\n## Defender Objective\nProvide one of several agreed realistic reactions.\n\nThe defender should vary reactions enough to require recognition while staying inside the intended training problem.\n\n## Allowed Actions\nThe attacker may use the preselected chain of responses.\n\nThe defender may use the agreed defensive reactions.\n\nActions outside the target reaction tree should initially be restricted.\n\n## Constraints\nPossible constraints include:\n- limited number of defensive reactions\n- one primary attack family\n- no disengagement\n- attacker must respond rather than reset\n- defender changes reaction unpredictably\n- continuation required after failed first attempt\n\n## Reset Condition\nReset when:\n- the attacker completes the target outcome\n- the defender fully escapes the reaction chain\n- the exchange leaves the intended position\n- the athlete fails to recognise the relevant reaction\n- the agreed sequence reaches its endpoint\n\n## Duration / Reps\nCan be performed through:\n- repeated reaction cycles\n- timed rounds\n- fixed number of successful chains\n- alternating attacker and defender roles\n\nQuality of recognition should matter more than raw repetition count.\n\n## Success Metric\nSuccess may be measured through:\n- correct reaction recognition\n- appropriate response selection\n- reduced hesitation\n- smooth transitions\n- maintenance of positional control\n- reduced forcing of inappropriate techniques\n- successful continuation after the first option fails\n\n## Common Errors\n- memorising the order instead of reading the reaction\n- forcing the first technique\n- reacting too late\n- skipping positional control between attacks\n- defender making reactions unrealistic\n- adding too many branches too early\n- confusing speed with recognition quality\n\n## Progression\nProgress by:\n- increasing reaction variety\n- reducing predictability\n- increasing resistance\n- allowing secondary counters\n- increasing speed\n- expanding the reaction tree\n- transitioning into positional sparring\n\n## Regression\nSimplify by:\n- reducing the number of reactions\n- making reactions predictable\n- slowing the exchange\n- isolating one branch\n- returning to repetition drilling for weak responses\n\n## Athlete Model Signal\nThe drill can reveal:\n- reaction recognition speed\n- technical rigidity\n- hesitation between options\n- poor transition mechanics\n- forcing behaviour\n- preferred branches\n- weak branches\n- whether decision quality degrades under pressure or fatigue\n\n## Core Rule\nTrain the athlete to follow the opponent’s reaction, not a memorised sequence.\n\n## Backlinks\n- [[Repetition Drill]]\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]]\n- [[Transition Drill]]\n- [[Recognition Decision Drill]]\n- [[Constraint Based Positional Sparring]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Repetition Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Repetition Drill","body":"# Repetition Drill\n\n## Definition\nA Repetition Drill repeatedly rehearses a movement, micro-movement, technical sequence, or positional action to improve efficiency, consistency, timing, and automaticity.\n\n## Purpose\nUse when the athlete needs cleaner execution of a known action rather than more decision complexity.\n\nBest suited for:\n- technical refinement\n- movement efficiency\n- micro-adjustments\n- grip or hand-fighting sequences\n- entries\n- transitions\n- finishing mechanics\n- position-specific movement patterns\n\n## Starting Position\nBegin from the exact position immediately before the target action occurs.\n\nThe starting position should be close enough to the target movement that unnecessary setup does not dilute repetitions.\n\n## Attacker Objective\nExecute the target movement with:\n- correct mechanics\n- consistent structure\n- minimal unnecessary movement\n- appropriate timing\n- increasing smoothness\n\n## Defender Objective\nProvide the level of structure or resistance required for accurate repetitions.\n\nResistance may range from:\n- passive positioning\n- realistic posture\n- predictable reaction\n- light resistance\n\nThe defender should not turn the drill into open sparring unless progression specifically requires it.\n\n## Allowed Actions\nOnly actions necessary to complete and reset the target movement.\n\nAdditional techniques should be restricted if they reduce repetition quality.\n\n## Constraints\nPossible constraints include:\n- one grip only\n- one direction of movement\n- fixed starting position\n- no alternative finish\n- slow execution\n- precision before speed\n- continuous repetitions without disengagement\n\n## Reset Condition\nReset when:\n- the target movement is completed\n- the athlete loses the required structure\n- execution becomes significantly distorted\n- the prescribed endpoint is reached\n\nReturn immediately to the starting position to maximise useful repetitions.\n\n## Duration / Reps\nCan be prescribed by:\n- number of repetitions\n- timed blocks\n- alternating partner sets\n- quality threshold\n\nVolume should remain below the point where fatigue significantly corrupts the movement unless fatigue exposure is intentionally part of the drill.\n\n## Success Metric\nSuccess may be measured through:\n- technical consistency\n- reduced unnecessary movement\n- smoother sequencing\n- positional accuracy\n- completion rate\n- reduced execution time without loss of quality\n\n## Common Errors\n- performing repetitions too quickly before mechanics are stable\n- allowing setup time to dominate the drill\n- adding unnecessary techniques\n- repeating poor mechanics\n- confusing volume with useful practice\n- allowing fatigue to degrade movement without noticing\n\n## Progression\nProgress by introducing:\n- greater speed\n- realistic partner posture\n- predictable resistance\n- variable starting conditions\n- a required reaction\n- transition into a decision drill\n- progressive resistance\n\n## Regression\nSimplify by:\n- reducing the movement\n- isolating a micro-movement\n- slowing execution\n- removing resistance\n- fixing the starting position\n- reducing the number of variables\n\n## Athlete Model Signal\nThe drill can reveal:\n- mechanical inconsistency\n- coordination limitations\n- unnecessary tension\n- poor movement sequencing\n- difficulty retaining corrections\n- whether execution deteriorates under fatigue\n- whether the athlete understands the movement but cannot reproduce it reliably\n\n## Core Rule\nRepeat the smallest useful unit until execution becomes reliable enough to add complexity.\n\n## Backlinks\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]]\n- [[Reaction-Chain Drill]]\n- [[Transition Drill]]\n- [[Finishing-Isolation Drill]]\n- [[Constraint Based Positional Sparring]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Retention Stabilisation Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Retention Stabilisation Drill","body":"# Retention / Stabilisation Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop the ability to preserve a useful state or consolidate an advantage while an opponent attempts to disrupt it.\n\n## Training Target\nBalance, posture, positioning, distance management, contact control and adaptive maintenance.\n\nRetention preserves a useful state. Stabilisation establishes reliable control after gaining or recovering it.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- The sport and applicable ruleset.\n- The state or advantage to maintain.\n- Whether it is established or newly acquired.\n- Starting posture, distance, contact and boundaries.\n- Each participant’s permitted actions.\n- Success conditions and reset points.\n- Equipment and contact intensity.\n\nThe target must serve a tactical purpose rather than reward holding a static shape.\n\n## Task\nThe retaining athlete maintains the target state by adjusting to disruption.\n\nThe opposing athlete attempts to displace, bypass, escape or otherwise invalidate that state within the permitted actions.\n\nWhere appropriate, require the retaining athlete to preserve the ability to attack, defend or transition.\n\n## Information and Resistance\nIdentify relevant cues:\n- Changes in pressure or contact.\n- Shifts in balance and alignment.\n- Opponent movement or attacking preparation.\n- Changes in distance and angle.\n- Emerging escape or transition opportunities.\n\nBegin with a defined disruption if needed. Progress towards varied opposition that requires continuous adjustment.\n\n## Constraints\nSelect constraints around the observed maintenance problem:\n- Permitted grips, contacts and defensive actions.\n- Opponent routes and attacking options.\n- Movement boundaries.\n- Required distance or positional relationship.\n- Available transitions.\n- Duration of maintenance.\n- Conditions requiring release, movement or a change of strategy.\n\nAllow useful adjustments. Do not make immobility a requirement unless holding a specific position is the intended task.\n\n## Dosage\nUse short contested exchanges with consistent starting conditions.\n\nSet duration and rest according to the demands of the target state, athlete capability and learning objective.\n\nAvoid extending exchanges until fatigue replaces the intended technical problem.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- The target state remains functionally useful.\n- Posture and balance support the intended task.\n- Adjustments occur before disruption becomes decisive.\n- Control is maintained without unnecessary tension.\n- The athlete remains able to perform a relevant next action.\n- An unsustainable state is recognised and changed appropriately.\n\nMeasure maintenance quality alongside duration. Holding longer does not demonstrate success if tactical usefulness is lost.\n\n## Progression\n1. Maintain the target against a predictable disruption.\n2. Increase resistance or movement.\n3. Introduce multiple disruption routes.\n4. Begin immediately after an entry, transition or recovery.\n5. Require maintenance followed by a relevant action.\n6. Assess transfer within a representative live task.\n\nIncrease difficulty through uncertainty, timing and opponent freedom as well as physical resistance.\n\n## Regression\nReduce disruption options, resistance or duration.\n\nBegin from a more secure state, increase available space or isolate the adjustment the athlete cannot yet perform.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish between:\n- Failure to establish the target state.\n- Failure to recognise disruption.\n- Delayed or inappropriate adjustment.\n- Excessive rigidity or energy expenditure.\n- Maintenance that prevents useful continuation.\n- Persisting with a state that should be abandoned.\n\nIdentify whether the problem concerns acquiring the advantage, maintaining it or acting from it.\n\n## Sport Adaptation\nPreserve the shared function while adapting the target:\n- Grappling: retain guard, maintain a pin or preserve useful grip and positional control.\n- Striking: maintain usable stance, balance, distance or angle under pressure.\n- Mixed combat: stabilise control while accounting for permitted strikes and transitions between phases.\n\nUse the selected ruleset and tactical objective to determine what makes the state valuable.\n\nDo not prescribe prolonged holding where movement or immediate continuation is more appropriate.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\n\nSelect when an athlete reaches useful positions or distances but repeatedly loses them before acting effectively.\n\nDefine what must remain stable and what must remain free to change.\n\nIf the athlete cannot acquire the target state, prioritise entry or recovery work. If maintenance succeeds but continuation fails, prioritise transition or execution work.\n\n## Safety\nDefine contact intensity, prohibited actions and stopping conditions.\n\nUse suitable equipment and controlled partner opposition. Honour taps and stop signals immediately.\n\nDo not reward maintaining control through painful joint positions, uncontrolled impact or ignored submission threats.\n\n## Links\n- [[Entry Setup Drill]] — establish the state that will be retained or stabilised.\n- [[Escape Recovery Drill]] — consolidate a useful state after resolving a disadvantage.\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]] — increase disruption as maintenance competence improves.\n- [[Transition Drill]] — connect stabilisation to a useful next action.\n- [[Constraint Based Positional Sparring]] — contest maintenance within a bounded positional task.\n- [[Individualised Constrained Live Rounds]] — assess maintenance against the athlete’s recurring live problem."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Drill Templates/Transition Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Transition Drill","body":"# Transition Drill\n\n## Definition\nA Transition Drill repeatedly trains movement from one position, control state, or technical phase into another.\n\n## Purpose\nUse when the athlete can perform isolated techniques but needs cleaner movement between positions.\n\nBest suited for:\n- guard-to-pass transitions\n- pass-to-control transitions\n- control-to-submission transitions\n- scramble recovery\n- front headlock chains\n- back exposure sequences\n- leg entanglement transitions\n- positional advancement\n\n## Starting Position\nBegin immediately before the transition being trained.\n\nThe starting position should make the transfer between states the main problem rather than the setup leading into it.\n\n## Attacker Objective\nMove from the starting position into the target position while maintaining:\n- control\n- balance\n- connection\n- pressure\n- positional advantage\n\nThe objective is not simply to reach the destination, but to arrive without creating unnecessary escape windows.\n\n## Defender Objective\nProvide the agreed posture, reaction, or level of resistance that makes the transition realistic.\n\nThe defender may attempt to disrupt the transition within the limits of the drill.\n\n## Allowed Actions\nActions should remain focused on completing, preserving, or recovering the intended transition.\n\nUnrelated techniques should be restricted if they reduce useful transition repetitions.\n\n## Constraints\nPossible constraints include:\n- one starting position\n- one target position\n- no disengagement\n- transition must occur through a specified pathway\n- control must be maintained throughout\n- no submission before target control is established\n- defender limited to specific escape directions\n\n## Reset Condition\nReset when:\n- the target position is established\n- control is lost\n- the defender escapes the intended pathway\n- the athlete bypasses the target transition\n- the exchange leaves the intended training problem\n\n## Duration / Reps\nCan be trained through:\n- fixed repetitions\n- timed continuous transitions\n- alternating partner sets\n- successful transition thresholds\n- chained transitions across several positions\n\n## Success Metric\nSuccess may be measured through:\n- transition completion rate\n- control retained during movement\n- reduction in escape windows\n- balance and connection\n- transition speed without loss of structure\n- ability to recover when the transition is disrupted\n\n## Common Errors\n- treating positions as isolated endpoints\n- releasing control too early\n- moving faster than connection allows\n- creating unnecessary space\n- reaching the target position without stabilising it\n- forcing the transition after the pathway has closed\n\n## Progression\nProgress by:\n- increasing resistance\n- varying defensive reactions\n- adding multiple possible target positions\n- chaining several transitions\n- adding decision requirements\n- moving into positional sparring\n\n## Regression\nSimplify by:\n- fixing the defender reaction\n- reducing resistance\n- isolating one micro-transition\n- slowing execution\n- shortening the distance between starting and target positions\n\n## Athlete Model Signal\nThe drill can reveal:\n- where control is lost between positions\n- poor positional sequencing\n- hesitation during advancement\n- overreliance on isolated techniques\n- weak connection during movement\n- inability to stabilise after transition\n- preferred transition pathways\n\n## Core Rule\nDo not only train positions. Train the movement that connects them.\n\n## Backlinks\n- [[Repetition Drill]]\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]]\n- [[Reaction-Chain Drill]]\n- [[Finishing-Isolation Drill]]\n- [[Constraint Based Positional Sparring]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/50 50 Leg Attack Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"50 50 Leg Attack Schema","body":"# 50 50 Leg Attack Schema\n\n## Definition\n\n50 50 Leg Attack is an offensive structure built from 50 50 Guard in which both athletes have entangled access to each other's legs.\n\nThe position requires careful management of mutual threat and rotational access.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nIncrease control of the opponent's leg while reducing the opponent's ability to generate equivalent offensive access.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Stabilise the 50 50 configuration.\n2. Control the opponent's knee line.\n3. Monitor reciprocal leg attacks.\n4. Improve hip and torso orientation.\n5. Create asymmetry within the otherwise mirrored position.\n6. Expose the desired finishing structure.\n7. Prevent the opponent from matching the attack.\n8. Finish or transition once positional advantage is established.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**50 50 begins symmetrical but does not have to remain symmetrical.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent gains stronger heel or ankle access:\n\n- address defensive control before attacking\n\nIf the opponent's knee line becomes exposed:\n\n- increase positional dominance\n\nIf both athletes remain structurally equal:\n\n- create angle or control asymmetry before committing\n\nIf the opponent disengages:\n\n- follow or transition according to positional value\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- attacking while losing the reciprocal leg battle\n- treating mutual access as one-sided control\n- ignoring hip orientation\n- failing to create asymmetry\n- forcing attacks from a structurally neutral state\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** available submissions vary significantly by ruleset.\n\n**No-Gi:** 50 50 commonly becomes a central leg-lock battle.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\n50 50 Leg Attack is an **asymmetry-generation problem inside a mirrored state**.\n\nThe athlete who first converts mutual access into unequal control gains the offensive advantage.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[50 50 Guard]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Reorientation]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Americana Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Americana Finishing Schema","body":"# Americana Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nAmericana Finishing is a rotational shoulder lock in which the opponent's wrist and elbow are controlled against a stable surface while the shoulder is externally rotated.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nImmobilise the opponent's upper arm and create controlled shoulder rotation without allowing the torso to follow.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Control the opponent's wrist.\n2. Keep the elbow positioned against a stable surface.\n3. Establish the figure-four grip.\n4. Prevent the opponent's shoulder from freely rotating with the torso.\n5. Maintain the wrist close to the surface.\n6. Adjust elbow position as necessary.\n7. Create controlled external rotation at the shoulder.\n8. Apply pressure progressively.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Pin first, rotate second.**\n\nWithout immobilisation, the opponent can move their torso instead of absorbing shoulder rotation.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the elbow begins moving:\n\n- restore the pin before continuing\n\nIf the opponent turns their torso:\n\n- control their body position rather than forcing the arm\n\nIf the wrist lifts away from the surface:\n\n- restore hand position\n\nIf structural isolation is achieved:\n\n- apply gradual rotational pressure\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- lifting the wrist excessively\n- allowing the elbow to move freely\n- using arm strength instead of positional control\n- failing to restrict torso rotation\n- applying pressure too quickly\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\nThe finishing mechanics remain largely consistent.\n\n**Gi:** grips may make wrist control more persistent.\n\n**No-Gi:** direct wrist control and upper-body pressure become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nAmericana Finishing is a **pin-to-rotation schema**.\n\n**Immobilisation → rotational isolation → controlled finish**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Mount]]\n- [[Side Control]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Anaconda Choke Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Anaconda Choke Finishing Schema","body":"# Anaconda Choke Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nThe Anaconda Choke is a head-and-arm strangulation in which the attacking arm wraps around the opponent's neck and trapped arm before the bodies are repositioned to tighten the finishing structure.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nSecure the head-and-arm connection and progressively remove space through controlled rotation and body positioning.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish control around the head and arm.\n2. Secure the hand connection.\n3. Keep the opponent's head compressed toward the trapped shoulder.\n4. Control their ability to posture.\n5. Use rotation where necessary to tighten the structure.\n6. Maintain connection during the positional change.\n7. Remove remaining space.\n8. Apply progressive compression.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Rotation should tighten an existing structure, not replace one that was never established.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the initial connection is loose:\n\n- improve control before rolling or rotating\n\nIf the opponent follows the rotation:\n\n- preserve chest and head connection\n\nIf the trapped shoulder separates from the neck:\n\n- restore compression before finishing\n\nIf positional control becomes unstable:\n\n- stabilise before continuing the choke\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- rolling before securing the structure\n- losing head control during rotation\n- allowing the trapped arm to withdraw\n- creating space while attempting to tighten\n- relying on momentum instead of positional closure\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** fabric can alter friction during rotational transitions.\n\n**No-Gi:** connection must remain particularly precise during movement.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nAnaconda finishing is a **rotation-assisted compression schema**.\n\nThe positional movement is useful only when it reduces available space around an already controlled head-and-arm structure.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Chokes/Cranks]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Reorientation]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Arm In Guillotine Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Arm In Guillotine Schema","body":"# Arm In Guillotine Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nThe Arm In Guillotine is a guillotine configuration in which one of the opponent's arms remains inside the choking structure.\n\nBecause the shoulder occupies additional space, finishing mechanics differ from a clean neck-only guillotine.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nUse head control, shoulder positioning and torso compression to close the available space around the neck despite the trapped arm.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Secure the opponent's head and included arm.\n2. Maintain deep control around the neck.\n3. Prevent posture recovery.\n4. Compress the opponent's shoulder toward the neck.\n5. Control torso orientation.\n6. Remove space through body positioning.\n7. Maintain connection during defensive movement.\n8. Apply progressive finishing pressure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The included arm changes the geometry but does not remove the need for neck compression.**\n\nThe shoulder must become part of the finishing structure rather than simply occupying space.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent's shoulder creates excessive space:\n\n- improve body angle before squeezing\n\nIf the opponent drives forward:\n\n- maintain head control and adapt hip position\n\nIf the arm begins escaping:\n\n- recognise whether the position is transitioning into a standard guillotine\n\nIf posture begins recovering:\n\n- restore head control first\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- treating the finish exactly like a neck-only guillotine\n- failing to use the opponent's shoulder structurally\n- shallow neck control\n- allowing posture recovery\n- losing torso connection\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\nThe central finishing mechanics remain similar.\n\n**Gi:** clothing can affect friction and hand access.\n\n**No-Gi:** positional compression and body alignment become particularly important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nArm In Guillotine is a **shared-space compression schema**.\n\nThe opponent's arm occupies part of the finishing structure and must be integrated rather than ignored.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Front Headlock to Guillotine Schema]]\n- [[Chokes/Cranks]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Arm Triangle Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Arm Triangle Finishing Schema","body":"# Arm Triangle Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nArm Triangle Finishing is a strangulation finishing structure in which the opponent's own shoulder and the attacker's arm compress both sides of the neck.\n\nThe finish depends on alignment and shoulder positioning rather than raw squeezing force.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nPosition the opponent's shoulder against one side of the neck while the attacker's arm closes the opposite side.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Move the opponent's arm across the neck.\n2. Control head position.\n3. Remove space between the opponent's shoulder and neck.\n4. Establish deep arm position.\n5. Align the attacker's torso with the finishing direction.\n6. Apply progressive shoulder and chest pressure.\n7. Maintain position as the opponent attempts to create space.\n8. Finish through structural compression rather than rapid squeezing.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The opponent's shoulder is part of the choke.**\n\nIf the shoulder is not positioned correctly, additional squeezing usually compensates poorly.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf space remains near the neck:\n\n- improve shoulder position before increasing force\n\nIf the opponent turns:\n\n- follow the head and shoulder alignment\n\nIf the arm begins slipping back:\n\n- restore arm position before continuing\n\nIf the structure is correct:\n\n- apply pressure progressively\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- squeezing before the shoulder is positioned\n- leaving space between neck and shoulder\n- poor head control\n- incorrect torso alignment\n- using arm strength instead of structural pressure\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\nThe core finishing mechanics remain similar.\n\n**Gi:** fabric can affect friction and head movement.\n\n**No-Gi:** chest pressure and positional alignment are more exposed and therefore more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nArm Triangle Finishing is an **alignment-dependent compression schema**.\n\nFinishing probability increases as unnecessary space approaches zero.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Mount]]\n- [[Side Control]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Line of Attack and Line of Movement]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Armbar Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Armbar Finishing Schema","body":"# Armbar Finishing Schema\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Armbar]]\n\n## Function\nA reusable coaching schema for recognising, diagnosing, and improving armbar finishing mechanics.\n\nThis node should help FRAME decide:\n\n- whether the arm is genuinely isolated\n- whether the elbow line is secure enough to finish\n- what defensive structure is stopping progression\n- whether the athlete should adjust position, break grips, or transition\n- what single cue or drill is most useful\n\nThe purpose is not to explain what an armbar is.\n\nThe purpose is to help FRAME identify why an apparently strong armbar is or is not finishing.\n\n## Starting Conditions\nA finishing armbar becomes viable when:\n\n- the opponent’s arm is separated from the torso\n- the attacker controls the shoulder line\n- the opponent’s elbow remains inside the attacking structure\n- the attacker’s hips are close enough to apply leverage\n- rotational escape is sufficiently restricted\n\nFRAME should not treat wrist control alone as a finishing condition.\n\nThe full arm must be controlled as a connected structure.\n\n## Core Sequence\nA reliable finishing progression usually follows this logic:\n\n1. isolate the arm\n2. secure the elbow line\n3. control shoulder rotation\n4. remove defensive grip structure\n5. keep the hips close\n6. align the opponent’s thumb and elbow appropriately\n7. remove slack\n8. apply controlled hip extension\n\nThe exact grip-breaking method may vary.\n\nThe invariant is:\n\ncontrol before extension.\n\n## Primary Mechanical Priorities\n\n### Elbow Line\nThe elbow should remain controlled above or inside the attacker’s hip structure.\n\nIf the opponent’s elbow slips beyond the controlling line, the finishing position rapidly weakens.\n\nFRAME should treat loss of elbow line as a major diagnostic event.\n\n### Shoulder Control\nThe opponent should not be able to freely rotate the shoulder.\n\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- torso rotation\n- shoulder angle\n- knee connection\n- space around the opponent’s upper body\n\nA loose shoulder allows the defender to follow the extension and relieve pressure.\n\n### Hip Proximity\nThe attacker’s hips should remain close to the opponent’s shoulder and elbow.\n\nExcessive distance creates:\n\n- slack\n- weaker leverage\n- easier rotation\n- greater chance of elbow escape\n\n### Knee Connection\nThe attacker’s knees should contribute to controlling the opponent’s upper body.\n\nFRAME should look for whether the legs are:\n\n- connected\n- controlling shoulder movement\n- limiting posture\n- preventing turning\n\nLoose legs often explain failed finishes.\n\n## Decision Branches\n\n### Opponent Connects Hands\nThe finishing problem becomes a grip-breaking problem.\n\nFRAME should prioritise:\n\n- maintaining elbow line\n- preserving shoulder control\n- breaking the defensive connection without creating space\n\nDo not cue hip extension while the opponent’s arm remains structurally protected.\n\n### Opponent Turns Toward the Attacker\nLikely issue:\n\n- shoulder control weakening\n- legs too loose\n- elbow line beginning to escape\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nstabilise the upper body before attempting to finish again.\n\n### Opponent Stacks\nFRAME should assess whether:\n\n- the attacker can maintain elbow control\n- the opponent’s posture is changing the finishing angle\n- a positional adjustment is safer than continuing to extend\n\nThe system should not encourage forcing the submission through a compromised structure.\n\n### Opponent Pulls the Elbow Free\nThe finishing window has effectively closed.\n\nFRAME should shift from finishing logic to positional recovery or transition.\n\n### Arm Is Straight but Submission Is Weak\nLikely causes:\n\n- elbow not aligned with the hips\n- shoulder still rotating\n- slack in the structure\n- hip distance too great\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nfix alignment before adding force.\n\n## Failure Diagnosis\n\n### Failure Pattern: Defender Keeps Pulling Arm Out\nLikely causes:\n\n- elbow line weak\n- hips too far away\n- upper-body control too loose\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nsecure the elbow deeper before extending.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Athlete Cannot Break Defensive Grip\nLikely causes:\n\n- trying to separate the hands without controlling the elbow\n- poor angle\n- excessive space\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nstabilise the arm first, then break the grip.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Defender Turns Into the Armbar\nLikely causes:\n\n- shoulder control weak\n- knees too open\n- attacker loses torso alignment\n\nUseful coaching preference:\ncontrol rotation before finishing.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Arm Is Extended but No Finish\nLikely causes:\n\n- elbow positioned incorrectly relative to the hips\n- shoulder rotation still available\n- wrist alignment poor\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nre-align the arm rather than extending harder.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Attacker Falls Back and Loses Position\nLikely causes:\n\n- arm isolation incomplete\n- submission attempted before control\n- poor transition timing\n\nUseful coaching preference:\ndo not sacrifice positional security before the arm is genuinely trapped.\n\n## Coaching Cues\nFRAME should prefer concise cues such as:\n\n- “Keep the elbow inside your hip line.”\n- “Control the shoulder before you extend.”\n- “Stay close to the arm.”\n- “Don’t open your knees.”\n- “Break the grip without giving them space.”\n- “Fix the angle before you bridge.”\n- “You lost the elbow — recover position first.”\n\nAvoid giving the athlete the whole armbar sequence when one structural issue explains the failure.\n\n## Video Recognition\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- isolated arm\n- elbow position relative to the attacker’s hips\n- opponent’s hand connection\n- attacker knee position\n- shoulder rotation\n- hip distance\n- defender attempting to stack or turn\n- moment the elbow line is secured or lost\n\nFRAME should distinguish:\n\narm extension\n\nfrom\n\na mechanically secure finishing armbar.\n\n## Drill Selection Logic\n\n### Elbow Line Failure\nUse drills around:\n- maintaining elbow capture\n- preventing elbow withdrawal\n- controlling the arm through resistance\n\n### Grip-Breaking Failure\nUse drills where the defender begins with connected hands and the attacker must separate them without losing position.\n\n### Shoulder-Control Failure\nUse drills around:\n- knee connection\n- preventing rotation\n- maintaining torso control\n\n### Finishing Alignment Failure\nUse controlled repetitions focused on:\n- hip distance\n- elbow placement\n- shoulder restriction\n- removing slack before extension\n\n### Reaction Failure\nUse progressive resistance where the defender alternates between:\n- connecting hands\n- stacking\n- turning inward\n- pulling the elbow free\n\nFRAME should coach the correct response to the reaction rather than forcing one fixed finishing path.\n\n## Coaching Preference\nFRAME should treat armbar finishing as a control problem before an extension problem.\n\nPrefer:\n\nsecure elbow line\n→ control shoulder\n→ address defensive structure\n→ remove slack\n→ extend only when alignment is correct\n\nIf the opponent has already escaped the elbow line, FRAME should stop coaching the finish and recognise the state change.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nArmbar finishing is an isolation-and-alignment problem.\n\nThe coaching question is:\n\nWhich part of the arm-control chain is still free?\n\nFRAME should distinguish between:\n\n- elbow-line failure\n- shoulder-control failure\n- defensive-grip failure\n- alignment failure\n- decision failure\n\nThe useful logic is:\n\nverify isolation\n→ locate remaining freedom\n→ remove that freedom\n→ apply controlled extension\n→ evaluate outcome\n→ update coaching preference"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Back Control Maintenance Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Back Control Maintenance Schema","body":"# Back Control Maintenance Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nBack Control Maintenance is the process of preserving chest-to-back connection, rotational control and lower-body attachment while the opponent attempts to turn, clear hooks or bring their shoulders to the mat.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nPrevent the opponent from separating the attacker's upper-body control from lower-body control.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Maintain chest-to-back connection.\n2. Control shoulder rotation.\n3. Preserve at least one effective lower-body attachment.\n4. Follow hip movement without losing upper-body alignment.\n5. Prevent the opponent from bringing both shoulders safely to the mat.\n6. Rebuild hooks or body control as attachments are challenged.\n7. Maintain head position relative to the opponent.\n8. Transition to finishing attacks only when control is stable.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Back Control is maintained by preserving alignment between upper-body connection and lower-body attachment.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf a hook is cleared:\n\n- preserve chest connection and replace lower-body control\n\nIf the opponent turns toward the mat:\n\n- follow shoulder rotation and maintain upper-body attachment\n\nIf the opponent reaches the safe side:\n\n- prioritise preventing full shoulder clearance\n\nIf the opponent becomes static:\n\n- increase offensive pressure\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- chasing hooks while losing chest connection\n- allowing both shoulders to reach the mat\n- separating upper- and lower-body control\n- attacking before positional alignment is stable\n- allowing head position to drift\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** collar controls create additional finishing and maintenance options.\n\n**No-Gi:** chest connection, seatbelt-style upper-body control and hip attachment become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nBack Control Maintenance is an **attachment integrity problem**.\n\nControl weakens when upper-body and lower-body attachments separate.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Back Control]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Control Migration]]\n- [[Reorientation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Body Fold Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Body Fold Passing Schema","body":"# Body Fold Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nBody Fold Passing is a compression-based passing structure in which the opponent's knees are driven toward the torso, reducing hip extension, rotation and independent leg movement.\n\nThe opponent's guard is converted from a mobile structure into a compressed defensive shape.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nReduce available hip range until the legs can no longer reorganise effectively.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- both legs can be collected\n- the knees can be compressed toward the torso\n- the pelvis can be controlled\n- the opponent cannot freely restore hip extension\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Collect the legs.\n2. Compress the knees toward the torso.\n3. Maintain control over the pelvis.\n4. Prevent hip re-extension.\n5. Redirect the compressed legs away from the intended passing direction.\n6. Advance around the legs.\n7. Clear the knee line.\n8. Consolidate upper-body control.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Reduce range before attempting to pass through it.**\n\nA mobile hip can repeatedly regenerate guard.\n\nA compressed hip has fewer available responses.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent attempts to extend:\n\n- maintain compression and stop the pelvis escaping\n\nIf the opponent rotates:\n\n- follow the pelvis rather than chasing the feet\n\nIf the opponent begins to invert:\n\n- prioritise pelvic control\n\nIf the legs separate:\n\n- recollect them before continuing\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- controlling the feet without controlling the hips\n- allowing the opponent to regain extension\n- moving around the legs before establishing compression\n- failing to follow pelvic rotation\n- allowing independent knee movement\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** trouser grips can increase persistence of leg control.\n\n**No-Gi:** compression depends more on body position, forearm structure and torso pressure.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nBody Fold Passing is a **degrees-of-freedom reduction schema**.\n\nAs hip extension, rotation and independent leg movement disappear, guard regeneration becomes harder.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Degrees of Freedom]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Weight Transfer]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Body Lock Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Body Lock Passing Schema","body":"# Body Lock Passing Schema\n\n## Function\nA reusable coaching schema for recognising, diagnosing, and improving body lock passing.\n\nThis node should help FRAME decide:\n\n- whether the body lock is actually stable\n- which part of the connection is failing\n- whether the athlete should keep progressing or first rebuild control\n- what cue is most useful in the moment\n- which drill best targets the observed failure\n\nThe purpose is not to explain body lock passing generally.\n\nThe purpose is to help FRAME intervene intelligently.\n\n## Starting Conditions\nBody lock passing becomes viable when:\n\n- the passer has closed distance safely\n- the opponent’s torso or waist can be meaningfully connected to\n- the passer has enough head and chest position to limit rotation\n- the bottom athlete cannot freely extend away or rebuild strong frames\n- the passer can begin controlling the opponent’s hips while staying balanced\n\nFRAME should not recommend body lock passing simply because the passer is close.\n\nThe connection must already be useful.\n\n## Core Sequence\nA reliable body lock progression usually follows this logic:\n\n1. close distance\n2. connect around the torso or hips\n3. establish head position\n4. reduce shoulder and hip rotation\n5. neutralise hooks or knee position\n6. advance the hips\n7. clear the legs progressively\n8. stabilise chest-to-chest top control\n\nThe exact grip may vary.\n\nThe controlling relationship matters more than one fixed hand configuration.\n\n## Primary Mechanical Priorities\n\n### Chest Connection\nThe passer should maintain close upper-body contact.\n\nIf the torso connection becomes loose, the bottom athlete gains access to:\n\n- frames\n- rotation\n- underhooks\n- hook recovery\n- distance creation\n\nFRAME should treat loss of chest connection as a primary failure, not a secondary detail.\n\n### Head Position\nHead position helps control the opponent’s upper-body direction.\n\nFRAME should look for whether the passer’s head is:\n\n- reinforcing chest pressure\n- blocking rotation\n- preventing the opponent from turning inward\n- helping steer the shoulders\n\nPoor head position often explains why an otherwise strong body lock feels weak.\n\n### Hip Proximity\nThe passer’s hips should remain close enough to limit the bottom athlete’s lower-body freedom.\n\nIf the upper body is connected but the hips drift away, the opponent may still:\n\n- insert hooks\n- extend the knees\n- create elevation\n- rebuild guard\n\n### Progressive Leg Clearance\nThe passer should clear defensive structures one layer at a time.\n\nFRAME should distinguish between:\n\n- body lock established\n- hooks neutralised\n- knee line cleared\n- pass completed\n\nThis allows better diagnosis of where progression stopped.\n\n## Decision Branches\n\n### Opponent Establishes Strong Frames\nFRAME should prioritise re-closing distance.\n\nPossible intervention:\n\n- improve head position\n- reconnect chest pressure\n- remove the frame before trying to pass further\n\nDo not cue leg clearance while the upper-body connection is already broken.\n\n### Opponent Inserts Butterfly Hooks\nFRAME should assess whether the passer still controls the torso strongly enough to continue.\n\nUseful responses may involve:\n\n- flattening the hook\n- changing hip angle\n- widening base\n- controlling elevation before progressing\n\n### Opponent Turns Onto a Side\nThis may create:\n\n- easier hip access\n- back exposure\n- underhook battles\n- alternate passing angles\n\nFRAME should treat the turn as information, not automatically as a failed pass.\n\n### Opponent Recovers Knee Inside\nThis usually indicates that hip distance or lower-body control has weakened.\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nrestore hip connection before trying to advance again.\n\n### Passer Gets Stuck in Half Guard\nFRAME should recognise that the body lock has already done part of its job.\n\nThe problem has shifted.\n\nIntervention should now focus on:\n\n- upper-body consolidation\n- freeing the trapped leg\n- preventing the bottom athlete rebuilding structure\n\n## Failure Diagnosis\n\n### Failure Pattern: Athlete Gets Close but Cannot Progress\nLikely causes:\n\n- chest connection is shallow\n- hips are too far away\n- head position is not controlling direction\n- the opponent still has active hooks\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nimprove control before trying to move around the guard.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Opponent Keeps Turning In\nLikely causes:\n\n- weak head position\n- loose shoulder control\n- insufficient chest connection\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nfix upper-body directional control.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Opponent Keeps Recovering Hooks\nLikely causes:\n\n- hips too far away\n- lower-body progression too passive\n- passer focusing only on the arms\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nclose the hip gap.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Passer Gets Elevated\nLikely causes:\n\n- base too narrow\n- weight too forward\n- active butterfly hooks not neutralised\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nstabilise base before progressing.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Pass Stalls After Reaching Half Guard\nLikely causes:\n\n- trapped leg not being addressed\n- chest control incomplete\n- bottom athlete still has inside frames\n\nUseful coaching preference:\ntreat it as a completion problem, not an entry problem.\n\n## Coaching Cues\nFRAME should prefer short, specific cues such as:\n\n- “Close the hip gap.”\n- “Keep your chest connected.”\n- “Win the head position first.”\n- “Flatten the hook before you move.”\n- “Stop them turning into you.”\n- “You’ve already passed most of the guard — finish the trapped leg.”\n\nAvoid giving the athlete the whole body lock system unless they ask for it.\n\n## Video Recognition\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- arms connected around the torso or hips\n- sustained chest-to-body contact\n- head position controlling the opponent’s rotation\n- hips remaining close\n- butterfly hooks being neutralised\n- knees being progressively cleared\n- transition into half guard or chest-to-chest control\n\nFRAME should avoid labelling every close-range passing exchange as body lock passing.\n\nThe defining feature is sustained torso control combined with progressive lower-body clearance.\n\n## Drill Selection Logic\n\n### Connection Failure\nUse drills around:\n- closing distance\n- chest connection\n- maintaining the lock against framing\n\n### Head Position Failure\nUse drills around:\n- blocking rotation\n- steering shoulders\n- maintaining pressure while the opponent turns\n\n### Hook Management Failure\nUse drills around:\n- butterfly hook neutralisation\n- base adjustment\n- hip positioning\n\n### Completion Failure\nUse drills around:\n- clearing the final knee\n- freeing trapped legs\n- stabilising chest-to-chest control\n\n### Reaction Failure\nUse progressive resistance where the defender alternates between:\n- framing\n- inserting hooks\n- turning inward\n- recovering a knee\n- forcing half guard\n\nThe drill should target the athlete’s actual breakdown point.\n\n## Coaching Preference\nFRAME should not prescribe more movement when the athlete’s control structure is already weak.\n\nPrefer:\n\nconnection first\n→ identify the specific leak\n→ apply one correction\n→ retest\n→ progress only when the structure holds\n\nThe system should favour minimal, high-value interventions over technical overload.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nBody lock passing is a constraint-management problem.\n\nThe coaching question is:\n\nWhich layer of connection is leaking?\n\nThe system should use this schema to distinguish between:\n\n- upper-body connection failure\n- rotational control failure\n- hip distance failure\n- hook-management failure\n- completion failure\n\nThe useful logic is:\n\nrecognise connection state\n→ identify the weakest layer\n→ correct that layer\n→ observe response\n→ update coaching preference\n\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Body Lock Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Butterfly Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Butterfly Passing Schema","body":"# Butterfly Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nButterfly Passing is the process of neutralising the opponent's seated or supine hook-based guard by reducing the effectiveness of their hooks, controlling their hip orientation and progressing beyond the knees.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nPrevent the opponent's hooks from creating elevation, rotation or meaningful separation.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the opponent has established Butterfly Guard\n- one or both hooks can be controlled or made ineffective\n- the passer can maintain stable base\n- the opponent's upper-body connection can be managed\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Stabilise base.\n2. Identify the active hook.\n3. Reduce the hook's ability to elevate.\n4. Control the opponent's knees or hips.\n5. Prevent effective reorientation.\n6. Collapse or bypass the hook structure.\n7. Advance beyond the knees.\n8. Consolidate control.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**A butterfly hook matters only while it can meaningfully influence the passer's centre of mass.**\n\nNeutralise the hook's mechanical relationship to the passer before progressing.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent elevates:\n\n- widen or relocate base and reduce the active hook\n\nIf the opponent sits upright:\n\n- manage upper-body connection before advancing\n\nIf one hook disappears:\n\n- exploit the asymmetry immediately\n\nIf the opponent transitions into another guard:\n\n- follow the positional transition rather than forcing the old pass\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- driving weight directly onto active hooks\n- allowing both hooks to remain mechanically useful\n- narrowing the base unnecessarily\n- ignoring upper-body connection\n- progressing without monitoring the opponent's hip rotation\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can limit upper-body movement and leg recovery.\n\n**No-Gi:** base, head position and close body connection become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nButterfly Passing is a **load-management problem**.\n\nThe opponent's hooks become dangerous when they can meaningfully redirect the passer's weight.\n\nPassing begins by reducing that influence.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Butterfly Guard]]\n- [[Butterfly Half]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Reorientation]]\n- [[Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Closed Guard Opening Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Closed Guard Opening Schema","body":"# Closed Guard Opening Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nClosed Guard Opening is the process of breaking the opponent's closed lower-body connection while maintaining posture, base and protection against immediate attacks.\n\nOpening the guard is not itself a pass.\n\nIt is the transition from a closed defensive structure into a passable guard state.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nForce separation of the opponent's legs without sacrificing posture or creating a high-value attacking opportunity.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes active whenever:\n\n- the opponent has established Closed Guard\n- the passer cannot meaningfully progress while the ankles remain connected\n- posture and base can be stabilised\n- an opening mechanism can be applied safely\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish stable posture and base.\n2. Control the opponent's ability to break posture.\n3. Position the body so the guard cannot remain closed without increasing effort.\n4. Apply mechanical pressure to the closed structure.\n5. Force the ankles to separate.\n6. Immediately control the resulting leg position.\n7. Prevent the guard from closing again.\n8. Transition directly into a passing state.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Opening Closed Guard creates a transition window.**\n\nThe opening has little value if the opponent is allowed to immediately establish another strong guard.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf posture breaks:\n\n- restore posture before continuing\n\nIf the opponent climbs their guard higher:\n\n- address the new threat before attempting the opening\n\nIf the guard opens:\n\n- immediately establish control over the legs\n\nIf the opponent transitions to Open Guard:\n\n- recognise the new positional state and apply the appropriate passing structure\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- attempting to open while posture is compromised\n- placing limbs into vulnerable positions\n- opening the guard without controlling the transition\n- allowing immediate re-closing\n- treating the opening itself as positional success\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips provide additional posture-control and opening options for both athletes.\n\n**No-Gi:** posture, elbow position and body alignment become proportionally more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nClosed Guard Opening is a **state-transition schema**.\n\nThe real objective is not:\n\n**closed → open**\n\nIt is:\n\n**closed structure → controlled transition → favourable passing state**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Close Guard]]\n- [[Open Guard]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Positional State Transition]]\n- [[Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Crucifix Control Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Crucifix Control Schema","body":"# Crucifix Control Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nCrucifix Control is a dominant control structure that isolates one of the opponent's arms using the legs while controlling the head and upper torso.\n\nThe position creates strong asymmetry by reducing the opponent's available defensive limbs.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nMaintain simultaneous control of the opponent's arm, head and torso while preventing rotational escape.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Isolate one arm with the legs.\n2. Control the head and upper torso.\n3. Prevent the opponent from turning toward the trapped arm.\n4. Maintain hip connection.\n5. Monitor the free arm.\n6. Adjust leg position as the opponent rotates.\n7. Preserve control before attacking.\n8. Transition if the arm escapes.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Crucifix control becomes strong because one defensive limb is removed from the system.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the trapped arm begins escaping:\n\n- restore leg control before attacking\n\nIf the opponent turns toward the attacker:\n\n- maintain head control and follow rotation\n\nIf the opponent turns away:\n\n- preserve hip connection and prevent shoulder clearance\n\nIf control deteriorates:\n\n- transition rather than forcing the position\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- attacking while the trapped arm is loose\n- losing head control\n- allowing the opponent to rotate both shoulders freely\n- focusing on one limb while losing torso connection\n- failing to transition when control is compromised\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** collar controls add attacking options.\n\n**No-Gi:** limb isolation and torso connection carry more of the control burden.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nCrucifix Control is an **asymmetry-amplification state**.\n\nThe opponent becomes easier to control because one defensive limb is functionally removed.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Crucifix]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]\n- [[Control Migration]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Darce Choke Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Darce Choke Finishing Schema","body":"# Darce Choke Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nThe Darce Choke is an arm-triangle strangulation entered by threading the attacking arm beneath the opponent's near arm and around the neck.\n\nThe opponent's shoulder forms one side of the strangulation while the attacker's arm closes the opposite side.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nBring the opponent's shoulder into the neck while eliminating space around the head-and-arm structure.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish access beneath the opponent's arm.\n2. Thread deeply around the neck.\n3. Secure the finishing connection.\n4. Control head and shoulder alignment.\n5. Compress the opponent's shoulder toward their neck.\n6. Remove space through chest and torso positioning.\n7. Prevent the opponent from extending or rotating free.\n8. Apply progressive finishing pressure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The shoulder must become an active component of the choke.**\n\nMore arm strength cannot compensate efficiently for poor shoulder position.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent's shoulder remains far from the neck:\n\n- improve compression before finishing\n\nIf the opponent turns:\n\n- follow the rotation while preserving head-and-arm connection\n\nIf the grip remains shallow:\n\n- increase threading depth rather than forcing pressure\n\nIf the opponent collapses underneath:\n\n- use the positional change to improve alignment\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- shallow arm penetration\n- leaving space around the neck\n- squeezing before the shoulder is positioned\n- allowing the opponent to extend their posture\n- losing chest connection\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** friction and grips may slow defensive rotation.\n\n**No-Gi:** deep threading and tight torso connection become particularly important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nDarce finishing is an **arm-triangle compression problem**.\n\n**Access → shoulder integration → space removal → finishing pressure**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Chokes/Cranks]]\n- [[Front Headlock to Guillotine Schema]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Inside Position]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Double Under Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Double Under Passing Schema","body":"# Double Under Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nDouble Under Passing is a pressure passing structure built around controlling both legs from underneath, elevating the opponent's hips, compressing their knees toward their torso, and removing their ability to freely rotate or re-guard.\n\nThe pass succeeds by turning leg mobility into spinal and hip compression.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nConvert bilateral leg control into upper-body exposure while preventing the opponent from recovering hip angle.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- both legs can be collected from underneath\n- the opponent's hips can be elevated or folded\n- the passer's head and shoulders can enter beneath the leg line\n- the opponent cannot freely retract both knees\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Secure both legs above the knees.\n2. Bring the opponent's hips off the floor where possible.\n3. Compress the knees toward the chest.\n4. Remove lateral hip mobility.\n5. Angle the opponent's legs away from the intended passing side.\n6. Advance chest and shoulder position beyond the knees.\n7. Clear the legs while maintaining compression.\n8. Consolidate upper-body control before releasing the legs.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Do not try to outrun the legs. Make the legs unable to move independently.**\n\nThe pass becomes easier as the opponent's pelvis loses freedom.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent frames strongly with the arms:\n\n- maintain lower-body compression\n- progress incrementally rather than abandoning the position\n\nIf the opponent turns onto one hip:\n\n- redirect the knees toward the opposite shoulder\n- use the rotation to expose a passing lane\n\nIf the opponent begins inverting:\n\n- control the pelvis before attempting to clear the legs\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- controlling the ankles instead of the upper legs\n- allowing the hips to remain flat and mobile\n- releasing compression before establishing torso control\n- driving directly forward without creating an angle\n- allowing one knee to escape independently\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** trouser and belt grips may increase control of the legs and hips.\n\n**No-Gi:** control relies more heavily on elbow position, shoulder pressure, hip elevation and connection to the hamstrings.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nDouble Under Passing is a **mobility-compression problem**.\n\nAs pelvic freedom decreases, passing probability increases.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Weight Transfer]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Float Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Float Passing Schema","body":"# Float Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nFloat Passing is a dynamic passing structure in which the passer maintains mobile weight over the opponent's hips and legs rather than immediately committing to a fixed pin.\n\nThe passer uses balance, weight transfer and reactive movement to remain structurally superior while the guard reorganises underneath.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nMaintain superior mobility and balance while forcing repeated defensive adjustment.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the passer can safely place weight over the lower body\n- stable base can be maintained during movement\n- the legs can be monitored without immediate static control\n- enough mobility exists to respond to changing guard configurations\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish a stable but mobile base.\n2. Position weight over the hips and legs.\n3. Allow controlled movement underneath.\n4. Track changes in hip and knee orientation.\n5. Adjust weight in response.\n6. Prevent stable opponent connection.\n7. Exploit openings created during reorganisation.\n8. Convert into a more stable passing structure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Mobility can function as control.**\n\nThe opponent may be allowed to move without being allowed to organise.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent establishes strong connection:\n\n- change angle or remove the connection\n\nIf the legs separate:\n\n- exploit the exposed space\n\nIf the opponent turns strongly:\n\n- follow the rotation into the available passing route\n\nIf the opponent becomes static:\n\n- convert mobility into pressure\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- confusing mobility with uncontrolled movement\n- allowing the centre of mass to drift beyond the base\n- moving without monitoring the legs\n- becoming excessively upright\n- failing to convert openings into progression\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can supplement balance and directional control.\n\n**No-Gi:** base, weight transfer and connection management carry more of the control burden.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nFloat Passing is a **dynamic control state**.\n\nControl is created through continuous adjustment rather than immediate immobilisation.\n\n**Base stability + weight placement + reaction speed + opponent reorganisation**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Weight Transfer]]\n- [[Centre of Mass]]\n- [[Reaction Mapping]]\n- [[Dynamic Pinning]]\n- [[Positional State Transition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Front Headlock to Guillotine Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Front Headlock to Guillotine Schema","body":"# Front Headlock to Guillotine Schema\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Guillotine]]\n\n## Function\nA reusable coaching schema for recognising, diagnosing, and improving transitions from front head control into a guillotine finish.\n\nThis node should help FRAME decide:\n\n- whether the guillotine is actually available\n- whether posture has been sufficiently broken\n- whether the athlete should finish, reposition, or maintain control\n- which mechanical layer is failing\n- what cue or drill best targets the observed problem\n\nThe purpose is not to describe the guillotine.\n\nThe purpose is to help FRAME recognise when head control has become a viable submission opportunity and coach the smallest useful intervention.\n\n## Starting Conditions\nA guillotine becomes viable when:\n\n- the opponent’s head is controlled below a useful posture line\n- the attacker has meaningful neck access\n- the opponent cannot immediately withdraw the head\n- the attacker can connect the choking structure without sacrificing control\n- the opponent’s posture or direction of movement can be constrained\n\nFRAME should not recommend attacking the neck simply because the opponent’s head is nearby.\n\nThe head must already be vulnerable to control.\n\n## Core Sequence\nA reliable progression usually follows this logic:\n\n1. capture the head\n2. break or maintain broken posture\n3. secure useful choking-arm depth\n4. close space around the neck\n5. prevent posture recovery\n6. create the appropriate finishing angle\n7. apply controlled compression\n8. maintain positional options if the finish fails\n\nThe exact grip can vary.\n\nThe invariant is:\n\ncontrol posture before chasing compression.\n\n## Primary Mechanical Priorities\n\n### Head Capture\nThe attacker needs enough control that the opponent cannot simply retract the head.\n\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- head depth\n- shoulder connection\n- whether the opponent’s neck remains accessible\n- whether the grip survives movement\n\nA shallow head capture usually produces a weak submission attempt.\n\n### Posture Control\nThe opponent’s spine and head position should remain compromised.\n\nIf the defender can straighten the posture, the choke usually weakens.\n\nFRAME should treat posture recovery as a primary diagnostic event.\n\n### Choking Arm Depth\nThe attacker should establish enough depth to create meaningful neck control.\n\nIf the arm is shallow:\n\n- space remains around the neck\n- the opponent can turn or posture\n- the attacker becomes dependent on squeezing strength\n\n### Elbow and Shoulder Connection\nThe choking structure should remain compact.\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- elbow position\n- shoulder position\n- chest connection\n- space between attacker and defender\n\nThe goal is to close space rather than pull harder.\n\n### Finishing Angle\nThe attacker may need to change torso or hip angle to improve compression.\n\nFRAME should distinguish:\n\nmore force\n\nfrom\n\nbetter alignment.\n\nThe preferred coaching intervention should usually be alignment first.\n\n## Decision Branches\n\n### Opponent Begins to Posture\nUseful coaching preference:\n\nrestore head control before squeezing harder.\n\nFRAME should avoid recommending continued finishing pressure against improving posture.\n\n### Opponent Turns Toward Open Space\nPossible responses include:\n\n- adjust angle\n- follow the head\n- re-close the elbow\n- maintain control rather than forcing the original finish\n\nFRAME should treat rotational escape as a positional problem.\n\n### Opponent Drives Forward\nThe athlete may still have a strong guillotine if structure remains intact.\n\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether the head stays trapped\n- whether the attacker keeps useful angle\n- whether the opponent’s pressure is actually improving the choke or collapsing the attacker’s structure\n\n### Grip Is Established but Nothing Happens\nLikely issue:\n\n- posture too strong\n- choking arm too shallow\n- elbow too open\n- excessive space\n- poor finishing angle\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nidentify the missing structural component rather than cueing harder squeezing.\n\n### Opponent Clears the Head\nThe submission opportunity has ended.\n\nFRAME should stop coaching the finish and recognise the positional reset or transition.\n\n## Failure Diagnosis\n\n### Failure Pattern: Opponent Keeps Pulling Head Free\nLikely causes:\n\n- shallow capture\n- poor shoulder connection\n- head control established too late\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nimprove capture before building the grip.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Athlete Burns Arms Without Finishing\nLikely causes:\n\n- posture not controlled\n- excessive space\n- poor elbow position\n- reliance on squeezing\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nfix structure before applying more force.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Opponent Postures Out\nLikely causes:\n\n- attacker allows head height to increase\n- chest and shoulder connection lost\n- grip is present without positional control\n\nUseful coaching preference:\ncontrol posture before reattacking.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Athlete Loses Position Chasing the Choke\nLikely causes:\n\n- submission prioritised over control\n- uncontrolled fall or rotation\n- head capture weaker than perceived\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nretain control unless the finishing structure is genuinely established.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Choke Looks Tight but Defender Can Turn\nLikely causes:\n\n- rotational control missing\n- torso angle incorrect\n- elbow structure loose\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nclose the escape direction.\n\n## Coaching Cues\nFRAME should prefer concise cues such as:\n\n- “Keep the head down first.”\n- “Don’t squeeze yet — close the space.”\n- “Your arm is too shallow.”\n- “Keep the elbow tight.”\n- “Follow their posture.”\n- “Fix the angle before adding pressure.”\n- “Don’t give up control just to chase the finish.”\n\nAvoid stacking multiple technical details unless the athlete requests a full breakdown.\n\n## Video Recognition\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- opponent’s head captured beneath the attacker’s arm\n- posture visibly broken\n- choking arm depth\n- hand connection\n- elbow position\n- shoulder and chest connection\n- defender attempting to posture or rotate\n- attacker changing angle before finishing\n- whether head control survives a failed submission attempt\n\nFRAME should distinguish:\n\nhead contact\n\nfrom\n\nmeaningful head control.\n\n## Drill Selection Logic\n\n### Head Capture Failure\nUse drills around:\n- capturing the head during wrestling exchanges\n- maintaining connection against posture recovery\n- following head movement\n\n### Posture Control Failure\nUse drills where the defender progressively attempts to posture while the attacker maintains head control without finishing.\n\n### Choking Structure Failure\nUse positional repetitions focused on:\n- arm depth\n- elbow position\n- shoulder connection\n- reducing space\n\n### Finishing Angle Failure\nUse controlled finishing drills where pressure only increases after correct alignment is established.\n\n### Decision Failure\nUse progressive resistance where the defender alternates between:\n- posturing\n- driving forward\n- turning\n- pulling the head free\n\nThe attacker must decide whether to:\n\nfinish,\nadjust,\nor maintain control.\n\n## Coaching Preference\nFRAME should not treat every front head-control exchange as an automatic guillotine attempt.\n\nPrefer:\n\ncapture\n→ assess posture\n→ assess neck access\n→ establish structure\n→ read reaction\n→ finish only when conditions remain favourable\n\nIf control is strong but the submission is weak, FRAME should preserve the advantageous control state rather than encouraging a low-quality finish.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe transition into a guillotine is a control-to-submission decision problem.\n\nThe coaching question is:\n\nHas the athlete created enough positional constraint for the submission to become the highest-value next action?\n\nFRAME should distinguish between:\n\n- head-capture failure\n- posture-control failure\n- choking-structure failure\n- angle failure\n- decision failure\n\nThe useful logic is:\n\ncapture\n→ constrain posture\n→ verify submission structure\n→ read reaction\n→ finish or preserve control\n→ evaluate outcome\n→ update coaching preference"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Gift Wrap Control Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Gift Wrap Control Schema","body":"# Gift Wrap Control Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nGift Wrap Control is an upper-body control structure in which one of the opponent's arms is carried across their own torso and secured, reducing their ability to frame, rotate or defend the back.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate upper-body asymmetry and use the trapped arm to control shoulder rotation.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Bring the opponent's arm across their torso.\n2. Secure the wrist or forearm.\n3. Maintain chest or shoulder connection.\n4. Prevent the opponent from retracting the arm.\n5. Use the trapped arm to control rotation.\n6. Progress toward Mount, Back Control or another dominant position.\n7. Maintain the control during transition.\n8. Release only after the new position is secure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The opponent's own arm becomes part of the control structure.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent turns away:\n\n- use the trapped arm to expose the back\n\nIf the opponent turns toward the passer:\n\n- maintain shoulder control and preserve the pin\n\nIf the arm begins escaping:\n\n- prioritise re-securing control before transitioning\n\nIf the back becomes available:\n\n- maintain upper-body connection during the transition\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- holding the wrist without controlling the shoulder\n- allowing the elbow to retract\n- transitioning before the opponent's rotation is controlled\n- losing chest connection\n- releasing the arm too early\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** sleeve control can reinforce the structure.\n\n**No-Gi:** wrist control, elbow positioning and chest connection become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nGift Wrap Control is an **internal asymmetry schema**.\n\nThe opponent's defensive limb is converted into a mechanism that restricts their own rotation.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Mount]]\n- [[Back Control]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]\n- [[Control Migration]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Guillotine Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Guillotine Finishing Schema","body":"# Guillotine Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nGuillotine Finishing is a front-headlock strangulation structure in which the opponent's neck is controlled with the arm while body position prevents them from relieving pressure through posture, rotation or positional escape.\n\nThe finish depends on controlling the relationship between the opponent's head, neck and torso rather than simply squeezing the neck.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate effective neck compression while preventing the opponent from restoring posture or rotating into a safer alignment.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish secure neck access.\n2. Control head position.\n3. Remove unnecessary space around the neck.\n4. Align the choking structure with the intended finishing direction.\n5. Control the opponent's ability to posture.\n6. Use torso and hip position to reinforce the choke.\n7. Maintain connection as the opponent reacts.\n8. Apply progressive finishing pressure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The neck is finished through alignment plus positional control, not isolated arm strength.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent begins restoring posture:\n\n- prioritise head control before increasing finishing pressure\n\nIf the opponent rotates:\n\n- follow the head and preserve connection\n\nIf neck access becomes shallow:\n\n- improve the grip before continuing\n\nIf the opponent's defensive movement creates a positional transition:\n\n- preserve control rather than forcing the original finish\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- squeezing from shallow neck control\n- relying primarily on arm strength\n- allowing the opponent to restore posture\n- losing body connection\n- forcing the finish after alignment has deteriorated\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** additional gripping structures may alter hand positioning and finishing pathways.\n\n**No-Gi:** direct neck control, wrist connection and body alignment become central.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nGuillotine Finishing is an **alignment-and-posture denial schema**.\n\n**Neck access → posture control → structural alignment → compression**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Front Headlock to Guillotine Schema]]\n- [[Chokes/Cranks]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Line of Attack and Line of Movement]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Half Guard Smash Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Half Guard Smash Passing Schema","body":"# Half Guard Smash Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nHalf Guard Smash Passing is a pressure passing structure used to collapse the opponent's lower-body frames, rotate or flatten the hips, and progressively free the trapped leg.\n\nThe trapped leg is not the first problem to solve.\n\nThe opponent's structure is.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nRemove the opponent's ability to maintain effective hip angle, inside position and upper-body structure before extracting the trapped leg.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the opponent controls one of the passer's legs from Half Guard\n- meaningful upper-body connection can be established\n- the opponent's knees can be compressed or redirected\n- the hips or shoulders can be restricted\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish upper-body connection.\n2. Control head and shoulder orientation.\n3. Compress the opponent's knees.\n4. Reduce effective inside position.\n5. Flatten or rotate the pelvis.\n6. Apply chest and hip pressure.\n7. Progressively free the trapped knee.\n8. Clear the remaining leg.\n9. Consolidate the new position.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Control the body before extracting the leg.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent creates upper-body space:\n\n- restore connection before progressing\n\nIf the opponent regains strong inside position:\n\n- resolve that structural problem first\n\nIf the opponent turns away:\n\n- follow the rotation while preserving pressure\n\nIf hip mobility returns:\n\n- rebuild the pin before attempting extraction\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- pulling the trapped leg out too early\n- allowing strong frames to remain active\n- losing head position\n- leaving space between the torsos\n- trying to progress before defensive structure is reduced\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** lapel, belt and trouser grips can reinforce the pin.\n\n**No-Gi:** head position, underhook structure, chest connection and pressure become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nHalf Guard Smash Passing is a **constraint accumulation process**.\n\nThe trapped leg becomes easier to free as defensive resources are progressively removed.\n\n**Connection → structural collapse → hip restriction → leg extraction → consolidation**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Half Guard]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Head Position]]\n- [[Constraint Accumulation]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Half Guard Underhook Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Half Guard Underhook Passing Schema","body":"# Half Guard Underhook Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nHalf Guard Underhook Passing is a pressure passing structure in which the passer wins upper-body inside position through an underhook and uses it to control torso rotation while freeing the trapped leg.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nPrevent the opponent from turning toward the passer, building height or accessing the passer's back while progressively clearing Half Guard.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the passer is engaged in Half Guard\n- an underhook can be established\n- the opponent's torso can be connected to\n- the passer can maintain stable head and hip position\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish the underhook.\n2. Connect the shoulder and torso to the opponent.\n3. Prevent the opponent from turning inward.\n4. Flatten or redirect the upper body.\n5. Control hip orientation.\n6. Progress the trapped knee beyond the opponent's legs.\n7. Free the lower leg.\n8. Consolidate the resulting position.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Upper-body control creates lower-body freedom.**\n\nThe trapped leg becomes easier to extract when the opponent cannot rotate effectively.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent fights for their own underhook:\n\n- prioritise the upper-body battle before leg extraction\n\nIf the opponent turns away:\n\n- follow the rotation while preserving connection\n\nIf the opponent creates distance with frames:\n\n- rebuild chest connection before progressing\n\nIf the knee becomes free:\n\n- consolidate before chasing further advancement\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- extracting the leg before controlling the torso\n- loose underhook connection\n- allowing the opponent to build onto an elbow\n- allowing the opponent's hips to rotate freely\n- abandoning head position\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** lapel and belt controls can reinforce upper-body connection.\n\n**No-Gi:** underhook depth, head placement and chest pressure become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nHalf Guard Underhook Passing is an **upper-body control to lower-body liberation sequence**.\n\n**Inside access → rotational control → knee extraction → positional transition**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Half Guard]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Head Position]]\n- [[Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Headquarters Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Headquarters Passing Schema","body":"# Headquarters Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nHeadquarters is a transitional passing configuration used to reduce the opponent's available guard options while preserving multiple passing routes.\n\nIt is not a single pass.\n\nIt is a controlled decision state from which the passer can read the opponent's reactions and select an appropriate progression.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nRestrict the opponent's hip and leg organisation while maintaining enough mobility to transition between passing directions.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nHeadquarters becomes available when:\n\n- one of the opponent's legs can be isolated or trapped\n- the passer can maintain stable posture over the hips\n- the opponent cannot freely align both knees toward the passer\n- the passer maintains access to more than one passing direction\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish stable control over one leg.\n2. Prevent the opponent from freely squaring their hips.\n3. Maintain posture and base.\n4. Monitor the free leg.\n5. Apply directional pressure.\n6. Observe the opponent's reaction.\n7. Select the passing route created by that reaction.\n8. Transition without surrendering control.\n\n## Reaction Logic\n\nIf the opponent's knee turns inward:\n\n- knee cut pathways may become available.\n\nIf the hips rotate away:\n\n- leg drag pathways may become available.\n\nIf the knees collapse together:\n\n- compression-based passing becomes stronger.\n\nIf the opponent extends away:\n\n- outside movement becomes easier.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Headquarters reduces opponent options while preserving passer options.**\n\nIts value is therefore not the position itself.\n\nIts value is the number of reliable transitions it keeps available.\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- becoming static\n- allowing both knees to square toward the passer\n- committing before the opponent's reaction is understood\n- losing posture during directional changes\n- allowing the trapped leg to recover freely\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grip persistence can make the position easier to stabilise.\n\n**No-Gi:** stance, connection and positional pressure become more important because distal grips are less persistent.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nHeadquarters is a **branching decision state**.\n\nSynochi should interpret the position through available transitions rather than as an isolated technique.\n\nThe important information is:\n\n**current control → opponent reaction → available branch → selected progression**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[Knee Cut Pass]]\n- [[Leg Drag]]\n- [[Reaction Mapping]]\n- [[Decision Point Recognition]]\n- [[Positional State Transition]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Base]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Heel Hook Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Heel Hook Finishing Schema","body":"# Heel Hook Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nHeel Hook Finishing is a rotational lower-limb submission in which the heel is controlled while the opponent's knee and hip are restricted enough that rotation transfers into the knee.\n\nBecause the structures involved are vulnerable, finishing pressure must be applied with exceptional control.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate rotational isolation of the opponent's leg while preventing the hip and knee from freely escaping the imposed rotation.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish secure leg entanglement.\n2. Control the opponent's knee line.\n3. Expose and secure the heel.\n4. Prevent uncontrolled hip rotation.\n5. Maintain connection to the trapped leg.\n6. Align the heel-control structure.\n7. Apply minimal progressive rotational pressure.\n8. Stop immediately once the finishing threshold is reached.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The heel is the handle. The knee is the target of rotational isolation.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the knee line is escaping:\n\n- restore control before applying rotation\n\nIf the opponent begins turning:\n\n- follow the leg safely rather than forcing against uncontrolled movement\n\nIf heel exposure is poor:\n\n- improve access before increasing force\n\nIf structural control is incomplete:\n\n- do not finish\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- applying rotation without knee-line control\n- uncontrolled torque\n- chasing the heel while losing the leg\n- allowing the opponent to spin freely\n- accelerating the finish unnecessarily\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** heel hooks are often restricted or prohibited depending on ruleset.\n\n**No-Gi:** heel-hook systems are substantially more common.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nHeel Hook Finishing is a **rotational isolation schema with narrow safety margins**.\n\n**Knee-line control → heel access → rotational containment → minimal finishing pressure**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/High Elbow Guillotine Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"High Elbow Guillotine Schema","body":"# High Elbow Guillotine Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nThe High Elbow Guillotine is a guillotine finishing configuration in which the choking-side elbow rises over or around the opponent's shoulder, increasing closure around the neck and making traditional defensive hand positioning more difficult.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate a tighter finishing structure by closing the space around the neck while obstructing the opponent's ability to address the choking arm.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish deep neck control.\n2. Secure the choking hand.\n3. Bring the choking-side elbow high.\n4. Close the space around the opponent's head and shoulder.\n5. Maintain head control.\n6. Prevent posture recovery.\n7. Align the torso with the finishing direction.\n8. Apply progressive compression.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The elevated elbow changes the defensive geometry.**\n\nThe opponent has less direct access to the choking structure while the neck becomes more tightly enclosed.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent attacks the choking wrist:\n\n- maintain elbow height and protect the grip\n\nIf the opponent drives forward:\n\n- preserve neck depth and adjust body angle\n\nIf the opponent begins rotating:\n\n- follow the head rather than allowing the neck to slip free\n\nIf grip depth deteriorates:\n\n- rebuild before increasing force\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- raising the elbow before establishing neck depth\n- leaving space around the neck\n- losing control of the head\n- relying on arm flexion alone\n- sacrificing position to maintain the grip\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** fabric friction may change how the opponent's head and shoulders move.\n\n**No-Gi:** the configuration is particularly valuable because the elevated elbow helps protect the choking structure without fabric grips.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nThe High Elbow Guillotine is a **defensive-access restriction schema**.\n\nThe finishing configuration improves not only compression but also the opponent's difficulty accessing the mechanism producing it.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Front Headlock to Guillotine Schema]]\n- [[Chokes/Cranks]]\n- [[Access Control]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Inside Ashi Ankle Lock Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Inside Ashi Ankle Lock Schema","body":"# Inside Ashi Ankle Lock Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nInside Ashi Ankle Lock is a straight ankle lock finishing structure in which the attacker's lower body occupies inside position around the opponent's trapped leg.\n\nThe configuration uses internal leg control to restrict movement and preserve access to the foot.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nMaintain inside lower-body control while isolating the ankle and preventing the opponent from freely withdrawing or rotating the leg.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish Inside Ashi control.\n2. Secure the opponent's knee line.\n3. Keep the trapped leg connected to the attacker's hips.\n4. Isolate the ankle.\n5. Restrict defensive rotation.\n6. Align the finishing structure.\n7. Preserve the entanglement during resistance.\n8. Apply pressure progressively.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Inside position around the leg stabilises distal control.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent turns:\n\n- track the knee and maintain the entanglement\n\nIf the knee line begins escaping:\n\n- restore proximal control before attacking the foot\n\nIf the ankle becomes exposed:\n\n- secure the finish without sacrificing hip connection\n\nIf the opponent changes entanglement state:\n\n- reassess the new position rather than forcing the old finish\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- controlling the foot while losing the knee\n- loose inside-leg connection\n- allowing free rotation\n- failing to adapt when the entanglement changes\n- overcommitting to the finish\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** trouser grips may assist retention.\n\n**No-Gi:** leg configuration and positional alignment carry most of the control.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nInside Ashi Ankle Lock is an **inside-control submission structure**.\n\n**Inside position → knee-line control → ankle isolation → finish**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Single Leg X]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Inside Position Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Inside Position Passing Schema","body":"# Inside Position Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nInside Position Passing is a passing strategy built around occupying the space between the opponent's limbs and torso before attempting positional advancement.\n\nRather than attacking the perimeter of the guard, the passer progressively wins access to the opponent's internal defensive space.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nEstablish controlling structures inside the opponent's frames so that their knees and feet can no longer maintain effective separation.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- access exists between the opponent's knees or elbows\n- the passer can establish meaningful internal control\n- the opponent's frames can be separated\n- progression can occur without sacrificing base\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Identify the opponent's strongest internal frames.\n2. Insert hands, arms, knees or torso into available inside space.\n3. Prevent the opponent from recovering equivalent inside position.\n4. Separate or redirect the defensive limbs.\n5. Connect to the hips or torso.\n6. Advance beyond the knee line.\n7. Convert inside access into pressure or positional control.\n8. Consolidate the pass.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Inside position creates access before progression.**\n\nPassing becomes easier when the opponent must defend from outside the passer's structure.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent closes the inside space:\n\n- create angle or movement before attempting re-entry\n\nIf one side becomes available:\n\n- prioritise asymmetric control rather than forcing bilateral access\n\nIf the opponent retreats their knees:\n\n- use the gained space to advance position\n\nIf the opponent establishes stronger internal frames:\n\n- resolve inside position before continuing forward\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- entering without stable base\n- reaching inside without controlling distance\n- progressing while the opponent still owns the central space\n- allowing recovered knees to replace lost frames\n- treating inside position as static rather than contested\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can reinforce internal control after access is established.\n\n**No-Gi:** wedges, limb positioning and body connection become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nInside Position Passing is an **access-control schema**.\n\nThe passer progressively acquires access to spaces the opponent requires for defensive organisation.\n\n**Access → internal control → structural disruption → progression**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Access Control]]\n- [[Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/K Guard Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"K Guard Passing Schema","body":"# K Guard Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nK Guard Passing is the process of denying the opponent the hip angle, leg access and rotational pathway required to convert K Guard into stronger attacks or leg entanglements.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nRemove the opponent's access to the passer's leg while preventing rotational progression underneath the passer.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the opponent has established or is entering K Guard\n- the passer can identify the controlling knee and foot structures\n- the passer retains enough balance to deny deeper leg access\n- the opponent has not yet completed a dominant entanglement\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Identify the opponent's primary connection to the leg.\n2. Control the distance between the opponent's hips and the trapped leg.\n3. Prevent deeper access behind the knee.\n4. Restrict the opponent's rotational direction.\n5. Remove or redirect the controlling leg.\n6. Restore the passer's knee line.\n7. Prevent re-entry.\n8. Convert into passing pressure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**K Guard becomes dangerous when access and rotation combine.**\n\nDeny either component early and the structure loses much of its progression.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent gains deeper leg access:\n\n- prioritise knee-line recovery\n\nIf the opponent begins rotating underneath:\n\n- control the rotational pathway before advancing\n\nIf the primary connection breaks:\n\n- immediately increase distance or pressure to prevent re-entry\n\nIf the opponent transitions into a leg entanglement:\n\n- treat the position as the new state rather than continuing the K Guard sequence\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- allowing the knee line to become deeply exposed\n- focusing on the foot while ignoring hip rotation\n- attempting to pass while the opponent still has deep access\n- failing to recognise transition into another leg entanglement\n- leaving re-entry space after clearing the guard\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can alter the opponent's ability to maintain upper-body connection.\n\n**No-Gi:** K Guard commonly links directly into leg-entanglement systems, making control of the knee line particularly important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nK Guard Passing is an **access-denial and rotational-control problem**.\n\n**Deny access → stop rotation → recover alignment → prevent re-entry → pass**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[K Guard]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Access Control]]\n- [[Reorientation]]\n- [[Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Kimura Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Kimura Finishing Schema","body":"# Kimura Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nKimura Finishing is a rotational shoulder-lock structure created by controlling the opponent's wrist and connecting the attacker's arms around the opponent's upper arm.\n\nThe submission depends on isolating the shoulder and preventing the torso from rotating with the trapped arm.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate rotational separation between the opponent's arm and torso while maintaining control of the elbow and shoulder.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Control the opponent's wrist.\n2. Secure the figure-four connection.\n3. Keep the elbow structurally isolated.\n4. Restrict torso rotation.\n5. Position the opponent's hand away from their defensive alignment.\n6. Create controlled internal shoulder rotation.\n7. Maintain wrist and elbow alignment.\n8. Apply pressure progressively.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The shoulder locks when the arm rotates independently of the torso.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent rotates their whole body:\n\n- control the torso before continuing\n\nIf the elbow slips position:\n\n- restore elbow control\n\nIf the opponent connects their hands defensively:\n\n- treat the grip as a new control problem rather than forcing rotation\n\nIf the shoulder becomes isolated:\n\n- apply finishing pressure gradually\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- rotating the wrist without controlling the elbow\n- allowing the torso to rotate freely\n- relying on arm strength\n- losing the figure-four connection\n- applying uncontrolled finishing pressure\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** sleeve and belt grips can affect defensive hand connections.\n\n**No-Gi:** wrist control and body positioning become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nKimura Finishing is a **rotational isolation schema**.\n\n**Wrist control → elbow isolation → torso restriction → shoulder rotation**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Line of Attack and Line of Movement]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Kimura Trap Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Kimura Trap Schema","body":"# Kimura Trap Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nThe Kimura Trap is a control and transition system built around maintaining a figure-four grip on the opponent's arm while using that attachment to control rotation and access multiple positional outcomes.\n\nThe Kimura grip is therefore used as more than a submission.\n\nIt becomes a control interface.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nMaintain ownership of the opponent's arm while using its connection to control shoulder rotation and guide positional transitions.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish the Kimura grip.\n2. Keep the opponent's elbow separated from a strong defensive position.\n3. Use the arm to influence shoulder rotation.\n4. Maintain connection during positional movement.\n5. Follow the opponent's attempts to turn or escape.\n6. Use their reaction to expose positional transitions.\n7. Preserve the grip while moving between control states.\n8. Finish or transition according to the resulting structure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The grip creates a control relationship that can survive positional change.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent turns away:\n\n- use the grip to follow and expose the back\n\nIf the opponent turns toward the attacker:\n\n- maintain shoulder control and redirect their rotation\n\nIf the arm becomes isolated:\n\n- transition toward the finish\n\nIf the positional route becomes stronger than the submission:\n\n- preserve control and take the position\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- treating the Kimura only as a submission attempt\n- losing elbow control during movement\n- holding the wrist while losing torso connection\n- forcing the finish when a stronger positional transition exists\n- releasing the grip during useful opponent movement\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** additional grips may reinforce positional control.\n\n**No-Gi:** the figure-four itself becomes a particularly valuable persistent connection.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nThe Kimura Trap is a **control-migration schema**.\n\nA single attachment can remain useful while the positional state changes around it.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Control Migration]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Back Control]]\n- [[Side Control]]\n- [[Positional State Transition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Knee Bar Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Knee Bar Finishing Schema","body":"# Knee Bar Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nKnee Bar Finishing is a lower-limb submission that isolates the opponent's leg and applies controlled extension through the knee joint.\n\nThe finish depends on stabilising the hip and controlling the leg above and below the knee.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nPrevent the opponent's hip and knee from rotating freely while applying progressive extension through the leg.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Isolate the opponent's leg.\n2. Control the knee line.\n3. Connect the opponent's thigh to the attacker's body.\n4. Secure the lower leg.\n5. Prevent hip rotation.\n6. Align the knee with the finishing direction.\n7. Extend through the hips in a controlled manner.\n8. Apply pressure progressively.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The knee must be isolated between controlled structures.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent rotates:\n\n- follow the hip and preserve knee alignment\n\nIf the knee begins slipping:\n\n- restore proximal control\n\nIf the leg bends or changes angle:\n\n- re-establish alignment before extending\n\nIf positional control deteriorates:\n\n- prioritise retention over the finish\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- attacking below the knee without controlling the thigh\n- allowing free hip rotation\n- poor knee alignment\n- explosive extension\n- losing control while chasing the finish\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** gripping can reinforce leg retention.\n\n**No-Gi:** hip connection and lower-body configuration become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nKnee Bar Finishing is a **linear joint-isolation schema**.\n\n**Hip control → knee-line isolation → alignment → extension**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Line of Attack and Line of Movement]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Knee Cut Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Knee Cut Passing Schema","body":"# Knee Cut Passing Schema\n\n## Function\nA reusable coaching schema for recognising, diagnosing, and improving knee cut passing.\n\nThis node should help FRAME decide:\n\n- whether a knee cut is actually available\n- what prerequisite is missing when it fails\n- what cue to give next\n- what reaction should trigger a branch\n- what drill would best target the failure\n\nThe value of the schema is not describing the knee cut. It is helping FRAME choose the next useful intervention.\n\n## Starting Conditions\nThe knee cut becomes viable when several conditions are present:\n\n- the passer has meaningful access to one side of the opponent’s hips\n- the opponent’s inside knee can be cleared, pinned, redirected, or bypassed\n- the passer can begin controlling upper-body rotation\n- the passer has enough balance to advance without being elevated or reversed\n\nA knee cut should not be recommended simply because the passer is standing near an open guard.\n\nFRAME should first assess whether the structure for the pass exists.\n\n## Core Sequence\nA reliable knee cut usually follows this logic:\n\n1. establish useful passing angle\n2. control or clear the inside knee\n3. win upper-body control\n4. deny the opponent’s ability to turn inward\n5. drive the cutting knee across the thigh line\n6. maintain chest and hip connection\n7. free the trailing leg\n8. stabilise top control\n\nThe exact grips can vary.\n\nThe sequence matters more than one fixed grip configuration.\n\n## Primary Mechanical Priorities\n\n### Upper Body Control\nThe passer should usually establish a meaningful connection above the hips.\n\nThis may involve:\n\n- underhook\n- crossface\n- head position\n- shoulder control\n- control of the far arm or torso\n\nIf the opponent can freely rotate their shoulders toward the passer, the knee cut becomes unstable.\n\n### Inside Knee Control\nThe opponent’s inside knee should not remain freely positioned between the bodies.\n\nFRAME should look for whether the passer has:\n\n- stapled it\n- redirected it\n- collapsed it\n- cleared past it\n\nIf the knee remains structurally useful, guard recovery remains available.\n\n### Hip Direction\nThe passer should prevent the opponent from freely rotating the pelvis underneath them.\n\nThe pass is stronger when the opponent’s shoulders and hips are prevented from rebuilding alignment.\n\n### Trailing Leg Freedom\nThe pass is not complete if the trailing leg remains trapped.\n\nFRAME should distinguish between:\n\n- successful upper-body progression\n- full pass completion\n\nThis prevents false positives in video analysis.\n\n## Decision Branches\n\n### Opponent Maintains Knee Shield\nFRAME should not simply cue “drive harder.”\n\nPossible intervention:\n\n- improve upper-body control\n- collapse the shield\n- change head position\n- redirect the hips\n- switch passing direction if the shield remains structurally strong\n\n### Opponent Wins Underhook\nRisk increases for:\n\n- dogfight\n- single leg\n- reversal\n- wrestle-up\n\nFRAME should prioritise restoring upper-body control before continuing the knee cut.\n\n### Opponent Turns Away\nThe passer may have access to:\n\n- back exposure\n- hip control\n- transition into another passing direction\n\nFRAME should recognise this as a reaction opportunity rather than forcing the original finish.\n\n### Opponent Squares Back Up\nThis usually indicates loss of:\n\n- hip control\n- upper-body connection\n- knee-line advantage\n\nFRAME should diagnose which layer was lost rather than giving a generic passing cue.\n\n### Trailing Foot Gets Trapped\nFRAME should recognise that the pass has progressed but is incomplete.\n\nIntervention should shift from entry mechanics to leg extraction and stabilisation.\n\n## Failure Diagnosis\n\n### Failure Pattern: Knee Gets Across but Opponent Recovers\nLikely causes:\n\n- weak upper-body control\n- poor hip control\n- opponent still able to rotate\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nprioritise torso control before more lower-body movement.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Knee Cannot Clear\nLikely causes:\n\n- inside knee still structurally strong\n- poor passing angle\n- weight distribution too passive\n- insufficient redirection\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nfix the entry rather than forcing the cut.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Athlete Gets Reversed\nLikely causes:\n\n- centre of gravity too far forward\n- weak base\n- opponent controls underhook or elevation\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nrestore balance and positional control before progression.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Pass Stalls in Half Guard\nLikely causes:\n\n- trailing leg trapped\n- opponent retains useful frames\n- chest-to-chest control incomplete\n\nUseful coaching preference:\ntreat the problem as a half-guard completion problem rather than restarting the entire pass.\n\n## Coaching Cues\nFRAME should prefer short, situation-specific cues such as:\n\n- “Win the upper body before you cut.”\n- “Clear the inside knee first.”\n- “Stop them turning into you.”\n- “Keep your hips connected as the knee crosses.”\n- “Your pass is there — free the trailing foot.”\n- “You lost the underhook. Fix that before driving.”\n\nAvoid giving multiple cues at once unless the athlete asks for a full breakdown.\n\n## Video Recognition\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- cutting knee travelling across the opponent’s thigh\n- inside knee being pinned, redirected, or cleared\n- underhook or equivalent upper-body control\n- passer’s head position\n- opponent’s shoulder rotation\n- hip alignment\n- whether the trailing leg remains trapped\n- transition into stable top control\n\nFRAME should avoid labelling every diagonal knee movement as a knee cut.\n\nThe surrounding control structure matters.\n\n## Drill Selection Logic\nIf FRAME detects:\n\n### Entry Failure\nUse drills around:\n- entering from open guard\n- knee-line control\n- establishing angle\n\n### Upper Body Failure\nUse drills around:\n- underhook battles\n- head position\n- crossface connection\n- preventing inward rotation\n\n### Completion Failure\nUse drills around:\n- freeing the trailing leg\n- settling chest-to-chest\n- preventing late guard recovery\n\n### Reaction Failure\nUse progressive resistance drills where the defender alternates between:\n- knee shield\n- underhook\n- turning away\n- squaring back up\n\nThe drill should target the athlete’s actual failure pattern rather than the entire technique.\n\n## Coaching Preference\nFRAME should favour the simplest intervention that changes the athlete’s outcome.\n\nDo not prescribe a full knee cut rebuild when one missing condition explains the failure.\n\nPrefer:\n\nobserve failure\n→ identify missing condition\n→ give one cue\n→ test again\n→ update\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe knee cut should be treated as a conditional sequence rather than a memorised move.\n\nThe coaching problem is:\n\nWhich prerequisite is missing right now?\n\nThe system should use this schema to move from technique recognition into targeted intervention.\n\nThe useful logic is:\n\nrecognise structure\n→ identify failure point\n→ select smallest useful correction\n→ observe response\n→ update coaching preference\n\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Knee Cut Pass]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Knee Shield Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Knee Shield Passing Schema","body":"# Knee Shield Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nKnee Shield Passing is the process of neutralising the structural barrier created by the opponent's knee between the passer and their torso.\n\nThe knee shield must first lose its ability to manage distance and angle before meaningful pressure passing can occur.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nRemove or bypass the shield while preventing the opponent from replacing it with another effective frame.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the opponent is using a knee shield from Half Guard\n- the passer can control distance around the shield\n- access exists to the opponent's upper or lower body\n- the shield can be compressed, redirected or bypassed\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Identify the orientation of the knee shield.\n2. Prevent the shield from extending effectively.\n3. Control the opponent's hip angle.\n4. Compress, redirect or bypass the knee.\n5. Close the space previously maintained by the shield.\n6. Establish upper-body connection.\n7. Progress beyond the defensive knee.\n8. Consolidate the pass.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The knee shield is primarily a distance-management structure.**\n\nDo not attack only the knee.\n\nAttack the space and angle it is protecting.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the shield is extended:\n\n- reduce its usable range before entering\n\nIf the knee collapses inward:\n\n- occupy the newly available space\n\nIf the opponent retracts the shield:\n\n- close distance before it can be rebuilt\n\nIf the opponent changes hip angle:\n\n- adjust passing direction rather than forcing the original line\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- driving directly into a structurally strong shield\n- controlling the knee without controlling the hip\n- leaving space after collapsing the shield\n- allowing the knee to return inside\n- attempting pressure before resolving distance\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** gripping can help immobilise the shield and far leg.\n\n**No-Gi:** wedges, head position and bodyweight must perform more of the control.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nKnee Shield Passing is a **barrier-resolution problem**.\n\nThe shield controls access by regulating distance.\n\nRemove its structural function and the passing environment changes immediately.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Half Guard]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Distance]]\n- [[Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Knee on Belly Control Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Knee on Belly Control Schema","body":"# Knee on Belly Control Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nKnee on Belly Control is a mobile top-control structure that concentrates pressure through the opponent's torso while preserving rapid transition options.\n\nIt combines pinning pressure with directional mobility.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate enough pressure to restrict movement while retaining the ability to follow, attack or transition quickly.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Place the knee across the opponent's torso.\n2. Maintain a stable post with the opposite leg.\n3. Control upper-body orientation.\n4. Keep weight directed through the opponent rather than floating above them.\n5. Monitor hip and shoulder movement.\n6. Adjust knee position as the opponent turns.\n7. Preserve transition access to Mount, Side Control or attacks.\n8. Avoid becoming overcommitted to the pin.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Knee on Belly is a mobile pressure state, not a static pose.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent turns toward the knee:\n\n- redirect pressure and follow the rotation\n\nIf the opponent turns away:\n\n- transition toward Mount or Side Control\n\nIf the opponent frames strongly:\n\n- remove or bypass the frame before increasing pressure\n\nIf the opponent becomes static:\n\n- use the stabilised state to attack or advance\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- placing weight on the knee without stable base\n- leaning too far forward\n- allowing the opponent to turn underneath freely\n- sacrificing mobility for pressure\n- failing to transition when the position changes\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can improve torso control.\n\n**No-Gi:** balance and dynamic weight transfer become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nKnee on Belly Control is a **pressure-mobility hybrid state**.\n\nThe value comes from maintaining control without sacrificing transition speed.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Weight Transfer]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Side Control]]\n- [[Mount]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Leg Drag Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Leg Drag Passing Schema","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Leg Drag]]\n\n## Function\nA reusable coaching schema for recognising, diagnosing, and improving leg drag passing.\n\nThis node should help FRAME decide:\n\n- whether the leg drag is structurally established\n- whether the passer has actually controlled the hip line\n- what recovery route the opponent still has available\n- what single intervention is most useful\n- whether to continue the drag, switch direction, or stabilise\n\nThe purpose is not to describe the leg drag.\n\nThe purpose is to help FRAME convert observed failure into a specific coaching response.\n\n## Starting Conditions\nA leg drag becomes viable when:\n\n- the opponent’s legs can be redirected toward one side\n- the passer can begin occupying the opposite side of the hips\n- the bottom athlete is losing the ability to square the knees back toward the passer\n- the passer can maintain enough connection to prevent immediate guard recovery\n\nFRAME should not identify a true leg drag simply because the opponent’s legs have been moved sideways.\n\nThe hip relationship must also change.\n\n## Core Sequence\nA reliable leg drag progression usually follows this logic:\n\n1. control the legs\n2. redirect them toward one side\n3. move around the opposite side\n4. occupy the space beside the hips\n5. prevent the opponent from squaring back\n6. connect to the upper body\n7. stabilise top control or exploit back exposure\n\nThe defining transition is from leg displacement into hip control.\n\n## Primary Mechanical Priorities\n\n### Leg Displacement\nThe passer must meaningfully redirect the opponent’s defensive leg structure.\n\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- whether both legs are actually displaced\n- whether the knees remain pointed away from the passer\n- whether the bottom athlete can immediately recover centre-line position\n\nIf the legs return freely, the drag has not been established.\n\n### Hip Occupation\nThis is the most important diagnostic layer.\n\nThe passer should move into the space created beside the opponent’s hips.\n\nIf the passer remains too far away, the bottom athlete can simply square back up.\n\nFRAME should strongly prioritise hip occupation over continued hand fighting.\n\n### Rotational Control\nThe bottom athlete should not be able to freely turn the pelvis and shoulders back toward the passer.\n\nFRAME should assess:\n\n- hip orientation\n- shoulder orientation\n- frame recovery\n- knee recovery\n\nLoss of rotational control usually explains late guard recovery.\n\n### Upper Body Connection\nOnce the hips are controlled, the passer should connect to the torso.\n\nThe exact control may vary, but the purpose is the same:\n\nstop the opponent rebuilding alignment.\n\n## Decision Branches\n\n### Opponent Squares Back Up\nLikely issue:\n\n- passer did not reach the hip line quickly enough\n- leg control was released too early\n- upper-body connection was delayed\n\nUseful coaching preference:\noccupy the hip space before reaching for anything else.\n\n### Opponent Turns Away\nThis may create:\n\n- back exposure\n- rear control opportunities\n- easier upper-body connection\n\nFRAME should recognise this as a useful reaction rather than automatically trying to force side control.\n\n### Opponent Frames Strongly\nLikely issue:\n\n- passer reached the hips but failed to connect to the torso\n\nUseful coaching preference:\naddress the frame before progressing.\n\n### Opponent Recovers Inside Knee Position\nLikely issue:\n\n- lower-body displacement was incomplete\n- passer created too much distance\n- hip control weakened\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nrestore hip occupation before chasing upper-body control.\n\n### Passer Controls Legs but Cannot Advance\nLikely issue:\n\n- athlete is treating the leg drag as a grip configuration rather than a positional transition\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nmove the body around the hips rather than continuing to manipulate the legs.\n\n## Failure Diagnosis\n\n### Failure Pattern: Legs Move but Guard Immediately Recovers\nLikely causes:\n\n- passer remains in front\n- hip space is not occupied\n- displacement is temporary\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nmove around the hips faster.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Opponent Squares Up Late\nLikely causes:\n\n- upper-body control missing\n- passer releases hip control too early\n- shoulder rotation remains available\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nconnect to the torso before settling.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Opponent Turns Away and Escapes\nLikely causes:\n\n- passer fails to recognise back exposure\n- rear movement is not controlled\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nfollow the reaction rather than forcing the original finish.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Passer Gets Stuck Beside the Hips\nLikely causes:\n\n- strong defensive frames\n- upper-body connection incomplete\n- opponent still has useful shoulder rotation\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nsolve the upper-body layer rather than re-dragging the legs.\n\n## Coaching Cues\nFRAME should prefer short cues such as:\n\n- “Move around the hips.”\n- “Don’t just drag the legs — occupy the space.”\n- “Stop them squaring back up.”\n- “Connect to the upper body now.”\n- “They’re turning away — take the reaction.”\n- “Keep the hip pinned before you release the legs.”\n\nAvoid explaining the full leg drag system unless the athlete asks for a full breakdown.\n\n## Video Recognition\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- both legs displaced toward one side\n- passer moving around the opposite side\n- passer reaching the hip line\n- opponent attempting to square the knees back\n- opponent turning away\n- transition from leg control into torso control\n- final stabilisation or back exposure\n\nFRAME should distinguish:\n\nleg displacement\n\nfrom\n\nan established leg drag position.\n\n## Drill Selection Logic\n\n### Displacement Failure\nUse drills around:\n- controlling both legs\n- redirecting knees\n- synchronising hand movement with footwork\n\n### Hip Occupation Failure\nUse drills around:\n- moving around the legs\n- reaching the hip line quickly\n- preventing immediate squaring\n\n### Rotational Control Failure\nUse drills around:\n- hip pinning\n- shoulder control\n- denying late recovery\n\n### Reaction Recognition Failure\nUse progressive drills where the defender alternates between:\n- squaring back\n- framing\n- turning away\n- recovering inside knee position\n\nFRAME should select the drill based on the exact failure pattern.\n\n## Coaching Preference\nFRAME should avoid treating the leg drag as a fixed finishing sequence.\n\nThe athlete should be coached according to the reaction created.\n\nPrefer:\n\ndisplace\n→ occupy\n→ read reaction\n→ control the next layer\n→ stabilise\n\nIf the opponent gives back exposure, the system should not insist on side control simply because that was the original intended pass.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe leg drag is a positional displacement problem.\n\nThe coaching question is:\n\nDid the athlete convert leg displacement into control of the opponent’s alignment?\n\nThe system should distinguish between:\n\n- failed displacement\n- failed hip occupation\n- failed rotational control\n- failed upper-body connection\n- reaction-recognition failure\n\nThe useful logic is:\n\nrecognise displacement\n→ verify hip occupation\n→ identify defensive reaction\n→ select smallest correction\n→ observe outcome\n→ update coaching preference"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Leg Pin Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Leg Pin Passing Schema","body":"# Leg Pin Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nLeg Pin Passing is a passing structure in which one or both of the opponent's legs are immobilised against the floor, the passer, or another stable structure.\n\nThe pin removes the leg's ability to return between the passer and the opponent's torso.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nConvert a mobile defensive leg into a local constraint and use that loss of mobility to cross the defensive leg line.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- a leg can be directed toward a stable surface\n- weight or structural control can be maintained over it\n- the opponent cannot immediately retract the knee\n- the passer can progress without surrendering base\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Identify the leg maintaining the current defensive line.\n2. Direct it toward a stable surface.\n3. Apply weight or a wedge to prevent recovery.\n4. Restrict pelvic rotation.\n5. Move around or across the pinned leg.\n6. Prevent the knee from returning inside.\n7. Progress beyond the leg line.\n8. Consolidate upper-body control.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**A leg that cannot reposition cannot reliably rebuild guard.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent retracts the leg:\n\n- increase pinning pressure before advancing\n\nIf the hips rotate:\n\n- follow the pelvis while preserving the pin\n\nIf another leg replaces the defensive function:\n\n- transfer control rather than forcing the original pathway\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- pinning the foot without controlling the knee\n- placing weight without maintaining base\n- leaving space for the knee to recover\n- attempting to pass before the leg is contained\n- continuing to control a limb that is no longer tactically relevant\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** cloth grips can reinforce the leg pin.\n\n**No-Gi:** wedges, bodyweight and positional trapping become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nLeg Pin Passing is a **local constraint strategy**.\n\nThe passer does not need to solve the whole guard simultaneously.\n\nRemoving one critical defensive limb can destabilise the wider structure.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[Wedges]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]\n- [[Control Migration]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Mount Control Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Mount Control Schema","body":"# Mount Control Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nMount Control is the system used to preserve top positional dominance while adapting to the opponent's attempts to bridge, frame, turn, elbow escape or recover their legs.\n\nControl is maintained through continuous adjustment rather than rigidly holding one configuration.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nKeep the opponent's hips and shoulders sufficiently controlled that meaningful escape progression cannot accumulate.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Maintain alignment above the opponent's torso.\n2. Monitor hip and shoulder movement.\n3. Adjust base according to the direction of escape force.\n4. Remove or neutralise defensive frames.\n5. Prevent knee and elbow connection.\n6. Follow controlled rotation without surrendering top position.\n7. Increase positional control as defensive options decrease.\n8. Transition toward attacks when stability permits.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Mount is maintained by controlling escape pathways, not by remaining motionless.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent bridges:\n\n- redistribute base and follow the direction of force\n\nIf the opponent turns:\n\n- adapt the mount configuration to the new orientation\n\nIf the elbows move toward the hips:\n\n- deny knee-elbow reconnection\n\nIf escape movement stops:\n\n- use the stabilised state to increase control or attack\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- maintaining a fixed base against changing force\n- sitting too high or too low without purpose\n- allowing knee-elbow connection\n- attacking before positional stability exists\n- reacting after escape progression has already accumulated\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips may provide additional methods of upper-body immobilisation.\n\n**No-Gi:** weight distribution, wedges and reactive base become proportionally more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nMount Control is a **dynamic containment system**.\n\nThe opponent generates escape vectors.\n\nThe top player continuously redistributes control to close those vectors before meaningful progression accumulates.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Mount]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Wedges]]\n- [[Weight Transfer]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Mount Maintenance Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Mount Maintenance Schema","body":"# Mount Maintenance Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nMount Maintenance is the higher-level process through which Mount is preserved across changing defensive states.\n\nWhere Mount Control describes immediate control mechanics, Mount Maintenance describes the ongoing adaptation required to remain dominant as the opponent changes escape strategy.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nPrevent isolated defensive movements from combining into a complete escape sequence.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Detect the opponent's current escape direction.\n2. Identify which control point is being challenged.\n3. Adjust base, pressure or limb positioning.\n4. Stop escape progress before it compounds.\n5. Restore structural dominance.\n6. Allow the opponent's next reaction to reveal the next control requirement.\n7. Repeat until attacking opportunities appear.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Escape prevention is cheaper than escape recovery.**\n\nA small adjustment made early can prevent a much larger positional correction later.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent begins building an escape:\n\n- address the earliest meaningful progression point\n\nIf one escape pathway is closed:\n\n- anticipate transition into another\n\nIf control becomes stable:\n\n- increase offensive pressure without sacrificing maintenance\n\nIf the opponent creates meaningful space:\n\n- restore positional integrity before attacking\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- waiting until the escape is nearly complete\n- overcommitting to stopping one escape direction\n- attacking while positional structure deteriorates\n- failing to recognise connected escape sequences\n- treating every defensive movement independently\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** persistent grips may slow escape transitions.\n\n**No-Gi:** defensive movement may occur more quickly, increasing the importance of early recognition.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nMount Maintenance is an **escape-progression suppression system**.\n\nThe relevant sequence is:\n\n**detect progression → interrupt progression → restore control → observe next direction**\n\nThe strongest maintenance occurs before the opponent's escape movements connect into a full pathway.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Mount]]\n- [[Progressive Escape]]\n- [[Escape Progress Preservation]]\n- [[Escape Direction Selection]]\n- [[Timing Windows]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Mounted Triangle Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Mounted Triangle Schema","body":"# Mounted Triangle Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nMounted Triangle is a triangle finishing structure created from top Mount in which the opponent's head and one arm are isolated inside the attacker's legs.\n\nThe position combines submission threat with continued top control.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nIsolate the head and arm while maintaining enough positional dominance that the opponent cannot use posture or gravity to relieve the strangulation.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Control the opponent's upper-body frames.\n2. Isolate one arm across the body.\n3. Position one leg around the neck.\n4. Bring the second leg into the triangle configuration.\n5. Maintain control of the opponent's head and shoulder.\n6. Adjust angle and leg position.\n7. Remove remaining space.\n8. Finish while preserving positional control.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Use Mount to build the triangle rather than abandoning Mount to chase it.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent's arm remains structurally strong:\n\n- improve isolation before committing the legs\n\nIf the opponent turns:\n\n- follow their shoulder orientation\n\nIf the triangle is structurally secure:\n\n- adjust angle before applying maximal pressure\n\nIf the finish deteriorates:\n\n- retain Mount rather than falling into a weaker position unnecessarily\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- throwing the leg over before controlling the arm\n- losing Mount during entry\n- poor shoulder positioning\n- staying square when angle adjustment is available\n- prioritising the submission over positional integrity\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can assist arm isolation and posture control.\n\n**No-Gi:** pressure, wedges and upper-body control become more important during entry.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nMounted Triangle is a **dominant-position-to-submission conversion schema**.\n\nThe submission should emerge from accumulated control rather than require sacrificing it.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Mount]]\n- [[Chokes/Cranks]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/North South Control Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"North South Control Schema","body":"# North South Control Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nNorth South Control is a top-control structure in which the passer positions their torso above or beyond the opponent's head and shoulders while controlling upper-body rotation.\n\nThe position reduces direct knee access and changes the opponent's escape geometry.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nControl the shoulders and upper torso while keeping the opponent's hips too far away to easily rebuild guard.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Move beyond the opponent's shoulder line.\n2. Keep chest connection through the upper torso.\n3. Control shoulder rotation.\n4. Prevent the opponent from turning freely underneath.\n5. Maintain base while the opponent bridges or rotates.\n6. Keep the hips outside immediate knee recovery range.\n7. Follow escape attempts without surrendering position.\n8. Transition to attacks or adjacent control positions.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**North South changes the escape problem by increasing distance between the opponent's knees and the passer's torso.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent turns onto one side:\n\n- follow the shoulder line and preserve upper-body control\n\nIf the opponent begins rotating underneath:\n\n- widen base and track the torso\n\nIf the opponent creates space near the hips:\n\n- re-establish chest connection before progressing\n\nIf the opponent exposes an arm or neck:\n\n- attack only after control remains stable\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- drifting too far away from the shoulders\n- allowing the opponent to freely rotate underneath\n- narrowing the base unnecessarily\n- attacking before positional stability exists\n- allowing the opponent to recover meaningful knee access\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can reinforce shoulder and torso control.\n\n**No-Gi:** chest pressure, head position and weight distribution become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nNorth South Control is a **geometry-shift control state**.\n\nThe position changes the opponent's available recovery pathways by moving control away from the hips and toward the shoulders.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Side Control]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Weight Transfer]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Reorientation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Outside Ashi Ankle Lock Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Outside Ashi Ankle Lock Schema","body":"# Outside Ashi Ankle Lock Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nOutside Ashi Ankle Lock is a straight ankle lock finishing structure applied from an outside-leg entanglement in which the attacker's legs control the opponent's trapped leg from the outside.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nUse lower-body connection to restrict rotational escape while maintaining reliable access to the ankle.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish Outside Ashi leg configuration.\n2. Control the opponent's knee line.\n3. Maintain hip-to-leg connection.\n4. Secure the ankle.\n5. Prevent uncontrolled rotation.\n6. Align the torso with the finishing direction.\n7. Maintain lower-body attachment.\n8. Apply progressive ankle-lock pressure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The entanglement must stabilise the leg before the finish becomes reliable.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent rotates:\n\n- preserve knee-line control\n\nIf the trapped leg begins slipping:\n\n- restore hip connection\n\nIf ankle exposure improves:\n\n- secure the finishing grip without sacrificing control\n\nIf the opponent begins clearing the entanglement:\n\n- prioritise leg retention before continuing the submission\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- loose knee-line control\n- insufficient hip connection\n- chasing the ankle while losing the entanglement\n- uncontrolled rotational pressure\n- attempting to finish from poor alignment\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** gripping options may reinforce leg retention.\n\n**No-Gi:** precise lower-body configuration becomes more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nOutside Ashi Ankle Lock is an **entanglement-to-finish conversion schema**.\n\n**Leg capture → knee-line control → ankle access → finish**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Single Leg X]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Outside Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Outside Passing Schema","body":"# Outside Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nOutside Passing is a family of passing strategies in which the passer moves around the external edge of the opponent's guard rather than remaining engaged with the strongest central leg structures.\n\nIt is a directional strategy rather than a single technique.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nMove outside the opponent's effective knee and foot orientation before the guard can rotate to face the passer.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nOutside passing becomes attractive when:\n\n- lateral movement is available\n- central connection is weak or can be disengaged\n- the passer has space to circle\n- the opponent's hips cannot instantly follow the movement\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Manage or remove direct connection.\n2. Control the nearest relevant leg.\n3. Establish lateral movement.\n4. Move beyond the opponent's knee orientation.\n5. Continue around the perimeter of the guard.\n6. Prevent hip reorientation.\n7. Close distance after clearing the legs.\n8. Consolidate control.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Do not move through a defensive structure when you can move beyond its useful orientation.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent follows quickly:\n\n- change direction or change passing route\n\nIf the opponent extends the legs:\n\n- increase lateral displacement\n\nIf the opponent retracts into a compact structure:\n\n- change range\n\nIf connection becomes strong:\n\n- resolve the connection before prioritising movement\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- circling while strongly connected\n- moving without controlling the nearest leg\n- stopping at the hip line\n- allowing the knees to continually track the passer\n- treating outside passing as speed without positional control\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** sleeve and trouser grips can either assist or restrict outside movement depending on who controls the connection.\n\n**No-Gi:** movement freedom is often greater, but persistent leg control is reduced.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nOutside Passing is an **orientation attack**.\n\nThe central contest is whether the guard can continue facing the passer.\n\n**If passer movement exceeds opponent reorientation capacity, the defensive structure degrades.**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[Toreando Passing Schema]]\n- [[Line of Attack and Line of Movement]]\n- [[Reorientation]]\n- [[Distance]]\n- [[Timing]]\n- [[Range and Distance State]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Over Under Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Over Under Passing Schema","body":"# Over Under Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nOver Under Passing is an asymmetric pressure passing structure in which one leg is controlled from underneath while the opposite leg is controlled from above.\n\nThe asymmetry creates rotational control of the opponent's pelvis.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nSplit the opponent's leg configuration and rotate their hips away from an effective guard structure.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- one arm can enter underneath a leg\n- the opposite leg can be pinned or wrapped from above\n- the passer can establish chest-to-thigh or shoulder-to-hip connection\n- the opponent's knees are no longer aligned symmetrically\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Secure one leg underneath.\n2. Control the opposite leg from above.\n3. Keep the head and shoulder connected to the opponent.\n4. Flatten or rotate the pelvis.\n5. Walk the underhooked leg away from the opponent's centre.\n6. Clear the knee line.\n7. Progress the upper body beyond the hips.\n8. Consolidate side control or another dominant position.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The pass works by controlling opposing directions at the same time.**\n\nOne leg is elevated or collected while the other is pinned.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent turns toward the underhooked side:\n\n- increase shoulder pressure\n- use the rotation to flatten the hips\n\nIf the opponent turns away:\n\n- maintain leg control and follow the pelvis\n\nIf the knee begins recovering inside:\n\n- re-establish control before advancing the torso\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- allowing the underhooked leg to become loose\n- keeping the head too far away from the opponent\n- moving the torso before controlling the pelvis\n- failing to clear the knee line completely\n- allowing the opponent to square their hips\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** trouser grips and lapel control may reinforce the asymmetric structure.\n\n**No-Gi:** elbow clamps, shoulder positioning and hip connection become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nOver Under Passing is a **pelvic asymmetry schema**.\n\nThe passer wins by preventing the opponent's legs from functioning as a coordinated pair.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Positional State Transition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Rear Body Lock Mat Return Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Rear Body Lock Mat Return Schema","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Rear Body Lock]]\n\n## Function\nA reusable coaching schema for recognising, diagnosing, and improving mat returns from rear body lock.\n\nThis node should help FRAME decide:\n\n- whether rear control is stable enough to attempt the return\n- which part of the opponent’s base is available to attack\n- whether the athlete should trip, block, lift, or redirect\n- why a mat return is failing\n- whether rear control survives the transition to the floor\n- what single correction or drill is most useful\n\nThe purpose is not simply to produce a takedown.\n\nThe mat return should preserve or improve the attacker’s control state.\n\n## Starting Conditions\nA mat return becomes viable when:\n\n- the attacker is genuinely behind the opponent\n- chest-to-back connection is established\n- the hands or arms control the opponent’s waist or torso\n- the opponent cannot freely turn to face\n- the attacker can influence the opponent’s hips and base\n\nFRAME should not recommend a mat return while rear control itself is unstable.\n\nIf the defender is already turning out, the first intervention should restore rear alignment.\n\n## Core Sequence\nA reliable rear body lock mat return generally follows this logic:\n\n1. secure rear alignment\n2. connect chest and hips\n3. identify where the opponent is carrying weight\n4. remove or redirect part of the base\n5. send the hips toward the available space\n6. follow the descent\n7. preserve rear control on landing\n8. stabilise before progressing\n\nThe exact takedown may vary.\n\nThe invariant is:\n\ncontrol first,\nbase disruption second,\nreturn third.\n\n## Primary Mechanical Priorities\n\n### Rear Alignment\nThe attacker should remain behind the opponent’s hips.\n\nIf the defender turns enough to become side-on or chest-to-chest, the mat return structure changes.\n\nFRAME should first determine whether rear position still exists.\n\n### Chest Connection\nThe attacker’s chest should remain connected to the opponent’s back.\n\nLoose torso connection allows:\n\n- turning\n- grip fighting\n- hip separation\n- balance recovery\n\nIf the hands remain locked but the chest disconnects, FRAME should diagnose a connection problem rather than a takedown-selection problem.\n\n### Hip Proximity\nThe attacker’s hips should be close enough to influence the opponent’s centre of mass.\n\nDistant hips often produce:\n\n- inefficient lifting\n- weak trips\n- excessive reliance on upper-body strength\n- easy defensive turning\n\n### Base Identification\nBefore selecting the return, FRAME should assess:\n\n- stance width\n- weight distribution\n- which leg is loaded\n- whether the defender is leaning forward or upright\n- whether one foot is already light\n\nThe mat return should exploit the current base rather than forcing a predetermined technique.\n\n## Decision Branches\n\n### Opponent Widens the Stance\nPossible response:\n\n- attack one leg rather than trying to lift the entire structure\n- redirect weight before attempting the return\n- use a trip or block that exploits the widened base\n\nFRAME should avoid cueing “lift harder.”\n\n### Opponent Leans Forward\nThis may make direct lifting inefficient but can expose:\n\n- trips\n- blocks\n- rotational returns\n- controlled descent into turtle\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nuse the forward weight rather than fighting against it.\n\n### Opponent Stands Tall\nThis may increase access to:\n\n- elevation\n- hip connection\n- lifting returns\n\nFRAME should first confirm the attacker’s hips are sufficiently close.\n\n### Opponent Peels the Hands\nThe mat return is no longer the primary problem.\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nprotect the connection before attacking the base.\n\n### Opponent Begins Turning In\nFRAME should prioritise denying rotation.\n\nDo not force a mat return while losing the defining positional advantage.\n\n### Opponent Drops to the Mat Voluntarily\nFRAME should recognise the objective has changed.\n\nThe athlete should transition from standing mat-return logic into preserving rear control during the descent.\n\n## Failure Diagnosis\n\n### Failure Pattern: Athlete Cannot Move the Opponent\nLikely causes:\n\n- hips too far away\n- chest connection weak\n- attempting to move the entire base at once\n- attacking the wrong direction\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nidentify and remove one component of the base.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Defender Keeps Turning Out\nLikely causes:\n\n- rear alignment poor\n- head or chest position weak\n- attacker prioritising the takedown over control\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nre-secure rear position before attempting another return.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Athlete Gets the Takedown but Loses the Back\nLikely causes:\n\n- attacker separates during descent\n- hands released too early\n- attacker watches the takedown rather than following the hips\n- landing position not anticipated\n\nUseful coaching preference:\ncoach the landing as part of the technique.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Athlete Relies on Strength\nLikely causes:\n\n- base not disrupted first\n- poor hip proximity\n- no reaction being created\n\nUseful coaching preference:\noff-balance before lifting or rotating.\n\n### Failure Pattern: Trip Repeatedly Misses\nLikely causes:\n\n- attacking an unloaded leg\n- timing disconnected from weight transfer\n- upper and lower body moving independently\n\nUseful coaching preference:\nwait for or create weight commitment before attacking the leg.\n\n## Coaching Cues\nFRAME should prefer concise cues such as:\n\n- “Stay behind the hips.”\n- “Bring your hips closer.”\n- “Move the base before you move the body.”\n- “Attack the leg they’re actually standing on.”\n- “Don’t lose the back to finish the takedown.”\n- “Follow them all the way to the floor.”\n- “Protect the lock before you return them.”\n\nThe cue should target the athlete’s current failure, not describe every mat return available.\n\n## Video Recognition\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- clear rear body lock or equivalent rear torso control\n- chest-to-back connection\n- attacker hip distance\n- defender stance width\n- weight shifts before the return\n- leg blocks, trips, lifts, or rotational actions\n- whether rear alignment survives the descent\n- whether the defender turns during the attempt\n- final landing relationship\n\nFRAME should distinguish:\n\nsuccessful takedown\n\nfrom\n\nsuccessful mat return with preserved control.\n\n## Drill Selection Logic\n\n### Rear Control Failure\nUse drills around:\n- maintaining chest-to-back connection\n- following hip movement\n- denying turns\n\n### Base Reading Failure\nUse drills where the defender changes:\n- stance width\n- weight distribution\n- posture\n\nThe attacker must select the appropriate return rather than perform a predetermined one.\n\n### Trip Timing Failure\nUse drills around:\n- reading loaded versus unloaded legs\n- creating weight transfer\n- synchronising upper- and lower-body action\n\n### Connection During Descent Failure\nUse controlled mat-return repetitions where success is only counted if rear control remains intact after landing.\n\n### Reaction Selection Failure\nUse progressive resistance where the defender alternates between:\n- widening stance\n- standing tall\n- leaning forward\n- peeling hands\n- dropping to the mat\n- turning inward\n\nFRAME should select drills around the observed weak decision point.\n\n## Coaching Preference\nFRAME should treat the mat return as a continuation of rear control rather than an isolated takedown.\n\nThe preferred intervention logic is:\n\nsecure rear control\n→ read base\n→ choose smallest effective disruption\n→ return\n→ preserve connection\n→ stabilise\n\nA technically completed takedown that sacrifices rear control should not automatically be interpreted as a successful execution.\n\n## Synochi Interpretation\nThe rear body lock mat return is a base-disruption problem inside an already advantageous control state.\n\nThe coaching question is:\n\nHow can the athlete move the opponent to the floor without surrendering the positional advantage they already own?\n\nFRAME should distinguish between:\n\n- rear-control failure\n- base-reading failure\n- weight-transfer failure\n- mat-return execution failure\n- landing-control failure\n\nThe useful logic is:\n\nrecognise control state\n→ read base\n→ select appropriate return\n→ preserve control through transition\n→ evaluate outcome\n→ update coaching preference"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Rear Naked Choke Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Rear Naked Choke Finishing Schema","body":"# Rear Naked Choke Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nRear Naked Choke Finishing is a strangulation structure applied from behind the opponent using the attacker's arm around the neck while controlling the opponent's ability to hand fight, rotate or create neck space.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nEstablish deep neck access, remove defensive hand interference and apply bilateral neck compression.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Maintain Back Control.\n2. Win access beneath the opponent's chin where required.\n3. Position the choking arm deeply around the neck.\n4. Control or bypass defensive hands.\n5. Hide and protect the finishing hand.\n6. Connect the arms into a stable finishing structure.\n7. Remove space around the neck.\n8. Apply progressive compression while maintaining chest-to-back connection.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Control creates the choke.**\n\nThe finish becomes easier when defensive hand access and rotational freedom have already been reduced.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent protects the neck:\n\n- prioritise hand control and positional dominance\n\nIf the chin is tucked:\n\n- improve access rather than forcing the finish through the jaw\n\nIf the opponent rotates:\n\n- maintain Back Control before re-entering the choke\n\nIf the finishing arm becomes deep:\n\n- protect the hand and close remaining space\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- sacrificing Back Control to chase the neck\n- exposing the finishing hand\n- leaving space around the neck\n- attempting to force access without controlling the hands\n- squeezing from poor alignment\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** collar chokes may create alternative pathways.\n\n**No-Gi:** the Rear Naked Choke becomes one of the primary finishing structures from Back Control.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nRear Naked Choke Finishing is an **access-to-compression sequence**.\n\n**Back control → hand control → neck access → structural closure → finish**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Back Control]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Access Control]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Saddle Entry Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Saddle Entry Schema","body":"# Saddle Entry Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nSaddle Entry is the process of transitioning into a leg entanglement that controls one of the opponent's legs while positioning the attacker's lower body inside and across the opponent's hip line.\n\nThe entry is successful only when control persists after the positional transition.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nGain dominant access to the opponent's leg while restricting their ability to immediately withdraw, rotate or counter-entangle.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Create access to the opponent's leg.\n2. Insert the legs into the intended entanglement.\n3. Capture the knee line.\n4. Control the opponent's hip orientation.\n5. Connect the trapped leg to the attacker's pelvis.\n6. Prevent immediate rotational escape.\n7. Stabilise the entanglement.\n8. Transition into attacks or further control.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**An entry is incomplete until the resulting entanglement is stable.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf knee-line access is shallow:\n\n- deepen control before attacking\n\nIf the opponent rotates during entry:\n\n- follow the hip and preserve leg connection\n\nIf the opponent withdraws the knee:\n\n- prioritise retention over submission hunting\n\nIf control stabilises:\n\n- begin attack sequencing\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- entering without knee-line control\n- prioritising the heel before stabilising the position\n- losing hip connection\n- allowing the opponent to freely rotate out\n- treating entry and control as separate events\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** rules often restrict available follow-up attacks.\n\n**No-Gi:** Saddle frequently functions as a central leg-attack platform.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nSaddle Entry is a **state-acquisition schema**.\n\n**Access → insertion → knee-line capture → hip control → stabilised entanglement**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Access Control]]\n- [[Positional State Transition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Side Control Maintenance Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Side Control Maintenance Schema","body":"# Side Control Maintenance Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nSide Control Maintenance is the process of preserving dominant top control while the opponent attempts to recover frames, turn onto their side, connect knees to elbows, or create enough space to re-guard.\n\nThe objective is not to remain fixed.\n\nIt is to keep the opponent from rebuilding functional structure.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nSuppress the opponent's ability to create space and connect upper-body movement to lower-body recovery.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Control head and shoulder orientation.\n2. Monitor the near-side elbow and hip.\n3. Maintain chest connection.\n4. Use wedges to prevent structural recovery.\n5. Adjust weight as the opponent changes escape direction.\n6. Block knee-elbow connection.\n7. Follow rotation without surrendering top position.\n8. Re-establish stable control before attacking.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Side Control is maintained by denying reconnection between the opponent's limbs and torso.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent frames strongly:\n\n- redirect pressure and remove the frame before progressing\n\nIf the opponent turns toward the passer:\n\n- control the near-side shoulder and hip\n\nIf the opponent turns away:\n\n- follow the rotation and preserve chest connection\n\nIf the knees begin recovering inside:\n\n- prioritise hip control and block knee-elbow connection\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- remaining static against changing escape force\n- losing chest connection\n- attacking while frames are still structurally active\n- allowing the near-side elbow to reconnect to the hip\n- allowing the opponent to rotate freely underneath\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can increase control persistence.\n\n**No-Gi:** chest pressure, wedges, head position and weight transfer become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nSide Control Maintenance is a **reconnection denial problem**.\n\nThe opponent escapes by rebuilding structure.\n\nThe top player maintains control by preventing those structures from reconnecting.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Side Control]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Wedges]]\n- [[Weight Transfer]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Single Leg X Entry Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Single Leg X Entry Schema","body":"# Single Leg X Entry Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nSingle Leg X Entry is the process of gaining inside access to one of the opponent's legs and organising the body into Single Leg X before the opponent can withdraw or square their base.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nIsolate one leg, connect the opponent's hip to the attacker's lower body and establish a stable off-balancing platform.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Create access to one leg.\n2. Establish inside position.\n3. Insert the hips beneath or beside the opponent's base.\n4. Connect to the trapped leg.\n5. Position the feet into the Single Leg X structure.\n6. Control distance.\n7. Prevent the opponent from immediately withdrawing the leg.\n8. Begin off-balancing or attack progression.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The entry succeeds when access becomes connection.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent steps away:\n\n- follow the leg before completing the configuration\n\nIf the knee line remains distant:\n\n- improve hip position\n\nIf inside access is blocked:\n\n- create angle before forcing entry\n\nIf the structure stabilises:\n\n- move into off-balancing or attack sequences\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- reaching for the leg without hip access\n- entering too far from the opponent's base\n- failing to control distance\n- loose leg configuration\n- allowing immediate withdrawal\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can assist entry and retention.\n\n**No-Gi:** timing, hip position and leg connection become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nSingle Leg X Entry is an **access-to-connection transition**.\n\n**Inside access → hip positioning → leg capture → stabilised Single Leg X**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Single Leg X]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Access Control]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Single Leg X Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Single Leg X Passing Schema","body":"# Single Leg X Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nSingle Leg X Passing is the process of dismantling the opponent's control around one isolated leg before crossing the defensive leg line.\n\nThe passer must restore alignment and remove the opponent's control of the trapped leg before attempting unrestricted movement.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nRecover ownership of the trapped leg while preventing the opponent from maintaining effective control of distance, balance and rotation.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the opponent has established Single Leg X\n- one leg is isolated\n- the passer can maintain or recover balance\n- the opponent's controlling feet and knees can be addressed\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Stabilise posture and base.\n2. Identify the opponent's primary controlling structures.\n3. Prevent further off-balancing.\n4. Remove or neutralise the strongest control.\n5. Restore alignment of the trapped knee and hip.\n6. Clear the leg from the entanglement.\n7. Prevent immediate re-entry.\n8. Convert directly into passing pressure where possible.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Escape the control structure before attempting to pass the guard around it.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf balance is compromised:\n\n- prioritise base before stripping controls\n\nIf the opponent extends the trapped leg:\n\n- restore knee and hip alignment\n\nIf a control is removed:\n\n- occupy the resulting space before it is replaced\n\nIf the entanglement transitions:\n\n- reassess the new positional state rather than continuing the old sequence\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- attempting to step away while structurally trapped\n- ignoring balance disruption\n- stripping controls without preventing replacement\n- leaving the knee line exposed\n- clearing the leg but failing to prevent re-entry\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can increase the opponent's ability to maintain the position.\n\n**No-Gi:** control is more dependent on leg configuration and body alignment.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nSingle Leg X Passing is a **control-removal sequence**.\n\n**Stabilise → identify dominant control → remove constraint → restore alignment → pass**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Single Leg X]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Straight Ankle Lock Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Straight Ankle Lock Finishing Schema","body":"# Straight Ankle Lock Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nStraight Ankle Lock Finishing is a lower-limb submission structure that immobilises the opponent's leg while applying controlled pressure through the ankle and foot.\n\nThe finish depends on controlling the leg line and preventing the opponent from freely rotating, retracting or relieving pressure.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate a stable lower-body control structure in which the opponent's ankle is isolated and pressure can be applied without losing positional control.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Secure the opponent's leg.\n2. Control the knee line.\n3. Position the attacking forearm around the ankle.\n4. Remove unnecessary space around the foot.\n5. Stabilise the opponent's ability to rotate.\n6. Align the attacker's hips and torso with the finishing direction.\n7. Maintain leg control while extending through the finish.\n8. Apply pressure progressively.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Control the leg before attacking the ankle.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent rotates:\n\n- maintain knee-line control and follow the movement\n\nIf the foot begins slipping:\n\n- improve ankle connection before increasing pressure\n\nIf the opponent retracts the leg:\n\n- restore positional control before continuing\n\nIf control is stable:\n\n- apply the finish gradually\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- attacking the foot without controlling the knee\n- leaving space around the ankle\n- poor hip alignment\n- allowing free rotational escape\n- applying force before the leg is stabilised\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** trouser grips may reinforce leg control.\n\n**No-Gi:** body positioning and leg entanglement become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nStraight Ankle Lock Finishing is a **distal finish supported by proximal control**.\n\n**Knee-line control → ankle isolation → alignment → progressive pressure**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Single Leg X]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Toe Hold Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Toe Hold Finishing Schema","body":"# Toe Hold Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nToe Hold Finishing is a rotational foot-lock structure in which the attacker controls the opponent's foot and applies combined plantar flexion and rotation while the leg is stabilised.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nPrevent the opponent's leg from freely rotating while creating controlled rotational pressure through the foot and ankle.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Secure the opponent's foot.\n2. Establish figure-four hand control.\n3. Control the opponent's knee line or hip.\n4. Prevent the leg from freely rotating with the foot.\n5. Align the foot with the intended rotational direction.\n6. Maintain close connection.\n7. Apply controlled rotational pressure.\n8. Release immediately at the finishing threshold.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Foot rotation becomes meaningful only when the rest of the leg cannot freely follow it.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent's knee rotates with the foot:\n\n- improve leg control before continuing\n\nIf grip security deteriorates:\n\n- reset before applying pressure\n\nIf the opponent spins:\n\n- maintain positional safety rather than forcing the submission\n\nIf the leg becomes stable:\n\n- apply pressure progressively\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- twisting the foot without controlling the leg\n- uncontrolled rotational force\n- poor grip positioning\n- allowing the knee to follow freely\n- continuing after positional control has failed\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\nRuleset availability varies significantly in both Gi and No-Gi competition.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nToe Hold Finishing is a **distal rotation with proximal containment schema**.\n\n**Leg control → foot isolation → rotational restriction → finish**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Leg Entanglements]]\n- [[Control Before Progression]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Toreando Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Toreando Passing Schema","body":"# Toreando Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nToreando Passing is an outside passing structure in which the passer redirects the opponent's legs while moving laterally around the guard.\n\nThe technique separates leg orientation from hip orientation.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nMove beyond the opponent's effective leg line before they can reorient the hips and knees toward the passer.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the legs can be controlled from outside\n- lateral movement is available\n- the opponent's feet are not maintaining dominant connection\n- the knees can be redirected away from the intended movement path\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Establish control over the legs.\n2. Create useful extension.\n3. Redirect the knees laterally.\n4. Move the passer's body in the opposite direction.\n5. Stay outside the effective leg line.\n6. Prevent the hips from re-squaring.\n7. Close distance once the legs are cleared.\n8. Consolidate control.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The hands redirect. The feet complete the pass.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent follows with the hips:\n\n- continue lateral movement\n\nIf the opponent retracts the knees:\n\n- close distance and change passing range\n\nIf the legs collapse together:\n\n- transition toward compression-based passing\n\nIf the opponent establishes strong connection:\n\n- resolve the connection before continuing to circle\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- moving the legs without moving the feet\n- stopping after clearing the feet\n- allowing the hips to re-square\n- reaching from excessive distance\n- trying to circle while strongly connected\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** trouser grips provide persistent distal control.\n\n**No-Gi:** control is more transient, making timing and movement speed more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nToreando Passing is a **reorientation race**.\n\n**Passer lateral movement speed vs opponent hip reorientation speed.**\n\nThe pass appears when the passer moves outside the opponent's effective defensive orientation.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Passing]]\n- [[Reorientation]]\n- [[Line of Attack and Line of Movement]]\n- [[Distance]]\n- [[Timing]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Positional State Transition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Triangle Finishing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Triangle Finishing Schema","body":"# Triangle Finishing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nTriangle Finishing is a strangulation structure in which the attacker's legs surround the opponent's neck and one arm, using the opponent's shoulder to complete bilateral compression.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nAlign the opponent's neck and trapped shoulder inside a closed leg structure while eliminating unnecessary internal space.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Secure the opponent's head and one arm inside the leg configuration.\n2. Control posture.\n3. Position the trapped shoulder toward the neck.\n4. Adjust body angle.\n5. Close the leg structure efficiently.\n6. Remove internal space.\n7. Prevent the opponent from regaining posture or rotating free.\n8. Apply progressive finishing pressure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Angle and space removal matter more than simply squeezing the knees together.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent's shoulder is not entering the neck:\n\n- change angle before increasing pressure\n\nIf posture begins recovering:\n\n- restore head control\n\nIf excessive space exists inside the triangle:\n\n- tighten the configuration before finishing\n\nIf the opponent stacks forward:\n\n- manage angle and position before continuing the finish\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- remaining square when angle is required\n- squeezing with poor leg configuration\n- failing to control posture\n- allowing the trapped arm to move into a safer position\n- attempting to finish with excessive internal space\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\nThe underlying strangulation mechanics remain similar.\n\n**Gi:** grips can improve posture control.\n\n**No-Gi:** head control, leg positioning and angle become more mechanically explicit.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nTriangle Finishing is an **angle-dependent enclosure schema**.\n\n**Posture control → shoulder integration → angle creation → enclosure → compression**\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Chokes/Cranks]]\n- [[Close Guard]]\n- [[Open Guard]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Line of Attack and Line of Movement]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Turtle Breakdown Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Turtle Breakdown Schema","body":"# Turtle Breakdown Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nTurtle Breakdown is the process of converting a compact defensive Turtle into a structure that exposes the opponent's hips, shoulders, back or limbs.\n\nThe objective is not simply to push the opponent flat.\n\nIt is to remove the structural relationships that make Turtle stable.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate asymmetry in the opponent's base and expose a controllable pathway to Back Control, Side Control or another dominant position.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Identify the opponent's strongest posts and connections.\n2. Control the hips or upper body.\n3. Remove one defensive support.\n4. Create rotational or directional imbalance.\n5. Prevent the opponent from rebuilding their base.\n6. Follow the collapse or turn.\n7. Insert controlling attachments.\n8. Consolidate the resulting position.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Turtle breaks when its support structure becomes asymmetric.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent maintains strong elbow-knee connection:\n\n- attack the support structure rather than forcing rotation\n\nIf one hip becomes exposed:\n\n- control it before advancing\n\nIf the opponent rolls:\n\n- follow the rotation and preserve connection\n\nIf the opponent stands:\n\n- transition into the appropriate rear-control or mat-return structure\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- pushing without removing structural supports\n- allowing the opponent to rebuild base\n- losing hip connection during rotation\n- chasing the back before establishing control\n- using force without directional imbalance\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can provide strong handles for directional control.\n\n**No-Gi:** wrist rides, hip control and body connection become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nTurtle Breakdown is a **support-removal problem**.\n\nThe objective is to identify which structural support currently stabilises the opponent and remove it.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Turtle]]\n- [[Back Control]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]\n- [[Reorientation]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/Wrist Ride Breakdown Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"Wrist Ride Breakdown Schema","body":"# Wrist Ride Breakdown Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nWrist Ride Breakdown is a control structure used primarily against Turtle or partially seated opponents in which wrist control is used to distort posture, deny posting and create rotational access.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nUse control of the wrist and arm to remove a key defensive post and expose the opponent's torso or back.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Secure wrist control.\n2. Keep the controlled arm separated from effective posting position.\n3. Apply directional pressure through the shoulder line.\n4. Control the hips or torso with the opposite side.\n5. Create rotation.\n6. Prevent the wrist from returning underneath the opponent.\n7. Follow the resulting opening.\n8. Transition into Back Control, Side Control or another dominant structure.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**The wrist is valuable because it controls a post.**\n\nRemoving the post changes the opponent's whole defensive structure.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent retracts the elbow:\n\n- maintain wrist control and follow the shoulder\n\nIf the opponent posts with the opposite arm:\n\n- redirect rotational pressure\n\nIf the opponent begins standing:\n\n- maintain rear control and transition accordingly\n\nIf the wrist becomes inaccessible:\n\n- move to another controlling structure rather than forcing it\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- controlling the wrist without controlling the shoulder\n- allowing the arm to return underneath the opponent\n- applying force without directional purpose\n- ignoring hip movement\n- becoming attached to the wrist after the position changes\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** sleeve grips can increase persistence.\n\n**No-Gi:** mechanical wrist control and elbow positioning become more important.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nWrist Ride Breakdown is a **post-removal schema**.\n\nOne controlled limb can alter the stability of the entire defensive system.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Wrist Rides]]\n- [[Turtle]]\n- [[Constraint Creation]]\n- [[Control Migration]]\n- [[Back Control]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/X Guard Entry Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"X Guard Entry Schema","body":"# X Guard Entry Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nX Guard Entry is the process of moving underneath the opponent's base and establishing opposing hook structures that control both the near and far sides of their stance.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nGain enough lower-body connection to manipulate the opponent's centre of mass and disrupt stable standing alignment.\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Create access beneath the opponent's base.\n2. Establish connection to one leg.\n3. Move the hips underneath the opponent.\n4. Insert the secondary hook.\n5. Organise the legs into the X Guard structure.\n6. Control the opponent's distance.\n7. Prevent immediate disengagement.\n8. Begin off-balancing or transition.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**X Guard requires depth beneath the opponent, not just contact with the legs.**\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent retreats:\n\n- maintain connection and follow the base\n\nIf one hook cannot be established:\n\n- stabilise the first connection before progressing\n\nIf the opponent's balance breaks:\n\n- transition immediately into the appropriate sweep or attack\n\nIf the opponent changes stance:\n\n- reorganise the hook configuration rather than forcing the original geometry\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- entering without sufficient hip depth\n- weak connection to the opponent's leg\n- allowing excessive distance\n- establishing hooks without controlling the base\n- failing to act after off-balancing occurs\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** grips can reinforce control of posture and distance.\n\n**No-Gi:** leg connection and body position carry more of the control burden.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nX Guard Entry is a **base-access schema**.\n\nThe entry converts proximity beneath the opponent into structural control over their balance.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[X Guard]]\n- [[Single Leg X]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Access Control]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Technique Schemas/X Guard Passing Schema.md","folder":"Technique Schemas","title":"X Guard Passing Schema","body":"# X Guard Passing Schema\n\n## Definition\n\nX Guard Passing is the process of dismantling the opponent's ability to control the passer's base through crossed hook structures underneath the hips.\n\nThe passer must restore weight distribution and remove hook influence before attempting positional advancement.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nReclaim control of base and leg alignment while progressively reducing the opponent's ability to elevate, rotate or extend the passer.\n\n## Entry Conditions\n\nThe schema becomes available when:\n\n- the opponent has established X Guard\n- the passer remains capable of maintaining or recovering posture\n- individual hooks can be identified\n- the opponent's control can be progressively dismantled\n\n## Core Mechanics\n\n1. Stabilise posture.\n2. Identify which hook is currently carrying the greatest control.\n3. Reduce the opponent's ability to manipulate the base.\n4. Remove one controlling structure.\n5. Prevent immediate replacement.\n6. Restore the trapped leg's alignment.\n7. Clear the remaining hooks.\n8. Convert directly into passing position.\n\n## Key Principle\n\n**Do not treat both hooks as equally important at every moment.**\n\nIdentify the structure currently controlling the system and remove it first.\n\n## Decision Rules\n\nIf the opponent begins elevating:\n\n- prioritise base and weight distribution\n\nIf one hook becomes weak:\n\n- remove it and immediately occupy the resulting space\n\nIf the opponent transitions toward Single Leg X:\n\n- recognise the positional transition and change the passing response\n\nIf the legs become disconnected:\n\n- advance before the guard reorganises\n\n## Failure Modes\n\n- attempting to jump over an intact X Guard\n- stripping random controls without priority\n- allowing removed hooks to return\n- leaning into the opponent's strongest elevation direction\n- failing to convert escape into passing pressure\n\n## Gi / No-Gi Distinction\n\n**Gi:** trouser and sleeve grips can increase persistence of the guard.\n\n**No-Gi:** hook configuration and positional alignment account for more of the control.\n\n## Synochi Read\n\nX Guard Passing is a **base-restoration problem**.\n\nThe guard is effective because it distributes control across the passer's legs and centre.\n\nThe passer reverses this process by removing control sequentially.\n\n## Backlinks\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[X Guard]]\n- [[Single Leg X]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Passing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Alignment.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Alignment","body":"# Alignment\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nAlignment is the organisation of body segments, posture, base, and force direction into a structure that can efficiently transmit, resist, or redirect force.\n\nGood alignment allows the body to act as a connected system.\n\nPoor alignment causes force to leak through weak angles, disconnected joints, unstable posture, or compromised base.\n\n## Core Principle\nStructure determines how much of an athlete’s force can actually reach the target.\n\n**Position → Alignment → Force transfer → Mechanical effect**\n\nMore effort does not compensate efficiently for poor alignment.\n\n## Structural Alignment\nStructural alignment describes how the body is arranged relative to the force being produced or resisted.\n\nKey relationships may include:\n\n- head over spine\n- shoulders connected to torso\n- hips under or behind force direction\n- knees aligned with base\n- elbows connected to ribs\n- feet positioned to support intended movement\n\nAlignment is task-dependent.\n\nThe same posture may be well aligned for one action and poorly aligned for another.\n\n## Force-Line Alignment\nForce is most efficiently transferred when body structure supports the intended direction.\n\nExamples:\n\n- driving through opponent with hips and shoulders connected\n- framing with skeletal structure rather than isolated arm strength\n- finishing a takedown with posture aligned behind the direction of movement\n- applying pressure through chest, shoulder, and base as one chain\n\n## Alignment and Base\nAlignment requires a base capable of receiving and transmitting force.\n\nIf the force line falls outside the athlete’s base, the athlete may:\n\n- lose balance\n- require a post\n- become light on a limb\n- redirect effort into stabilisation\n\nBase and alignment therefore operate as a coupled system.\n\n## Alignment and Connection\nConnection determines whether aligned structures can transmit force between athletes.\n\nStrong alignment without effective connection may produce little external effect.\n\nStrong connection with poor alignment may create high muscular demand.\n\nEffective control often requires both.\n\n## Local Alignment\nAlignment can also exist at individual joints.\n\nExamples:\n\n- wrist stacked behind forearm\n- elbow positioned beneath shoulder\n- knee tracking with hip and foot\n- spine organised behind frame\n\nPoor local alignment may reduce force capacity or expose the joint to mechanical stress.\n\n## Global Alignment\nGlobal alignment refers to how the entire body coordinates around a task.\n\nExample:\n\nDuring pressure passing, the athlete may connect:\n\nfoot  \n→ leg  \n→ hip  \n→ torso  \n→ shoulder  \n→ opponent\n\nIf one segment becomes disconnected, pressure efficiency decreases.\n\n## Alignment Breaking\nOne athlete can intentionally disrupt the other’s alignment.\n\nExamples:\n\n- turning the head away from the hips\n- separating elbow from torso\n- twisting shoulders against pelvis\n- forcing weight beyond the base\n- folding posture\n\nBreaking alignment often reduces the opponent’s ability to generate force even before positional control is achieved.\n\n## Alignment Restoration\nDefence frequently begins by restoring structure.\n\nExamples:\n\n- reconnect elbow to knee\n- bring head back over hips\n- rebuild spinal posture\n- recover stance width\n- stack joints behind frame\n\nRestoring alignment can reopen movement options without immediately changing position.\n\n## Alignment and Strength\nStrength is expressed through structure.\n\nPoor alignment can make a strong athlete functionally weak.\n\nGood alignment can allow a smaller athlete to resist significant force through skeletal support and mechanical organisation.\n\n## Alignment and Fatigue\nPoor alignment often increases muscular demand.\n\nIf structure cannot carry the load, muscles must compensate.\n\nThis can accelerate fatigue during:\n\n- clinching\n- pin defence\n- guard retention\n- wrestling exchanges\n- prolonged pressure\n\n## Dynamic Alignment\nAlignment is not static posture.\n\nIt changes continuously during movement.\n\nEffective athletes reorganise structure while:\n\n- changing angle\n- transferring weight\n- attacking\n- defending\n- transitioning\n\nSynochi should therefore evaluate whether alignment remains functional through motion, not only in isolated frames.\n\n## Alignment Failure\nCommon alignment failures include:\n\n- head disconnected from hips\n- hips too far behind force\n- elbow flared from torso\n- knee collapsing inward\n- spine rotated under load\n- shoulder separated from support structure\n- base positioned away from intended force direction\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You need to push harder.\"\n\nAlignment-aware analysis:\n\n\"Your shoulder is making contact, but your hips are behind your base and your torso is rotated away from the pressure line. Most of your effort is stabilising yourself rather than transferring into him.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nAlignment helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why force is leaking\n- why frames collapse\n- why pressure feels weak\n- why an athlete becomes easy to off-balance\n- why muscular effort is excessive\n- how posture and base interact\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about alignment through:\n\n**Identify intended force → Observe body structure → Assess segment connection → Compare force line with base → Detect structural leakage → Estimate efficiency → Observe outcome**\n\nAlignment should act as a mechanical bridge between posture, base, leverage, pressure, and connection.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Posture]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Connection]]\n[[Leverage]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Weight Transfer]]\n[[Angle]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Anchoring.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Anchoring","body":"# Anchoring\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nAnchoring is the use of a stable structural connection to create a reliable point from which force, movement, control, or redirection can be produced.\n\nAn anchor gives the athlete something mechanically stable to work from.\n\nWithout an anchor, effort may dissipate because the athlete is moving themselves rather than meaningfully affecting the opponent.\n\n## Core Principle\nMovement becomes more effective when force is organised around a stable reference point.\n\n**Anchor → Structure → Force transfer → Mechanical effect**\n\n## Why It Matters\nAnchors appear throughout combat.\n\nExamples:\n\n- foot planted into the mat during a takedown finish\n- elbow connected to the floor during a technical stand-up\n- shoulder pressure connected through a stable base\n- grip anchored to the opponent’s torso\n- head position used as a structural post in clinch exchanges\n- hook connected around a hip during back control\n\nAnchoring often makes techniques feel stronger without requiring more muscular effort.\n\n## Anchor Types\n\n### Ground Anchor\nA contact point with the floor that stabilises movement.\n\nExamples:\n\n- planted foot\n- knee\n- elbow\n- hand\n\n### Opponent Anchor\nA stable connection to the opponent.\n\nExamples:\n\n- body lock\n- underhook\n- wrist control\n- seatbelt\n- hook\n- collar tie\n\n### Boundary Anchor\nUse of the cage, wall, or edge as a structural reference.\n\n### Internal Anchor\nA body segment stabilised against another part of the athlete’s own structure.\n\nExamples:\n\n- elbow connected to ribs\n- forearm braced against hip\n- knee connected to torso\n\n## Functional Role\n\nAnchors can support:\n\n- pulling\n- pushing\n- rotation\n- elevation\n- pressure\n- posture control\n- directional change\n- positional retention\n\n## Anchoring and Force Transfer\nForce requires somewhere to travel.\n\nIf an athlete attempts to pull without a stable anchor, the body may simply move toward the opponent.\n\nIf they anchor through base or connection, the same pull can move the opponent instead.\n\n## Anchoring and Connection\nConnection alone does not guarantee control.\n\nA connection becomes more powerful when anchored into a stable structure.\n\nExample:\n\nA loose underhook creates contact.\n\nAn underhook connected through shoulder, torso, hip, and base creates meaningful control.\n\n## Anchoring and Base\nBase often provides the primary anchor for force generation.\n\nHowever, the opponent can also act as part of the athlete’s base.\n\nIn grappling, the two bodies frequently form one connected mechanical system.\n\n## Dynamic Anchoring\nAnchors do not need to remain fixed.\n\nEffective athletes can transfer anchoring points during movement.\n\nExample:\n\nfoot anchor  \n→ knee anchor  \n→ hip connection  \n→ shoulder pressure\n\nThe anchor changes while the force pathway remains functional.\n\n## Anchor Transfer\nAnchor transfer allows continuous movement without losing structural control.\n\nSynochi should recognise sequences where one stable point replaces another.\n\nThis is especially important in:\n\n- passing\n- scrambling\n- wall wrestling\n- rides\n- transitions\n- technical stand-ups\n\n## Anchoring and Rotation\nRotational movements often require one point to remain relatively stable while another segment moves around it.\n\nExamples:\n\n- pivoting around planted foot\n- rotating hips around locked upper-body connection\n- turning opponent around controlled head position\n\nThe anchor creates the axis around which motion becomes effective.\n\n## Weak Anchoring\nAnchoring becomes ineffective when:\n\n- base is unstable\n- connection slips\n- joint alignment is weak\n- anchor point is too mobile\n- force direction does not pass through the anchor\n- opponent removes the supporting structure\n\n## Anchor Disruption\nRemoving an opponent’s anchor can collapse their mechanical advantage.\n\nExamples:\n\n- lifting a planted foot\n- breaking grip connection\n- removing head position\n- forcing elbow away from torso\n- redirecting a post\n\nThis can create immediate opportunities.\n\n## Anchoring vs Posting\nA post is usually a temporary support point used to maintain balance or structure.\n\nAn anchor is broader.\n\nIt can support force production, movement, rotation, or control even when balance is not threatened.\n\nA post can function as an anchor, but not every anchor is a post.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"Your pull was too weak.\"\n\nAnchor-aware analysis:\n\n\"You had the grip, but nothing was anchoring the pull into your base. Your body moved toward him instead of transferring force through him.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nAnchoring helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why grips feel weak or strong\n- why pressure disappears during transitions\n- why certain rotational movements fail\n- why athletes lose control while moving\n- how force should connect back into base\n- where to disrupt opponent structure\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about anchoring through:\n\n**Detect stable contact → Assess structural connection → Identify force pathway → Determine anchor role → Observe transfer or disruption → Evaluate mechanical effect**\n\nAnchoring should interact with connection, base, alignment, leverage, pressure, and weight transfer.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Connection]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Alignment]]\n[[Leverage]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Weight Transfer]]\n[[Posts]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Angle.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Angle","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nAngle is the directional relationship between two athletes that changes what each system can access, defend, see, and influence.\n\nA small angular change can alter the whole exchange.\n\nIt can:\n\n- shorten one athlete’s path to the target,\n- lengthen the opponent’s return path,\n- expose structure,\n- remove defensive alignment,\n- improve leverage,\n- and change which reactions are available.\n\nAngle therefore acts as a form of **positional reorientation**.\n\nThe athlete is not only asking:\n\nHow far away am I?\n\nbut:\n\nFrom which direction am I interacting with their structure?\n\n## **Angle and Access**\n\nDirectly facing an opponent often means interacting with their strongest available structure.\n\nChanging angle can create access around that structure.\n\nFor example, an angular shift may:\n\n- open a striking lane,\n- expose a flank,\n- improve a takedown finish,\n- bypass a frame,\n- weaken a defensive line,\n- or create a better path to positional control.\n\nThe value of the angle comes from changing the opponent’s available response architecture.\n\n## **Directionality**\n\nAngle changes the direction through which force enters the system.\n\nThis matters because bodies are not equally strong in every direction.\n\nA structure that is stable against force from the front may be vulnerable to force applied diagonally or rotationally.\n\nGood angle selection therefore seeks a direction where:\n\n- the opponent’s structure is weaker,\n- their response options are slower,\n- or their base and posture are less aligned.\n\n## **Angle and Distance**\n\n[[Distance]] is not purely linear.\n\nChanging angle can make a target functionally closer without moving directly toward it.\n\nIt can also create effective separation while maintaining similar physical distance.\n\nThis means usable range is partly shaped by geometry.\n\nA technically correct action may fail because the athlete is at the wrong angle, even if the nominal distance appears correct.\n\n## **Angle and Timing**\n\nAngle is often most valuable when changed during a transition.\n\nIf the opponent is already:\n\n- stepping,\n- rotating,\n- striking,\n- posting,\n- resetting,\n- or shifting weight,\n\nan angular change can amplify the instability of that moment.\n\nThis creates a powerful interaction:\n\n**Timing identifies the opening.** **Angle determines how the opening is entered.**\n\n## **Angle and Exposure**\n\nChanging angle can reveal areas that were previously protected.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- the side of the head,\n- the back,\n- the hip line,\n- a leg,\n- the far shoulder,\n- a defensive seam.\n\nThe athlete is effectively changing what part of the opponent’s structure becomes available to perception and action.\n\n## **Angle and Defensive Burden**\n\nA strong angle can force the opponent to reorient before they can respond effectively.\n\nThat creates a temporary asymmetry.\n\nOne athlete is already aligned to act.\n\nThe other must first:\n\n- turn,\n- reset stance,\n- recover posture,\n- rebuild guard,\n- or reposition their base.\n\nThat reorientation cost creates time.\n\nAngle therefore changes not only geometry, but also **decision load**.\n\n## **Angle Creation**\n\nAngles can be created through:\n\n- footwork,\n- pivots,\n- lateral movement,\n- level change,\n- head position,\n- hip movement,\n- frames,\n- grips,\n- rotational pressure,\n- and forced reactions.\n\nThe athlete can create angle actively or allow the opponent’s movement to create it.\n\n## **Angle Denial**\n\nDefensively, good positioning can deny useful angles.\n\nThis may involve:\n\n- squaring up,\n- maintaining head position,\n- keeping hips aligned,\n- recovering stance,\n- preventing lateral access,\n- or controlling the opponent’s direction of travel.\n\nAngle control therefore includes both:\n\n**creating favourable orientation**\n\nand:\n\n**preventing the opponent from doing the same.**\n\n## **Angle and Constraint**\n\nA strong angle can reduce the opponent’s available responses.\n\nIf the opponent must first reorient before defending, some options temporarily disappear.\n\nThis makes angle part of [[Constraint Accumulation]].\n\nThe athlete is shaping the opponent’s response field before the next action occurs.\n\n## **Perceptual Advantage**\n\nAngle also affects perception.\n\nA favourable angle can improve:\n\n- visual access,\n- tactile information,\n- recognition of movement,\n- and prediction of likely reactions.\n\nThe opponent may experience the reverse:\n\n- reduced visibility,\n- delayed orientation,\n- or narrower defensive awareness.\n\nThis creates a small but meaningful information asymmetry.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether the athlete attacks directly into the opponent’s strongest structure,\n- whether a better angle was available,\n- whether the athlete created angle before committing,\n- whether the opponent was forced to reorient,\n- and whether the failure came from geometry rather than technique.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nThe action was technically available, but you entered from a square angle where their defensive structure was strongest. A small lateral shift would have changed both the line of attack and their ability to respond.\n\nOr:\n\nYou created the correct angle, but waited too long and allowed them to re-square before acting.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only saying:\n\n“Move around them.”\n\nFRAME should identify the purpose of the reorientation.\n\nFor example:\n\n“Step outside their lead side so your attack travels down a line they are not currently aligned to defend.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Create the angle first, then apply force through the exposed side of their structure.”\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nAngle can be understood as a change in **system orientation**.\n\nThe exchange may appear unchanged at a surface level, but a small rotational shift can alter:\n\n- available actions,\n- defensive latency,\n- structural integrity,\n- perceptual access,\n- and expected reaction paths.\n\nThis is why angle often produces disproportionate effects.\n\nA small geometric change can reorganise the entire local decision field.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Angle changes the meaning of distance, force, and access.**\n\nStrong combat positioning is not only about being in the right place.\n\nIt is about being oriented in the right direction relative to the opponent’s structure.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Distance]]\n- [[Timing]]\n- [[Constraint Accumulation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Asymmetry.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Asymmetry","body":"# **Asymmetry**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nAsymmetry is the condition in which the two athletes no longer occupy equivalent structural, informational, or decision states.\n\nOne side may have:\n\n- better angle,\n- stronger posture,\n- more options,\n- cleaner perception,\n- more initiative,\n- better balance,\n- or lower exposure.\n\nThe exchange becomes easier to understand when viewed not only as:\n\nWhat position are they in?\n\nbut:\n\nWhich athlete currently holds the more favourable configuration?\n\n## **Structural Asymmetry**\n\nStructural asymmetry appears when one athlete is better organised than the other.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- one athlete has stable base while the other is off-balanced,\n- one maintains posture while the other is folded,\n- one controls inside space while the other is displaced,\n- one is connected while the other is structurally fragmented.\n\nSmall asymmetries can compound quickly.\n\n## **Informational Asymmetry**\n\nOne athlete may understand the exchange better than the other.\n\nThis can happen when:\n\n- one athlete created the reaction,\n- one is reading rhythm,\n- one sees the angle change,\n- one recognises exposure earlier,\n- one understands the likely next branch.\n\nThe informationally advantaged athlete acts from anticipation.\n\nThe other acts from delayed interpretation.\n\n## **Option Asymmetry**\n\n[[Option Density]] may differ sharply between athletes.\n\nA strong state often looks like:\n\n**My option field remains broad.**\n\n**Your option field is compressed.**\n\nThis is more meaningful than simply counting attacks.\n\nThe athlete with more usable pathways has greater adaptive capacity.\n\n## **Initiative Asymmetry**\n\n[[Initiative]] creates another form of asymmetry.\n\nThe initiator is shaping the next problem.\n\nThe responder is solving it.\n\nIf this relationship persists, the responder can become progressively compressed into reactive behaviour.\n\n## **Temporal Asymmetry**\n\nTiming can create moments where one athlete is ready while the other is still recovering.\n\nFor example:\n\n- one athlete has completed reorientation,\n- the other is still rebuilding stance,\n- one has recognised the opening,\n- the other is still processing the previous exchange.\n\nThis creates a temporary temporal advantage.\n\nEven a small timing difference can become decisive.\n\n## **Perceptual Asymmetry**\n\nPerceptual asymmetry occurs when one athlete can process more of the exchange than the other.\n\nThis may result from:\n\n- better angle,\n- lower cognitive load,\n- clearer line of sight,\n- more stable state,\n- stronger anticipation.\n\nThe opponent may still be physically capable of responding but fail to recognise the need in time.\n\n## **Creating Asymmetry**\n\nHigh-level combat often involves deliberately creating unequal states.\n\nThis can be achieved through:\n\n- angle,\n- off-balancing,\n- feints,\n- pressure,\n- connection,\n- rhythm disruption,\n- exposure creation,\n- option compression,\n- positional control.\n\nThe goal is not merely to become “better positioned.”\n\nIt is to create a state where the opponent must solve more problems than you do.\n\n## **Asymmetry Compounds**\n\nOne small advantage can create another.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Angle advantage**\n\n↓\n\n**better access**\n\n↓\n\n**forced reaction**\n\n↓\n\n**exposure**\n\n↓\n\n**option compression**\n\n↓\n\n**initiative advantage**\n\nThe exchange begins to cascade.\n\nThis is why small structural improvements can create disproportionate outcomes.\n\n## **Restoring Symmetry**\n\nDefence often begins by reducing the asymmetry.\n\nThe athlete may need to:\n\n- recover posture,\n- restore base,\n- rebuild distance,\n- square orientation,\n- regain inside position,\n- slow the exchange,\n- or create enough space to perceive clearly again.\n\nThe first defensive goal is often not immediate offence.\n\nIt is re-establishing a state in which both athletes have more comparable functional access.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- where the first meaningful asymmetry appears,\n- which advantage begins compounding,\n- whether the athlete recognises when the exchange becomes structurally unequal,\n- and whether defensive actions actually reduce the imbalance.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nThe exchange was still neutral until you lost the angle. From there, they gained better access, forced the reaction, and your option field narrowed quickly.\n\nOr:\n\nYou were technically defending, but the asymmetry kept growing because you never restored posture or distance.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only asking:\n\n“Who is winning the exchange?”\n\nFRAME can ask:\n\n“Where is the asymmetry, and what is causing it?”\n\nThis produces more precise coaching.\n\nFor example:\n\n“Your main problem is not the final attack. You are entering the exchange already structurally asymmetric because your posture collapses during the entry.”\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nAsymmetry describes a **local imbalance in system quality**.\n\nTwo athletes may occupy the same apparent phase of combat while having very different:\n\n- stability,\n- perception,\n- option width,\n- timing,\n- and control.\n\nThe decisive question is often not:\n\nWhat state are we in?\n\nbut:\n\nWhose state is more coherent?\n\nCombat advantage emerges when one athlete can preserve coherence while progressively degrading the other’s.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Combat advantage is often the accumulation of asymmetry.**\n\nThe strongest athlete in an exchange is frequently the one who has more structure, more information, more options, and less urgency than the opponent."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Base.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Base","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nBase is the structure that allows an athlete to support their body, resist displacement, and produce force without losing balance.\n\nIt is created by the relationship between:\n\n- contact points,\n- centre of mass,\n- posture,\n- weight distribution,\n- and direction of force.\n\nThe stronger the base relative to the force being applied, the harder the athlete is to move.\n\n## **Base Is Directional**\n\nBase is not simply:\n\nwide stance = strong.\n\nAn athlete can be very stable in one direction and weak in another.\n\nA useful question is:\n\nWhere can this structure currently resist force, and where can it not?\n\nThis is why angle matters.\n\nA stable opponent may become vulnerable as soon as force is redirected outside the direction their base can support.\n\n## **Contact Points**\n\nBase is created through contact with the floor or another supporting surface.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- feet,\n- knees,\n- hands,\n- elbows,\n- hips,\n- shoulder,\n- or combinations of these.\n\nAdding a post can temporarily increase base.\n\nRemoving one can make the structure easier to disrupt.\n\n## **Centre of Mass**\n\nThe centre of mass must remain sufficiently supported by the available base.\n\nWhen it moves toward the boundary of that support, stability decreases.\n\nIf it moves beyond the available base, the athlete must:\n\n- step,\n- post,\n- rotate,\n- widen their structure,\n- or fall.\n\nThis is the foundation of [[Off-Balancing]].\n\n## **Active Base**\n\nBase is not always static.\n\nAthletes constantly rebuild it.\n\nFor example:\n\n- stepping during wrestling,\n- posting during a sweep,\n- widening knees during a pin,\n- placing a hand during a scramble.\n\nGood grappling requires recognising both:\n\n**the opponent’s current base**\n\nand:\n\n**how they are likely to rebuild it.**\n\n## **Base Removal**\n\nA powerful way to attack balance is to remove or occupy one of the structures the opponent needs for support.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- controlling a posting arm,\n- trapping a foot,\n- loading weight onto one leg,\n- lifting a knee,\n- controlling the hips,\n- preventing a hand from reaching the floor.\n\nInstead of overpowering the opponent’s entire structure, the athlete removes part of the structure supporting it.\n\n## **Base and Posting**\n\nA post is often an emergency expansion of base.\n\nIf an opponent is forced to post, that response creates information.\n\nThe post may:\n\n- expose an arm,\n- reduce defensive gripping,\n- alter posture,\n- open the back,\n- or create a new attack.\n\nBase therefore interacts directly with [[Reaction Mapping]].\n\n## **Base in Standing**\n\nStanding base depends heavily on:\n\n- foot placement,\n- stance width,\n- weight distribution,\n- knee position,\n- hip position,\n- and posture.\n\nAn athlete with both feet available and weight centred is harder to move than one whose weight has been committed to a single leg.\n\nThis makes weight transfer a major part of takedown setup.\n\n## **Base in Ground Grappling**\n\nBase still matters on the ground.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- a passer posting a hand to prevent being swept,\n- a mounted athlete widening knees for stability,\n- a bottom athlete using an elbow and foot to support a bridge,\n- a turtle player using knees and elbows to maintain structure.\n\nGround positions can often be attacked by identifying which supporting point is essential and removing it.\n\n## **Base and Mobility**\n\nA larger base can increase stability but reduce mobility.\n\nA narrower or lighter base may improve movement but decrease resistance to displacement.\n\nAthletes constantly trade between:\n\n**stability**\n\nand:\n\n**mobility.**\n\nThe correct base depends on the objective.\n\n## **Base and Connection**\n\nStrong [[Connection]] can allow an athlete to manipulate the opponent’s base more effectively.\n\nIf meaningful connection exists, movement of the attacker can transfer into the opponent’s centre.\n\nWithout connection, force may only move a limb while the opponent’s base remains intact.\n\n## **Defensive Application**\n\nWhen defending, rebuilding base can be more important than immediately attacking.\n\nAn athlete who has lost their base may need to:\n\n- post,\n- widen,\n- reconnect to the floor,\n- recover posture,\n- or shift weight\n\nbefore attempting a more complex action.\n\nTrying to attack without sufficient base often creates further instability.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- what currently supports each athlete,\n- where the centre of mass sits relative to that support,\n- whether weight becomes committed to one side,\n- when a post becomes necessary,\n- and whether an opportunity appears because a base point has been removed.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou tried to finish the sweep while both of their posts were still available. Once you remove the far hand, their remaining base becomes much easier to collapse.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only saying:\n\n“Knock them over.”\n\nFRAME can identify:\n\n“Remove the post first.”\n\nor:\n\n“Shift their weight onto the leg you want to attack.”\n\nThis teaches the athlete to attack structure rather than rely on force.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Balance depends on support.**\n\nTo move an opponent efficiently, understand what is currently supporting them, where that support is weak, and what happens when one part of the base is removed.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Off-Balancing]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Centre of Mass.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Centre of Mass","body":"# Centre of Mass\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nCentre of Mass is the effective point around which an athlete’s body mass is balanced and organised.\n\nIts position relative to the athlete’s base, opponent, and direction of force strongly influences:\n\n- balance\n- mobility\n- pressure\n- stability\n- weight transfer\n- off-balancing\n- ability to change direction\n\nThe centre of mass is not fixed.\n\nIt shifts continuously as posture, limb position, stance, and body orientation change.\n\n## Core Principle\nBalance depends on the relationship between mass and support.\n\n**Centre of mass + Base + Momentum = Current stability**\n\nThe closer the centre of mass moves toward or beyond the limits of the usable base, the greater the demand for structural correction.\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany combat actions work by changing where mass is organised rather than directly overpowering the opponent.\n\nExamples:\n\n- pulling the head moves upper-body mass forward\n- lifting a leg reduces the available base beneath the centre of mass\n- rotating shoulders can shift mass outside the stance\n- pressure passing moves mass through the opponent rather than simply toward them\n\n## Centre of Mass and Base\nA stable athlete generally keeps their centre of mass supported by their effective base.\n\nIf it moves outside that base, the athlete must respond through:\n\n- stepping\n- posting\n- widening stance\n- rotating\n- lowering level\n- creating new contact\n\nThis makes centre-of-mass displacement central to off-balancing.\n\n## Effective Base\nThe effective base is not limited to the feet.\n\nIn grappling it may include:\n\n- feet\n- knees\n- hands\n- elbows\n- hips\n- opponent contact\n- wall or cage\n\nThe same centre-of-mass position may therefore be stable in one configuration and unstable in another.\n\n## Horizontal Displacement\nMoving the centre of mass forward, backward, or laterally can create instability.\n\nExamples:\n\n- snapdown shifts mass forward\n- arm drag shifts torso laterally\n- push-pull sequences move mass across changing base boundaries\n\n## Vertical Displacement\nChanging height also affects mechanical behaviour.\n\nLower centre of mass may improve stability.\n\nHigher centre of mass may improve mobility or elevation but can increase susceptibility to some off-balancing actions.\n\nVertical position should therefore be evaluated relative to task.\n\n## Rotational Displacement\nThe body may remain spatially close to the same place while mass rotates around the base.\n\nExamples:\n\n- shoulder rotation against fixed hips\n- turning opponent around a planted foot\n- steering the upper body past the lower-body base\n\nRotational displacement can create instability without large linear movement.\n\n## Centre of Mass Projection\nSynochi should distinguish the approximate body centre from where that mass is projected relative to support.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete leaning heavily forward may still have feet in the same location, but the projected mass has moved toward the front edge of the base.\n\nThis changes stability and available movement.\n\n## Centre of Mass and Weight Distribution\nWeight distribution reflects how the athlete’s mass is currently supported.\n\nA centre-of-mass shift usually changes load across the available base.\n\nExample:\n\nMass shifts right  \n→ right leg becomes heavily loaded  \n→ left leg becomes lighter  \n→ left leg may become easier to attack\n\n## Centre of Mass and Pressure\nPressure becomes mechanically effective when body mass is organised through a useful force path.\n\nExample:\n\nhips positioned correctly  \n→ centre of mass projected through shoulder  \n→ opponent receives sustained pressure\n\nPressure should therefore be understood partly as controlled centre-of-mass placement.\n\n## Centre of Mass and Mobility\nStability and mobility exist in tension.\n\nA deeply centred stance may be difficult to move but slower to reposition.\n\nA mobile stance may allow rapid movement but create shorter windows of reduced stability.\n\nOptimal positioning depends on the task.\n\n## Centre of Mass and Momentum\nOnce mass is moving, stopping or redirecting it requires force.\n\nAn athlete can exploit this by adding force in the same direction as existing momentum.\n\nExample:\n\nopponent steps forward  \n→ mass already travelling forward  \n→ snapdown amplifies existing movement\n\n## Centre of Mass Manipulation\nAn athlete can manipulate an opponent’s centre of mass through:\n\n- head control\n- underhooks\n- grips\n- frames\n- pressure\n- elevation\n- angle changes\n- push-pull actions\n\nThe objective is often not simply to move the body.\n\nIt is to move the body relative to its supporting structure.\n\n## Centre of Mass Recovery\nWhen displaced, the athlete can recover stability by reorganising support beneath the mass.\n\nExamples:\n\n- step under body\n- post hand\n- widen stance\n- lower hips\n- rotate base\n- reconnect to opponent\n\n## False Stability\nAn athlete may appear stationary while being structurally unstable.\n\nIf the centre of mass is near the edge of the base, only a small additional disturbance may force a post or step.\n\nSynochi should therefore distinguish:\n\n**not moving**\n\nfrom\n\n**mechanically stable**\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"He was standing too tall.\"\n\nCentre-of-mass analysis:\n\n\"His hips were high and his mass had shifted beyond his rear foot. Once you continued pulling forward, he had no base underneath the direction his weight was travelling.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nCentre of Mass helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why off-balancing works\n- why posts appear\n- why certain legs become light\n- why pressure feels heavy\n- why some stances are stable but immobile\n- why movement becomes difficult after weight commitment\n- when sweeps and takedowns become mechanically available\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about centre of mass through:\n\n**Observe posture → Estimate mass position → Identify effective base → Compare mass projection with support → Track displacement → Predict required correction → Observe outcome**\n\nCentre of Mass should serve as a foundational mechanical variable connecting balance, base, weight distribution, movement, pressure, and off-balancing.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Weight Transfer]]\n[[Load Transfer]]\n[[Off-Balancing]]\n[[Posts]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Momentum]]\n[[Force Direction]]\n[[Alignment]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Commitment.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Commitment","body":"# **Commitment**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nCommitment is the degree to which an athlete allocates structure, momentum, attention, and positional resources toward an action.\n\nEvery meaningful action carries a commitment cost.\n\nLow commitment preserves optionality.\n\nHigh commitment can create greater force, certainty, or consequence, but it also reduces the athlete’s ability to redirect, recover, or select another pathway.\n\nThe central relationship is:\n\n**Commitment ↑ → Optionality ↓**\n\n## **Commitment as Resource Allocation**\n\nTo commit is to concentrate resources toward a predicted outcome.\n\nThose resources may include:\n\n- bodyweight,\n- balance,\n- stance,\n- momentum,\n- limb position,\n- attention,\n- time,\n- or tactical intent.\n\nOnce those resources are allocated, they become temporarily less available elsewhere.\n\nThis is why commitment can generate both:\n\n**power**\n\nand:\n\n**exposure.**\n\n## **Undercommitment**\n\nUndercommitment occurs when the athlete does not allocate enough structure or intent for the action to create meaningful consequence.\n\nThis may produce:\n\n- weak entries,\n- incomplete attacks,\n- poor force transfer,\n- hesitation,\n- shallow contact,\n- or actions that are easy to ignore.\n\nThe athlete preserves options, but fails to change the state.\n\n## **Overcommitment**\n\nOvercommitment occurs when more resources are allocated than the action requires.\n\nThis may create:\n\n- loss of balance,\n- poor recovery,\n- excessive exposure,\n- reduced ability to redirect,\n- predictable follow-through,\n- or unnecessary positional risk.\n\nThe action may succeed locally while degrading the athlete’s next state.\n\n## **Minimum Effective Commitment**\n\nGood combat decision-making often seeks the minimum commitment required to create the intended state change.\n\nThe question becomes:\n\nHow much commitment is necessary to make this threat, entry, defence, or attack function?\n\nThis preserves more of the athlete’s wider system.\n\nA well-calibrated action creates enough consequence without unnecessarily collapsing future options.\n\n## **Commitment and Threat**\n\nA credible [[Threat]] does not always require full commitment.\n\nAn athlete can often force:\n\n- attention,\n- defensive allocation,\n- weight shift,\n- guard change,\n- or retreat\n\nwith partial commitment.\n\nThis is strategically efficient.\n\nThe opponent is forced to spend resources while the athlete preserves the ability to redirect.\n\n## **Commitment and Feint**\n\n[[Feint]] depends on commitment calibration.\n\nToo little commitment and the signal lacks credibility.\n\nToo much commitment and the athlete may no longer be able to convert the feint into another action.\n\nThe ideal feint presents enough commitment to force prediction without surrendering selection.\n\n## **Commitment and Exposure**\n\nCommitment frequently creates [[Exposure]].\n\nA committed action may temporarily:\n\n- remove a defensive limb,\n- shift weight onto one side,\n- narrow stance,\n- extend posture,\n- reduce visual access,\n- or lengthen recovery time.\n\nThe deeper the commitment, the greater the potential cost if the action fails or is redirected.\n\n## **Commitment and Recovery**\n\nEvery commitment produces a recovery demand.\n\nThe more structure, momentum, or attention invested in the action, the more reorganisation may be required afterward.\n\nThis connects directly to [[Recovery]].\n\nA useful action should therefore be evaluated not only by:\n\nDid it work?\n\nbut:\n\nWhat recovery cost did it create?\n\n## **Commitment and Momentum**\n\n[[Momentum]] increases commitment once movement is underway.\n\nAn athlete accelerating strongly in one direction becomes less capable of instant reversal.\n\nThis means apparent technical commitment may begin before contact.\n\nThe system has already begun narrowing its future states.\n\n## **Commitment and Initiative**\n\nCommitment can establish [[Initiative]] when it creates a meaningful problem.\n\nBut excessive commitment can also surrender initiative if the opponent survives the action and the athlete requires too long to recover.\n\nThe useful balance is:\n\n**enough commitment to shape the exchange, not so much that the exchange owns you afterward.**\n\n## **Defensive Commitment**\n\nDefence also requires commitment.\n\nAn athlete may overcommit to:\n\n- blocking,\n- retreating,\n- framing,\n- sprawling,\n- shelling,\n- or protecting one target.\n\nThis may successfully remove one threat while opening another.\n\nStrong defence therefore preserves enough structure to remain selectable after the immediate danger is resolved.\n\n## **Commitment Windows**\n\nAn opponent becomes especially vulnerable when commitment crosses a threshold.\n\nOnce they can no longer easily reverse the action, their likely next state becomes easier to predict.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- weight fully transferring,\n- hips fully rotating,\n- stance narrowing,\n- an arm extending,\n- momentum crossing the base,\n- attention locking onto one target.\n\nThese moments create high-value timing windows.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether the athlete commits enough to create consequence,\n- whether they commit more than necessary,\n- when the opponent crosses into irreversible or difficult-to-reverse commitment,\n- whether commitment creates unnecessary exposure,\n- and whether recovery cost exceeds the value of the action.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou had already forced the reaction with the initial entry, but continued committing forward after the opening had changed. The extra commitment reduced your ability to redirect.\n\nOr:\n\nTheir weight fully committed onto the lead side before the action completed. That was the window to attack the transition.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only saying:\n\n“Commit more.”\n\nor:\n\n“Don’t overcommit.”\n\nFRAME should identify the resource allocation problem.\n\nFor example:\n\n“Commit enough weight to force the reaction, but keep your rear side available so you can redirect.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Wait until their weight crosses the point where they can no longer easily recover before you counter.”\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nCommitment is **temporary option compression created by resource allocation**.\n\nThe system selects one predicted pathway and shifts structure toward making that pathway real.\n\nAs commitment rises:\n\n- alternative actions become less accessible,\n- recovery demand increases,\n- prediction becomes easier,\n- and the consequence of being wrong increases.\n\nCommitment is therefore not simply intensity.\n\nIt is a change in the architecture of available future states.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Commitment creates consequence by sacrificing optionality.**\n\nStrong combat intelligence calibrates that trade:\n\n**enough commitment to change the state, enough preserved structure to remain adaptive.**"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Connection.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Connection","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nConnection is the controlled transfer of force, pressure, movement, and information through physical contact.\n\nGood connection allows an athlete to influence the opponent without needing to chase every movement separately.\n\nPoor connection creates gaps, delays, and opportunities for the opponent to move independently.\n\nThe central principle is:\n\n**If two bodies are meaningfully connected, movement in one can directly affect the other.**\n\n## **What Connection Does**\n\nConnection can allow the athlete to:\n\n- transmit pressure,\n- control posture,\n- follow movement,\n- redirect force,\n- prevent separation,\n- maintain positional dominance,\n- and feel changes in balance or intent.\n\nConnection is therefore both mechanical and informational.\n\n## **Mechanical Connection**\n\nMechanical connection means contact is strong enough that force transfers efficiently.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- chest-to-chest control,\n- hip-to-hip connection,\n- shoulder pressure,\n- body locks,\n- tight upper-body control,\n- leg entanglements.\n\nThe closer the connection to the opponent’s centre, the more influence it can usually create.\n\n## **Informational Connection**\n\nConnection also gives feedback.\n\nThrough contact, an athlete can feel:\n\n- weight shifts,\n- tension,\n- direction of movement,\n- attempts to turn,\n- posture changes,\n- loading before an escape.\n\nGood connection therefore helps the athlete respond earlier.\n\nIt reduces reliance on visual reaction alone.\n\n## **Connection vs Space**\n\nConnection is not always desirable everywhere.\n\nStrong grappling often depends on controlling:\n\n**where to connect**\n\nand:\n\n**where to preserve space.**\n\nFor example:\n\nA guard player may want strong leg connection to the opponent’s hips while preserving enough upper-body space to frame and move.\n\nA passer may want chest connection while denying the bottom player inside space.\n\nThe goal is not maximum contact.\n\nIt is **useful contact**.\n\n## **Connection and Wedges**\n\n[[Wedges]] often determine where connection can and cannot exist.\n\nA wedge may create separation in one area while allowing stronger connection somewhere else.\n\nFor example:\n\nA forearm frame may prevent chest connection while maintaining hip contact.\n\nThis creates controlled asymmetry.\n\n## **Connection and Control**\n\nControl improves when connection limits independent movement.\n\nIf the opponent can freely move one major body segment while the rest is controlled, that freedom may become the beginning of an escape.\n\nStrong control often links:\n\n- upper body,\n- hips,\n- head,\n- and directional pressure\n\ninto one connected system.\n\n## **Breaking Connection**\n\nDefensively, escape often begins by breaking a critical connection.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- removing chest pressure,\n- disconnecting the opponent’s hips,\n- clearing head control,\n- separating a body lock,\n- rebuilding inside frames.\n\nThe athlete does not always need to escape the entire position immediately.\n\nThey may only need to break the connection that makes the position stable.\n\n## **Reconnection**\n\nAfter creating space, the athlete often needs to reconnect in a more favourable way.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Break opponent connection → create space → insert frame → reconnect with inside position**\n\nConnection is therefore not simply something to avoid or maintain.\n\nIt is something to manage.\n\n## **Directional Connection**\n\nConnection is strongest when it supports a clear directional objective.\n\nPressure without direction may feel heavy but achieve little.\n\nA better connection asks:\n\nWhat movement am I trying to create or prevent?\n\nExamples:\n\n- connecting chest pressure to prevent rotation,\n- connecting a body lock to turn the opponent,\n- connecting hip pressure to flatten the legs,\n- connecting head position to control shoulder direction.\n\n## **Connection Failure**\n\nConnection can fail when:\n\n- contact becomes too shallow,\n- the athlete’s body segments become disconnected,\n- pressure is applied in conflicting directions,\n- the opponent creates a frame,\n- the athlete follows with the hands instead of the body,\n- or space opens between controlling surfaces.\n\nA position may appear correct visually while lacking meaningful connection.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- where meaningful connection exists,\n- where gaps appear,\n- whether the athlete’s force is actually transferring through the opponent,\n- whether a lost position begins with broken connection,\n- and whether the athlete creates space without rebuilding useful contact.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYour upper-body control looked secure, but your hips disconnected during the transition. That gap gave them enough independent movement to recover their knees.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only coaching grips or final positions, FRAME should identify the connection that makes them work.\n\nFor example:\n\nInstead of:\n\n“Hold the body lock tighter.”\n\nUse:\n\n“Keep your hips connected as you turn them so their lower body can’t move independently.”\n\nThis explains the function behind the technique.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Control comes from managing connection, not simply creating contact.**\n\nUseful connection transfers force and information while limiting independent movement.\n\nStrong grappling depends on knowing when to connect, where to connect, and when to deliberately create space.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Wedges]]\n- [[Inside Position]]\n- [[Constraint Accumulation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Counterforce.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Counterforce","body":"# Counterforce\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nCounterforce is force applied in response to an opponent’s force in order to resist, redirect, neutralise, or exploit it.\n\nCounterforce is not simply “pushing back.”\n\nIt can oppose force directly, absorb it structurally, redirect it, or use it to create a new movement.\n\n## Core Principle\nForce should not always be met with equal force.\n\n**Incoming force → Read direction → Choose resistance, redirection, absorption, or exploitation**\n\nThe quality of the response depends on understanding the structure behind the force.\n\n## Why It Matters\nCombat exchanges constantly involve competing force systems.\n\nExamples:\n\n- resisting a takedown drive\n- framing against pressure\n- countering a snapdown\n- redirecting a push into an angle\n- using an opponent’s pull to enter\n- meeting rotational force with base and posture\n\nThe athlete who understands how to respond to incoming force can often spend less energy while creating better outcomes.\n\n## Direct Counterforce\nDirect counterforce opposes the incoming force along a similar line.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent drives forward.\n\nAthlete drives backward into the same line.\n\nThis may work when:\n\n- base is stronger\n- alignment is superior\n- force capacity is higher\n- timing is favourable\n\nBut direct opposition is often energetically expensive.\n\n## Redirective Counterforce\nInstead of stopping force, the athlete changes its path.\n\nExamples:\n\n- parrying a strike\n- turning with pressure\n- guiding a passer past the hip line\n- steering a snapdown into a front headlock angle\n\nRedirection can create advantage while reducing the amount of force required.\n\n## Absorptive Counterforce\nThe athlete allows structure to receive force without immediate displacement.\n\nExamples:\n\n- lowering centre of mass\n- widening base\n- using skeletal frames\n- connecting force into the floor\n\nThe athlete does not necessarily create visible counter-movement.\n\nThey simply prevent collapse.\n\n## Exploitative Counterforce\nThe athlete uses the opponent’s force as part of their own action.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent pulls  \n→ athlete steps with pull  \n→ angle improves  \n→ takedown entry appears\n\nOr:\n\nOpponent drives forward  \n→ athlete rotates  \n→ momentum carries opponent past base\n\nThe opponent partially supplies the energy.\n\n## Counterforce Timing\nCounterforce quality depends heavily on timing.\n\nToo early:\nthe athlete may reveal intention or create unnecessary tension.\n\nToo late:\nthe structure may already be compromised.\n\nEffective timing often occurs as force begins to commit but before control consolidates.\n\n## Counterforce and Structure\nThe athlete must have sufficient structural integrity to respond effectively.\n\nPoor alignment can cause incoming force to collapse the body before counterforce develops.\n\nUseful counterforce often depends on:\n\n- base\n- alignment\n- posture\n- connection\n- anchoring\n\n## Counterforce and Direction\nThe most efficient response may not be opposite the incoming vector.\n\nExample:\n\nForward pressure may be best answered by lateral rotation rather than backward resistance.\n\nCounterforce should therefore be selected relative to directional vulnerability.\n\n## Counterforce and Yielding\nYielding is not absence of counterforce.\n\nControlled yielding can be an intentional response that lets force travel into a less useful direction.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent pushes strongly.\n\nAthlete allows limited movement  \n→ opponent overcommits  \n→ base narrows  \n→ reversal becomes available\n\nThe counter occurs through timing and redirection rather than collision.\n\n## Counterforce and Momentum\nExisting momentum can amplify either athlete’s force.\n\nA counter that opposes fast momentum may require significant force.\n\nA counter that redirects or adds to the momentum can be much cheaper.\n\n## Counterforce and Pressure\nUnder sustained pressure, athletes may alternate between:\n\n- resisting\n- framing\n- redirecting\n- yielding\n- rebuilding structure\n\nConstant direct resistance often produces unnecessary fatigue.\n\n## Counterforce Failure\nCommon failures include:\n\n- opposing stronger force head-on\n- countering before base is established\n- resisting in the wrong direction\n- over-tensing\n- reacting after structure has already collapsed\n- failing to recognise when yielding is the higher-value option\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You got driven backwards.\"\n\nCounterforce-aware analysis:\n\n\"You met his forward pressure directly while your base was already narrow. A lateral redirect would likely have been cheaper than trying to stop the drive head-on.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nCounterforce helps Synochi explain:\n\n- when to resist\n- when to redirect\n- when to yield\n- why direct force battles are costly\n- how opponent momentum can be used\n- why structure matters before applying force\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about counterforce through:\n\n**Detect incoming force → Infer vector → Assess structure and base → Identify response options → Predict cost and effect → Observe outcome**\n\nCounterforce should interact with force direction, base, momentum, alignment, timing, pressure, and yielding.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Force Direction]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Momentum]]\n[[Alignment]]\n[[Timing]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Tension Management]]\n[[Centre of Mass]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Degrees of Freedom.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Degrees of Freedom","body":"# Degrees of Freedom\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nDegrees of Freedom are the number and quality of meaningful movement options available to an athlete within the current structure of the exchange.\n\nA position with many usable movement pathways has high freedom.\n\nA position where movement is restricted, redirected, or mechanically blocked has low freedom.\n\n## Core Principle\nControl is often the progressive removal of useful movement options.\n\n**More constraints → Fewer degrees of freedom → More predictable behaviour**\n\nThe athlete with more meaningful freedom usually has greater adaptability.\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany positions are strong not because the opponent is completely immobilised, but because only a small number of useful responses remain.\n\nExamples:\n\n- pinning one shoulder reduces torso rotation\n- controlling the head limits spinal movement\n- trapping a wrist removes a post\n- controlling the knee line limits hip escape\n- occupying inside space blocks pummelling routes\n\nEach control removes part of the opponent’s option-space.\n\n## Degrees of Freedom vs Movement\nVisible movement does not necessarily mean high freedom.\n\nAn athlete may still be moving but only inside a very narrow set of forced reactions.\n\nSynochi should distinguish:\n\n**movement occurring**\n\nfrom\n\n**meaningful options remaining**\n\n## High Freedom State\nA high freedom state may include:\n\n- multiple escape directions\n- available posts\n- unrestricted hip rotation\n- free head movement\n- multiple grip options\n- ability to disengage\n\n## Low Freedom State\nA low freedom state may include:\n\n- trapped limb\n- fixed head position\n- flattened hips\n- occupied posts\n- boundary restriction\n- isolated joint\n- blocked rotation\n\n## Freedom Reduction\nControl often develops through sequential reduction.\n\nExample:\n\nhead controlled  \n→ shoulder pinned  \n→ near elbow isolated  \n→ hips blocked  \n→ escape routes collapse\n\nThe final pin is the product of accumulated freedom loss.\n\n## Freedom Restoration\nDefence often succeeds by restoring one degree of freedom first.\n\nExamples:\n\n- free head\n- recover inside elbow\n- rebuild knee line\n- release trapped wrist\n- create hip angle\n\nOne restored movement pathway may reopen several others.\n\n## Local vs Global Freedom\nAn athlete may have freedom in one region but not another.\n\nExample:\n\nupper body pinned  \nwhile  \nlegs remain mobile\n\nThis creates opportunities for lower-body recovery even when torso movement is constrained.\n\n## Degrees of Freedom and Prediction\nAs freedom decreases, behaviour often becomes more predictable.\n\nIf only one escape route remains, the opponent can prepare for it.\n\nThis creates a control advantage beyond simple positional dominance.\n\n## Degrees of Freedom and Threats\nThreats can reduce freedom even without physical immobilisation.\n\nExample:\n\nA submission threat may make one movement strategically unavailable.\n\nThe movement is mechanically possible but tactically unsafe.\n\nThis still reduces functional degrees of freedom.\n\n## Degrees of Freedom and Boundary\nWalls, cages, and mat edges reduce available movement pathways.\n\nAn athlete near a boundary may lose:\n\n- retreat options\n- rotational space\n- lateral escape\n- ability to re-angle\n\nEnvironment therefore changes the freedom profile.\n\n## Degrees of Freedom and Fatigue\nFatigue can reduce usable freedom.\n\nA movement may remain mechanically possible but no longer executable at sufficient speed or force.\n\nSynochi should treat functional freedom as dependent on both structure and athlete state.\n\n## Freedom Compression\nHigh-level control often compresses option-space progressively.\n\n**Many options → Several options → Few options → Forced response**\n\nThis is one of the clearest markers of dominant control.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You were stuck.\"\n\nFreedom-aware analysis:\n\n\"Your head was fixed, near arm trapped, and hip rotation blocked. By that point you had almost no functional degrees of freedom left.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nDegrees of Freedom helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why control feels suffocating\n- why certain escapes become predictable\n- which restriction should be removed first\n- how threats shape movement\n- why some positions remain recoverable despite disadvantage\n- how control should be built systematically\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about degrees of freedom through:\n\n**Identify possible movements → Detect structural and tactical constraints → Estimate remaining viable options → Track freedom loss or restoration → Predict likely reactions**\n\nDegrees of Freedom should interact with constraint, control, threat, position, base, and recovery.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Threat Recognition]]\n[[Structural Integrity]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Recovery]]\n[[Mechanical Advantage]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Distance.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Distance","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nDistance is the spatial relationship between athletes that determines which actions are available, effective, or dangerous.\n\nEvery technique has a functional range.\n\nIf the athlete is too far away, the action may not reach.\n\nIf the athlete is too close, the action may lose structure, power, or timing.\n\nCombat therefore depends heavily on controlling:\n\n**when distance opens, when it closes, and who benefits from that change.**\n\n## **Functional Range**\n\nDistance should not be understood only as physical space.\n\nIt should be understood as:\n\nWhat can each athlete successfully do from here?\n\nDifferent ranges favour different actions.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- long striking range,\n- punching range,\n- kicking range,\n- clinch range,\n- takedown range,\n- entanglement range,\n- ground-control range.\n\nThe exact expression changes between sports, but the principle remains the same.\n\n## **Distance Is Relative**\n\nA distance that is safe for one athlete may be dangerous for another.\n\nThis depends on:\n\n- reach,\n- stance,\n- speed,\n- available techniques,\n- ruleset,\n- posture,\n- angle,\n- and reaction time.\n\nThe useful question is not:\n\nHow far apart are they?\n\nIt is:\n\nWho currently has effective access?\n\n## **Entry Distance**\n\nMany attacks require the athlete to first cross distance.\n\nAn entry succeeds when the athlete can close enough space to act without allowing the opponent an easier response.\n\nThis applies to:\n\n- punches,\n- kicks,\n- takedowns,\n- clinch entries,\n- submissions,\n- positional transitions.\n\nGood entries change distance while preserving structure and reducing exposure.\n\n## **Exit Distance**\n\nAfter attacking, the athlete may need to create distance again.\n\nA successful attack can still create danger if the athlete remains in the opponent’s preferred range.\n\nExit distance therefore matters as much as entry distance.\n\nThe sequence may be:\n\n**Enter → Act → Exit**\n\nor:\n\n**Enter → Connect → Transition**\n\ndepending on the objective.\n\n## **Distance Control**\n\nDistance can be controlled through:\n\n- footwork,\n- stance,\n- frames,\n- head position,\n- grips,\n- posts,\n- pressure,\n- feints,\n- movement,\n- and positional barriers.\n\nControl does not always mean keeping the opponent away.\n\nSometimes the athlete wants to deny separation.\n\nSometimes they want to deny entry.\n\nThe objective determines the preferred distance.\n\n## **Closing Distance**\n\nClosing distance can remove certain weapons while enabling others.\n\nFor example:\n\nA long-range striker may become less effective once an opponent enters clinch range.\n\nLikewise, a grappler may be unable to initiate meaningful control until sufficient connection is established.\n\nDistance changes the available tactical menu.\n\n## **Creating Distance**\n\nCreating distance can:\n\n- reset an exchange,\n- remove immediate threat,\n- create striking space,\n- restore defensive structure,\n- improve visibility,\n- or force the opponent to re-enter.\n\nDistance creation is therefore not always retreat.\n\nIt can be a deliberate tactical reset.\n\n## **Distance and Timing**\n\nCorrect distance without correct timing may still fail.\n\nAn athlete may enter the right range at the wrong moment and meet resistance.\n\nLikewise, excellent timing cannot compensate for being physically unable to reach the target.\n\nDistance and timing therefore interact continuously.\n\n## **Distance and Angle**\n\nChanging angle also changes effective distance.\n\nA target may become closer or farther without either athlete moving directly forward or backward.\n\nAn angle can:\n\n- shorten the path to the target,\n- lengthen the opponent’s return path,\n- expose a side,\n- or remove a defensive barrier.\n\nDistance should therefore be understood three-dimensionally rather than only as straight-line separation.\n\n## **Distance as Information**\n\nDistance also communicates intent.\n\nWhen an opponent suddenly:\n\n- steps closer,\n- retreats,\n- changes stance,\n- lowers level,\n- or shifts weight,\n\nthey may be preparing to enter or leave a particular range.\n\nRecognising these changes allows earlier decision-making.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether the athlete acts from functional range,\n- whether attacks begin too far away or too close,\n- whether the athlete enters safely,\n- whether they remain in danger after attacking,\n- and whether distance changes favour one athlete repeatedly.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nThe technique itself was available, but you initiated from outside effective range, which gave the opponent enough time to recognise and react.\n\nOr:\n\nYou landed the exchange but stayed inside their preferred range afterward instead of resetting.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only correcting technique execution, FRAME should ask:\n\nWas the athlete at the correct distance for the technique to work?\n\nA technically sound action performed from the wrong range may fail consistently.\n\nDistance problems should therefore be separated from technique problems.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Combat actions only exist inside usable range.**\n\nStrong athletes do not simply know techniques.\n\nThey understand how to enter, maintain, manipulate, and exit the distances where those techniques become effective.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Exposure.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Exposure","body":"# **Exposure**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nExposure is the state in which a target, pathway, or vulnerability becomes sufficiently available to act on.\n\nExposure is not the same as simple visibility.\n\nA target can be visible but not meaningfully accessible.\n\nLikewise, something can become exposed through:\n\n- structural opening,\n- positional displacement,\n- weight transfer,\n- attention shift,\n- defensive commitment,\n- or forced reaction.\n\nThe central question is:\n\nWhat has become available that was previously protected?\n\n## **Exposure as a State Change**\n\nCombat positions are not static.\n\nA target may move through states such as:\n\n**Protected → Partially Exposed → Fully Exposed → Recovered**\n\nThe useful skill is recognising when the system has crossed the threshold from:\n\ntheoretically attackable\n\nto:\n\npractically available.\n\n## **Sources of Exposure**\n\nExposure can emerge from:\n\n- broken posture,\n- lost base,\n- overextension,\n- committed weight,\n- defensive reactions,\n- angle change,\n- failed attack,\n- poor recovery,\n- frame removal,\n- guard displacement,\n- or attention being pulled elsewhere.\n\nThe cause matters because it influences how long the opening will last.\n\n## **Structural Exposure**\n\nStructural exposure occurs when protective alignment breaks down.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- a limb separating from the torso,\n- the chin lifting,\n- the hips becoming square,\n- the back becoming visible,\n- one side of the body becoming isolated,\n- stance narrowing or overextending.\n\nThe target becomes available because the surrounding structure no longer protects it efficiently.\n\n## **Tactical Exposure**\n\nExposure can also be created by decision commitment.\n\nIf an opponent commits strongly to one action, they may temporarily abandon another layer of defence.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- posting one hand,\n- overcommitting to a strike,\n- driving heavily into one direction,\n- defending one threat with both hands,\n- committing weight to one leg.\n\nThe action itself creates the opening.\n\n## **Perceptual Exposure**\n\nAttention is limited.\n\nAn athlete can become exposed when their perception narrows around one threat.\n\nA feint, combination, grip sequence, or positional threat may pull attention toward one problem while another pathway opens.\n\nThis creates a useful Synochi distinction:\n\n**Physical exposure** — the target becomes structurally available.\n\n**Perceptual exposure** — the opponent becomes less able to detect or respond to the next action.\n\nThe strongest attacks often create both.\n\n## **Exposure and Reaction**\n\n[[Reaction Mapping]] is fundamentally about exposure creation.\n\nA threat induces a response.\n\nThat response alters structure.\n\nThe altered structure exposes something new.\n\nThe cycle becomes:\n\n**Threat → Reaction → Exposure → Selection**\n\nThe athlete is not simply searching for openings.\n\nThey are engineering them.\n\n## **Exposure and Initiative**\n\nThe athlete with [[Initiative]] often has more control over which exposures emerge.\n\nBy creating the first meaningful problem, they can influence:\n\n- which defence appears,\n- where attention moves,\n- which side becomes available,\n- and what structure is abandoned.\n\nThis turns exposure from something accidental into something shaped.\n\n## **Exposure and Angle**\n\n[[Angle]] strongly affects exposure.\n\nA target may be well protected from one line but available from another.\n\nChanging orientation can expose:\n\n- a flank,\n- a strike lane,\n- a takedown path,\n- a joint line,\n- or a route around defensive structure.\n\nThe target did not necessarily move.\n\nThe relationship to it changed.\n\n## **Temporary Windows**\n\nExposure is often transient.\n\nAn opening may exist for only a fraction of a second before:\n\n- posture returns,\n- guard recovers,\n- stance resets,\n- the limb reconnects,\n- or awareness returns.\n\nRecognition must therefore connect directly to [[Timing]].\n\nThe problem is often not:\n\nThe athlete did not know the attack.\n\nIt is:\n\nThe athlete recognised the exposure after the window had already collapsed.\n\n## **Overexposure**\n\nAthletes can also expose themselves.\n\nThis may happen through:\n\n- chasing an attack too far,\n- overcommitting weight,\n- losing stance,\n- abandoning defensive position,\n- remaining in range too long,\n- or creating a large movement without recovery.\n\nA successful action may still produce a poor exchange if it generates greater exposure than advantage.\n\nCombat Intelligence should therefore evaluate both:\n\n**What did the action create?**\n\nand:\n\n**What did it leave open?**\n\n## **Exposure Management**\n\nStrong athletes manage exposure continuously.\n\nThey aim to:\n\n- maximise opponent exposure,\n- minimise their own,\n- act during favourable windows,\n- and recover structure quickly after action.\n\nThis creates a repeating loop:\n\n**Create exposure → exploit → recover → reorient**\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- when a target first becomes meaningfully available,\n- what created the exposure,\n- whether the athlete recognised it,\n- whether the window was acted on in time,\n- and whether the athlete created unnecessary exposure during their own action.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nThe opening appeared when their weight shifted outside the lead leg, but you attacked after they had already rebuilt stance.\n\nOr:\n\nYou landed cleanly, but your recovery left your centreline exposed and gave them the stronger return.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of simply saying:\n\n“Attack when it’s there.”\n\nFRAME should identify:\n\n“That target appears when they commit to the post.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Your angle creates the exposure; don’t attack before the structure actually opens.”\n\nThis teaches recognition of the state change that makes the action viable.\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nExposure can be understood as a **temporary drop in protective constraint**.\n\nThe opponent’s system moves from:\n\n**high protection / low access**\n\nto:\n\n**reduced protection / increased access**\n\nbecause some combination of structure, attention, balance, or decision commitment has changed.\n\nExposure is therefore not merely an opening.\n\nIt is a **transient change in the constraint field**.\n\nThe athlete who detects that change early gains selection advantage.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Openings are not random gaps. They are states of reduced protection.**\n\nStrong combat intelligence recognises what creates those states, how long they persist, and whether the athlete can exploit them before the system re-stabilises."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Feint.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Feint","body":"# **Feint**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nA feint is a deliberately presented signal that suggests one action while preserving the ability to select another.\n\nIts purpose is not deception for its own sake.\n\nIts purpose is to create a predictable change in the opponent’s state.\n\nA good feint can alter:\n\n- attention,\n- posture,\n- guard,\n- weight distribution,\n- distance,\n- timing,\n- and option selection\n\nbefore the real action begins.\n\nThe central sequence is:\n\n**Signal → Prediction → Reaction → Exposure → Selection**\n\n## **Feint as Signal Engineering**\n\nA feint works by presenting information that the opponent interprets as meaningful.\n\nThe signal may resemble:\n\n- an entry,\n- a strike,\n- a level change,\n- a grip,\n- a step,\n- a posture shift,\n- or another credible attack.\n\nThe opponent then updates their prediction of what is about to happen.\n\nIf the signal is believable, their system begins organising around that expected future state.\n\nThe feint exploits that reorganisation.\n\n## **Credibility**\n\nA feint only works if it resembles something real enough to require attention.\n\nCredibility can come from:\n\n- prior successful attacks,\n- realistic movement,\n- correct distance,\n- matching posture,\n- believable timing,\n- and occasional reinforcement.\n\nA signal with no consequence attached to it eventually loses value.\n\nThis links directly to [[Threat]].\n\n## **Reaction Creation**\n\nThe true output of a feint is not the fake action.\n\nIt is the reaction it produces.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- hands rising,\n- hands dropping,\n- stance narrowing,\n- weight shifting,\n- a step backward,\n- a level change,\n- a defensive shell,\n- a post,\n- or a premature counter.\n\nThe useful question is:\n\nWhat response am I trying to create?\n\nWithout that answer, the feint has no clear function.\n\n## **Feint and Prediction**\n\nFeints manipulate expectation.\n\nThe opponent sees a familiar signal and begins predicting the next state.\n\nA successful feint creates:\n\n**Expected state ≠ Actual state**\n\nThat mismatch produces a small prediction error.\n\nThe opponent must then reorient.\n\nThat reorientation costs time.\n\n## **Feint and Timing**\n\n[[Timing]] determines whether the athlete acts while the opponent is still committed to the false prediction.\n\nIf the athlete waits too long, the opponent re-stabilises.\n\nThe useful window often exists immediately after the reaction begins.\n\nThe sequence becomes:\n\n**Feint → Reaction begins → Real action enters**\n\nnot:\n\n**Feint → Wait → Attack**\n\n## **Feint and Exposure**\n\nA successful feint creates [[Exposure]].\n\nThe opponent reorganises protection around one expected threat and weakens another area.\n\nThis can create:\n\n- structural exposure,\n- perceptual exposure,\n- or both.\n\nThe feint therefore acts as a way of manufacturing openings instead of waiting for them.\n\n## **Feint and Option Compression**\n\nA credible signal can narrow the opponent’s decision field.\n\nIf they believe one threat is imminent, their system may temporarily prioritise only a small number of defensive responses.\n\nThis reduces [[Option Density]].\n\nThe athlete gains an advantage by creating a more predictable response field.\n\n## **Feint and Initiative**\n\nA feint can establish [[Initiative]] without requiring heavy commitment.\n\nThe athlete presents the first meaningful problem.\n\nThe opponent reacts.\n\nThe athlete then selects from the reaction.\n\nThis is a low-cost way of gaining authorship over the exchange.\n\n## **Feint Chains**\n\nFeints can be chained.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Feint A → reaction**\n\n↓\n\n**Feint B built from reaction**\n\n↓\n\n**Real action**\n\nHowever, complexity only helps if each signal remains readable and credible.\n\nToo many meaningless signals create noise rather than useful reaction.\n\n## **Over-Feinting**\n\nFeints lose value when overused.\n\nIf the opponent repeatedly sees the same signal without consequence, they update their model:\n\nThis signal does not require immediate response.\n\nThe feint stops creating meaningful state change.\n\nThis means deception must remain connected to genuine threat.\n\n## **Defensive Feinting**\n\nFeints can also be used defensively.\n\nAn athlete may present:\n\n- apparent retreat,\n- apparent vulnerability,\n- an opening,\n- or a false timing cue\n\nto draw the opponent into a predictable action.\n\nThe athlete then acts on the commitment.\n\nThis converts defence into controlled invitation.\n\n## **Perceptual Load**\n\nFeints increase perceptual load.\n\nThe opponent must decide:\n\n- Is this real?\n- Do I respond?\n- If I respond, how much?\n- What else could follow?\n\nRepeated uncertainty can slow clean selection.\n\nThe athlete who feints effectively is not only moving the opponent physically.\n\nThey are shaping what the opponent must process.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether the feint actually produces a reaction,\n- whether that reaction is consistent,\n- whether the athlete acts while the reaction window is open,\n- whether repeated feints have lost credibility,\n- and whether the athlete is feinting without a clear intended response.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYour level change consistently drew their hands down, but you attacked after they had already reset. The reaction was there — the timing of the follow-up was late.\n\nOr:\n\nThe feint stopped working because it was shown repeatedly without ever being converted into a real attack.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of simply saying:\n\n“Use more feints.”\n\nFRAME should identify:\n\n“Show the signal that makes them protect the lower line, then attack as that protection appears.”\n\nThe feint should always connect:\n\n**signal → expected response → intended continuation**\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nA feint is **controlled prediction manipulation**.\n\nThe athlete deliberately introduces a signal that causes the opponent’s system to commit resources toward an anticipated state.\n\nWhen the anticipated state fails to occur, the opponent experiences a brief reorientation cost.\n\nThat cost is the opening.\n\nThe feint therefore works upstream of technique:\n\nit alters the opponent’s model before it alters their body.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**A good feint does not merely hide intention. It creates the reaction the athlete wants to act on.**\n\nThe value of the feint lies in the state change it causes, not in how convincing the fake looks."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Footwork and Pivoting.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Footwork and Pivoting","body":"## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nFootwork and Pivoting are the coordinated repositioning of the feet, base, hips, and body orientation to change angle, distance, alignment, or force direction without unnecessarily sacrificing balance.\n\nFootwork changes where the athlete is.\n\nPivoting changes how the athlete is oriented.\n\nTogether they reshape the geometry of the exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\nMovement should improve position, not merely create motion.\n\n**Step → Reorganise base → Change angle or distance → Preserve alignment → Create new action-space**\n\nGood footwork makes future actions easier.\n\nPoor footwork often forces the athlete to compensate with strength, speed, or recovery.\n\n## Why It Matters\nFootwork influences:\n\n- range\n- angle\n- balance\n- takedown entries\n- striking position\n- defensive exits\n- pressure\n- passing\n- wall wrestling\n- clinch control\n- re-attacks\n- transitions\n\nMany technical failures that appear to be upper-body problems are actually caused by poor lower-body positioning.\n\n## Stepping\nA step changes the athlete’s base location.\n\nUseful stepping should preserve enough structure to remain capable of:\n\n- attacking\n- defending\n- changing direction\n- absorbing force\n\nLarge steps may create distance quickly but can increase commitment.\n\nSmall steps may preserve stability but reduce speed of repositioning.\n\n## Pivoting\nA pivot changes orientation around a relatively stable point.\n\nCommon pivot points include:\n\n- lead foot\n- rear foot\n- planted leg\n- hip\n- opponent connection\n- post\n- boundary\n\nPivoting allows the athlete to change angle without necessarily giving up distance.\n\n## Angle Creation\nFootwork is one of the main ways an athlete creates asymmetric position.\n\nExample:\n\nSquare stance  \n→ lateral step  \n→ pivot  \n→ opponent remains facing original direction  \n→ attacker gains outside angle\n\nThe positional advantage may occur before any technique begins.\n\n## Base Reorganisation\nEvery step temporarily changes the base.\n\nDuring movement, Synochi should consider:\n\n- which foot is loaded\n- which foot is moving\n- how wide the stance becomes\n- where the centre of mass travels\n- whether the athlete can still change direction\n\nFootwork is therefore a continuous process of rebuilding base.\n\n## Weight Transfer\nStepping requires load transfer.\n\nTypical sequence:\n\n**load one leg → free opposite leg → step → receive weight → reorganise**\n\nThe timing of this transfer determines both mobility and vulnerability.\n\nA moving foot is usually unavailable as a strong base point.\n\n## Pivot and Rotation\nPivoting allows rotational force without excessive twisting through the knees, hips, or spine.\n\nEffective pivots can align:\n\n- hips with strike\n- shoulders with takedown finish\n- torso with defensive exit\n- pressure with opponent movement\n\n## Footwork and Distance\nFootwork regulates distance through:\n\n- advance\n- retreat\n- lateral movement\n- diagonal movement\n- circling\n- stance adjustment\n\nDistance changes should normally preserve the athlete’s ability to act immediately afterward.\n\n## Footwork and Initiative\nThe athlete controlling movement geometry often controls the exchange.\n\nExamples:\n\n- cutting off retreat\n- forcing opponent toward boundary\n- stepping outside stance\n- denying preferred angle\n- making opponent repeatedly reset\n\nFootwork can therefore create initiative without direct contact.\n\n## Footwork and Pressure\nPressure is not simply moving forward.\n\nEffective pressure often requires:\n\n- closing exits\n- maintaining stance\n- adjusting angle\n- keeping hips underneath force\n- preventing opponent from circling free\n\nAn athlete who walks directly forward may create movement without actually controlling space.\n\n## Footwork and Defence\nDefensive footwork can:\n\n- remove the target\n- reset angle\n- widen the defensive base\n- escape pressure\n- avoid linear retreat\n- create counter-attacking position\n\nA good defensive step should ideally solve the immediate threat while improving the next position.\n\n## Direction Change\nHigh-quality footwork preserves the ability to reverse or redirect movement.\n\nOvercommitted steps reduce adaptability.\n\nUseful movement tends to maintain:\n\n- balance\n- posture\n- loaded hips\n- available stance\n- directional options\n\n## Crossed Feet\nCrossing the feet can temporarily reduce base integrity.\n\nHowever, crossed-foot movement is not universally wrong.\n\nIt may be useful during:\n\n- rapid repositioning\n- running movement\n- specific transitional patterns\n\nThe important variable is whether the athlete remains able to manage force and direction during the crossing phase.\n\n## Stance Integrity\nFootwork should normally preserve a usable relationship between:\n\n- feet\n- knees\n- hips\n- torso\n- head\n\nIf the feet move without the rest of the structure reorganising, alignment may break.\n\n## Footwork Rhythm\nMovement can establish predictable rhythm.\n\nExamples:\n\nstep-step-pause  \ncircle-reset  \nforward-forward-back\n\nPredictable rhythm can be exploited.\n\nChanging cadence can create timing windows.\n\n## Footwork and Feints\nA partial step, weight shift, or pivot can create the appearance of movement without full commitment.\n\nThis can force:\n\n- stance adjustment\n- defensive reaction\n- post\n- weight transfer\n\nFootwork therefore contributes directly to feinting.\n\n## Boundary Interaction\nNear a wall, cage, or mat edge, footwork becomes more constrained.\n\nEffective movement may involve:\n\n- pivoting away from boundary\n- lateral escape\n- turning opponent\n- cutting off the exit\n- using boundary as an anchor\n\n## Footwork Failure\nCommon failures include:\n\n- stepping too wide\n- feet becoming too narrow\n- crossing under pressure\n- moving feet without hips\n- retreating in straight lines\n- pivoting without base\n- overcommitting weight during entry\n- failing to reset stance after movement\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"Your shot was too slow.\"\n\nFootwork-aware analysis:\n\n\"Your entry started from too far away and your first step travelled straight forward without changing the angle. By the time your hips arrived, his base was already organised to defend.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nFootwork and Pivoting help Synochi explain:\n\n- why entries fail before contact\n- why athletes struggle to create angles\n- why pressure becomes easy to escape\n- why balance breaks during movement\n- why defensive exits remain vulnerable\n- how movement creates or removes attack pathways\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about footwork through:\n\n**Observe stance → Detect load transfer → Track step or pivot → Update base → Measure angle and distance change → Assess alignment → Identify new action-space**\n\nFootwork should interact with base, angle, distance, centre of mass, weight transfer, timing, rhythm, and force direction.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Angle]]\n[[Distance]]\n[[Centre of Mass]]\n[[Weight Transfer]]\n[[Timing]]\n[[Rhythm]]\n[[Force Direction]]\n[[Off-Balancing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Force Direction.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Force Direction","body":"# Force Direction\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nForce Direction is the orientation along which force is applied, received, redirected, or resisted during a combat exchange.\n\nThe amount of force matters.\n\nThe direction of that force often matters more.\n\nA mechanically efficient action aligns force with the path most likely to disturb structure, move the opponent, preserve balance, or create a desired reaction.\n\n## Core Principle\nForce becomes useful when it is directed into a vulnerable line.\n\n**Force magnitude × Direction × Structure × Timing = Mechanical effect**\n\nPoor direction can make strong effort ineffective.\n\nGood direction can make modest force decisive.\n\n## Why It Matters\nCombat is full of situations where the same action succeeds or fails based on angle of force.\n\nExamples:\n\n- pushing directly into strong base\n- pulling across weak base\n- rotating around a fixed post\n- driving through the hip line\n- lifting against a weighted leg\n- redirecting pressure diagonally instead of resisting it head-on\n\n## Linear Force\nLinear force travels primarily along one direction.\n\nExamples:\n\n- push\n- pull\n- drive\n- lift\n- downward pressure\n\nLinear force is most effective when the opponent cannot reorganise their base along the same line.\n\n## Rotational Force\nRotational force turns the opponent around an axis.\n\nExamples:\n\n- turning the head\n- rotating shoulders against hips\n- steering through an underhook\n- twisting posture during a takedown\n- finishing a joint lock\n\nRotational force can be especially powerful when one part of the body is anchored and another is displaced.\n\n## Vertical Force\nVertical force changes height or loading.\n\nExamples:\n\n- lifting hips\n- elevating a leg\n- compressing downward\n- pulling posture toward the floor\n\nVertical force often changes the opponent’s relationship with base.\n\n## Lateral Force\nLateral force displaces the opponent sideways.\n\nThis can:\n\n- narrow effective base\n- force a post\n- expose an angle\n- create rotational follow-up\n\n## Diagonal Force\nMany effective combat actions operate diagonally.\n\nDiagonal force can combine:\n\n- forward pressure\n- lateral displacement\n- rotation\n- downward loading\n\nThis often bypasses stronger defensive lines.\n\n## Force Against Base\nForce should be interpreted relative to the opponent’s base.\n\nA strong base in one direction may be weak in another.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete with a wide lateral stance may resist side-to-side movement well but remain vulnerable to rotational or backward force.\n\n## Force Vector Change\nEffective athletes frequently change force direction mid-exchange.\n\nExample:\n\npush  \n→ opponent resists  \n→ pull  \n→ opponent steps  \n→ rotate\n\nThe first force may exist only to create the reaction that makes the second direction effective.\n\n## Opposing Force\nWhen two forces meet directly, the stronger structure often wins.\n\nSynochi should identify when an athlete is unnecessarily opposing force head-on rather than:\n\n- redirecting\n- yielding\n- rotating\n- changing angle\n- moving around the force\n\n## Redirection\nForce can be redirected instead of stopped.\n\nExamples:\n\n- guiding pressure past the body\n- turning with a snapdown\n- using frame angle to deflect pressure\n- rotating around an incoming drive\n\nRedirection can preserve energy and create new positional opportunities.\n\n## Force Coupling\nMultiple force directions can be combined.\n\nExamples:\n\npull head downward  \n+ move hips backward  \n+ rotate laterally\n\nThe combined vector may create an effect that no single component could produce alone.\n\n## Directional Vulnerability\nSynochi should attempt to identify which directions the opponent is least prepared to resist.\n\nThis may depend on:\n\n- stance\n- posture\n- weight distribution\n- current posts\n- joint position\n- boundary\n- existing momentum\n\n## Force and Momentum\nExisting movement changes how force should be applied.\n\nApplying force with the opponent’s momentum may amplify movement.\n\nApplying force against it may slow or stop movement but require more effort.\n\n## Force and Timing\nA good force direction used at the wrong moment may still fail.\n\nThe same directional input may become effective once:\n\n- weight shifts\n- post appears\n- base narrows\n- posture breaks\n- opponent commits\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You need more pressure.\"\n\nDirection-aware analysis:\n\n\"You’re driving straight into his strongest base. Shift the force diagonally across his shoulder line and his structure becomes much easier to move.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nForce Direction helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why strength is being wasted\n- why certain takedowns stall\n- why pressure does not move the opponent\n- why redirection works\n- why one angle suddenly makes a technique easier\n- how reactions can be created intentionally\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about force direction through:\n\n**Observe contact → Infer force vector → Compare against opponent structure and base → Detect resistance → Identify redirection or vector change → Observe structural consequence**\n\nForce Direction should interact with angle, leverage, base, weight transfer, momentum, timing, and off-balancing.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Angle]]\n[[Leverage]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Weight Transfer]]\n[[Momentum]]\n[[Timing]]\n[[Off-Balancing]]\n[[Alignment]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Frames.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Frames","body":"# Frames\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nFrames are structural barriers created through skeletal alignment and contact that maintain space, redirect force, block movement, or prevent an opponent from collapsing distance.\n\nA frame is not simply an arm or leg placed between two athletes.\n\nIt is a mechanically supported structure that changes how force travels through the exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\nFrames preserve structure by redirecting pressure into stronger skeletal pathways.\n\n**Contact → Alignment → Structural support → Space preservation or force redirection**\n\nA weak frame absorbs force muscularly.\n\nA strong frame transfers force through connected structure.\n\n## Why Frames Matter\nFrames are central to:\n\n- guard retention\n- pin escape\n- clinch defence\n- takedown defence\n- distance management\n- posture recovery\n- preventing chest-to-chest connection\n\nThey often determine whether pressure becomes control.\n\n## Frame Components\n\n### Contact Point\nWhere the frame meets the opponent.\n\nExamples:\n\n- forearm on shoulder\n- hand on hip\n- shin across torso\n- elbow against knee\n- head against chest\n\n### Supporting Structure\nThe body segment carrying the force.\n\nExamples:\n\n- forearm connected to elbow\n- elbow connected to rib cage\n- shin connected through hip\n- shoulder stacked behind arm\n\n### Base Connection\nThe frame should connect back into the athlete’s larger structure.\n\nWithout support from torso, hips, or base, the frame may collapse.\n\n## Long Frames\nLong frames preserve distance.\n\nExamples:\n\n- extended arm against shoulder\n- foot on hip\n- shin frame\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- maintain space\n- slow forward pressure\n- create room for movement\n\nRisks:\n\n- limb isolation\n- overextension\n- arm exposure\n\n## Short Frames\nShort frames operate closer to the body.\n\nExamples:\n\n- forearm across neck\n- elbow-to-rib structure\n- knee-elbow connection\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- stronger structural support\n- lower isolation risk\n- useful under pressure\n\n## Internal Frames\nSome frames operate inside the athlete’s own structure.\n\nExamples:\n\n- elbow-knee connection\n- forearm protecting neck\n- knees maintaining internal space\n\nThese may not directly push the opponent but prevent structural collapse.\n\n## External Frames\nExternal frames directly contact and manage the opponent.\n\nExamples:\n\n- hand on shoulder\n- shin across hip line\n- forearm against collarbone\n\n## Frame Direction\nFrames work best when oriented against the incoming force.\n\nA frame positioned poorly relative to pressure may collapse even if structurally strong.\n\nFrame effectiveness therefore depends on:\n\n- angle\n- alignment\n- contact position\n- direction of pressure\n- base support\n\n## Frames and Distance\nFrames regulate distance.\n\nThey can:\n\n- maintain distance\n- create space\n- prevent connection\n- delay pressure\n- redirect approach\n\nLoss of effective framing often allows the opponent to convert pressure into control.\n\n## Frame Collapse\nA frame may fail when:\n\n- elbow separates from body\n- joint angle becomes weak\n- opponent redirects around it\n- frame is overloaded\n- base support disappears\n- contact point slips\n- opponent changes angle\n\n## Frame Replacement\nOne frame may need to replace another as the exchange changes.\n\nExample:\n\nhand frame  \n→ forearm frame  \n→ knee frame  \n→ guard recovery\n\nFrame replacement creates continuity of structure.\n\n## Frame Chains\nMultiple frames can operate together.\n\nExamples:\n\nforearm + knee  \nhand + shin  \nelbow + hip frame\n\nCombined frames can create a stronger barrier than one isolated structure.\n\n## Frames and Wedges\nFrames resist or redirect force.\n\nWedges occupy space and prevent structural movement.\n\nThe two often overlap but are not identical.\n\nA frame can become a wedge when it prevents movement through fixed spatial occupation.\n\n## Frames and Connection\nFrames often regulate whether connection is allowed.\n\nDefensively:\n\nframe prevents chest connection.\n\nOffensively:\n\nframe may guide opponent into a predictable reaction.\n\nFrames are therefore not purely defensive.\n\n## Dynamic Framing\nFrames should adapt during movement.\n\nA static frame against changing pressure may become weak.\n\nEffective framing involves:\n\n- angle adjustment\n- contact replacement\n- posture adjustment\n- connection to hip movement\n- timing\n\n## Frame Economy\nAthletes should avoid using excessive muscular force where skeletal structure can do the work.\n\nEfficient framing reduces energy cost.\n\nPoor framing often creates:\n\n- fatigue\n- arm burnout\n- delayed reactions\n- collapsing posture\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"Your frame collapsed.\"\n\nFrame-aware analysis:\n\n\"Your forearm was in position, but your elbow drifted away from your ribs and lost structural support. Once that happened, the frame became muscular rather than skeletal.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nUnderstanding frames helps Synochi diagnose:\n\n- why pressure gets through\n- why distance collapses\n- why guard retention fails\n- why escapes stall\n- why athletes fatigue under pins\n- which structure should be rebuilt first\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about frames through:\n\n**Detect contact → Assess alignment → Identify structural support → Compare against incoming force → Track deformation → Detect collapse or replacement → Update space and constraint model**\n\nFrames should interact with distance, wedges, posture, alignment, pressure, and recovery.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Alignment]]\n[[Posture]]\n[[Distance]]\n[[Wedges]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Connection]]\n[[Recovery]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Head Position.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Head Position","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nHead Position is the use of the head as an active point of control, structure, and directional influence.\n\nThe head is not simply carried along with the body.\n\nIn close-range grappling, it can function as:\n\n- a frame,\n- a wedge,\n- a post,\n- a steering point,\n- a source of pressure,\n- and a way to deny the opponent preferred movement.\n\nGood head position can make the rest of the body stronger.\n\nPoor head position can weaken otherwise correct grips and body position.\n\n## **Head Position and Structure**\n\nThe head sits at the end of the spine.\n\nWhere the head goes affects:\n\n- spinal alignment,\n- posture,\n- balance,\n- shoulder position,\n- hip direction,\n- and the athlete’s ability to generate force.\n\nIf the opponent controls the head, they can often influence the rest of the body.\n\nThis is why head control frequently has an effect far greater than the size of the contact point suggests.\n\n## **Winning Head Position**\n\nWinning head position usually means placing the head where it:\n\n- strengthens your own alignment,\n- disrupts the opponent’s alignment,\n- blocks a preferred direction,\n- creates access to an attack,\n- or prevents the opponent establishing their own control.\n\nThis can include:\n\n- forehead under the jaw,\n- temple-to-temple pressure,\n- head inside the opponent’s head position,\n- head against the chest or shoulder,\n- using the skull to occupy space the opponent needs.\n\nThe exact position depends on the exchange.\n\n## **Inside vs Outside Head Position**\n\nHead position often interacts with inside position.\n\nIn many wrestling and clinch exchanges, inside head position can:\n\n- improve body-lock access,\n- strengthen underhooks,\n- reduce the opponent’s turning ability,\n- and make entries safer.\n\nHowever, outside head position can also be correct depending on the attack.\n\nThe important question is not:\n\nIs the head inside or outside?\n\nIt is:\n\nWhat does this head position allow, deny, or expose in this specific exchange?\n\n## **Directional Control**\n\nThe head can influence where the opponent is able to move.\n\nFor example:\n\nA strong head position may block the opponent from turning toward one side.\n\nThis can force movement in the opposite direction.\n\nThe head therefore contributes to [[Constraint Accumulation]].\n\nIt can help narrow available reactions without relying entirely on grips.\n\n## **Head Position in Takedowns**\n\nDuring takedown entries, head position can affect:\n\n- entry safety,\n- posture,\n- finishing angle,\n- ability to drive,\n- ability to turn the corner,\n- and exposure to counters.\n\nA technically correct shot can become weak if the head is badly placed.\n\nLikewise, strong head position can make a relatively simple takedown difficult to stop.\n\n## **Head Position in Passing**\n\nWhen passing guard, the head can be used to:\n\n- control upper-body direction,\n- prevent the opponent turning in,\n- pin the shoulders,\n- reinforce crossface pressure,\n- occupy defensive space,\n- and help connect upper-body control to hip control.\n\nThe head often acts as an additional limb.\n\nIt can maintain control while the hands are required elsewhere.\n\n## **Head Position in Pins**\n\nIn dominant positions, head position can help prevent:\n\n- turning,\n- bridging into space,\n- recovering inside frames,\n- or reconnecting the knees and elbows.\n\nA strong pin often uses the head as part of a larger system of wedges and pressure rather than treating it independently.\n\n## **Defensive Head Position**\n\nHead position also matters defensively.\n\nAn athlete who allows their head to be turned, lifted, or displaced may lose:\n\n- alignment,\n- ability to bridge,\n- ability to rotate,\n- access to frames,\n- or connection between upper and lower body.\n\nRecovering head position can therefore be an important early step in escaping.\n\n## **Head Position and Visibility**\n\nHead placement also affects what the athlete can perceive.\n\nPoor head position may:\n\n- reduce visual information,\n- turn the spine away from the action,\n- create unnecessary exposure,\n- or delay recognition of transitions.\n\nGood head position should support both control and awareness.\n\n## **Common Diagnostic Pattern**\n\nA frequent technical error is:\n\nCorrect grips, poor head position.\n\nThe athlete may appear to have the right configuration but still lack control because the opponent’s head and spine remain free.\n\nAnother common error is:\n\nHead follows the movement instead of leading or controlling it.\n\nThis makes the athlete structurally reactive.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- who has dominant head position,\n- whether the head supports the athlete’s grips,\n- whether head position blocks or enables movement,\n- whether the athlete loses alignment when the head is displaced,\n- and whether a failed action can be traced back to poor head placement.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYour underhook was present, but your head stayed outside their shoulder line, so they could turn back into you and neutralise the control.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nCoaching should treat head position as part of the technique rather than a minor detail.\n\nInstead of only saying:\n\n“Get the underhook.”\n\nA stronger instruction may be:\n\n“Get the underhook and win the head position so they can’t square back up.”\n\nThe head should contribute to the same directional objective as the rest of the body.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**The head is a control surface.**\n\nGood head position strengthens structure, shapes direction, and removes options.\n\nIn close-range grappling, losing the head battle often means losing more than just the head."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Initiative.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Initiative","body":"# **Initiative**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nInitiative is the temporary control of **who is setting the problem and who is solving it**.\n\nThe athlete with initiative is not necessarily attacking continuously.\n\nThey are shaping the exchange strongly enough that the opponent must adapt to them.\n\nThe basic distinction is:\n\n**Initiator → creates problem**\n\n**Responder → solves problem**\n\nInitiative therefore describes the directional flow of decision-making inside the exchange.\n\n## **Initiative as Decision Control**\n\nCombat is not only a competition of techniques.\n\nIt is also a competition over whose actions organise the next state.\n\nWhen one athlete:\n\n- creates the first credible threat,\n- forces a defensive reaction,\n- controls the pace,\n- dictates range,\n- or repeatedly determines the next transition,\n\nthe opponent’s decision space becomes more reactive.\n\nThe initiator is shaping the local architecture of the exchange.\n\n## **Initiative Is Not Constant Offence**\n\nInitiative should not be confused with reckless forward pressure.\n\nAn athlete may retain initiative while:\n\n- moving backward,\n- drawing an attack,\n- creating a trap,\n- changing range,\n- using feints,\n- or deliberately pausing.\n\nThe important question is:\n\nWho is currently determining what the other athlete has to respond to?\n\n## **Creating Initiative**\n\nInitiative can be established through:\n\n- credible threats,\n- first contact,\n- pressure,\n- angle,\n- distance control,\n- rhythm disruption,\n- feints,\n- positional control,\n- or forcing commitment.\n\nThe first meaningful action is often more important than the first visible action.\n\nA small movement that forces the opponent to reorganise may establish more initiative than a large attack that creates no reaction.\n\n## **Losing Initiative**\n\nInitiative can be lost when the athlete becomes:\n\n- purely reactive,\n- predictable,\n- hesitant,\n- structurally compromised,\n- trapped at the opponent’s preferred distance,\n- or unable to create meaningful threats.\n\nOnce the system becomes dominated by incoming problems, available attention shifts toward survival and response.\n\nThis can reduce offensive selection.\n\n## **Initiative and Reaction Mapping**\n\n[[Reaction Mapping]] becomes more useful when the athlete has initiative.\n\nIf the athlete can reliably create the first threat, they can begin learning:\n\nWhen I create this problem, what response do they tend to give me?\n\nThe exchange moves from passive reaction toward deliberate reaction generation.\n\n## **Initiative and Rhythm**\n\n[[Rhythm]] can establish initiative.\n\nIf one athlete controls the cadence of:\n\n- entry,\n- reset,\n- re-entry,\n- pressure,\n- and pause,\n\nthe opponent may begin synchronising to that temporal structure.\n\nOnce this occurs, even a rhythm break can be used to force adaptation.\n\n## **Initiative and Distance**\n\n[[Distance]] control can determine initiative before an attack begins.\n\nIf one athlete decides:\n\n- when the exchange closes,\n- when it resets,\n- and which range is available,\n\nthey are already shaping the opponent’s options.\n\nDistance control is therefore partly decision control.\n\n## **Initiative and Constraint**\n\nInitiative often grows as the opponent’s available choices shrink.\n\nThis connects to [[Constraint Accumulation]].\n\nThe more the athlete can constrain:\n\n- distance,\n- angle,\n- timing,\n- structure,\n- or response options,\n\nthe more predictable the opponent’s output becomes.\n\nPredictability reinforces initiative.\n\n## **Defensive Initiative**\n\nDefence does not have to mean surrendering initiative.\n\nA defensive action can immediately create a new problem.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Defend → Reorient → Counter**\n\nor:\n\n**Absorb → Redirect → Advance**\n\nThe athlete can move from responder back to initiator without requiring a full reset.\n\nThis transition is important.\n\n## **Initiative Transfer**\n\nInitiative is dynamic.\n\nIt can transfer several times within a single exchange.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Athlete A attacks**\n\n↓\n\n**Athlete B successfully redirects**\n\n↓\n\n**Athlete B immediately creates a new threat**\n\nThe decision direction has changed.\n\nCombat Intelligence should therefore recognise initiative as a moving state rather than a permanent property of either athlete.\n\n## **Initiative Collapse**\n\nSometimes an athlete loses initiative without losing position.\n\nThis may occur through:\n\n- hesitation,\n- overload,\n- loss of confidence,\n- poor recognition,\n- or repeated failed attempts.\n\nExternally, the athlete may still appear safe.\n\nInternally, the exchange has shifted from:\n\n**selection**\n\nto:\n\n**response**\n\nThis can precede visible positional or tactical failure.\n\n## **Perceptual Effects**\n\nInitiative affects perception.\n\nThe athlete setting the problems often has greater capacity to anticipate the next state because their own action created it.\n\nThe responder must process incoming information under tighter temporal pressure.\n\nThis creates an asymmetry:\n\n**Initiator predicts from authored action.**\n\n**Responder interprets under constraint.**\n\nThe difference can compound.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- who is repeatedly creating the first meaningful problem,\n- who is being forced into reactive decisions,\n- when initiative transfers,\n- whether the athlete fails to capitalise after creating a reaction,\n- and whether hesitation causes the athlete to surrender control of the exchange.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou created the first reaction, but then paused long enough for them to reset. The issue was not the entry — it was the loss of initiative immediately afterward.\n\nOr:\n\nYou spent most of the exchange answering their actions. Your best sequence began when you forced them to react first.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of simply telling the athlete:\n\n“Be more aggressive.”\n\nFRAME should identify whether the real issue is initiative.\n\nFor example:\n\n“Give them a problem first, even if it is only a credible feint or positional threat.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Once they react, stay ahead of the reset and create the next problem.”\n\nThis creates purposeful initiative rather than uncontrolled output.\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nInitiative can be understood as **temporary authorship over the exchange**.\n\nThe athlete with initiative has greater influence over:\n\n- what information enters the opponent’s system,\n- which responses become necessary,\n- and which next states become likely.\n\nInitiative therefore acts as a form of **decision-field control**.\n\nIt does not guarantee success.\n\nBut it changes who is adapting to whom.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Initiative belongs to the athlete who is most consistently shaping the next problem.**\n\nStrong combat intelligence is not only the ability to solve situations.\n\nIt is the ability to create situations that are easier for you to solve than for the opponent."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Inside Position.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Inside Position","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nInside Position refers to controlling the space between the opponent’s limbs and their centreline in a way that improves access to control, movement, and attack.\n\nIn many grappling exchanges, the athlete who controls the inside has better ability to:\n\n- connect to the opponent’s body,\n- influence posture,\n- control direction,\n- prevent frames,\n- enter dominant positions,\n- and deny the opponent clean access to their own attacks.\n\nInside Position is therefore not just a hand-fighting concept.\n\nIt is a broader positional advantage.\n\n## **Why It Matters**\n\nOutside control often has to work around the opponent’s structure.\n\nInside control interacts directly with it.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- inside biceps control,\n- underhooks,\n- inside elbow position,\n- inside knee position,\n- head position,\n- inside frames,\n- double-inside hand fighting,\n- controlling the space between the opponent’s arms and torso.\n\nThe inside player often has a shorter path to the opponent’s centre.\n\n## **Centreline Access**\n\nInside Position improves access to the opponent’s centreline.\n\nThis matters because control closer to the centre of the body generally produces more influence over:\n\n- posture,\n- rotation,\n- balance,\n- shoulder position,\n- hip direction,\n- and movement.\n\nControl further from the centre may still be useful, but usually provides less direct influence.\n\n## **Inside Position as a Battle**\n\nMany exchanges can be understood as a competition for inside access.\n\nExamples:\n\n### **Standing**\n\nAthletes fight for:\n\n- inside ties,\n- underhooks,\n- head position,\n- elbow position.\n\nWinning these battles can create easier entries to takedowns or body locks.\n\n### **Guard**\n\nThe bottom athlete may fight to place:\n\n- knees,\n- shins,\n- feet,\n- frames,\n- or hands\n\nbetween themselves and the passer.\n\nThis creates inside structure and prevents chest-to-chest control.\n\n### **Passing**\n\nThe passer often attempts to remove those inside structures and replace them with their own control.\n\nThe exchange can therefore become:\n\n**Bottom athlete builds inside position → passer clears inside position → passer occupies inside space**\n\n## **Inside Space and Control**\n\nInside Position is especially powerful when combined with connection.\n\nSimply placing a limb inside does not automatically create control.\n\nUseful inside position usually requires:\n\n- correct depth,\n- meaningful contact,\n- structural alignment,\n- and the ability to affect the opponent’s movement.\n\nThe goal is not merely to “be inside.”\n\nThe goal is to **own useful inside space**.\n\n## **Offensive Use**\n\nInside Position can create:\n\n- underhooks,\n- body locks,\n- back exposure,\n- passing routes,\n- head-and-arm control,\n- leg-entry access,\n- takedown entries,\n- and submission pathways.\n\nMany attacks become easier once the athlete has won the inside battle first.\n\n## **Defensive Use**\n\nInside Position is equally important defensively.\n\nFrames and inside structures can prevent:\n\n- chest-to-chest connection,\n- crossfaces,\n- upper-body control,\n- hip control,\n- and positional consolidation.\n\nFor a pinned athlete, recovering inside position is often more important than immediately trying to escape.\n\nThe sequence may be:\n\n**Recover inside structure → create separation → recover movement → escape**\n\n## **Losing Inside Position**\n\nWhen an athlete loses inside position, they often become structurally disadvantaged before the final position is fully lost.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- elbows being separated from the torso,\n- knees being cleared away from the centre,\n- an opponent establishing an underhook,\n- losing head position,\n- frames being removed.\n\nThese are early indicators that control is shifting.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- who owns the inside space,\n- when inside position changes hands,\n- whether the athlete recognises that loss early,\n- and whether the positional breakdown begins before the obvious failure.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nThe pass was not lost when the opponent settled side control. It began when your knee and elbow connection were cleared and you lost inside position.\n\nThis creates more actionable feedback.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only coaching the final position, FRAME can identify the earlier battle.\n\nFor example:\n\nRather than:\n\n“Don’t get flattened.”\n\nThe useful instruction may be:\n\n“Win the inside underhook before their chest connects.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Recover knee-elbow connection before trying to turn.”\n\nThis targets the structural cause rather than the final symptom.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Inside Position gives access to the opponent’s structure while protecting access to your own.**\n\nMany grappling exchanges are won or lost before the final position is established because one athlete has already gained control of the inside space.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Constraint Accumulation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Interception.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Interception","body":"# **Interception**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nInterception is the act of meeting an opponent’s action during its development rather than waiting for it to fully resolve.\n\nThe opponent begins changing state.\n\nThe athlete recognises that transition early enough to act inside it.\n\nThe central sequence is:\n\n**Opponent initiates → transition opens → interception occurs → original action is disrupted**\n\nInterception is therefore a form of action taken **inside the opponent’s commitment window**.\n\n## **Why Interception Works**\n\nAn action is often most vulnerable before it reaches full structure.\n\nDuring development, the opponent may be:\n\n- shifting weight,\n- extending a limb,\n- changing level,\n- rotating,\n- narrowing base,\n- committing attention,\n- or moving through a predictable line.\n\nThe athlete is not attacking a finished position.\n\nThey are acting on the transition that produces it.\n\n## **Interception vs Reaction**\n\nReaction waits for the action to become clear.\n\nInterception acts earlier.\n\nA purely reactive sequence may look like:\n\n**Threat develops → action completes → defence begins**\n\nInterception compresses that delay:\n\n**Threat develops → action recognised → response enters before completion**\n\nThis reduces the opponent’s available time to reorganise.\n\n## **Interception and Timing**\n\n[[Timing]] is central.\n\nToo early, and the signal may not yet be reliable.\n\nToo late, and the opponent may already have reached stable structure.\n\nThe useful window exists when commitment has become meaningful but recovery is not yet available.\n\nThis makes interception a high-value expression of timing.\n\n## **Interception and Commitment**\n\n[[Commitment]] creates the opportunity.\n\nAs the opponent allocates more structure toward one action, alternative pathways reduce.\n\nOnce enough commitment is present, their trajectory becomes easier to predict.\n\nInterception exploits that narrowing.\n\nThe athlete acts when:\n\n**prediction confidence rises while opponent optionality falls.**\n\n## **Interception and Distance**\n\n[[Distance]] determines whether interception is physically available.\n\nThe athlete must already be positioned close enough to act during the development window.\n\nPoor distance means the opportunity may be recognised but unreachable.\n\nGood distance allows perception to convert into action.\n\n## **Interception and Angle**\n\n[[Angle]] can make interception safer and more effective.\n\nRather than meeting the opponent’s force directly, the athlete may:\n\n- move outside the line,\n- redirect the trajectory,\n- enter on a flank,\n- or attack a newly exposed structure.\n\nThe best interception often changes both timing and geometry.\n\n## **Offensive Interception**\n\nInterception can be offensive.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- striking into an opponent’s entry,\n- attacking during a level change,\n- entering while the opponent resets,\n- taking space during a stance transition,\n- attacking a limb as it extends,\n- or exploiting a directional commitment.\n\nThe athlete uses the opponent’s action as the trigger for their own.\n\n## **Defensive Interception**\n\nInterception can also prevent the opponent from establishing the state they want.\n\nInstead of solving the completed problem, the athlete stops the problem from stabilising.\n\nThis may mean:\n\n- framing before connection is established,\n- denying an entry before positional control forms,\n- disrupting balance before the attack consolidates,\n- or changing angle before the line becomes dangerous.\n\nEarly defence often costs less than late recovery.\n\n## **Interception and Initiative**\n\nInterception can transfer [[Initiative]] immediately.\n\nThe opponent begins as initiator.\n\nThe athlete intercepts.\n\nThe exchange changes direction before the original action resolves.\n\nThe responder becomes the new problem-setter.\n\nThis makes interception one of the cleanest mechanisms for converting defence into offence.\n\n## **Perceptual Requirement**\n\nInterception depends on recognising **early signals**.\n\nThese may include:\n\n- weight shift,\n- posture change,\n- shoulder rotation,\n- hand movement,\n- level change,\n- stance compression,\n- rhythm break,\n- or directional loading.\n\nThe athlete must learn which signals are predictive and which are noise.\n\nInterception therefore depends on perception as much as speed.\n\n## **False Interception**\n\nActing too readily on weak signals creates vulnerability.\n\nIf the athlete attempts to intercept every cue, the opponent can exploit this with:\n\n- feints,\n- rhythm changes,\n- false entries,\n- and delayed actions.\n\nInterception must therefore remain confidence-weighted.\n\nThe athlete should not respond merely because something moved.\n\nThey should respond because the signal has crossed a meaningful threshold.\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nInterception is **early policy selection during opponent state transition**.\n\nThe opponent’s future state has become predictable enough to act on, but has not yet stabilised.\n\nThis creates a temporary asymmetry:\n\n**their system is committed to becoming something**\n\nwhile:\n\n**your system is still free to select around it.**\n\nThat is the interception window.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether the athlete recognises initiation early enough,\n- whether they wait until the opponent is already structurally established,\n- which signals reliably precede the opponent’s action,\n- whether interception attempts are triggered by real commitment or weak cues,\n- and whether successful defence could have occurred earlier in the sequence.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou defended the shot after their hips were already connected. The earlier window appeared during the level change, before the entry gained structure.\n\nOr:\n\nYou read the entry correctly, but reacted to the first feint as if it were full commitment. They used that early response to open the real attack.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of simply saying:\n\n“React faster.”\n\nFRAME should identify the earlier signal.\n\nFor example:\n\n“Do not wait for the attack to arrive. Their weight shift is the first reliable cue — that is where the interception window begins.”\n\nThis turns speed into recognition.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**The cheapest problem to solve is often the one that has not fully formed yet.**\n\nInterception acts on the opponent’s transition before it becomes a stable threat.\n\n**Recognise commitment early. Act before structure completes.**"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Leverage.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Leverage","body":"# Leverage\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nLeverage is the use of structure, distance, angle, attachment, and force application to create a mechanical advantage that allows one athlete to produce a greater effect with less direct force.\n\nLeverage changes the relationship between effort and outcome.\n\nIt allows an athlete to overcome strength, resistance, or positional disadvantage by improving how force is transmitted through the system.\n\n## Core Principle\nForce alone does not determine mechanical advantage.\n\n**Force × Structure × Position × Direction = Functional leverage**\n\nThe same amount of force can produce radically different outcomes depending on where and how it is applied.\n\n## Leverage Requires Relationship\nLeverage does not exist in isolation.\n\nIt emerges through the relationship between:\n\n- athlete structure\n- opponent structure\n- contact points\n- distance\n- angle\n- direction of force\n- available base\n\nA movement that has strong leverage in one configuration may have almost none in another.\n\n## Leverage Points\nA leverage point is a location where applied force produces disproportionate structural effect.\n\nCommon leverage targets include:\n\n- joints\n- distal limbs\n- head\n- hips\n- shoulders\n- centre of mass\n- posting limbs\n\nControlling further from a joint or rotational axis often increases available leverage.\n\n## Lever Length\nLonger effective levers can increase mechanical advantage.\n\nExample:\n\nControlling near the wrist may provide greater rotational influence over the arm than controlling near the shoulder.\n\nHowever, longer leverage often requires stronger positional control to prevent disengagement.\n\n## Fulcrum\nMany grappling actions contain an effective fulcrum.\n\nThe fulcrum may be:\n\n- hip\n- thigh\n- forearm\n- shin\n- shoulder\n- opponent's own body\n- floor\n- cage\n\nThe exact anatomical structure matters less than the mechanical role it performs.\n\n## Rotational Leverage\nMany combat movements are rotational rather than linear.\n\nExamples:\n\n- turning the head to redirect posture\n- rotating the shoulder during an arm lock\n- rotating hips during a sweep\n- twisting the torso during takedown finishing\n\nSmall rotational inputs can create large structural consequences when applied against constrained joints or posture.\n\n## Positional Leverage\nMechanical advantage may come from position rather than limb length.\n\nExamples:\n\n- being underneath the opponent's centre of mass\n- controlling the inside angle\n- placing hips beneath theirs\n- controlling the end of a lever while their base is restricted\n\nPosition can multiply the effectiveness of otherwise modest force.\n\n## Leverage and Constraint\nLeverage increases when the opponent cannot freely move with the applied force.\n\nIf the opponent can simply rotate, step, or retract, leverage may dissipate.\n\nConstraint therefore increases usable leverage.\n\n**Leverage + Constraint = Greater structural effect**\n\n## Leverage and Base\nThe athlete applying leverage also requires a structure capable of transmitting force.\n\nPoor base may cause the athlete to move themselves rather than the opponent.\n\nEffective leverage often depends on:\n\n- stable base\n- aligned posture\n- connected kinetic chain\n- controlled contact point\n\n## Leverage vs Strength\nStrength increases available force.\n\nLeverage increases the efficiency of that force.\n\nThey are not opposites.\n\nA stronger athlete with superior leverage may create overwhelming mechanical advantage.\n\n## Dynamic Leverage\nLeverage changes continuously during movement.\n\nA position may briefly create strong leverage before disappearing.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent posts hand  \n→ elbow extends  \n→ lever becomes available  \n→ opponent retracts  \n→ leverage disappears\n\nSynochi should therefore treat leverage as state-dependent and time-sensitive.\n\n## Leverage Chains\nMultiple leverage relationships may combine.\n\nExample:\n\nhead control  \n→ posture broken  \n→ weight shifts  \n→ base narrows  \n→ leg becomes light  \n→ takedown becomes available\n\nThe initial leverage does not necessarily produce the final outcome directly.\n\nIt may create the conditions for another leverage point.\n\n## Defensive Leverage\nLeverage also matters defensively.\n\nExamples:\n\n- framing against the shoulder\n- posting against the hip\n- using skeletal structure to maintain distance\n- connecting elbow to knee\n\nDefensive leverage allows structure to absorb or redirect force without matching force directly.\n\n## Leverage Failure\nLeverage may fail when:\n\n- fulcrum is misplaced\n- lever is too short\n- opponent is unconstrained\n- force direction is poor\n- base is unstable\n- contact point slips\n- athlete applies force through weak alignment\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You need to pull harder.\"\n\nLeverage-aware analysis:\n\n\"Your grip is strong enough, but you're controlling too close to the shoulder and your angle is poor. Extending the lever and changing the direction of force should create more effect without requiring more strength.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nUnderstanding leverage helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why techniques feel effortless or difficult\n- why stronger effort sometimes fails\n- why certain control points matter\n- how angles change mechanical advantage\n- where force should be applied\n- how structure can replace muscular effort\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about leverage through:\n\n**Identify contact → Detect lever and fulcrum relationship → Assess constraint → Assess base → Infer force direction → Estimate mechanical advantage → Observe outcome**\n\nLeverage should interact with posture, wedges, pressure, weight transfer, angle, and constraint rather than exist as an isolated concept.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Angle]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Wedges]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Weight Transfer]]\n[[Off-Balancing]]\n[[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Line of Attack and Line of Movement.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Line of Attack and Line of Movement","body":"## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nLine of Attack and Line of Movement describe the path along which an athlete directs force, body mass, limbs, or positional intent through space.\n\nThe line of attack is the path through which an offensive action is projected.\n\nThe line of movement is the path along which the athlete or opponent is actually travelling.\n\nThese lines may align, diverge, intersect, or change during the exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\nCombat geometry changes when movement and force travel along different lines.\n\n**Position → Line → Direction → Intersection → Mechanical consequence**\n\nA successful action often depends on creating a line the opponent is poorly organised to defend.\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany techniques fail not because the athlete chose the wrong action, but because they attacked along a strong defensive line.\n\nExamples:\n\n- shooting directly into square hips\n- striking down the centre against a well-covered guard\n- passing straight into frames\n- pulling against a stable base\n- retreating along the same line as incoming pressure\n\nChanging the line can change the entire mechanical problem.\n\n## Line of Attack\nThe line of attack is the direction along which offensive force or threat is projected.\n\nExamples:\n\n- straight-line strike\n- diagonal knee cut\n- rotational arm drag\n- level change into hips\n- snapdown through head-and-spine line\n\nThe same target can often be attacked through several different lines.\n\n## Line of Movement\nThe line of movement is the actual path taken by the athlete’s body or base.\n\nPossible lines include:\n\n- forward\n- backward\n- lateral\n- diagonal\n- circular\n- rotational\n\nMovement direction influences which attacks and defensive actions remain available.\n\n## Alignment Between Lines\nThe athlete’s line of movement and line of attack may align.\n\nExample:\n\nStep forward  \n→ hips follow  \n→ strike travels forward\n\nThis can maximise force transfer.\n\nThey may also deliberately differ.\n\nExample:\n\nStep laterally  \n→ attack travels diagonally inward\n\nThis creates angle and asymmetry.\n\n## Intersecting Lines\nMany successful exchanges occur when one athlete moves off the opponent’s primary line while maintaining their own offensive line.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent drives forward  \n→ athlete pivots outside  \n→ incoming line misses centre  \n→ counterattack enters from new angle\n\nThe key advantage comes from changing the relationship between the two lines.\n\n## Strong and Weak Lines\nAthletes tend to have directions in which their structure is stronger or weaker.\n\nA strong line may be supported by:\n\n- aligned base\n- hips behind force\n- stable posture\n- available posts\n\nA weak line may contain:\n\n- poor support\n- restricted rotation\n- narrow base\n- overloaded limb\n- limited recovery options\n\nSynochi should therefore evaluate line quality relative to structure.\n\n## Line Change\nLines can change mid-action.\n\nExample:\n\nInitial forward entry  \n→ opponent resists  \n→ athlete pivots  \n→ force redirects laterally\n\nChanging line can transform a stalled attack into a successful one.\n\n## Cutting the Line\nAn athlete can intercept an opponent’s movement path rather than chase their current position.\n\nExamples:\n\n- cutting off cage movement\n- stepping across retreat path\n- passing ahead of hip rotation\n- meeting a takedown entry before it reaches centre line\n\nThis converts prediction into positional advantage.\n\n## Escaping the Line\nDefensive movement often succeeds by leaving the incoming force path.\n\nExamples:\n\n- pivot\n- lateral step\n- angle change\n- hip movement\n- head movement\n\nMoving slightly off-line may be cheaper than resisting force directly.\n\n## Line and Distance\nDistance changes along different lines.\n\nA diagonal step may close distance faster to one target while increasing distance from another.\n\nSynochi should therefore reason about distance directionally rather than only as absolute separation.\n\n## Line and Footwork\nFootwork determines whether the athlete can establish or escape a line while preserving base.\n\nPoor footwork may create the correct angle but leave the athlete mechanically unable to act from it.\n\n## Line and Force Direction\nForce Direction describes where force travels.\n\nLine of Attack describes the broader spatial pathway through which the offensive action develops.\n\nThey overlap but are not identical.\n\nA strike may travel along one line while rotational force is generated through another.\n\n## Line and Threat\nA threat may exist because the attacker has established a strong line before the final action occurs.\n\nExample:\n\nOutside angle obtained  \n→ shoulder line exposed  \n→ opponent now vulnerable to takedown or strike\n\nThe geometric advantage precedes the technique.\n\n## Predictive Value\nRepeated line preferences can reveal tactical patterns.\n\nExamples:\n\n- opponent always exits clockwise\n- athlete consistently attacks straight forward\n- passer repeatedly enters on same diagonal\n- wrestler repeatedly circles toward lead leg\n\nThis can allow Synochi to identify interceptable movement patterns.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"Your entry got stuffed.\"\n\nLine-aware analysis:\n\n\"You attacked directly into his strongest defensive line. The outside step created the better route, but your hips never followed onto that new line.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nLine of Attack and Line of Movement help Synochi explain:\n\n- why angles matter\n- why direct attacks stall\n- how pivots create advantage\n- how pressure should cut off movement\n- how defensive movement can escape force\n- why some entries become available only after repositioning\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about lines through:\n\n**Identify orientation → Track movement path → Identify attack path → Compare lines → Detect intersection or divergence → Evaluate structural advantage → Observe outcome**\n\nThis should interact closely with footwork, angle, distance, force direction, base, and timing.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Footwork and Pivoting]]\n[[Angle]]\n[[Distance]]\n[[Force Direction]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Timing]]\n[[Off-Balancing]]\n[[Centre of Mass]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Load Transfer.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Load Transfer","body":"# Load Transfer\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nLoad Transfer is the movement of mechanical demand from one part of the body or structure to another during combat.\n\nLoad may shift between:\n\n- limbs\n- hips\n- shoulders\n- spine\n- base points\n- opponent contact points\n- floor or boundary\n\nUnderstanding load transfer helps explain why some structures become light, heavy, available, trapped, or vulnerable.\n\n## Core Principle\nWhen load moves somewhere, it leaves somewhere else.\n\n**Load shift → Structure changes → Some options weaken → Others become available**\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany combat actions depend on recognising what has become loaded and what has become free.\n\nExamples:\n\n- opponent shifts weight onto one leg\n- one hand becomes weight-bearing\n- pressure moves from hips to chest\n- head control transfers load into the shoulder\n- a posted limb becomes necessary for balance\n\nThis often creates the next opportunity.\n\n## Load-Bearing Structures\nA structure carrying significant load may become:\n\n- harder to move\n- more stable\n- less available for another task\n- more vulnerable if the load-bearing role is disrupted\n\nExamples:\n\n- loaded lead leg during takedown defence\n- posting arm during scramble\n- near-side hip carrying pressure in half guard\n\n## Unloaded Structures\nWhen load leaves a structure, that structure may become easier to:\n\n- lift\n- move\n- pummel around\n- attack\n- reposition\n- retract\n\nRecognising unloaded structures is often key to efficient technique.\n\n## Internal Load Transfer\nLoad can move within the athlete’s own body.\n\nExample:\n\nrear leg  \n→ lead leg  \n→ hand post\n\nThis may reveal changing balance and intention.\n\n## External Load Transfer\nLoad can also transfer into the opponent.\n\nExamples:\n\n- shoulder pressure into chest\n- hip pressure into thigh\n- head pressure into posture\n- bodyweight through a frame\n\nThis can create control without requiring large muscular force.\n\n## Load Transfer and Base\nAs load shifts, base requirements change.\n\nIf load moves outside the existing base, the athlete may need to:\n\n- step\n- post\n- widen stance\n- rotate\n- change level\n\nFailure to reorganise base can create instability.\n\n## Load Transfer and Pressure\nPressure is often the result of successful load transfer.\n\nThe athlete does not simply \"push.\"\n\nThey organise bodyweight so load travels through structure into the opponent.\n\nExample:\n\nfoot  \n→ leg  \n→ hip  \n→ torso  \n→ shoulder  \n→ opponent\n\n## Load Transfer and Mobility\nA loaded limb is usually less mobile.\n\nThis creates a recurring combat principle:\n\n**Make one structure carry load → attack or move around another**\n\nExample:\n\nForce opponent onto right leg  \n→ left leg becomes light  \n→ reap, sweep, or entry becomes easier\n\n## Load Transfer and Posts\nPosts are strong indicators of load transfer.\n\nIf an athlete suddenly posts a hand, Synochi can infer that part of the body’s load has shifted into that hand.\n\nThis can reveal:\n\n- balance disruption\n- directional force\n- exposed limb\n- narrowed defensive options\n\n## Load Transfer and Rotation\nRotational movements often shift load progressively across the body.\n\nExample:\n\nleft foot  \n→ left hip  \n→ torso  \n→ right shoulder\n\nEfficient rotation transfers load smoothly.\n\nPoor transfer may create disconnection or force leakage.\n\n## Load Transfer and Timing\nA technique may become available only during a brief transfer phase.\n\nExample:\n\nDuring a step, weight leaves one leg before fully arriving on the other.\n\nThat moment can create:\n\n- sweep timing\n- foot sweep opportunity\n- angle change\n- level-change entry\n\n## Load Transfer and Fatigue\nIf load repeatedly concentrates in weak or inefficient structures, fatigue may accelerate.\n\nExamples:\n\n- arms carrying bodyweight instead of skeletal frames\n- shoulders supporting pressure that could be transferred through hips\n- one leg repeatedly absorbing defensive load\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"His leg was there to attack.\"\n\nLoad-aware analysis:\n\n\"His weight had already transferred onto the far leg, which made the near leg light enough to attack with much less resistance.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nLoad Transfer helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why limbs become available\n- when foot sweeps should happen\n- why pressure feels heavy\n- why posts appear\n- why certain escapes or entries only work at specific moments\n- how bodyweight should move through structure\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about load through:\n\n**Observe posture → Detect loaded structures → Track load shift → Identify newly unloaded structures → Infer mechanical opportunities → Observe resulting action**\n\nLoad Transfer should interact with weight distribution, base, posts, pressure, force direction, timing, and movement.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Weight Transfer]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Posts]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Force Direction]]\n[[Timing]]\n[[Alignment]]\n[[Off-Balancing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Mechanical Advantage.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Mechanical Advantage","body":"# Mechanical Advantage\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nMechanical Advantage is the degree to which an athlete’s position, structure, leverage, angle, connection, and force application allow them to create greater effect than the raw muscular effort being used.\n\nMechanical advantage is the combined outcome of multiple mechanical conditions working in the athlete’s favour.\n\nIt is not one technique.\n\nIt is a relationship.\n\n## Core Principle\nThe athlete with the better mechanical relationship often needs less force to create more effect.\n\n**Structure + Leverage + Angle + Base + Connection + Timing = Mechanical advantage**\n\n## Why It Matters\nCombat athletes constantly seek positions where the mechanical relationship becomes asymmetric.\n\nExamples:\n\n- controlling the end of a limb while the opponent’s proximal structure is fixed\n- applying force across a weak line of base\n- placing bodyweight behind a small contact area\n- isolating one joint against the rest of the body\n- using two connected limbs against one\n\nMechanical advantage explains why some positions feel disproportionately powerful.\n\n## Advantage Through Leverage\nA longer effective lever can multiply force.\n\nExample:\n\nControlling the wrist while stabilising the elbow creates more rotational influence than controlling near the shoulder alone.\n\n## Advantage Through Angle\nChanging angle can reduce the opponent’s ability to resist.\n\nThe same force applied from a different orientation may bypass stronger structures.\n\n## Advantage Through Base\nA stable base allows force to transfer into the opponent rather than being lost in self-stabilisation.\n\n## Advantage Through Connection\nStrong connection allows force to move through the athlete as one unit.\n\nWeak connection causes force leakage.\n\n## Advantage Through Weight Placement\nBody mass can amplify mechanical effect.\n\nExamples:\n\n- chest pressure\n- hip pressure\n- shoulder pressure\n- weighted pins\n\nWhen bodyweight replaces muscular effort, efficiency increases.\n\n## Advantage Through Isolation\nIsolating one part of the opponent reduces the amount of structure available to resist.\n\nExamples:\n\n- isolate elbow\n- isolate wrist\n- isolate knee line\n- separate head from hips\n\nThe smaller the defending system becomes, the easier it is to control.\n\n## Advantage Through Multipoint Control\nMechanical advantage often increases when the athlete controls multiple points simultaneously.\n\nExample:\n\nhead + arm  \nhip + knee  \nwrist + elbow\n\nThis reduces the opponent’s degrees of freedom.\n\n## Relative Strength\nMechanical advantage changes the amount of muscular strength required.\n\nA stronger athlete may succeed with poor mechanics.\n\nA mechanically advantaged athlete may succeed despite lower absolute strength.\n\nThe highest performance combines both.\n\n## Mechanical Advantage and Constraint\nConstraints increase mechanical advantage by preventing the opponent from moving with the force.\n\nExample:\n\nRotational force against a free limb may simply move the limb.\n\nRotational force against a trapped limb can produce significant joint stress.\n\n## Mechanical Advantage and Direction\nForce is most effective when applied into the opponent’s weak structural direction.\n\nMechanical advantage therefore depends on direction as much as magnitude.\n\n## Dynamic Mechanical Advantage\nMechanical advantage changes continuously.\n\nA dominant position can lose mechanical advantage if:\n\n- base collapses\n- connection breaks\n- opponent restores alignment\n- force direction changes\n- weight shifts poorly\n\nLikewise, an apparently neutral exchange can suddenly become mechanically asymmetric.\n\n## Mechanical Advantage Transfer\nAdvantage can move from one athlete to another during transitions.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete A controls head position.\n\nAthlete B recovers inside position.\n\nA loses angle.\n\nB establishes underhook.\n\nMechanical advantage shifts before the nominal position changes.\n\n## Local vs Global Advantage\nAn athlete may hold mechanical advantage in one area while losing elsewhere.\n\nExample:\n\nTop athlete controls upper body.\n\nBottom athlete controls lower-body inside position.\n\nThis creates a mixed state rather than total dominance.\n\n## Advantage Stacking\nMultiple small advantages can combine into a large one.\n\nExample:\n\nbetter angle  \n+ stronger base  \n+ head control  \n+ weight placement  \n+ isolated limb\n\nEach alone may be manageable.\n\nTogether they create dominant mechanics.\n\n## Mechanical Disadvantage\nAn athlete is mechanically disadvantaged when they must use disproportionately more effort for less effect.\n\nSigns may include:\n\n- straining\n- collapsing posture\n- repeated failed movement\n- excessive muscular tension\n- inability to move opponent despite effort\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"He was just stronger.\"\n\nMechanical analysis:\n\n\"He had the stronger position mechanically: better head position, wider base, your elbow isolated, and his weight aligned through the control. Strength contributed, but the structure amplified it.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nMechanical Advantage helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why certain positions feel overwhelmingly strong\n- why some techniques work with little effort\n- why strength appears to matter more in bad structure\n- how several small positional wins compound\n- where the athlete should improve the relationship before adding force\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should estimate mechanical advantage through:\n\n**Assess structure → Evaluate leverage → Compare angle → Assess base → Detect connection → Evaluate constraint → Estimate force efficiency → Observe outcome**\n\nMechanical Advantage should function as a synthesis concept connecting multiple universal mechanics.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Leverage]]\n[[Alignment]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Connection]]\n[[Angle]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Force Direction]]\n[[Centre of Mass]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Off-Balancing.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Off-Balancing","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nOff-Balancing is the process of moving an opponent away from a stable base so they must react before they can effectively defend or attack.\n\nThe goal is not simply to move the opponent.\n\nIt is to make their current structure insufficient for the forces acting on it.\n\nA useful sequence is:\n\n**Stable Base → Displacement → Corrective Reaction → Exposure**\n\n## **Why It Matters**\n\nA well-balanced opponent can resist force efficiently.\n\nAn off-balanced opponent must first recover stability.\n\nThat recovery creates opportunity.\n\nThis can make:\n\n- takedowns easier,\n- sweeps easier,\n- passes easier,\n- positional transitions cleaner,\n- and attacks harder to defend.\n\n## **Base and Centre**\n\nBalance depends on the relationship between:\n\n- centre of mass,\n- base of support,\n- posture,\n- contact points,\n- and direction of force.\n\nOff-balancing occurs when the opponent’s centre moves toward or beyond the limits of their current base, or when their base becomes insufficient to support the movement being imposed.\n\n## **Direction**\n\nOff-balancing is directional.\n\nThe useful question is:\n\nWhere is the opponent currently weak?\n\nAn opponent may be stable backward but weak diagonally.\n\nThey may be strong while square but vulnerable once one foot, knee, hand, or hip becomes committed.\n\nGood off-balancing attacks the direction where recovery is hardest.\n\n## **Reaction Creation**\n\nOff-balancing is valuable because it forces correction.\n\nThe opponent may need to:\n\n- step,\n- post,\n- widen their base,\n- shift weight,\n- turn,\n- straighten,\n- or abandon a grip.\n\nThat reaction can create the next attack.\n\nSo the real value may be:\n\n**Off-Balance → Forced Recovery → New Opening**\n\n## **Static vs Dynamic Off-Balancing**\n\n### **Static Off-Balancing**\n\nThe opponent is displaced from a stable structure through controlled pressure, pulling, lifting, or angle.\n\n### **Dynamic Off-Balancing**\n\nThe athlete uses movement, timing, momentum, or an existing reaction to amplify instability.\n\nDynamic off-balancing often requires less force because the opponent is already moving.\n\n## **Off-Balancing in Standing**\n\nIn standing exchanges, off-balancing can come from:\n\n- pulling,\n- pushing,\n- steering,\n- head position,\n- foot sweeps,\n- level changes,\n- body locks,\n- angle changes,\n- and forcing weight onto one leg.\n\nA takedown often becomes easier once the opponent is already correcting balance.\n\n## **Off-Balancing in Guard**\n\nFrom guard, off-balancing can:\n\n- force a hand to post,\n- bring weight forward,\n- lighten one leg,\n- collapse posture,\n- or create rotational instability.\n\nThis can expose:\n\n- sweeps,\n- back takes,\n- leg entries,\n- or upper-body attacks.\n\n## **Off-Balancing in Passing**\n\nPassing can also depend on disrupting the bottom athlete’s ability to maintain a stable defensive structure.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- shifting their hips away from their frames,\n- rotating their shoulders away from their knees,\n- forcing weight onto one side,\n- or making them post instead of frame.\n\nThe passer is not only moving around the guard.\n\nThey are destabilising the structure that makes the guard functional.\n\n## **Timing**\n\nOff-balancing is especially effective during transitions.\n\nAn opponent who is:\n\n- stepping,\n- posting,\n- recovering guard,\n- changing grips,\n- or turning\n\nis already reorganising their base.\n\nThis can create a window where less force produces more disruption.\n\n## **Off-Balancing and Connection**\n\nEffective off-balancing usually requires meaningful [[Connection]].\n\nWithout enough connection, force may move only a limb rather than the opponent’s structure.\n\nThe goal is to affect the opponent’s centre, not simply create superficial motion.\n\n## **Off-Balancing and Reaction Mapping**\n\nOff-balancing should connect to [[Reaction Mapping]].\n\nDifferent forms of instability produce different corrective reactions.\n\nFor example:\n\nPulling weight forward may create a post.\n\nRotating the hips may expose a back step.\n\nLoading weight onto one leg may make the opposite leg easier to attack.\n\nThe reaction becomes part of the attack.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether the athlete attacks a stable opponent directly,\n- whether balance is disrupted before the main attack,\n- what corrective reaction appears,\n- and whether the athlete exploits that reaction.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou entered the takedown while they were still fully balanced. The entry would likely become easier if you first forced their weight onto the lead leg.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only coaching:\n\n“Finish the takedown harder.”\n\nFRAME may identify:\n\n“Create the balance reaction before committing to the finish.”\n\nThis changes the problem from force production to setup quality.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**A stable opponent can defend. An unstable opponent must recover.**\n\nOff-balancing creates opportunity by forcing the opponent to spend movement and attention restoring structure before they can respond freely.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Reaction Mapping]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Option Density.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Option Density","body":"# **Option Density**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nOption Density is the number and quality of viable actions available to an athlete in a given combat state.\n\nNot all positions are equally open.\n\nSome states contain many meaningful possibilities.\n\nOthers are highly compressed.\n\nThe central question is:\n\nHow many useful options remain available, and how easy are they to access?\n\nA state with high Option Density gives the athlete multiple credible pathways.\n\nA state with low Option Density restricts selection and increases predictability.\n\n## **High Option Density**\n\nHigh Option Density may occur when the athlete has:\n\n- strong posture,\n- stable base,\n- good distance,\n- multiple angles,\n- preserved mobility,\n- usable defensive structure,\n- several credible attacks,\n- and enough perceptual bandwidth to recognise them.\n\nThe athlete can branch.\n\nThis creates adaptability.\n\n## **Low Option Density**\n\nLow Option Density occurs when:\n\n- structure is compromised,\n- distance is poor,\n- movement is blocked,\n- base is reduced,\n- posture is broken,\n- attention is overloaded,\n- exposure is high,\n- or several constraints accumulate simultaneously.\n\nThe athlete’s action-space contracts.\n\nTheir next response becomes easier to predict.\n\n## **Compression**\n\nConstraint does not only reduce movement.\n\nIt reduces **decision-space**.\n\nIf an athlete has ten plausible responses, the exchange remains difficult to predict.\n\nIf only two responses remain viable, reaction mapping becomes far more reliable.\n\nThis is why [[Constraint Accumulation]] is so powerful.\n\nIt produces:\n\n**Constraint → Option Compression → Predictable Response**\n\n## **Option Quality**\n\nOption Density is not simply a count.\n\nTen poor options may be less useful than three strong ones.\n\nA viable option should be:\n\n- mechanically accessible,\n- tactically sensible,\n- compatible with the current ruleset,\n- and executable within the available time.\n\nSynochi should therefore distinguish:\n\n**available options**\n\nfrom:\n\n**meaningful options.**\n\n## **Initiative**\n\n[[Initiative]] often creates asymmetry in Option Density.\n\nThe initiator may have multiple planned continuations.\n\nThe responder may have only a narrow set of urgent defensive choices.\n\nThis creates a decision advantage.\n\nThe initiator operates from a broad tree.\n\nThe responder operates from a compressed branch.\n\n## **Exposure**\n\n[[Exposure]] often appears when Option Density falls.\n\nA structurally compromised athlete may technically still have many theoretical actions, but only one or two are realistically usable before the opening is exploited.\n\nThis is a meaningful distinction:\n\n**Theoretical possibility ≠ practical availability.**\n\n## **Distance and Angle**\n\n[[Distance]] and [[Angle]] can rapidly change Option Density.\n\nAt one distance, an athlete may have:\n\n- several strikes,\n- an exit,\n- an entry,\n- and multiple defensive responses.\n\nAt another, those options may disappear entirely.\n\nA small angular change may create several new pathways while denying the opponent the same.\n\n## **Timing**\n\n[[Timing]] determines whether an option remains available long enough to be selected.\n\nAn option can exist briefly and then vanish.\n\nThis means Option Density changes dynamically.\n\nThe athlete is not choosing from a fixed menu.\n\nThe menu itself is constantly changing.\n\n## **Cognitive Option Density**\n\nAn athlete may have many mechanical options but only perceive a few of them.\n\nStress, fatigue, overload, and urgency can compress perceived choice.\n\nThis creates two layers:\n\n**Physical Option Density**  \nWhat actions are actually available.\n\n**Perceived Option Density**  \nWhat actions the athlete can currently recognise and select.\n\nThis distinction matters.\n\nAn athlete may look technically stuck when the deeper issue is perceptual compression.\n\n## **Training Implication**\n\nSkill development should sometimes increase Option Density.\n\nBut more options are not always better.\n\nToo many choices can create hesitation.\n\nThe goal is not maximal complexity.\n\nIt is:\n\n**enough viable options to remain adaptive without overwhelming selection.**\n\nIn some contexts, deliberate option reduction can improve performance.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- how many meaningful options each athlete appears to have,\n- whether one athlete is progressively compressing the other’s decision-space,\n- whether a technically available option is not being perceived,\n- whether hesitation appears when Option Density becomes too high,\n- and whether a position becomes predictable because too few responses remain.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nBy the time you attacked, they had already removed your angle, inside space, and exit route. Your option density had collapsed to one predictable response.\n\nOr:\n\nYou had several viable continuations after the first exchange, but paused while trying to select between them. The problem may be excess option load rather than lack of technique.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nFRAME should distinguish between two opposite problems:\n\n**Too few viable options**  \n→ build access, structure, or technical pathways.\n\n**Too many competing options**  \n→ compress the decision tree and prioritise a smaller set.\n\nThis prevents the same coaching response from being applied to opposite constraints.\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nOption Density describes the **local width of the decision field**.\n\nCombat states can expand or compress that field.\n\nStrong control often works by narrowing the opponent’s decision architecture while preserving your own.\n\nThis creates a useful asymmetry:\n\n**My options remain broad.** **Your options become narrow.**\n\nAs the opponent’s field compresses, prediction becomes easier and initiative strengthens.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Control is partly the management of available choice.**\n\nA strong combat state preserves enough options for adaptive selection while progressively reducing the opponent’s meaningful alternatives."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Posts.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Posts","body":"# Posts\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nA post is a temporary structural contact used to support balance, prevent collapse, create distance, redirect force, or stabilise movement.\n\nA post can be made through:\n\n- hand\n- forearm\n- elbow\n- foot\n- knee\n- head\n- shoulder\n- opponent contact\n- floor or boundary contact\n\nA post is not merely contact.\n\nIt becomes functionally meaningful when the athlete depends on it to preserve or redirect structure.\n\n## Core Principle\nPosts temporarily expand the athlete’s usable base.\n\n**Loss of balance → Post created → Base restored or redirected**\n\nBecause of this, posts often reveal where balance is vulnerable.\n\n## Why Posts Matter\nPosts are central to:\n\n- takedown defence\n- scrambling\n- guard retention\n- sweeps\n- technical stand-ups\n- pin escapes\n- striking balance\n- wall wrestling\n\nA post can be both a defensive necessity and an offensive opportunity.\n\n## Post Function\n\n### Balance Preservation\nThe athlete uses a post to stop falling or rotating.\n\n### Base Expansion\nThe post increases the area supporting the athlete.\n\n### Force Redirection\nIncoming force is transferred through the post into the floor, opponent, or boundary.\n\n### Distance Creation\nA post can prevent an opponent from closing space.\n\n### Transition Support\nA temporary post may allow the athlete to move the hips, legs, or torso into a new configuration.\n\n## Required vs Optional Posts\nSynochi should distinguish between posts that are:\n\n### Structurally Required\nThe athlete would likely lose balance or collapse if the post disappeared.\n\n### Strategically Chosen\nThe post improves the position but is not necessary for survival.\n\n### Incidental\nThe limb touches the surface without meaningful structural dependence.\n\nThis distinction matters because required posts are often attackable.\n\n## Post Dependence\nA post becomes increasingly important when:\n\n- centre of mass moves outside the primary base\n- stance narrows\n- opponent creates rotational force\n- another base point becomes unavailable\n- posture is compromised\n\nSynochi should therefore infer how dependent the athlete is on each post.\n\n## Post Removal\nRemoving a required post can create immediate structural consequences.\n\nExamples:\n\n- sweep\n- takedown\n- collapse\n- rotational exposure\n- inability to defend another threat\n\nThis creates a common combat sequence:\n\n**Force post → Remove post → Exploit imbalance**\n\n## Forced Posting\nAn athlete can intentionally create a reaction that forces the opponent to post.\n\nExamples:\n\n- off-balancing\n- snapdown\n- sweep threat\n- rotational pressure\n- elevation\n\nThe post itself can then expose:\n\n- arm\n- back\n- head\n- angle\n- leg\n\nThe initial action may therefore be valuable even if it does not complete the attack.\n\n## Posting Direction\nThe location and direction of the post reveals the direction in which balance is threatened.\n\nExample:\n\nRight hand posts laterally.\n\nThis suggests the athlete’s structure is being displaced toward the right side.\n\nPost direction can therefore provide evidence about:\n\n- weight shift\n- centre-of-mass movement\n- rotational force\n- off-balancing direction\n\n## Post Timing\nEarly posts can stabilise structure efficiently.\n\nLate posts may occur after balance has already deteriorated.\n\nSynochi should distinguish:\n\n**anticipatory post**\n\nfrom\n\n**emergency post**\n\n## Post Vulnerability\nA posting limb is often less available for other functions.\n\nA hand supporting bodyweight may become temporarily unavailable for:\n\n- grip fighting\n- framing\n- striking\n- submission defence\n\nThis creates an option trade-off.\n\n## Post Trapping\nIf a required post becomes trapped, the athlete’s ability to restore balance may collapse.\n\nExamples:\n\n- wrist ride\n- hand trap\n- leg trap\n- elbow control\n\nPost trapping can convert temporary instability into sustained control.\n\n## Internal vs External Base\nThe athlete’s legs may form the primary base.\n\nPosts create temporary external support beyond that base.\n\nThis distinction helps Synochi understand why removing one contact point can suddenly destabilise an apparently stable athlete.\n\n## Dynamic Posting\nPosts appear and disappear rapidly.\n\nSynochi should track:\n\n**balance disruption → post appearance → weight loading → post release → new base**\n\nThis sequence can reveal both defensive competence and offensive opportunity.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"He put his hand down.\"\n\nPost-aware analysis:\n\n\"Your off-balance forced his right hand to the mat. Once that became weight-bearing, the arm was temporarily unavailable for defence and the back-side angle opened.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nPost analysis helps Synochi identify:\n\n- whether off-balancing is working\n- where the opponent is structurally vulnerable\n- which limb can be attacked or trapped\n- why a sweep failed\n- why an athlete repeatedly loses balance\n- when an opponent has committed weight outside their base\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about posts through:\n\n**Detect contact → Determine whether load-bearing → Infer balance dependency → Identify post direction → Detect post removal or trapping → Observe structural consequence**\n\nPosts should interact with base, weight transfer, off-balancing, frames, timing, and exposure.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Base]]\n[[Weight Transfer]]\n[[Off-Balancing]]\n[[Frames]]\n[[Timing]]\n[[Exposure]]\n[[Alignment]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Posture.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Posture","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nPosture is the structural organisation of the body that allows force to be produced, transferred, absorbed, and resisted efficiently.\n\nGood posture does not simply mean being upright.\n\nIt means the body is organised in a way that supports the current task.\n\nPosture affects:\n\n- balance,\n- force production,\n- defensive integrity,\n- movement efficiency,\n- breathing,\n- and access to attacks.\n\n## **Posture Is Task-Dependent**\n\nThere is no single universally correct posture.\n\nA useful posture for:\n\n- wrestling,\n- passing,\n- guard retention,\n- pinning,\n- escaping,\n- or submission defence\n\nmay look very different.\n\nThe important question is:\n\nIs the athlete’s structure organised for what they are currently trying to do?\n\n## **Structural Alignment**\n\nPosture becomes stronger when major body segments support one another.\n\nThis includes the relationship between:\n\n- head,\n- spine,\n- shoulders,\n- hips,\n- knees,\n- and base.\n\nWhen these structures become poorly aligned, force leaks through the system.\n\nThe athlete may then rely more heavily on muscular effort.\n\n## **Posture Breaking**\n\nBreaking posture means disrupting the opponent’s ability to maintain an efficient structure.\n\nThis may involve:\n\n- pulling the head forward,\n- rotating the spine,\n- separating elbows from the torso,\n- moving the hips away from the base,\n- forcing weight onto one side,\n- or preventing the opponent from stacking their body effectively.\n\nOnce posture is broken, attacks often require less force.\n\n## **Posture and Balance**\n\nPosture and [[Base]] are closely connected.\n\nA strong base with poor posture may still be unstable.\n\nLikewise, good posture without sufficient base may offer little resistance.\n\nEffective structure requires both.\n\nThis is why [[Off-Balancing]] often involves changing posture before the opponent is actually displaced.\n\n## **Posture and Connection**\n\nStrong [[Connection]] can be used to influence posture.\n\nFor example:\n\n- head control can flex or rotate the spine,\n- an underhook can lift or turn the shoulder,\n- hip connection can prevent the opponent from rebuilding alignment.\n\nOnce posture is compromised, the rest of the opponent’s structure becomes easier to manipulate.\n\n## **Offensive Application**\n\nOffensively, posture breaking can create:\n\n- takedown entries,\n- sweeps,\n- back exposure,\n- submissions,\n- passing opportunities,\n- and stronger pins.\n\nMany attacks become easier once the opponent is forced to operate from an inefficient structure.\n\n## **Defensive Application**\n\nDefensively, rebuilding posture may be more important than immediately escaping or attacking.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- recovering spinal alignment,\n- reconnecting elbows to the torso,\n- bringing the head back into structure,\n- rebuilding hip position,\n- restoring base.\n\nA technically correct escape attempted from broken posture may fail because the athlete cannot generate force efficiently.\n\n## **Posture Under Pressure**\n\nPressure often works by forcing the opponent into progressively worse structure.\n\nThe objective is not simply to make them uncomfortable.\n\nIt is to create a posture from which:\n\n- movement becomes weaker,\n- frames lose effectiveness,\n- breathing becomes harder,\n- and defensive options narrow.\n\n## **Posture and Fatigue**\n\nPoor posture also increases energy cost.\n\nIf an athlete repeatedly supports themselves through muscular tension instead of structure, fatigue accumulates faster.\n\nThis means posture affects not only immediate control but also long-term efficiency during a match.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- when posture first begins to break,\n- whether the athlete attempts an action from compromised structure,\n- whether the opponent is allowed to rebuild posture,\n- and whether excessive muscular effort is compensating for poor alignment.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou tried to finish the takedown after your posture had already collapsed forward. Rebuilding your head and hip alignment first would give you a stronger finishing structure.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only correcting the final technique, FRAME should identify whether the athlete had a usable structure before attempting it.\n\nFor example:\n\nInstead of:\n\n“Drive harder.”\n\nUse:\n\n“Rebuild your posture first, then drive.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Break their posture before committing to the attack.”\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Technique becomes stronger when structure supports it.**\n\nGood posture allows force to travel through the body efficiently.\n\nBroken posture creates weakness before the visible failure occurs.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[Off-Balancing]]\n- [[Connection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Pressure.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Pressure","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nPressure is the deliberate application of force through structure, connection, angle, and bodyweight to restrict movement, degrade posture, and shape the opponent’s reactions.\n\nPressure is not simply:\n\nbeing heavy.\n\nUseful pressure has:\n\n- direction,\n- purpose,\n- connection,\n- and consequence.\n\n## **Pressure as Directed Force**\n\nPressure should answer a clear question:\n\nWhat movement or structure am I trying to affect?\n\nExamples include:\n\n- turning the head,\n- flattening the shoulders,\n- pinning the hips,\n- collapsing a frame,\n- shifting weight onto one side,\n- preventing rotation,\n- or forcing the opponent to carry bodyweight inefficiently.\n\nPressure without direction may create discomfort but little actual control.\n\n## **Structural Pressure**\n\nThe most efficient pressure is transmitted through structure rather than constant muscular effort.\n\nThis can involve:\n\n- skeletal alignment,\n- hip position,\n- chest connection,\n- head position,\n- shoulder pressure,\n- controlled weight transfer.\n\nWhen structure is correct, gravity and bodyweight can do more of the work.\n\n## **Pressure and Posture**\n\nPressure becomes especially effective when it degrades [[Posture]].\n\nFor example:\n\n- turning the head can weaken spinal alignment,\n- lifting an elbow can disconnect the shoulder from the torso,\n- driving the hips away from the base can weaken lower-body structure.\n\nThe aim is often not simply to hold the opponent still.\n\nIt is to make their available structure worse.\n\n## **Pressure and Connection**\n\nPressure requires meaningful [[Connection]].\n\nIf contact is shallow or unstable, force may leak rather than transfer.\n\nStrong pressure usually requires:\n\n- stable contact,\n- correct angle,\n- body alignment,\n- and the ability to follow movement.\n\n## **Pressure and Constraint**\n\nPressure contributes to [[Constraint Accumulation]] by removing or weakening defensive options.\n\nFor example:\n\n- shoulder pressure may prevent turning inward,\n- hip pressure may stop knee recovery,\n- head pressure may prevent posture rebuilding.\n\nSeveral pressure points can combine into a stronger control system.\n\n## **Static and Dynamic Pressure**\n\nPressure does not need to be constant.\n\n### **Static Pressure**\n\nUsed to hold position and restrict movement.\n\n### **Dynamic Pressure**\n\nUsed while advancing, redirecting, or following the opponent.\n\nDynamic pressure is often more useful in transitions because it preserves control while position changes.\n\n## **Pressure as Reaction Creation**\n\nPressure can also be used to provoke predictable reactions.\n\nAn opponent may respond by:\n\n- turning away,\n- framing,\n- posting,\n- exposing an elbow,\n- bridging,\n- or opening space elsewhere.\n\nThis makes pressure useful for [[Reaction Mapping]].\n\nThe pressure itself becomes a threat that shapes the next exchange.\n\n## **Pressure and Efficiency**\n\nGood pressure should create a poor energy trade for the opponent.\n\nThe controlling athlete uses:\n\n- structure,\n- gravity,\n- connection,\n- and alignment.\n\nThe defending athlete is forced to use:\n\n- muscular effort,\n- repeated framing,\n- bridging,\n- or inefficient movement.\n\nOver time, this can create fatigue and reduced defensive quality.\n\n## **Defensive Application**\n\nDefending pressure begins by identifying what makes the pressure effective.\n\nUseful questions include:\n\n- Where is the connection strongest?\n- What part of my posture is compromised?\n- Which wedge is preventing movement?\n- Which direction is the pressure travelling?\n- Can I redirect rather than resist it directly?\n\nThe goal is often to break or misalign the pressure pathway.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether pressure has a clear direction,\n- whether the athlete’s bodyweight is actually transferring into the opponent,\n- whether pressure is supported by posture,\n- whether the athlete is muscling rather than structurally loading,\n- and what reaction the pressure creates.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou were heavy, but the pressure was vertical rather than turning their shoulders away from their hips. That let them keep enough alignment to recover guard.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of:\n\n“More pressure.”\n\nFRAME should prefer:\n\n“Drive your shoulder across their jawline to turn the head and weaken their rotation.”\n\nor:\n\n“Keep your hip connected and direct the pressure through the far shoulder.”\n\nThe instruction should explain both direction and purpose.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Pressure is useful when it changes structure, movement, or decision-making.**\n\nBeing heavy is not enough.\n\nEffective pressure is:\n\n**connected, directional, structurally supported, and strategically applied.**\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Posture]]\n- [[Connection]]\n- [[Constraint Accumulation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Recovery.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Recovery","body":"# **Recovery**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nRecovery is the process of restoring a usable combat state after action, disruption, or structural loss.\n\nEvery meaningful action changes the system.\n\nA strike extends.\n\nA shot commits weight.\n\nA kick temporarily removes a base point.\n\nA throw changes orientation.\n\nA defensive movement may sacrifice position to remove immediate danger.\n\nThe athlete must then reorganise.\n\nThe central sequence is:\n\n**Action → Disruption → Recovery → Readiness**\n\nRecovery determines how quickly the athlete becomes capable of selecting effectively again.\n\n## **Recovery Is Part of the Technique**\n\nA technique does not end at impact, contact, or positional gain.\n\nIt ends when the athlete has regained enough:\n\n- posture,\n- base,\n- distance,\n- orientation,\n- defensive integrity,\n- and perceptual availability\n\nto continue functioning.\n\nThis means two athletes may perform the same action successfully while producing very different outcomes.\n\nOne finishes the action already prepared for the next state.\n\nThe other succeeds but remains structurally exposed.\n\n## **Structural Recovery**\n\nStructural recovery restores the organisation required to move, resist, or produce force.\n\nThis may involve:\n\n- rebuilding stance,\n- reconnecting posture,\n- replacing a missing base point,\n- recovering inside position,\n- returning the hands to useful defensive structure,\n- or reorganising the body after rotation.\n\nThe important question is:\n\nHas the athlete actually regained functional structure?\n\nnot simply:\n\nHave they returned to the starting position?\n\n## **Positional Recovery**\n\nRecovery does not always mean returning to neutral.\n\nSometimes the new state is better.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Attack → transition → dominant position → stabilise**\n\nThe athlete has recovered into a new architecture rather than resetting the exchange.\n\nGood recovery therefore means reaching the next **coherent state**, not necessarily recreating the previous one.\n\n## **Perceptual Recovery**\n\nAfter intense action, attention may remain locked onto the previous event.\n\nThe athlete may technically regain posture while still processing what just happened.\n\nPerceptual recovery means restoring enough awareness to recognise:\n\n- the opponent’s next action,\n- changes in distance,\n- new exposure,\n- positional shifts,\n- and emerging threats.\n\nThis matters because physical recovery without perceptual recovery can still produce delayed response.\n\n## **Recovery and Exposure**\n\n[[Exposure]] often exists during incomplete recovery.\n\nAn athlete may become vulnerable because:\n\n- the strike has not returned,\n- the stance has not rebuilt,\n- the hips remain committed,\n- posture remains extended,\n- or attention is still attached to the previous action.\n\nThe opponent can therefore attack the **recovery phase**, not just the technique itself.\n\n## **Recovery Windows**\n\nEvery action creates a recovery window.\n\nThe size of that window depends on:\n\n- commitment,\n- balance,\n- momentum,\n- distance,\n- technique selection,\n- fatigue,\n- and quality of execution.\n\nSome actions preserve readiness almost immediately.\n\nOthers create large temporary vulnerabilities.\n\nCombat intelligence should therefore evaluate not only:\n\nWhat does this action achieve?\n\nbut:\n\nWhat state does it leave the athlete in?\n\n## **Recovery and Rhythm**\n\nRecovery contributes to [[Rhythm]].\n\nIf an athlete repeatedly:\n\n**acts → pauses → resets → acts**\n\nthe recovery itself becomes predictable.\n\nAn opponent may begin attacking during that reset.\n\nAdvanced athletes often reduce this predictability by:\n\n- recovering into another action,\n- varying reset timing,\n- changing angle during recovery,\n- or maintaining initiative while rebuilding structure.\n\n## **Recovery and Initiative**\n\nPoor recovery can surrender [[Initiative]].\n\nAn athlete may create a successful first action but require so long to reorganise that the opponent gains control of the next state.\n\nBetter recovery preserves continuity.\n\nThe sequence becomes:\n\n**Action → recover while maintaining threat → next selection**\n\nrather than:\n\n**Action → disengage mentally and structurally → opponent acts**\n\n## **Recovery Under Pressure**\n\nFatigue and stress often degrade recovery before they degrade obvious technique.\n\nThe athlete may still perform the action correctly but begin taking longer to:\n\n- regain stance,\n- replace defensive structure,\n- recognise the next threat,\n- or reconnect posture.\n\nThis makes recovery quality a useful early signal of system degradation.\n\n## **Defensive Recovery**\n\nDefence should also restore function.\n\nA defensive action that avoids immediate damage but leaves the athlete unable to act may only partially solve the problem.\n\nStrong defence seeks:\n\n**Threat removed → structure restored → selection restored**\n\nWhenever possible, recovery should transition toward initiative rather than merely survival.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- what state the athlete enters immediately after action,\n- whether structure is restored before the opponent responds,\n- whether recovery becomes predictable,\n- whether the athlete remains exposed after technically successful actions,\n- and whether fatigue is increasing recovery time.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nThe attack landed, but your stance remained square during the recovery. Their counter came through the structure you had not yet rebuilt.\n\nOr:\n\nYou defended the entry successfully, but paused after the defence instead of recovering directly into a position where you could regain initiative.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only correcting the action itself, FRAME should coach the **state that follows it**.\n\nFor example:\n\n“Finish the strike back in stance.”\n\n“Recover your base as you complete the turn.”\n\n“Do not treat the defence as the end of the exchange — recover into your next usable position.”\n\nThis turns recovery into part of execution rather than an afterthought.\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nRecovery is **system re-coherence after disruption**.\n\nCombat actions temporarily reorganise:\n\n- structure,\n- attention,\n- balance,\n- distance,\n- and available decisions.\n\nRecovery is the process through which those variables become sufficiently coherent for useful selection to resume.\n\nA well-recovered athlete regains authorship quickly.\n\nA poorly recovered athlete remains governed by the consequences of the previous action.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Every action creates a new state. Recovery determines whether that state becomes readiness or vulnerability.**\n\nThe best techniques do not merely work.\n\nThey leave the athlete capable of acting again."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Rhythm.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Rhythm","body":"Rhythm\n\nCore Idea\n\nRhythm is the recurring temporal structure of an exchange.\n\nIt emerges from repeated patterns in:\n\n* movement,\n* striking,\n* entries,\n* resets,\n* breathing,\n* pressure,\n* footwork,\n* reaction,\n* and decision cadence.\n\nRhythm matters because repeated timing creates predictability.\n\nOnce a pattern stabilises, the system begins to expect what comes next.\n\nThat expectation can be used, disrupted, or exploited.\n\nRhythm as Temporal Structure\n\nCombat does not unfold as isolated actions.\n\nIt unfolds as a sequence.\n\nThat sequence can develop a recognisable cadence:\n\nAction → Reset → Action → Reset\n\nor:\n\nFeint → Step → Strike → Exit\n\nor:\n\nPressure → Reaction → Pause → Re-entry\n\nWhen these patterns repeat, they form temporal structure.\n\nThe athlete begins to operate not only in space, but in time.\n\nPrediction Through Rhythm\n\nRhythm allows prediction.\n\nIf an opponent repeatedly:\n\n* resets after two strikes,\n* steps before kicking,\n* pauses after defending,\n* shoots after hand-fighting,\n* or changes direction on a regular cadence,\n\ntheir future behaviour becomes easier to anticipate.\n\nThe repeated pattern becomes a signal.\n\nSynochi should interpret rhythm as:\n\nrepeated temporal structure that reduces uncertainty about what happens next.\n\nRhythm and Timing\n\n[[Timing]] is the selection of the moment.\n\nRhythm helps reveal where those moments are likely to appear.\n\nAn athlete with good rhythm recognition can anticipate:\n\n* when an opponent will reset,\n* when weight will shift,\n* when attention will narrow,\n* and when the next action is likely to begin.\n\nTiming then becomes less reactive.\n\nIt becomes predictive.\n\nRhythm Breaking\n\nPredictable rhythm creates vulnerability.\n\nAn athlete can deliberately disrupt their own cadence through:\n\n* pauses,\n* accelerations,\n* decelerations,\n* delayed attacks,\n* double actions,\n* broken combinations,\n* false resets,\n* or unexpected re-entry.\n\nThis creates a mismatch between:\n\nwhat the opponent predicts\n\nand:\n\nwhat actually occurs.\n\nThat mismatch creates decision lag.\n\nTempo\n\nTempo is the speed at which the rhythm unfolds.\n\nTwo athletes may use similar patterns at very different tempos.\n\nTempo can be:\n\n* increased to overload reaction,\n* reduced to draw premature action,\n* varied to create uncertainty,\n* or stabilised to control the exchange.\n\nGood fighters do not only move fast.\n\nThey control when the exchange feels fast or slow.\n\nRhythm Entrapment\n\nAn athlete can become trapped inside the opponent’s rhythm.\n\nThis happens when they begin responding on the opponent’s cadence rather than selecting their own.\n\nExamples include:\n\n* always defending after the second strike,\n* resetting whenever the opponent resets,\n* reacting immediately to every feint,\n* moving only after the opponent initiates.\n\nThe athlete becomes temporally reactive.\n\nTheir output is being organised by the opponent’s pattern.\n\nReclaiming Temporal Initiative\n\nBreaking rhythm can restore authorship over the exchange.\n\nThis may involve:\n\n* interrupting the expected sequence,\n* acting during the opponent’s reset,\n* refusing to respond to low-value feints,\n* changing tempo,\n* or forcing the opponent to react first.\n\nThe objective is to shift from:\n\nOpponent rhythm → athlete reaction\n\nto:\n\nAthlete-selected rhythm → opponent adaptation\n\nRhythm and Pressure\n\nPressure can have rhythm.\n\nContinuous pressure is not always more effective than variable pressure.\n\nFor example:\n\nPressure → slight release → opponent attempts recovery → renewed pressure\n\nmay create more useful reactions than constant force.\n\nThe temporary release becomes part of the pattern.\n\nRhythm and Distance\n\nChanges in [[Distance]] often have cadence.\n\nAn athlete may repeatedly:\n\n* enter,\n* act,\n* exit,\n* reset.\n\nIf the sequence becomes predictable, the opponent can intercept it.\n\nChanging the timing of entry or exit can therefore alter the entire exchange without changing the technique itself.\n\nRhythm and Feints\n\nFeints work partly by installing expectation.\n\nRepeated signals teach the opponent to associate one cue with one response.\n\nOnce that association forms, the athlete can break the rhythm:\n\nSignal → expected reaction → different action\n\nThe opponent reacts to the model they have formed rather than the action that is actually happening.\n\nState Effects\n\nInternal state affects rhythm.\n\nFatigue, fear, urgency, confidence, and overload may change cadence.\n\nAn athlete may become:\n\n* rushed,\n* hesitant,\n* repetitive,\n* overly reactive,\n* or rhythmically flat.\n\nThese changes can reveal state before obvious technical breakdown occurs.\n\nRhythm can therefore function as a behavioural signal.\n\nVideo Analysis\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n* repeated temporal patterns,\n* predictable resets,\n* recurring pauses,\n* whether the athlete becomes trapped in the opponent’s cadence,\n* whether tempo changes produce different reactions,\n* and whether an opening appears because rhythm was disrupted.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou entered on the same beat three times in a row. By the third exchange, they were already reading the timing rather than reacting to the technique itself.\n\nOr:\n\nYour best opening appeared when you delayed the second action and broke the rhythm they had started anticipating.\n\nCoaching Implication\n\nInstead of only saying:\n\n“Be less predictable.”\n\nFRAME can identify the temporal pattern.\n\nFor example:\n\n“You reset after every two actions. Occasionally hold the position or re-enter immediately so they cannot key onto the same cadence.”\n\nOr:\n\n“They pause after defending. Use that reset window before their structure fully reorganises.”\n\nSynochi Interpretation\n\nRhythm can be understood as temporal compression.\n\nRepeated sequences reduce uncertainty because the system begins predicting the next state from the previous one.\n\nThis creates efficiency when the prediction is accurate.\n\nIt also creates vulnerability when the pattern becomes externally readable.\n\nBreaking rhythm introduces controlled prediction error.\n\nThe athlete deliberately violates the opponent’s expectation and forces rapid reorientation.\n\nIn this sense:\n\nRhythm creates expectation.\nRhythm disruption creates opportunity.\n\nPrinciple\n\nCombat has temporal architecture.\n\nThe athlete who can detect, shape, and disrupt that architecture gains more than speed.\n\nThey gain influence over when the opponent expects action to occur.\n\nConnections\n\n* [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n* [[Timing]]\n* [[Distance]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Space Creation and Space Denial.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Space Creation and Space Denial","body":"# Space Creation and Space Denial\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nSpace Creation and Space Denial describe the deliberate opening, preservation, occupation, compression, or removal of usable space within a combat exchange.\n\nSpace is not merely physical distance.\n\nIt is the room required for movement, rotation, framing, striking, pummelling, escape, entry, or force generation.\n\n## Core Principle\nUseful movement requires usable space.\n\n**Create space to move.  \nDeny space to control.**\n\nThe value of space depends on where it exists and what it allows.\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany combat problems are fundamentally spatial.\n\nExamples:\n\n- bottom athlete needs hip space to recover guard\n- top athlete needs to remove elbow-knee space to pass\n- wrestler needs inside space for an underhook\n- striker needs range to punch or kick\n- clinch fighter needs to deny separation\n- escaping athlete needs rotational room\n- attacker may need to compress space before finishing\n\n## Space Creation\nSpace Creation is the process of increasing usable room for action.\n\nCommon methods include:\n\n- framing\n- hip movement\n- stepping\n- posting\n- extending structure\n- angle change\n- off-balancing\n- disengaging\n- redirecting pressure\n\n## Space Denial\nSpace Denial is the process of preventing the opponent from accessing the room required for useful movement.\n\nCommon methods include:\n\n- chest connection\n- hip control\n- head position\n- wedges\n- pinning\n- boundary pressure\n- inside control\n- limb trapping\n- positional compression\n\n## Internal Space\nInternal space exists within the athlete’s own body structure.\n\nExamples:\n\n- elbow-rib space\n- elbow-knee space\n- knee-chest space\n- shoulder-neck space\n\nPreserving internal space can maintain structural integrity.\n\nCollapsing internal space can severely reduce mobility.\n\n## External Space\nExternal space exists between the athletes.\n\nExamples:\n\n- striking range\n- pummelling space\n- hip escape room\n- space between chest and shoulder\n- distance between hips\n\nExternal space determines which actions are mechanically available.\n\n## Functional Space\nNot all visible space is usable.\n\nAn athlete may appear to have room, but constraints may prevent them from exploiting it.\n\nSynochi should distinguish:\n\n**physical gap**\n\nfrom\n\n**functional space**\n\nFunctional space exists only when the athlete can actually move or act through it.\n\n## Space and Frames\nFrames are one of the primary tools for creating and preserving space.\n\nA successful frame does not merely resist pressure.\n\nIt protects enough room for:\n\n- movement\n- posture\n- recovery\n- attack\n\n## Space and Wedges\nWedges deny space by occupying it.\n\nExample:\n\nKnee inserted between hip and torso can prevent full chest-to-chest connection.\n\nA wedge can therefore preserve space for one athlete while denying space to the other.\n\n## Space Compression\nCompression reduces available movement volume.\n\nExamples:\n\n- elbows driven toward torso\n- knees forced away from centre\n- shoulders flattened\n- hips pinned\n- opponent folded toward own body\n\nCompression often reduces degrees of freedom and increases predictability.\n\n## Space Expansion\nExpansion increases available movement volume.\n\nExamples:\n\n- creating hip separation\n- extending opponent away\n- widening stance\n- opening elbow-knee connection\n- recovering posture\n\nExpansion can restore options but may also create exposure if uncontrolled.\n\n## Space Trade-Off\nCreating space in one area may open vulnerability elsewhere.\n\nExample:\n\nPushing opponent away with extended arms creates chest space but may expose elbows or shoulders.\n\nSynochi should therefore evaluate the net effect of space creation.\n\n## Space and Distance\nDistance is the global separation between athletes.\n\nSpace is more local.\n\nTwo athletes can remain at close distance while one athlete creates a small but critical pocket of space at the hip, shoulder, or elbow.\n\nThis distinction is important.\n\n## Space and Control\nControl often works by reducing the opponent’s usable space while preserving the controller’s own.\n\nA dominant position may therefore be understood as spatial asymmetry.\n\n**My movement space remains available.  \nYour movement space collapses.**\n\n## Space and Threat\nSome attacks require space.\n\nOthers require removing it.\n\nExamples:\n\n- armbar may require limb separation\n- rear naked choke often requires eliminating defensive hand space\n- takedown entry may require penetration space\n- pressure pass may require collapsing knee-elbow space\n\nSynochi should reason about which spatial condition each action requires.\n\n## Space and Boundary\nThe environment changes the available spatial field.\n\nNear a wall or cage:\n\n- retreat space disappears\n- pivot options narrow\n- posture may change\n- pressure becomes easier to maintain\n\nBoundary should therefore modify space analysis.\n\n## Space Recovery\nDefensive recovery often begins with restoring a small amount of space.\n\nExample:\n\ncreate forearm frame  \n→ regain shoulder room  \n→ turn onto side  \n→ recover knee  \n→ rebuild guard\n\nThe initial space may be tiny but functionally decisive.\n\n## Space Closure\nOffensive control often follows the reverse sequence.\n\nExample:\n\nremove frame  \n→ close shoulder space  \n→ flatten torso  \n→ block hip movement  \n→ consolidate pin\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You couldn't escape.\"\n\nSpace-aware analysis:\n\n\"Your hip escape was unavailable because the shoulder and hip space had already been compressed. You needed to restore local space before trying to move the whole body.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nSpace Creation and Space Denial help Synochi explain:\n\n- why escapes fail\n- why pressure becomes effective\n- how frames and wedges function\n- why certain attacks require separation\n- why other attacks require compression\n- how control develops through spatial restriction\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about space through:\n\n**Observe body relationships → Identify usable space → Detect creation or compression → Link space to available actions → Track space gain/loss → Observe outcome**\n\nSpace should interact with frames, wedges, pressure, distance, constraint, degrees of freedom, and positional control.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Frames]]\n[[Wedges]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Distance]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Degrees of Freedom]]\n[[Structural Integrity]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Structural Integrity.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Structural Integrity","body":"# Structural Integrity\n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nStructural Integrity is the degree to which an athlete’s body remains organised as a connected, mechanically useful system under force, movement, pressure, and changing position.\n\nA structure has integrity when its parts continue to support one another.\n\nIntegrity is lost when key segments become disconnected, folded, twisted, isolated, or forced outside useful alignment.\n\n## Core Principle\nThe body performs best when force can travel through connected structure without unnecessary collapse.\n\n**Connection → Alignment → Support → Structural integrity → Functional action**\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany combat failures happen before a technique visibly fails.\n\nThe athlete may first lose:\n\n- elbow-knee connection\n- head-spine-hip relationship\n- shoulder-hip coordination\n- base support\n- internal space\n- joint stacking\n\nOnce enough integrity is lost, movement becomes more expensive and options begin to disappear.\n\n## Structural Integrity vs Position\nAn athlete can occupy a recognisable position while having very poor structural integrity.\n\nExample:\n\nTwo athletes may both be in bottom half guard.\n\nAthlete A:\n- elbows connected\n- knees inside\n- head aligned\n- hips mobile\n\nAthlete B:\n- shoulders twisted\n- elbow isolated\n- head turned away\n- hips flattened\n\nThe positional label is the same.\n\nThe mechanical reality is not.\n\n## Integrity Components\n\n### Segment Connection\nRelevant body segments remain functionally linked.\n\nExamples:\n\n- elbow to ribs\n- knee to elbow\n- shoulder to torso\n- hips to base\n\n### Alignment\nThe body is arranged to transmit or resist force efficiently.\n\n### Internal Space\nCritical space within the athlete’s structure remains available.\n\nExamples:\n\n- elbow space\n- knee space\n- neck protection\n- hip mobility\n\n### Base Support\nThe structure is supported by a usable base.\n\n### Joint Organisation\nJoints remain within mechanically useful ranges rather than becoming collapsed or isolated.\n\n## Integrity Loss\nCommon integrity failures include:\n\n- elbow separated from torso\n- knees pushed away from centre\n- head detached from hip direction\n- shoulder rotated against pelvis\n- spine folded under pressure\n- base collapsed onto one side\n\n## Progressive Collapse\nStructural integrity often deteriorates progressively.\n\nExample:\n\nframe weakens  \n→ elbow separates  \n→ shoulder becomes pinned  \n→ torso rotates  \n→ hips flatten  \n→ guard collapses\n\nThe final positional loss is the end of a structural collapse chain.\n\n## Integrity Preservation\nDefensive skill often involves preserving integrity before attempting movement.\n\nExamples:\n\n- maintaining elbow-knee connection\n- keeping head aligned with torso\n- preventing shoulder isolation\n- retaining inside knee position\n- preserving base during scramble\n\n## Integrity Restoration\nWhen structure has already deteriorated, recovery may require rebuilding it in stages.\n\nExample:\n\nprotect neck  \n→ reconnect elbow  \n→ rebuild frame  \n→ restore hip movement  \n→ recover position\n\nThis sequence is often more realistic than attempting an immediate full escape.\n\n## Structural Integrity and Pressure\nPressure becomes more effective when it breaks internal organisation.\n\nEffective pressure may:\n\n- separate limbs from torso\n- rotate spine\n- flatten hips\n- turn head away\n- collapse frames\n\nPressure that does not disturb structure may feel heavy without creating meaningful control.\n\n## Structural Integrity and Submission Risk\nSubmission exposure often increases when structural integrity breaks.\n\nExamples:\n\n- elbow separation creates arm attack exposure\n- neck extension breaks spinal organisation\n- knee line isolation exposes leg attacks\n- shoulder rotation creates joint vulnerability\n\n## Structural Integrity and Fatigue\nMaintaining poor structure often requires muscular compensation.\n\nWhen skeletal organisation is lost, muscles must work harder to stabilise the body.\n\nThis can accelerate fatigue and further degrade structure.\n\n## Integrity Threshold\nStructural degradation should be treated as a continuum.\n\nPossible states:\n\n- coherent\n- mildly compromised\n- unstable\n- fragmented\n- structurally collapsed\n\nSynochi should avoid binary interpretation.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"Your guard got smashed.\"\n\nIntegrity-aware analysis:\n\n\"Your guard started collapsing once your elbow separated from your knee and your upper and lower body stopped functioning as one structure.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nStructural Integrity helps Synochi identify:\n\n- why pressure becomes effective\n- why escapes fail\n- when internal structure begins collapsing\n- which connection should be restored first\n- why certain defensive positions remain resilient\n- why some athletes survive bad positions efficiently\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about structural integrity through:\n\n**Observe segment relationships → Assess alignment → Detect disconnection → Estimate collapse severity → Identify restoration pathway → Observe outcome**\n\nStructural Integrity should act as a bridge between posture, frames, alignment, connection, base, pressure, and recovery.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Alignment]]\n[[Connection]]\n[[Frames]]\n[[Posture]]\n[[Base]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Recovery]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Tension Management.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Tension Management","body":"# \n\n## Type\nUniversal Concept\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nTension Management is the ability to create, maintain, reduce, or redirect muscular and structural tension according to the demands of the exchange.\n\nToo little tension can cause structure to collapse.\n\nToo much tension can reduce adaptability, speed, sensitivity, and endurance.\n\nEffective combat movement depends on using the right amount of tension at the right time.\n\n## Core Principle\nTension should be functional, selective, and temporary.\n\n**Need → Tension → Mechanical effect → Release or redistribution**\n\nThe goal is not maximum relaxation.\n\nThe goal is appropriate tension.\n\n## Why It Matters\nCombat exchanges constantly alternate between:\n\n- stabilising\n- resisting\n- accelerating\n- absorbing force\n- creating pressure\n- yielding\n- changing direction\n\nEach requires a different tension profile.\n\nAn athlete who stays maximally tense becomes rigid and fatigued.\n\nAn athlete who stays too relaxed may lose structure or connection.\n\n## Local vs Global Tension\n\n### Local Tension\nTension is concentrated in the body segments needed for the task.\n\nExample:\n\nStrong hand and forearm connection during grip control while shoulders remain relatively relaxed.\n\n### Global Tension\nLarge portions of the body contract simultaneously.\n\nThis may be useful briefly during explosive effort, but excessive global tension can reduce efficiency.\n\n## Structural Tension\nSome tension exists to preserve body organisation.\n\nExamples:\n\n- maintaining elbow position\n- stabilising trunk during pressure\n- holding posture in a clinch\n- preserving knee alignment\n\nThis should support structure rather than substitute for poor alignment.\n\n## Reactive Tension\nAthletes often tense automatically when they perceive threat.\n\nExamples:\n\n- gripping excessively\n- holding breath\n- stiffening arms\n- locking posture\n\nReactive tension can temporarily increase resistance but may reduce adaptability.\n\n## Tension and Sensitivity\nExcess tension can reduce the athlete’s ability to feel changes in:\n\n- pressure\n- direction\n- balance\n- grip quality\n- opponent movement\n\nA less rigid structure may detect and adapt to these changes earlier.\n\n## Tension and Movement\nMovement usually requires alternating phases of stabilisation and release.\n\nExample:\n\nplant  \n→ brace  \n→ generate force  \n→ release  \n→ reposition\n\nMaintaining the same tension throughout the entire sequence can slow transitions.\n\n## Tension and Speed\nFast movement often depends on avoiding unnecessary co-contraction.\n\nIf opposing muscle groups remain heavily engaged, acceleration may decrease.\n\nSelective relaxation can therefore improve:\n\n- hand fighting\n- level changes\n- striking\n- scrambling\n- transitions\n\n## Tension and Pressure\nPressure does not necessarily require maximal muscular contraction.\n\nEfficient pressure may come primarily from:\n\n- alignment\n- weight placement\n- base\n- connection\n- gravity\n\nExcess muscular tension can make pressure less adaptive and more tiring.\n\n## Tension and Fatigue\nUnnecessary tension increases energy cost.\n\nCommon signs include:\n\n- early forearm fatigue\n- shoulder fatigue\n- breath holding\n- stiff movement\n- reduced reaction speed\n\nOver time, fatigue can degrade technique and decision quality.\n\n## Tension Cycling\nHigh-level movement often cycles tension rather than maintaining it continuously.\n\n**Relax → Connect → Tension → Effect → Release → Reposition**\n\nThis allows force production without permanent rigidity.\n\n## Tension Matching\nAthletes can match the amount of tension to the opponent’s resistance.\n\nIf the opponent yields, continuing to apply maximal force may be inefficient.\n\nIf resistance increases, tension can rise temporarily.\n\nThis creates adaptive rather than fixed effort.\n\n## Yielding\nReducing tension can sometimes create positional advantage.\n\nExamples:\n\n- allowing pressure to travel past the body\n- rotating with force\n- slipping grips\n- creating space through redirection\n\nYielding is not passive.\n\nIt is controlled reduction of resistance to improve future position.\n\n## Tension Failure\nPoor tension management can appear as:\n\n- gripping too hard\n- rigid framing\n- stiff hips\n- inability to change direction\n- excessive muscular resistance under pins\n- inability to relax after explosive movement\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You’re using too much strength.\"\n\nTension-aware analysis:\n\n\"You’re staying globally tense after the initial grip exchange. Your shoulders and arms never release, so each following transition becomes slower and more expensive.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nTension Management helps Synochi explain:\n\n- why athletes fatigue unnecessarily\n- why movement becomes stiff\n- why grip fighting burns the forearms\n- why pressure feels inefficient\n- why transitions are slow\n- when relaxation should occur without losing structure\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should reason about tension through:\n\n**Observe posture and movement → Detect rigidity or collapse → Compare tension with task demands → Estimate efficiency → Identify unnecessary tension → Observe effect on movement and fatigue**\n\nTension should be interpreted alongside structure, alignment, breathing, pressure, timing, and rhythm.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Structural Integrity]]\n[[Alignment]]\n[[SPINE/Pressure]]\n[[Rhythm]]\n[[Timing]]\n[[Connection]]\n[[Base]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Threat.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Threat","body":"# **Threat**\n\n## **Core Idea**\n\nA threat is any action, position, signal, or possibility that forces the opponent to allocate attention, structure, or decision capacity toward preventing a negative outcome.\n\nA threat does not need to be completed to matter.\n\nIf the opponent believes the action is credible, the threat can already change:\n\n- posture,\n- distance,\n- guard,\n- weight distribution,\n- attention,\n- movement,\n- and available options.\n\nThe central principle is:\n\n**A credible threat alters behaviour before contact is completed.**\n\n## **Threat as Constraint**\n\nA threat functions as a form of behavioural constraint.\n\nIf an athlete presents a credible possibility of:\n\n- striking,\n- entering,\n- throwing,\n- clinching,\n- sweeping,\n- submitting,\n- countering,\n- or changing angle,\n\nthe opponent may begin protecting against that possibility.\n\nTheir response field narrows.\n\nThis means control can occur before physical control is established.\n\n## **Credibility**\n\nA threat only shapes behaviour if the opponent believes it can produce consequence.\n\nThreat credibility comes from:\n\n- previous successful actions,\n- technical positioning,\n- distance,\n- timing,\n- posture,\n- visible commitment,\n- tactical reputation,\n- and repeated reinforcement.\n\nA fake threat with no believable pathway produces little constraint.\n\n## **Threat Density**\n\nAn athlete becomes difficult to read when multiple threats remain credible at once.\n\nFor example:\n\nOne position may simultaneously threaten:\n\n- direct attack,\n- angle change,\n- level change,\n- entry,\n- or disengagement.\n\nThe opponent must preserve resources across several possibilities.\n\nThis increases their decision load.\n\n## **Threat Hierarchy**\n\nNot all threats have equal priority.\n\nSome demand immediate response.\n\nOthers can be delayed.\n\nA high-priority threat can force the opponent to abandon lower-priority objectives.\n\nThis creates:\n\n**Threat → Defensive Allocation → Secondary Exposure**\n\nThe response to one danger may open another pathway.\n\n## **Threat and Reaction Mapping**\n\n[[Reaction Mapping]] depends on credible threats.\n\nA reaction can only be meaningfully mapped if the stimulus repeatedly produces a recognisable response.\n\nThe sequence becomes:\n\n**Threat → Reaction → Exposure → Next Threat**\n\nThis turns offence into a process of shaping behaviour rather than simply launching isolated techniques.\n\n## **Threat and Initiative**\n\n[[Initiative]] often begins with the athlete who creates the first credible problem.\n\nThe threat may be subtle.\n\nA feint, stance shift, grip, angle, or forward step can establish initiative if it forces the opponent to reorganise.\n\nThe athlete with threat credibility can influence the exchange without constantly committing.\n\n## **Threat and Option Density**\n\nA credible threat compresses [[Option Density]].\n\nThe opponent may technically retain many possible actions, but some become too risky to use.\n\nFor example:\n\nAn athlete may stop stepping freely because one side is being threatened.\n\nThe options still exist mechanically.\n\nThey have become tactically unavailable.\n\n## **Threat and Exposure**\n\nThreats often create [[Exposure]] indirectly.\n\nThe athlete may attack one layer to reveal another.\n\nFor example:\n\nProtecting the head may expose the body.\n\nProtecting a takedown may alter striking posture.\n\nDefending one limb may expose another.\n\nThe first threat therefore does not need to be the final objective.\n\nIts function may be to reorganise the opponent.\n\n## **Threat Without Commitment**\n\nHigh-quality threat allows influence without unnecessary risk.\n\nAn athlete can create reaction while preserving the ability to:\n\n- withdraw,\n- redirect,\n- change level,\n- change angle,\n- or select another action.\n\nThis is more efficient than committing fully to every attack.\n\nThe goal is often:\n\n**maximum opponent response for minimum self-exposure.**\n\n## **Threat Saturation**\n\nToo many weak threats can reduce credibility.\n\nIf the athlete constantly feints without consequence, the opponent may stop reacting.\n\nThreat value therefore depends on reinforcement.\n\nA threat must occasionally become real.\n\nOtherwise the opponent’s model updates:\n\nThis signal does not require response.\n\nThreat credibility decays.\n\n## **Defensive Threat**\n\nThreat is not exclusively offensive.\n\nA counter threat can discourage attack.\n\nFor example:\n\nThe possibility of:\n\n- a counter strike,\n- reversal,\n- throw,\n- interception,\n- submission,\n- or immediate positional reversal\n\nmay limit the opponent’s willingness to commit.\n\nDefence can therefore create control by increasing the perceived cost of attack.\n\n## **Threat and Perception**\n\nThreat changes what the opponent attends to.\n\nA credible threat can narrow perceptual focus around one region, movement, or possibility.\n\nThis can create perceptual asymmetry.\n\nThe opponent begins filtering the exchange through:\n\nWhat am I trying not to let happen?\n\nThis creates openings elsewhere.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether the athlete’s threats are producing real reactions,\n- whether the opponent has stopped respecting repeated empty signals,\n- which threats are causing structural changes,\n- whether a secondary opening appears after defensive allocation,\n- and whether the athlete is overcommitting where a lighter threat would have been enough.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYour level change consistently made them bring their hands down, but you never used that reaction to open the higher target.\n\nOr:\n\nThey stopped reacting to the feint because it was never reinforced with a real attack.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of simply saying:\n\n“Feint more.”\n\nFRAME should ask:\n\n“What behaviour are you trying to create?”\n\nThe useful instruction may be:\n\n“Make the threat credible enough that they protect the lower line, then attack the opening created by that protection.”\n\nThis links signal to intended reaction.\n\n## **Synochi Interpretation**\n\nThreat is a form of **anticipatory constraint**.\n\nThe opponent does not need to experience the outcome itself.\n\nThey only need to assign enough probability and consequence to it that their system reorganises around prevention.\n\nThis means threat acts upstream of action.\n\nIt alters:\n\n- attention,\n- option selection,\n- posture,\n- and prediction\n\nbefore the exchange fully resolves.\n\nA strong threat changes the opponent’s policy before it changes their position.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**The best threats do not only attack the opponent. They reorganise the opponent.**\n\nA credible possibility can shape behaviour before the final action is ever chosen."},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Timing.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Timing","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nTiming is the selection of the moment when an action has the greatest chance of succeeding.\n\nAn action can be technically correct and still fail if it is performed at the wrong moment.\n\nGood timing means acting when:\n\n- the opponent is moving,\n- their structure is changing,\n- their attention is occupied,\n- their weight is committed,\n- their defence is transitioning,\n- or their available responses are reduced.\n\nThe central principle is:\n\n**The same action can be weak or powerful depending on when it happens.**\n\n## **Timing and Opportunity**\n\nTiming depends on recognising windows.\n\nA window may appear when the opponent:\n\n- steps,\n- strikes,\n- reaches,\n- posts,\n- turns,\n- changes level,\n- shifts weight,\n- resets posture,\n- transitions position,\n- or reacts to a threat.\n\nThese moments often create temporary vulnerability.\n\n## **Static vs Dynamic Targets**\n\nAttacking a static, prepared opponent is usually harder.\n\nA moving opponent is already reorganising:\n\n- base,\n- posture,\n- balance,\n- attention,\n- or defence.\n\nThat transition creates opportunity.\n\nGood timing therefore often means attacking **during change**, not after the opponent has already stabilised.\n\n## **Timing and Reaction**\n\nMany actions become easier when performed during a predictable reaction.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Threat → Reaction → Timed Follow-Up**\n\nThe athlete is not simply moving faster.\n\nThey are acting at the point where the opponent’s response has temporarily limited their options.\n\n## **Timing and Distance**\n\n[[Distance]] determines whether an action can physically reach or connect.\n\nTiming determines whether the moment is favourable.\n\nThese must align.\n\nAn athlete may be at perfect range but attack while the opponent is fully prepared.\n\nOr they may recognise the perfect moment but still be too far away to act.\n\nEffective execution requires both.\n\n## **Timing and Weight Transfer**\n\nWeight shifts create important timing windows.\n\nAn opponent who has just committed weight may be:\n\n- slower to reverse direction,\n- vulnerable to off-balancing,\n- unable to lift a loaded limb,\n- or temporarily unable to reposition their base.\n\nThe timing of the attack may matter more than raw force.\n\n## **Timing and Rhythm**\n\nRepeated movement often creates rhythm.\n\nOnce a rhythm becomes predictable, timing can exploit it.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- entering between repeated strikes,\n- attacking during a predictable reset,\n- changing tempo to disrupt expectation,\n- or acting just before the opponent completes a familiar sequence.\n\nGood timing can therefore involve both reading rhythm and breaking rhythm.\n\n## **Early vs Late Timing**\n\nTiming errors often fall into two categories.\n\n### **Too Early**\n\nThe opportunity has not fully formed.\n\nThe opponent still has:\n\n- stable posture,\n- available base,\n- intact defence,\n- or too much distance.\n\n### **Too Late**\n\nThe opportunity has already closed.\n\nThe opponent has:\n\n- recovered balance,\n- rebuilt structure,\n- reset distance,\n- or completed their defensive reaction.\n\nThe effective window often exists between these points.\n\n## **Defensive Timing**\n\nTiming also governs defence.\n\nA defensive action may be strongest:\n\n- before the attack fully develops,\n- during the opponent’s entry,\n- at the point of contact,\n- or during recovery.\n\nDifferent defensive timings create different outcomes.\n\nEarly recognition can prevent the attack.\n\nLater timing may require recovery rather than prevention.\n\n## **Timing and Feints**\n\nFeints can create timing windows by provoking premature reactions.\n\nIf an opponent reacts early:\n\n- their guard may move,\n- their weight may shift,\n- their posture may change,\n- or their attention may narrow.\n\nThe athlete can then attack the newly created window.\n\n## **Timing Under Pressure**\n\nPressure can reduce timing quality by narrowing perception and increasing urgency.\n\nAn athlete may begin acting:\n\n- too early,\n- too late,\n- or continuously without clear windows.\n\nStrong timing requires enough perceptual control to recognise meaningful changes rather than reacting to everything.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- whether the action itself was technically sound,\n- whether the opportunity existed when it was attempted,\n- whether the opponent was stable or transitioning,\n- whether a better window appeared immediately before or after,\n- and whether the athlete repeatedly misses the same timing cue.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nThe entry was technically fine, but you attacked after they had already recovered their stance. The better window was during the previous weight shift.\n\nOr:\n\nYou defended after the attack had fully developed instead of acting during the entry.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only saying:\n\n“Do it faster.”\n\nFRAME should identify:\n\n“Attack during the weight transfer.”\n\nor:\n\n“Enter as they reset their stance.”\n\nThis teaches the athlete to recognise the moment rather than simply increase speed.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Timing is the ability to act when the structure of the exchange is temporarily favourable.**\n\nThe best action is not only the correct action.\n\nIt is the correct action at the correct moment.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Distance]]\n- [[Off-Balancing]]\n- [[Reaction Mapping]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Wedges.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Wedges","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nA wedge is a structure placed into space to prevent movement, redirect force, or preserve positional control.\n\nIn grappling, wedges often work better than muscular effort because they use:\n\n- skeletal structure,\n- body positioning,\n- angle,\n- pressure,\n- and space occupation\n\nto limit what the opponent can do.\n\nThe central principle is:\n\n**A good wedge removes movement by occupying the space that movement requires.**\n\n## **What a Wedge Does**\n\nA wedge can:\n\n- block rotation,\n- stop an escape path,\n- separate body segments,\n- maintain distance,\n- prevent reconnection,\n- redirect force,\n- or preserve an angle.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- a knee preventing hip rotation,\n- a forearm blocking the shoulder line,\n- a head preventing the opponent from turning,\n- a shin maintaining separation,\n- a hip controlling space between the opponent’s legs.\n\n## **Structure Over Strength**\n\nA wedge should ideally rely on structure rather than continuous muscular tension.\n\nIf the athlete has to squeeze constantly to maintain control, the position may be mechanically inefficient.\n\nA well-placed wedge allows the skeleton and body mass to do more of the work.\n\nThis improves:\n\n- control,\n- endurance,\n- stability,\n- and responsiveness.\n\n## **Space Occupation**\n\nMany escapes require access to a specific piece of space.\n\nA wedge works by occupying that space first.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf an opponent needs to bring their knee inside to recover guard, preventing that knee from entering the inside space can be more effective than reacting after guard recovery has already begun.\n\nThis makes wedges preventative rather than purely reactive.\n\n## **Wedges and Constraint**\n\nWedges contribute directly to [[Constraint Accumulation]].\n\nOne wedge may remove one direction.\n\nSeveral coordinated wedges can progressively reduce movement until only a small number of reactions remain.\n\nFor example:\n\n- head blocks upper-body rotation,\n- knee blocks hip movement,\n- elbow prevents frame recovery.\n\nTogether, they create a much stronger control system than any single point alone.\n\n## **Dynamic Wedges**\n\nWedges do not have to remain fixed.\n\nThey can move as the opponent moves.\n\nA knee may become a shin.\n\nA forearm may become an elbow frame.\n\nA head wedge may shift to follow shoulder rotation.\n\nThe principle remains the same:\n\nOccupy the space the opponent needs before they can effectively use it.\n\n## **Offensive Wedges**\n\nOffensively, wedges can help:\n\n- isolate limbs,\n- expose the back,\n- prevent guard recovery,\n- maintain pinning positions,\n- reinforce submissions,\n- and trap defensive structures.\n\nA wedge often creates the stable platform from which the next attack becomes possible.\n\n## **Defensive Wedges**\n\nDefensively, wedges create separation and prevent connection.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- forearm frames,\n- knee shields,\n- elbow-knee connection,\n- shin frames,\n- head positioning.\n\nA defensive wedge can stop pressure from fully transferring through the opponent’s body.\n\nThis can create the space required to move, rotate, or recover position.\n\n## **Wedges and Connection**\n\nWedges and connection can appear opposite, but they often work together.\n\nA wedge may create separation in one area while maintaining strong connection elsewhere.\n\nFor example:\n\nA frame may keep the opponent’s shoulder away while the hips remain connected enough to control distance.\n\nThe important question is not simply:\n\nAm I connected or separated?\n\nIt is:\n\nWhere should connection exist, and where should space be preserved?\n\n## **Failure of a Wedge**\n\nA wedge fails when:\n\n- it is placed too shallow,\n- the angle is poor,\n- the opponent can move around it,\n- it collapses under pressure,\n- it relies too heavily on muscular effort,\n- or it blocks the wrong space.\n\nThe presence of a limb does not automatically mean a functional wedge exists.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- which spaces the athlete successfully occupies,\n- which escape route remains open,\n- whether a frame or wedge collapses,\n- whether the athlete is using strength where structure could do the work,\n- and whether lost position begins with removal of a critical wedge.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou lost the pass when your knee stopped blocking the hip line. Once that wedge disappeared, they could rotate underneath you and recover guard.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of coaching only the final technique, FRAME should identify the missing structural barrier.\n\nFor example:\n\nInstead of:\n\n“Hold them tighter.”\n\nUse:\n\n“Keep your knee wedged against the hip so they can’t rotate underneath you.”\n\nThis makes the instruction more specific and mechanically useful.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Wedges control movement by controlling space.**\n\nStrong grappling often feels efficient because the athlete is not constantly fighting movement after it begins.\n\nThey are occupying the space required for that movement before it becomes available.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Constraint Accumulation]]\n- [[Inside Position]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Universal Concepts/Weight Transfer.md","folder":"Universal Concepts","title":"Weight Transfer","body":"## **Core Idea**\n\nWeight Transfer is the deliberate movement of body mass from one support point, side, or direction to another in order to create force, remove force, change balance, or alter control.\n\nIn grappling, weight is not static.\n\nIt is continuously redistributed.\n\nThe central question is:\n\nWhere is the athlete’s weight now, and what does that make possible or impossible?\n\n## **Why It Matters**\n\nWeight distribution affects:\n\n- balance,\n- mobility,\n- pressure,\n- takedown defence,\n- sweeping,\n- passing,\n- posting,\n- and the ability to change direction.\n\nAn athlete can have technically correct positioning but still lose the exchange because their weight is committed to the wrong place.\n\n## **Weight and Base**\n\n[[Base]] tells us what supports the athlete.\n\nWeight Transfer tells us how much load is being placed through each part of that base.\n\nFor example:\n\nAn athlete may technically have both feet on the floor, but if 80–90% of their weight is loaded onto one leg, the other leg becomes much easier to attack.\n\nThis makes weight distribution more important than simply counting contact points.\n\n## **Commitment**\n\nThe more weight committed to one direction, the harder it becomes to move quickly in the opposite direction.\n\nThis creates opportunity.\n\nExamples:\n\n- loading weight onto one leg before a foot sweep,\n- forcing weight forward before a snap-down,\n- making a guard player post heavily before attacking the opposite side,\n- shifting a passer’s weight so one leg becomes light enough to manipulate.\n\nWeight commitment often creates temporary predictability.\n\n## **Light and Heavy Limbs**\n\nA limb becomes easier to move when it is unloaded.\n\nThis is especially important in sweeps and leg attacks.\n\nInstead of trying to move a heavily loaded limb directly, an athlete can first shift the opponent’s weight elsewhere.\n\nThe sequence becomes:\n\n**Shift Weight → Unload Target → Attack Target**\n\nThis can dramatically reduce the force required.\n\n## **Pressure**\n\n[[SPINE/Pressure]] depends heavily on Weight Transfer.\n\nSimply placing the body over the opponent is not enough.\n\nUseful pressure often requires transferring bodyweight through a specific contact point.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- shoulder into head or jaw,\n- chest into sternum,\n- hip into hip line,\n- knee into thigh or torso.\n\nIf weight remains elsewhere, the contact may look correct but feel weak.\n\n## **Weight Transfer in Standing**\n\nIn standing exchanges, athletes constantly shift weight between:\n\n- left and right leg,\n- heels and forefoot,\n- forward and backward,\n- inside and outside edges.\n\nThese shifts affect:\n\n- shot defence,\n- foot sweeps,\n- throws,\n- stance recovery,\n- and ability to change direction.\n\nRecognising weight commitment is often more valuable than reacting to the final attack.\n\n## **Weight Transfer in Guard**\n\nFrom guard, changing where the opponent carries weight can create:\n\n- sweeps,\n- posts,\n- leg exposure,\n- upper-body openings,\n- and back exposure.\n\nThe guard player is often not trying to overpower the passer.\n\nThey are trying to make the passer support themselves in an inconvenient place.\n\n## **Weight Transfer in Passing**\n\nA passer may use weight transfer to:\n\n- pin a hip,\n- collapse a knee,\n- force shoulders to the floor,\n- lighten their own leg,\n- or free a trapped limb.\n\nGood passing often involves moving weight progressively through the opponent rather than remaining centred in one place.\n\n## **Defensive Weight Transfer**\n\nDefensively, an athlete needs the ability to unload threatened structures.\n\nFor example:\n\n- shifting weight away from a trapped leg,\n- transferring weight to a post during a sweep,\n- moving weight onto the shoulder to create hip mobility,\n- or changing base during a scramble.\n\nPoor defensive weight transfer makes the athlete feel stuck even when space exists.\n\n## **Timing**\n\nWeight Transfer is especially important during transitions.\n\nAn athlete in motion must continually move their centre of mass from one support structure to another.\n\nThese moments create vulnerability because the body may briefly be:\n\n- partially committed,\n- lightly supported,\n- or unable to reverse direction efficiently.\n\nMany sweeps, foot sweeps, and takedowns succeed by attacking during this transition.\n\n## **Weight Transfer and Off-Balancing**\n\n[[Off-Balancing]] often works by manipulating where the opponent is forced to carry weight.\n\nFor example:\n\nForce weight onto one leg → remove or attack that leg.\n\nOr:\n\nForce weight onto the hands → attack underneath the hips.\n\nThe athlete is shaping the opponent’s base before attacking it.\n\n## **Video Analysis**\n\nFRAME should look for:\n\n- which support points are actually carrying weight,\n- whether a target limb is loaded or light,\n- whether the athlete commits weight too early,\n- whether pressure is failing because bodyweight is elsewhere,\n- and whether a sweep or takedown attempt is made before the opponent’s weight has shifted.\n\nA useful observation may be:\n\nYou attacked the foot while their weight was still fully loaded onto it. The opening appears when you first force their weight onto the opposite leg.\n\n## **Coaching Implication**\n\nInstead of only saying:\n\n“Move the leg.”\n\nFRAME may identify:\n\n“Shift their weight first, then attack the leg once it becomes light.”\n\nOr:\n\n“Transfer your weight through the shoulder before trying to free your knee.”\n\nThis teaches the mechanical condition that makes the technique easier.\n\n## **Principle**\n\n**Where the weight goes, opportunity changes.**\n\nGood grappling depends not only on where bodies are positioned, but on how mass is distributed through those positions.\n\n## **Connections**\n\n- [[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n- [[Base]]\n- [[SPINE/Pressure]]\n- [[Off-Balancing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Actor and Action Tagging.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Actor and Action Tagging","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should consistently identify who is doing what during combat footage.\n\nReliable video reasoning requires clear separation between:\n\n- Athlete action\n- Opponent action\n- Shared positional state\n- Resulting transition\n\n## Core Principle\n\nEvery observed action should be attached to an actor.\n\nSynochi should avoid ambiguous descriptions such as:\n\n“An underhook is established.”\n\nPrefer:\n\n“Athlete establishes the underhook.”\n\nor:\n\n“Opponent establishes the underhook.”\n\n## Actor Categories\n\n### Athlete\n\nThe primary user being analysed.\n\n### Opponent\n\nThe athlete’s current training partner or competitor.\n\n### External Actor\n\nUsed only when relevant.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Referee intervention\n- Coach instruction\n- Boundary reset\n\n## Action Tag Structure\n\nActor\n→ Action\n→ Target\n→ Direction\n→ Result\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete\n→ posts\n→ right hand\n→ outside\n→ prevents rotation\n\n## Core Action Classes\n\nSynochi should classify visible actions into broad categories.\n\n### Positioning\n\n- Step\n- Pivot\n- Turn\n- Level change\n- Hip shift\n- Base adjustment\n- Angle change\n- Posture change\n\n### Connection\n\n- Grip\n- Underhook\n- Overhook\n- Frame\n- Post\n- Tie\n- Clamp\n- Body lock\n- Head control\n\n### Offensive Action\n\n- Entry\n- Takedown attempt\n- Sweep\n- Pass\n- Submission attack\n- Strike\n- Off-balance\n- Snapdown\n- Back take\n\n### Defensive Action\n\n- Sprawl\n- Frame\n- Hand fight\n- Post\n- Hip escape\n- Bridge\n- Guard recovery\n- Submission defence\n- Technical stand-up\n- Disengagement\n\n### Transitional Action\n\n- Scramble\n- Turn\n- Re-attack\n- Re-shot\n- Switch side\n- Transfer\n- Follow-up\n- Reset\n\n## Target Tagging\n\nWhere useful, Synochi should identify what the action is directed toward.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Head\n- Arm\n- Wrist\n- Hip\n- Leg\n- Torso\n- Base\n- Space\n- Position\n- Connection\n\n## Direction Tagging\n\nDirection may matter.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Forward\n- Backward\n- Left\n- Right\n- Inside\n- Outside\n- Up\n- Down\n- Across\n- Around\n\nSynochi should only add direction when it is visually clear and relevant.\n\n## Action Granularity\n\nSynochi should use the smallest useful action description.\n\nToo vague:\n\n“Athlete moves.”\n\nToo detailed:\n\n“Athlete externally rotates shoulder while flexing elbow.”\n\nUseful:\n\n“Athlete pummels inside for an underhook.”\n\n## Simultaneous Actions\n\nCombat often contains multiple actions at once.\n\nSynochi should allow:\n\nAthlete action\n+\nOpponent action\n+\nState change\n\nto be represented together when necessary.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete drives forward\nwhile\nOpponent widens base\nresulting in\nNo positional change.\n\n## Action Sequence\n\nRepeated tags can form a sequence.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete level change\n→ Opponent sprawls\n→ Athlete maintains connection\n→ Athlete transitions to front headlock\n\nThese observations can later feed reaction-chain analysis.\n\n## Uncertain Action\n\nIf the action is unclear, Synochi should tag uncertainty rather than guess.\n\nExample:\n\n“Opponent appears to establish inside control.”\n\nConfidence:\nModerate.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should produce consistent, actor-specific observations that can be reused across:\n\n- Pattern detection\n- Technique recognition\n- Decision analysis\n- Failure-chain analysis\n- Opponent profiling\n- Drill prescription\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Combat Video Observation Schema]]\n\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]\n\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Combat Video Observation Schema.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Combat Video Observation Schema","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines the basic structure Synochi should use when observing combat footage.\n\nThe goal is to separate raw visual observation from interpretation so that later diagnosis is built on clear evidence.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should first describe what is visible.\n\nOnly after that should it infer meaning.\n\nThe observation layer should answer:\n\n“What can actually be seen happening in the footage?”\n\n## Observation Structure\n\nTimestamp\n→ Athlete State\n→ Opponent State\n→ Action\n→ Reaction\n→ Transition\n→ Outcome\n→ Confidence\n\n## Primary Observation Categories\n\n### Position\n\nWhat position or state are the athletes currently in?\n\nExamples:\n\n- Standing neutral\n- Clinch\n- Front headlock\n- Open guard\n- Half guard\n- Side control\n- Mount\n- Back control\n- Scramble\n- Transitional state\n\n### Athlete Action\n\nWhat does the athlete visibly do?\n\nExamples:\n\n- Shoots\n- Frames\n- Posts\n- Turns\n- Changes level\n- Reaches\n- Grips\n- Pummels\n- Bridges\n- Shrimp\n- Passes\n- Attacks submission\n- Disengages\n\n### Opponent Reaction\n\nWhat does the opponent visibly do in response?\n\nExamples:\n\n- Sprawls\n- Posts\n- Frames\n- Turns away\n- Turns in\n- Builds posture\n- Retreats\n- Drives forward\n- Recovers guard\n- Clears connection\n\n### Transition\n\nWhat state change occurs?\n\nExample:\n\nStanding\n→ Single-leg entry\n→ Sprawl\n→ Front headlock\n\n### Outcome\n\nWhat was the immediate result?\n\nExamples:\n\n- Position maintained\n- Position improved\n- Position lost\n- Score created\n- Submission exposure\n- Escape\n- Reset\n- No meaningful change\n\n## Observable vs Inferred\n\nSynochi should distinguish:\n\n### Observable\n\n“Opponent posts right hand.”\n\n### Inferred\n\n“Opponent may be posting to prevent being rotated.”\n\nThe first belongs in the observation layer.\n\nThe second belongs in later reasoning.\n\n## Temporal Segmentation\n\nVideo should be broken into meaningful combat events rather than arbitrary fixed intervals.\n\nA segment may begin when:\n\n- Position changes\n- A new attack begins\n- A reaction occurs\n- Initiative changes\n- A scramble starts\n- A submission threat appears\n- The exchange resets\n\n## Confidence\n\nEach observation should carry confidence.\n\n### High Confidence\n\nClearly visible.\n\n### Moderate Confidence\n\nMostly visible but partially obstructed or ambiguous.\n\n### Low Confidence\n\nImportant details are hidden, unclear, or outside the frame.\n\nSynochi should not manufacture certainty from poor footage.\n\n## Missing Information\n\nSynochi should explicitly recognise when footage does not show enough information.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Grip hidden\n- Leg position obscured\n- Camera angle blocks hip relationship\n- Action begins before clip starts\n- Exchange ends outside frame\n\nUnknown should remain unknown.\n\n## Observation Compression\n\nRepeated low-level observations may later be compressed into meaningful patterns.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent posts right hand\n→ athlete redirects\n→ opponent turns away\n\nRepeated across footage:\n\n“Right-hand posting repeatedly creates back exposure.”\n\nCompression should happen after observation, not before.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should create a reliable visual evidence layer that later Combat Intelligence can reason over.\n\nThe goal is:\n\nSee accurately\n→ structure the event\n→ preserve uncertainty\n→ then diagnose\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Combat Intelligence — Operating Definition]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Constraint Recognition.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Constraint Recognition","body":"# \n\n## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nConstraint Recognition is Synochi’s ability to identify the structural, positional, mechanical, spatial, or tactical factors that are limiting an athlete’s available actions during an exchange.\n\nA constraint is not simply a bad position.\n\nIt is anything that meaningfully reduces the athlete’s viable option-space.\n\n## Core Principle\nBefore judging a decision, identify what options were actually available.\n\n**State → Constraints → Available options → Chosen action → Outcome**\n\nAn action that appears poor in isolation may be reasonable under severe constraint.\n\n## Why It Matters\nVideo analysis becomes shallow when it evaluates only the visible action.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete fails to hip escape from side control.\n\nSurface interpretation:\n\n\"They should have shrimped.\"\n\nConstraint-aware interpretation:\n\n\"The near shoulder was pinned, the head was turned away, the far hip was blocked, and there was no effective frame. The hip escape option was already structurally degraded.\"\n\nThe meaningful problem occurred before the failed escape.\n\n## Constraint Classes\n\n### Positional Constraint\nThe current position limits available movement.\n\nExamples:\n\n- mounted\n- pinned against boundary\n- back exposed\n- trapped underneath hips\n\n### Mechanical Constraint\nBody structure prevents efficient movement.\n\nExamples:\n\n- shoulder pinned\n- elbow separated\n- spine rotated\n- hips flattened\n- knee line controlled\n\n### Connection Constraint\nOpponent attachment prevents separation or redirection.\n\nExamples:\n\n- body lock\n- wrist ride\n- crossface\n- seatbelt\n- leg entanglement\n\n### Spatial Constraint\nThe environment or available space restricts movement.\n\nExamples:\n\n- cage\n- wall\n- mat boundary\n- opponent blocking retreat path\n\n### Balance Constraint\nThe athlete cannot freely move because base is compromised.\n\nExamples:\n\n- weight shifted over one leg\n- posting hand occupied\n- centre of mass displaced\n- stance narrowed\n\n### Tactical Constraint\nThe athlete avoids an option because it creates another credible threat.\n\nExample:\n\nTurning inward may expose a choke.\n\nThe movement is physically available but strategically constrained.\n\n## Constraint Accumulation\nConstraints often become dangerous through accumulation.\n\nOne constraint may be manageable.\n\nSeveral simultaneous constraints can collapse the decision-space.\n\nExample:\n\nCrossface  \n+ underhook  \n+ flattened hips  \n+ trapped lower leg\n\nEach condition narrows the defensive system.\n\nTogether they may produce near-total positional compression.\n\n## Constraint Removal\nDefence is often not about immediately escaping the position.\n\nIt may first require removing one constraint.\n\nExample:\n\nBefore escaping mount:\n\nrecover elbow position  \n→ restore frame  \n→ recover hip movement  \n→ create angle  \n→ escape\n\nSynochi should recognise this sequence.\n\n## Constraint Priority\nNot all constraints carry equal importance.\n\nSome constraints are upstream.\n\nRemoving them restores multiple options at once.\n\nExample:\n\nRecovering head position may restore:\n\n- posture\n- rotation\n- base\n- framing ability\n- escape direction\n\nSynochi should attempt to identify the highest-leverage constraint rather than simply listing everything that is wrong.\n\n## Hidden Constraints\nSome constraints are not visually obvious from one frame.\n\nThey may emerge through repeated failed movement.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete repeatedly attempts to turn left but cannot.\n\nPossible hidden constraint:\n\nopponent controlling far hip outside camera angle.\n\nSynochi should mark this as inferred rather than directly observed.\n\n## Constraint vs Preference\nAn athlete choosing not to perform an action does not prove they were unable to perform it.\n\nSynochi should distinguish:\n\n- mechanically unavailable\n- tactically undesirable\n- not recognised\n- deliberately rejected\n- visually unclear\n\nThis protects against false diagnosis.\n\n## Constraint Release Event\nA meaningful release occurs when an athlete regains previously unavailable action-space.\n\nExamples:\n\n- frame recovered\n- grip broken\n- head freed\n- knee line cleared\n- posture restored\n- wall escaped\n\nConstraint release often marks the beginning of a successful transition.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You waited too long to escape.\"\n\nConstraint-aware analysis:\n\n\"By the time you initiated the escape, your head, near shoulder, and hip were already controlled. The higher-value intervention was preventing the second and third constraints from accumulating.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nConstraint Recognition helps Synochi identify:\n\n- why techniques failed\n- which defensive action should come first\n- when positional deterioration became significant\n- which control mattered most\n- whether the athlete lacked options or simply selected poorly\n\nThis moves analysis from:\n\n**What technique should you have used?**\n\ntoward:\n\n**What needed to become possible first?**\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should maintain a dynamic constraint map:\n\n**Observe state → detect restrictions → estimate severity → identify accumulated constraints → rank leverage → detect release → update available action-space**\n\nConstraints should feed directly into decision analysis and coaching recommendations.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Threat Recognition]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Control Change Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Control Change Detection","body":"# Control Change Detection\n\n## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nControl Change Detection is Synochi’s ability to identify when meaningful control over the exchange shifts between athletes.\n\nA control change may occur without an obvious positional change.\n\nTwo athletes can remain in the same nominal position while the actual structure of the exchange changes completely.\n\n## Core Principle\nDo not equate position with control.\n\n**Same position ≠ same control state**\n\nSynochi should track who currently has greater influence over:\n\n- posture\n- balance\n- orientation\n- movement\n- attachment\n- available reactions\n- positional direction\n\n## Why It Matters\nA position label can hide important changes.\n\nExample:\n\nBoth moments may still be \"half guard.\"\n\nBut:\n\nState 1:\nBottom athlete has inside frame, underhook, hip mobility, and head position.\n\nState 2:\nBottom athlete is flattened, crossfaced, underhook lost, hips pinned.\n\nThe positional label has barely changed.\n\nThe control state has.\n\n## Control Dimensions\n\n### Head Control\nWho is controlling head position or cervical alignment?\n\n### Shoulder Control\nWho is limiting shoulder rotation or upper-body mobility?\n\n### Hip Control\nWho is controlling hip direction, hip escape, or hip connection?\n\n### Inside Position\nWho owns the strategically valuable internal space?\n\n### Limb Control\nWho is controlling wrists, elbows, knees, ankles, or posting limbs?\n\n### Base Control\nWho is influencing balance, posts, stance, or weight distribution?\n\n### Orientation Control\nWho is determining which direction the exchange is facing or rotating?\n\n### Attachment Control\nWho is deciding whether distance closes, remains connected, or separates?\n\n## Control Transfer\nA control transfer occurs when meaningful structural influence shifts.\n\nExample:\n\nBottom half guard:\n\nInside frame present  \n→ frame collapsed  \n→ crossface established  \n→ shoulders flattened\n\nThe important event is not only the eventual pass.\n\nA control transfer has already occurred.\n\n## Partial Control Change\nControl does not always transfer all at once.\n\nSynochi should allow mixed states.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete A controls upper-body orientation.\n\nAthlete B retains lower-body inside position.\n\nThis creates a contested control state rather than a simple binary winner.\n\n## Control Accumulation\nControl can build progressively.\n\n**Initial contact → partial restriction → multiple constraints → dominant control**\n\nSynochi should recognise when several small control gains combine into a major structural advantage.\n\n## Control Loss\nControl loss may occur through:\n\n- grip failure\n- frame collapse\n- post removal\n- angle loss\n- hip separation\n- head-position loss\n- overextension\n- failed attachment\n- opponent re-pummelling inside\n\nThe observable loss should be identified before inferring why it occurred.\n\n## Control Stability\nTemporary contact should not automatically be classified as meaningful control.\n\nSynochi should consider:\n\n- duration\n- structural effect\n- resistance from opponent\n- whether movement options changed\n- whether the control survived subsequent motion\n\n## Control Change vs Positional Change\nA useful hierarchy is:\n\n**Control change → transition bias → positional change**\n\nMany positional outcomes are preceded by a detectable shift in control.\n\nThis gives Synochi an earlier diagnostic signal.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You got mounted.\"\n\nControl-aware analysis:\n\n\"Before the mount, your far elbow separated from your torso, your near-side frame collapsed, and your hips were turned away. The control shift happened before the positional score changed.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nControl Change Detection allows Synochi to identify earlier intervention points.\n\nThis is especially useful for:\n\n- guard passing\n- guard retention\n- pin escapes\n- scrambles\n- takedown finishes\n- back takes\n- submission defence\n\nThe coaching question becomes:\n\n**Where did control first begin to shift?**\n\nrather than:\n\n**Where did the final position go wrong?**\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should maintain a dynamic control state alongside positional state.\n\n**Observe structure → estimate control distribution → detect meaningful shift → identify control transfer → update transition momentum → observe outcome**\n\nThis creates a richer model of combat than position labels alone.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Control Relationship Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Control Relationship Detection","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nControl Relationship Detection identifies who currently has meaningful control within an exchange and what mechanisms are producing that control.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nBeing on top does not automatically mean being in control.\n\nControl should be inferred from functional constraints placed on the opponent.\n\n## Observable Control Mechanisms\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Head control\n- Hip control\n- Shoulder control\n- Inside position\n- Underhooks\n- Overhooks\n- Frames\n- Hooks\n- Leg entanglement\n- Wrist control\n- Body locks\n- Crossfaces\n- Wedges\n- Pinning pressure\n- Grip dominance\n- Posture disruption\n- Base disruption\n- Limb isolation\n\n## Control Questions\n\nFRAME should ask:\n\n- Who can move freely?\n- Who is being constrained?\n- Who is dictating direction?\n- Who controls inside space?\n- Who controls the opponent's posture?\n- Who controls the hips or shoulders?\n- Who has meaningful attacking options?\n- Who is reacting rather than initiating?\n\n## Control Is Graduated\n\nControl should not be treated as binary.\n\nPossible states include:\n\n- No clear control\n- Slight initiative\n- Temporary control\n- Stable control\n- Dominant control\n- Submission-level control\n\n## Local vs Global Control\n\nAn athlete may control one part of the body while losing the larger positional battle.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete may control a wrist while their opponent controls their hips, head and passing direction.\n\nFRAME should distinguish local control from overall control.\n\n## Dynamic Control\n\nControl may change rapidly.\n\nThe system should recognise moments where control migrates from one athlete to the other.\n\nThis connects directly to [[Control Migration]].\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nControl detection helps FRAME identify:\n\n- Why a pass succeeds\n- Why guard retention fails\n- Why escapes stall\n- Why submission entries become available\n- Which athlete is dictating the exchange\n\n## Related\n\n[[Position and State Recognition]]\n\n[[Transition and Positional Change Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Decision Window Recognition.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Decision Window Recognition","body":"# Decision Window Recognition\n\n## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nDecision Window Recognition is Synochi’s ability to identify the period in which an athlete had a meaningful opportunity to choose between viable actions before that opportunity narrowed or disappeared.\n\nA decision window is not simply \"the moment before something went wrong.\"\n\nIt is the interval during which multiple useful options were still available.\n\n## Core Principle\nGood coaching should identify the last useful moment for choice.\n\n**State → Opportunity opens → Viable options exist → Constraint increases → Window narrows → Window closes**\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany technical mistakes are diagnosed too late.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete is fully flattened in half guard.\n\nSurface analysis:\n\"They should frame and recover guard.\"\n\nBut the meaningful decision window may have existed two seconds earlier, when:\n\n- the crossface had not yet settled\n- the underhook was still contestable\n- the hips were still mobile\n- the head position could still be recovered\n\nBy the time the bad position is obvious, the useful decision window may already be closed.\n\n## Decision Window Components\n\n### Opening Condition\nThe structural event that makes a useful action available.\n\nExamples:\n\n- opponent posts\n- elbow separates\n- inside space opens\n- balance shifts\n- grip breaks\n- angle appears\n- pressure temporarily reduces\n\n### Available Options\nThe meaningful actions available during that interval.\n\nExamples:\n\n- frame\n- pummel\n- attack\n- retreat\n- angle change\n- recover base\n- disengage\n- transition\n\n### Window Duration\nHow long the opportunity remains viable.\n\nSome windows are large.\n\nOthers exist for fractions of a second.\n\n### Closing Condition\nThe event that removes or greatly reduces the available option.\n\nExamples:\n\n- grip settles\n- weight transfers\n- hip line clears\n- elbow reconnects\n- posture recovers\n- opponent establishes secondary control\n\n## Early vs Late Decision\nThe same action can have very different value depending on when it occurs.\n\nEarly frame:\nprevents pressure from settling.\n\nLate frame:\nattempts to remove already-established pressure.\n\nThe technique may be identical.\n\nThe decision quality is not.\n\n## Decision Window vs Reaction Time\nA missed window does not automatically mean poor reactions.\n\nPossible causes include:\n\n- athlete did not perceive the opportunity\n- athlete perceived it but chose another option\n- physical constraint prevented action\n- fatigue reduced execution speed\n- opponent disguised the opportunity\n- video angle hides relevant information\n\nSynochi should separate detection failure from execution failure.\n\n## Window Compression\nHigher-level opponents often reduce the amount of time available for useful decisions.\n\nA beginner may offer a large window.\n\nAn expert may create:\n\n**threat → reaction → control**\n\nwith almost no pause between them.\n\nSynochi should therefore consider the competitive level and speed of the exchange before judging timing.\n\n## Recurrent Missed Windows\nRepeated missed windows are more meaningful than isolated misses.\n\nExample:\n\nAcross several exchanges, the athlete consistently allows:\n\ninside position loss  \n→ pressure establishment  \n→ late defensive response\n\nThis may indicate a recognition or timing pattern rather than one technical error.\n\n## Opportunity Windows\nDecision Window Recognition also applies offensively.\n\nExample:\n\nOpponent posts hand  \n→ elbow separates  \n→ arm attack becomes available  \n→ posture recovers  \n→ opportunity disappears\n\nSynochi should recognise both defensive and offensive windows.\n\n## Critical Window\nSome decision windows have disproportionate effect on the exchange.\n\nA critical window is one where the chosen action strongly alters downstream probability.\n\nExamples:\n\n- preventing the crossface\n- securing the underhook\n- clearing the knee line\n- recovering posture\n- denying head position\n- taking the back during exposure\n\nThese should receive higher coaching priority.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You reacted too late.\"\n\nWindow-aware analysis:\n\n\"The useful window opened when his right hand posted and his elbow separated. Once he reconnected the elbow and settled his weight, the arm attack was largely gone.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nDecision Window Recognition helps Synochi distinguish between:\n\n- wrong technique\n- correct technique used too late\n- missed opportunity\n- recognition failure\n- execution failure\n- already-constrained situation\n\nThis produces more precise training targets.\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"Work on escapes.\"\n\nSynochi may prescribe:\n\n\"Train recognising the crossface entry before shoulder control settles.\"\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should model decision windows dynamically:\n\n**Observe structural change → detect opportunity → estimate viable actions → monitor narrowing constraints → identify closure → evaluate chosen response**\n\nOver repeated footage, Synochi can learn which decision windows the athlete consistently recognises, misses, or responds to late.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Threat Recognition]]\n[[Initiative and Reaction Detection]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Error Point Identification.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Error Point Identification","body":"# Error Point Identification\n\n## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nError Point Identification is Synochi’s ability to locate the earliest meaningful event that materially increased the probability of an undesirable outcome.\n\nThe visible failure is not always the true error.\n\nThe true error is often upstream.\n\n## Core Principle\nDiagnose from outcome backward until the first meaningful deterioration is found.\n\n**Outcome → preceding transition → control shift → constraint increase → decision point → earliest meaningful error**\n\n## Why It Matters\nCombat analysis often over-focuses on the final failure.\n\nExamples:\n\n- submission finished\n- guard passed\n- takedown completed\n- back taken\n- mount conceded\n\nBut by the time these outcomes occur, the exchange may already be structurally lost.\n\nSynochi should therefore search for the first point where the athlete’s position, control, options, or timing became meaningfully worse.\n\n## Error Point vs Outcome\nThe outcome is what happened.\n\nThe error point is where the probability of that outcome began to rise significantly.\n\nExample:\n\nOutcome:\nAthlete gets mounted.\n\nPossible error chain:\n\ninside frame lost  \n→ shoulder pinned  \n→ hips turned away  \n→ knee line cleared  \n→ mount established\n\nThe useful error point may be frame loss, not mount concession.\n\n## Error Point Classes\n\n### Technical Error\nA movement or structure was executed poorly.\n\nExamples:\n\n- elbow flared\n- grip misplaced\n- head position lost\n- base narrowed\n\n### Timing Error\nCorrect action performed too late or too early.\n\n### Recognition Error\nAthlete failed to perceive an emerging threat or opportunity.\n\n### Decision Error\nAthlete recognised the situation but selected a lower-value option.\n\n### Sequencing Error\nCorrect actions were used in the wrong order.\n\n### Constraint Management Error\nAthlete allowed multiple restrictions to accumulate before addressing them.\n\n### Strategic Error\nRepeated local decisions conflict with the broader tactical objective.\n\n## Earliest Meaningful Error\nSynochi should not simply identify the earliest imperfection.\n\nIt should identify the earliest event that had meaningful downstream consequence.\n\nA minor technical deviation that changes nothing should not be over-weighted.\n\nThe question is:\n\n**Did this event materially alter the future action-space or outcome probability?**\n\n## Error Chains\nMany failures emerge through linked errors.\n\nExample:\n\nlate grip fight  \n→ inside position lost  \n→ posture compromised  \n→ forced defensive reaction  \n→ submission exposure\n\nSynochi should preserve the chain rather than collapsing everything into one diagnosis.\n\n## Primary vs Secondary Error\nA primary error initiates meaningful deterioration.\n\nA secondary error occurs after the athlete is already disadvantaged.\n\nExample:\n\nPrimary:\nfailed to deny underhook.\n\nSecondary:\npoor bridge after being fully flattened.\n\nThe secondary error may still matter, but it should not replace the upstream diagnosis.\n\n## Recoverability\nNot every error creates immediate failure.\n\nAfter an error, Synochi should assess whether recovery remained realistic.\n\nPossible states:\n\n- fully recoverable\n- recoverable with narrow window\n- highly constrained\n- effectively lost\n\nThis helps distinguish:\n\n\"mistake\"\n\nfrom\n\n\"point of no return\"\n\n## Repeated Error Pattern\nOne isolated error may be noise.\n\nRepeated occurrences across footage can reveal a stable pattern.\n\nExamples:\n\n- consistently losing head position\n- always reacting after hip line is cleared\n- repeatedly posting same-side hand\n- conceding inside space during entries\n\nRepeated error points should influence Athlete Model confidence.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You got caught in the armbar.\"\n\nError-point analysis:\n\n\"The armbar became likely earlier when your elbow separated during the pass. The submission was the final expression of an upstream positional error.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nError Point Identification helps Synochi prescribe training around causes rather than symptoms.\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"Work on armbar defence.\"\n\nSynochi may prescribe:\n\n\"Train keeping elbow-knee connection during this passing transition.\"\n\nThis produces more transferable learning.\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should identify errors through backward causal tracing:\n\n**Observe outcome → reconstruct transition → detect control changes → detect constraint accumulation → locate decision windows → identify earliest meaningful deterioration → assign confidence**\n\nError points should remain probabilistic where causality is uncertain.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Decision Window Recognition]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Threat Recognition]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Escalation Point Recognition.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Escalation Point Recognition","body":"## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nEscalation Point Recognition is Synochi’s ability to identify the moment an exchange shifts from manageable disadvantage into a materially more dangerous state.\n\nAn escalation point is not necessarily the final error.\n\nIt is the point where risk, constraint, threat, or positional deterioration meaningfully accelerates.\n\n## Core Principle\nTrack not only whether the position is getting worse, but when the rate of deterioration increases.\n\n**Stable risk → Emerging pressure → Escalation point → Rapid deterioration → Critical state**\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany exchanges contain a period where the athlete is disadvantaged but still structurally functional.\n\nThen one event changes the character of the position.\n\nExamples:\n\n- second control point is established\n- posture collapses\n- elbow becomes isolated\n- head gets trapped\n- knee line is cleared\n- opponent connects upper and lower body control\n\nThat event may matter more than the initial disadvantage.\n\n## Escalation Signals\n\n### Constraint Multiplication\nOne restriction becomes several.\n\nExample:\n\ncrossface  \n→ underhook  \n→ flattened hips\n\nThe athlete’s option-space can collapse rapidly once constraints begin stacking.\n\n### Threat Intensification\nA latent threat becomes immediate.\n\nExample:\n\narm isolation  \n→ elbow crosses centre line  \n→ submission becomes urgent\n\n### Positional Consolidation\nA loose advantage becomes stable control.\n\nExample:\n\npass in progress  \n→ chest connection established  \n→ hips blocked  \n→ side control consolidated\n\n### Loss of Recovery Routes\nPreviously viable defensive pathways disappear.\n\nExamples:\n\n- frame removed\n- post trapped\n- knee line closed\n- boundary reached\n- head position lost\n\n### Forced Commitment\nThe athlete becomes committed to a defensive action that exposes another vulnerability.\n\n## Escalation vs Initial Error\nThe initial error and escalation point may be different.\n\nExample:\n\nInitial error:\nunderhook conceded.\n\nEscalation point:\ncrossface connects and flattens shoulders.\n\nThe first creates vulnerability.\n\nThe second sharply increases danger.\n\n## Escalation Gradient\nSynochi should treat deterioration as a gradient rather than binary states.\n\nPossible model:\n\nLow risk  \n→ Contested  \n→ Unstable  \n→ Constrained  \n→ Critical  \n→ Resolved\n\nThis allows more precise interpretation of when intervention was still realistic.\n\n## Multiple Escalations\nOne exchange may contain several escalation points.\n\nExample:\n\nGuard pass:\n\ninside position lost  \n→ knee line cleared  \n→ upper-body connection established  \n→ mount transition begins\n\nEach stage reduces recovery probability.\n\nSynochi should identify the highest-leverage escalation, not simply every worsening event.\n\n## Escalation Velocity\nSome positions deteriorate slowly.\n\nOthers collapse quickly.\n\nSynochi should estimate how rapidly the athlete’s viable options are disappearing.\n\nFast escalation may indicate:\n\n- strong opponent sequencing\n- delayed recognition\n- stacked constraints\n- poor structural recovery\n- high-threat transition\n\n## Escalation Reversal\nAn athlete may interrupt escalation before full failure.\n\nExample:\n\nshoulder flattened  \n→ frame recovered  \n→ head position restored  \n→ hip movement returns\n\nThis should be recognised as successful de-escalation.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You were already in trouble there.\"\n\nEscalation-aware analysis:\n\n\"You were disadvantaged once the underhook was lost, but the exchange became critical when the crossface connected and your shoulders flattened. That was the major escalation point.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nEscalation Point Recognition helps Synochi identify:\n\n- when defence must become urgent\n- which control cannot be allowed to consolidate\n- where recovery probability sharply drops\n- which opponent actions create the largest state change\n- whether the athlete is recognising danger early enough\n\nThis helps coaching distinguish:\n\n**problem present**\n\nfrom\n\n**problem becoming critical**\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should monitor deterioration dynamically:\n\n**Observe state → estimate risk → track constraint/threat growth → detect acceleration → identify escalation point → monitor recovery probability → observe resolution**\n\nEscalation should be inferred from changes in the structure of the exchange, not from dramatic movement alone.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Recovery Opportunity Detection]]\n[[Error Point Identification]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Threat Recognition]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Event Boundary Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Event Boundary Detection","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should decide where one meaningful combat event ends and another begins when analysing video.\n\nGood analysis depends on dividing footage into useful units.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should segment combat footage by meaningful state change, not by arbitrary time intervals.\n\nA new event should begin when the logic of the exchange changes.\n\n## Event Boundary Triggers\n\nA new event may begin when:\n\n- Position changes\n- A new attack starts\n- A submission threat appears\n- A takedown attempt begins\n- A scramble starts\n- Control is established\n- Control is lost\n- Initiative changes\n- Opponent reaction creates a new branch\n- The athletes disengage\n- The exchange resets\n- A score or finish occurs\n\n## Event Structure\n\nPrevious State\n→ Trigger\n→ New Event\n→ Outcome\n\n## Example\n\nStanding neutral\n→ athlete shoots\n→ takedown event begins\n\nOpponent sprawls\n→ front headlock established\n→ new event begins\n\nOpponent clears head\n→ athletes reset standing\n→ event ends\n\nSynochi should treat these as connected but distinct events.\n\n## Micro-Event vs Meaningful Event\n\nNot every movement deserves its own segment.\n\nSynochi should avoid breaking footage into excessive micro-events such as:\n\n- Individual grip adjustments\n- Small foot movements\n- Minor weight shifts\n\nunless they materially change the exchange.\n\n## Nested Events\n\nA larger sequence may contain smaller meaningful events.\n\nExample:\n\nGuard Passing Sequence\n\nContains:\n\n- Entry\n- Frame battle\n- Direction change\n- Pass attempt\n- Consolidation\n\nSynochi may preserve both the larger sequence and important sub-events.\n\n## Boundary Ambiguity\n\nSome transitions are gradual.\n\nWhen event boundaries are unclear, Synochi should prefer:\n\n- Wider segmentation\n- Lower confidence\n- Preserving context\n\nrather than forcing an artificial cut.\n\n## Event Continuity\n\nSeparate events should remain linked when they belong to the same larger chain.\n\nExample:\n\nFailed shot\n→ sprawl\n→ front headlock\n→ go-behind\n\nThese may be separate events but still part of one continuous sequence.\n\n## Why Boundaries Matter\n\nCorrect segmentation improves:\n\n- Pattern detection\n- Reaction mapping\n- Failure-chain analysis\n- Decision-point identification\n- Transition analysis\n- Drill prescription\n- Athlete-model updates\n\nPoor segmentation can make connected behaviour look unrelated.\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should divide combat footage into the smallest meaningful units that preserve the logic of the exchange.\n\nThe goal is enough structure for reasoning without destroying continuity.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Combat Video Observation Schema]]\n\n[[Positional State Transition]]\n\n[[Reaction Chain Mapping]]\n\n[[Failure Chain Analysis]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Initiation and Response Pairing 1.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Initiation and Response Pairing 1","body":"# Initiation and Response Pairing 1\n\nThe duplicate explanation is maintained in the unnumbered note below. This filename remains available as a compatibility route.\n\n![[COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Initiation and Response Pairing]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Initiation and Response Pairing.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Initiation and Response Pairing","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nInitiation and Response Pairing links an athlete's action with the opponent's immediate response and any subsequent counter-response.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nCombat actions should rarely be analysed in isolation.\n\nTechniques exist inside interaction loops.\n\n## Basic Structure\n\nInitiation → Response → Adjustment → Outcome\n\nExample:\n\nPass attempt → defensive frame → angle change → pass completion\n\n## Common Pairings\n\n- Shot → sprawl\n- Guard pull → passing entry\n- Underhook → whizzer\n- Knee cut → knee shield\n- Arm drag → posture recovery\n- Leg entry → leg extraction\n- Mount escape → remount\n- Submission attempt → defensive hand fighting\n\n## Timing\n\nFRAME should attempt to identify whether the response was:\n\n- Anticipatory\n- Immediate\n- Delayed\n- Absent\n- Incorrect\n- Successful but incomplete\n\n## Interaction Chains\n\nMore complex sequences may continue:\n\nInitiation → Response → Counter → Re-counter\n\nThese should remain linked as one interaction chain when possible.\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nPairing actions with responses allows FRAME to understand:\n\n- Whether an athlete anticipates common reactions\n- Whether attacks fail because of predictable defences\n- Whether follow-up options are available\n- Whether the athlete has coherent second and third actions\n\n## Related\n\n[[Actor and Action Tagging]]\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]\n[[Sequence and Exchange Reconstruction]]\n[[Success Failure and Outcome Tagging]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Initiative and Reaction Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Initiative and Reaction Detection","body":"## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nInitiative and Reaction Detection is Synochi’s ability to distinguish between the athlete who is currently shaping the exchange and the athlete who is primarily responding to it.\n\nInitiative is not simply \"who moved first.\"\n\nIt is the capacity to create conditions that force, narrow, or redirect the opponent’s available responses.\n\n## Core Principle\nTrack who is causing adaptation.\n\n**Action → Forced response → Follow-up → New constraint**\n\nThe athlete repeatedly generating this chain usually holds initiative.\n\n## Why It Matters\nTwo athletes may appear equally active while only one is actually directing the exchange.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete A repeatedly attacks grips, changes angle, and forces defensive posts.\n\nAthlete B moves constantly but only to recover balance and structure.\n\nBoth are moving.\n\nOnly one is controlling the decision flow.\n\n## Initiative Signals\n\n### Threat Creation\nAn athlete introduces a credible attack or positional threat.\n\n### Forced Reaction\nThe opponent must respond to avoid deterioration.\n\n### Response Prediction\nThe initiating athlete appears prepared for the likely reaction.\n\n### Follow-Up Capture\nThe reaction creates the next attack or positional gain.\n\n### Option Reduction\nThe opponent’s available choices become progressively narrower.\n\n## Reactive State\nAn athlete is in a reactive state when their actions are primarily driven by threats already imposed by the opponent.\n\nExamples:\n\n- framing after pressure has already arrived\n- posting after balance has already been broken\n- defending grips rather than establishing preferred grips\n- recovering guard after the passer has already created the angle\n- hand fighting only after the neck becomes exposed\n\nReactive does not mean technically poor.\n\nSome strategies deliberately invite reactions.\n\nSynochi should infer pattern before judging quality.\n\n## Initiative Transfer\nInitiative can change without positional control changing.\n\nExample:\n\nTop player pressures.\n\nBottom player creates an underhook.\n\nTop player reacts defensively.\n\nBottom player comes onto a single leg.\n\nThe position may still resemble half guard, but initiative has transferred.\n\n## False Initiative\nHigh activity does not necessarily equal initiative.\n\nSynochi should distinguish between:\n\n- purposeful threat generation\n- defensive scrambling\n- low-value movement\n- repeated failed attacks\n- motion without constraint creation\n\nAn athlete may attack frequently while the opponent remains structurally unaffected.\n\n## Initiative Chains\nInitiative is often best understood across multiple events.\n\nExample:\n\nSnapdown  \n→ opponent posts  \n→ angle change  \n→ front headlock  \n→ opponent exposes elbow  \n→ back take\n\nThe value lies in the chain, not any isolated action.\n\n## Reaction Quality\nWhen an athlete is reacting, Synochi should evaluate whether the response:\n\n- restores structure\n- creates counter-threat\n- merely delays deterioration\n- exposes a new weakness\n- successfully resets the exchange\n\nThis prevents \"reaction\" from automatically being interpreted negatively.\n\n## Timing Sensitivity\nEarly reactions preserve optionality.\n\nLate reactions often occur after the opponent has already constrained the available solution space.\n\nSynochi should therefore distinguish:\n\n**anticipatory response**\n\nfrom\n\n**late forced reaction**\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You kept defending his passing.\"\n\nInitiative-aware analysis:\n\n\"Once he established the first inside-angle threat, each of your following movements became reactive. You recovered individual positions, but never regained enough initiative to force him to respond to you.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nInitiative Detection helps Synochi identify whether the athlete needs to improve:\n\n- first action selection\n- threat credibility\n- follow-up sequencing\n- counter-offence\n- earlier reactions\n- positional resets\n- decision timing\n\nIt also reveals when technical competence exists but decision control is absent.\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should track initiative as a dynamic property:\n\n**Observe action → detect threat → observe opponent response → estimate forcedness → detect follow-up → assign initiative confidence → monitor transfer**\n\nInitiative should remain probabilistic rather than binary.\n\nBoth athletes may temporarily contest it.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Observation vs Interpretation Separation.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Observation vs Interpretation Separation","body":"## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nSynochi should separate what is directly visible in combat footage from what is inferred about why it happened.\n\nAn observation describes the detectable event.\n\nAn interpretation proposes meaning, intention, cause, strategy, or internal state.\n\nThese should not be collapsed into the same claim.\n\n## Core Principle\nSee first.\n\nInfer second.\n\nThe visual layer should preserve the distinction:\n\nObserved → Inferred → Confidence → Alternative Interpretation\n\n## Example\nObservation:\nAthlete repeatedly posts the right hand to the mat while escaping bottom half guard.\n\nInterpretation:\nThe athlete may be relying on the post because their hip escape is insufficient.\n\nAlternative interpretation:\nThe opponent may be forcing the post through upper-body pressure.\n\nThese are not equivalent claims.\n\n## Why It Matters\nWithout this separation, video analysis can produce false certainty.\n\nA visible action does not automatically reveal:\n\n- intention\n- tactical understanding\n- fatigue\n- fear\n- technical deficiency\n- strategic preference\n\nSynochi should therefore avoid converting visual correlation directly into causal explanation.\n\n## Observation Classes\n\n### Direct Observation\nClearly visible.\n\nExample:\nAthlete's elbow separates from the rib cage.\n\n### Derived Observation\nCalculated from multiple visible events.\n\nExample:\nAthlete consistently loses inside position during entries.\n\n### Interpretation\nA proposed explanation of the observation.\n\nExample:\nAthlete may not recognise when inside position has been lost.\n\n### Hidden-State Hypothesis\nInference about something not visually observable.\n\nExample:\nAthlete appears hesitant because they lack confidence in the position.\n\nThis requires substantially lower confidence.\n\n## Operational Rule\nWhenever Synochi produces a meaningful video diagnosis, it should internally distinguish:\n\n**What did I see?**\n\nfrom:\n\n**What do I think it means?**\n\nThe further a claim moves from visible evidence, the more uncertainty should be exposed.\n\n## Relationship to Confidence\nObservation certainty and interpretation certainty are separate variables.\n\nSynochi may be:\n\n95% confident an event occurred\n\nbut only\n\n55% confident about why it occurred.\n\nThis distinction should influence coaching language.\n\n## Coaching Language\nPrefer:\n\n\"The footage shows...\"\n\n\"It appears that...\"\n\n\"One possible reason is...\"\n\n\"This may indicate...\"\n\nAvoid:\n\n\"You did this because...\"\n\nunless sufficient contextual evidence exists.\n\n## Synochi Function\nThis creates an epistemic boundary between perception and diagnosis.\n\nVideo does not become truth merely because Synochi can see it.\n\nThe system should preserve uncertainty until observations, athlete context, repeated patterns, and outcomes converge.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Combat Video Observation Schema]]\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Occlusion and Missing Detail Handling.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Occlusion and Missing Detail Handling","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should reason when important parts of a combat exchange are hidden, off-camera, obstructed, or otherwise unavailable.\n\nMissing visual information should be treated explicitly rather than silently filled in.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSynochi should never convert an unseen detail into a confident observation.\n\nWhen information is missing, the system should preserve the gap.\n\n## Common Occlusion Sources\n\n- Bodies blocking relevant limbs\n- Camera angle hiding grips\n- Referee obstruction\n- Cage or ropes obstructing view\n- Action moving outside frame\n- Low-resolution footage\n- Motion blur\n- Poor lighting\n- Cropped clips\n- Sequence beginning too late\n- Sequence ending too early\n\n## Missing Detail Categories\n\n### Hidden Grip\n\nHand or grip relationship cannot be confirmed.\n\n### Hidden Limb Position\n\nArm, leg, knee, or foot position is partially or fully obscured.\n\n### Hidden Hip Relationship\n\nHip angle, rotation, or positional relationship cannot be seen reliably.\n\n### Hidden Head Position\n\nHead placement or direction is unclear.\n\n### Missing Setup\n\nThe clip begins after the initiating action has already occurred.\n\n### Missing Outcome\n\nThe clip ends before the exchange resolves.\n\n## Handling Rule\n\nWhen a critical detail is missing, Synochi should use one of three states:\n\n### Known\n\nClearly visible.\n\n### Probable\n\nNot fully visible, but surrounding evidence strongly supports the conclusion.\n\n### Unknown\n\nEvidence is insufficient.\n\nUnknown should remain unknown.\n\n## Example\n\nVisible:\n\nAthlete is reversed from top half guard.\n\nHidden:\n\nExact underhook battle is obscured.\n\nSynochi should not state:\n\n“The athlete lost the underhook.”\n\nunless that is actually visible.\n\nPreferred:\n\n“The reversal occurs after upper-body control deteriorates, but the exact grip relationship is obscured.”\n\n## Inference Restraint\n\nOcclusion should reduce downstream confidence.\n\nMissing Detail\n→ Lower Observation Confidence\n→ Lower Diagnostic Confidence\n→ Less Prescriptive Coaching\n\n## Alternative Hypotheses\n\nWhen important detail is hidden, Synochi may preserve multiple plausible explanations.\n\nExample:\n\nPossible causes:\n\n- Underhook loss\n- Head-position loss\n- Base disruption\n\nThe system should avoid selecting one without sufficient evidence.\n\n## Request for Better Evidence\n\nWhen useful, Synochi may recommend:\n\n- Longer clip\n- Earlier start point\n- Later end point\n- Different camera angle\n- Higher-resolution footage\n- Athlete description of the exchange\n\n## Partial Analysis\n\nMissing detail does not make the entire clip useless.\n\nSynochi should analyse what remains observable while clearly separating:\n\nObserved\nfrom\nUnknown\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should remain useful under imperfect video conditions without inventing unseen information.\n\nThe goal is robust analysis with explicit evidence boundaries.\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Combat Video Observation Schema]]\n\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]\n\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Opportunity Recognition.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Opportunity Recognition","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nOpportunity Recognition identifies moments where a viable technical or tactical option appears available based on the visible state of the exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nFRAME should identify opportunities conservatively.\n\nAn option should only be suggested when the required conditions appear to exist.\n\n## Opportunity Types\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Submission opening\n- Sweep opportunity\n- Guard recovery\n- Pass direction\n- Back exposure\n- Underhook availability\n- Front headlock opportunity\n- Leg-entry opportunity\n- Escape route\n- Positional consolidation\n- Grip improvement\n- Angle change\n\n## Opportunity Window\n\nOpportunities may exist briefly.\n\nFRAME should attempt to identify:\n\n- When the window opens\n- What creates it\n- Whether the athlete recognises it\n- When the window closes\n\n## Missed vs Rejected Opportunity\n\nNot every unused option is a mistake.\n\nAn athlete may deliberately choose another strategy.\n\nFRAME should prefer:\n\n\"There appears to be an opportunity to...\"\n\nrather than:\n\n\"You should have...\"\n\nunless context strongly supports the judgement.\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nOpportunity recognition can produce actionable observations such as:\n\n- \"Build a drill around this.\"\n- \"Work on recognising this position.\"\n- \"Ask FRAME for options from here.\"\n- \"This entry appeared several times.\"\n\n## Related\n\n[[Technical Error Observation]]\n[[Position and State Recognition]]\n[[Control Relationship Detection]]\n[[Video Observation to Coaching Inference]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Pattern Recurrence Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Pattern Recurrence Detection","body":"# Pattern Recurrence Detection\n\n## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nPattern Recurrence Detection is Synochi’s ability to recognise when the same meaningful structure, error, opportunity, reaction, or tactical sequence appears repeatedly across one or more combat exchanges.\n\nA repeated event becomes more valuable when Synochi can determine whether it represents a stable pattern rather than isolated noise.\n\n## Core Principle\nDo not over-learn from one moment.\n\n**Single event → Repetition → Structural similarity → Pattern confidence → Athlete Model update**\n\n## Why It Matters\nOne clip can mislead.\n\nRepeated behaviour is more informative.\n\nExamples:\n\n- repeatedly losing inside position on the same side\n- consistently reacting late to crossfaces\n- repeatedly creating the same back-take pathway\n- habitually posting the same hand during scrambles\n- repeatedly succeeding from one leg-entanglement entry\n\nThese patterns are more useful than isolated successes or failures.\n\n## Pattern Types\n\n### Error Pattern\nThe same failure mechanism occurs repeatedly.\n\nExample:\n\nframe loss  \n→ shoulder flattened  \n→ late hip escape\n\n### Success Pattern\nA repeatable structure consistently produces good outcomes.\n\nExample:\n\nsnapdown  \n→ front headlock  \n→ go-behind\n\n### Reaction Pattern\nThe athlete repeatedly responds to a stimulus in the same way.\n\nExample:\n\nopponent pressures forward  \n→ athlete always retreats straight back\n\n### Opportunity Pattern\nThe same useful opening repeatedly appears.\n\nExample:\n\nopponent posts during passing exchanges  \n→ underhook becomes available\n\n### Tactical Pattern\nMultiple actions form a recurring strategic sequence.\n\nExample:\n\nthreaten pass  \n→ force knee recovery  \n→ attack far-side leg\n\n### Constraint Pattern\nThe same structural restriction repeatedly causes downstream problems.\n\nExample:\n\nhead position lost before every failed takedown defence.\n\n## Structural Similarity\nTwo events do not need to look visually identical to belong to the same pattern.\n\nSynochi should match deeper structure.\n\nExample:\n\nDifferent techniques may share the same underlying pattern:\n\n- overcommitted forward weight\n- lost base\n- forced post\n- reversal\n\nThe visible techniques differ.\n\nThe mechanism is the same.\n\n## Frequency vs Importance\nA frequent event is not automatically important.\n\nSynochi should consider:\n\n- frequency\n- severity\n- downstream consequence\n- recoverability\n- competitive context\n- relevance to athlete goals\n\nA rare catastrophic pattern may deserve more attention than a frequent low-cost imperfection.\n\n## Pattern Confidence\nConfidence should increase when:\n\n- the same structure appears repeatedly\n- similar triggers produce similar responses\n- the pattern appears across different opponents\n- outcomes are consistent\n- visual confidence is high\n\nConfidence should remain lower when:\n\n- footage is limited\n- events are ambiguous\n- opponent behaviour differs substantially\n- sample size is small\n\n## Context Dependence\nPatterns should not be treated as universal traits without context.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete repeatedly concedes underhooks from bottom half guard.\n\nThis does not mean:\n\n\"Athlete has poor inside position everywhere.\"\n\nThe pattern should initially remain scoped to the environment in which it was observed.\n\n## Pattern Generalisation\nOnly after repeated evidence should Synochi widen the pattern.\n\nPossible progression:\n\nSpecific:\nloses underhook from right-side half guard\n\nBroader:\nstruggles to maintain inside upper-body position from bottom guard\n\nGeneralised:\ninside-position recognition may be a recurring defensive constraint\n\nGeneralisation should be earned.\n\n## Pattern Break\nSynochi should also detect when an established pattern stops occurring.\n\nExample:\n\nPrevious footage:\nathlete consistently reacts late to knee cuts.\n\nRecent footage:\nathlete frames early and recovers guard.\n\nThis may indicate adaptation.\n\nPattern disappearance is evidence too.\n\n## Coaching Value\nPattern Recurrence Detection helps Synochi avoid random technical advice.\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"You lost the underhook here.\"\n\nSynochi can say:\n\n\"This is the fourth exchange where you concede the near-side underhook before your guard starts collapsing. That looks like a recurring upstream pattern.\"\n\nThis creates stronger intervention targets.\n\n## Athlete Model Integration\nRepeated patterns can update the Athlete Model with:\n\n- strengths\n- vulnerabilities\n- preferred reactions\n- common decision errors\n- reliable offensive pathways\n- recurring missed windows\n\nPattern confidence should increase gradually rather than becoming permanent after one session.\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should compare observations across time:\n\n**Observe event → Encode structure → Compare with prior events → Detect similarity → Estimate recurrence → Update pattern confidence → Monitor future confirmation or contradiction**\n\nThis allows the system to move from clip analysis toward longitudinal combat intelligence.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Error Point Identification]]\n[[Recovery Opportunity Detection]]\n[[Decision Window Recognition]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Resolution State Classification]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Position and State Recognition.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Position and State Recognition","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nPosition and State Recognition is the process of identifying the current grappling or combat state shown in video.\n\nThe goal is not simply to name a position, but to determine the functional state of the exchange at a given moment.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nA position should be classified by the control relationships and available actions it creates, not only by visual appearance.\n\nTwo visually similar positions may represent different functional states.\n\n## Common States\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Neutral standing\n- Clinch\n- Takedown engagement\n- Seated guard\n- Supine guard\n- Open guard\n- Closed guard\n- Half guard\n- Butterfly guard\n- Single-leg X\n- X guard\n- K guard\n- Leg entanglement\n- Passing position\n- Side control\n- Knee-on-belly\n- Mount\n- Back control\n- Turtle\n- Front headlock\n- Scramble\n- Submission engagement\n- Transitional / unresolved state\n\n## Recognition Requirements\n\nFRAME should attempt to identify:\n\n- Athlete orientation\n- Top / bottom relationship\n- Hip relationship\n- Head position\n- Limb configuration\n- Grip or connection structure\n- Degree of positional stability\n- Whether the position is established or transitional\n\n## Transitional States\n\nNot every frame should be forced into a named position.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Guard recovery in progress\n- Pass partially completed\n- Scramble\n- Takedown transition\n- Back exposure without established control\n- Leg entanglement forming but not secured\n\nThese should be labelled as transitional when appropriate.\n\n## Ambiguity Handling\n\nIf the position cannot be determined confidently:\n\n- Use the closest broader state\n- Reduce confidence\n- Avoid inventing specific positional detail\n- Preserve multiple plausible interpretations where useful\n\nExample:\n\n\"Open guard / leg-entry transition — exact configuration unclear.\"\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nPosition recognition allows FRAME to understand:\n\n- Where exchanges begin\n- Where athletes repeatedly succeed or fail\n- Which positions dominate training or competition footage\n- Whether technical actions make sense in context\n- How positional transitions occur\n\n## Video Analysis Rule\n\nPosition recognition should describe what is visibly supported before attempting to infer why the athlete chose the position.\n\n## Related\n\n[[Actor and Action Tagging]]\n[[Control Relationship Detection]]\n[[Transition and Positional Change Detection]]\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Positional State Recognition.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Positional State Recognition","body":"# Positional State Recognition\n\n## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nPositional State Recognition is Synochi’s ability to identify the current structural relationship between athletes before interpreting individual techniques.\n\nThe position establishes the action-space.\n\nThe same movement can mean something completely different depending on the positional state in which it occurs.\n\n## Core Principle\nDo not interpret an action without first understanding the state surrounding it.\n\n**Position → Constraints → Available actions → Observed action → Interpretation**\n\n## Positional State\nA positional state is more than a simple label such as:\n\n- half guard\n- mount\n- side control\n- standing\n\nIt should include the structural conditions that define the exchange.\n\n## State Components\n\n### Base Position\nThe broad recognised position.\n\nExamples:\n\n- standing\n- open guard\n- closed guard\n- half guard\n- side control\n- mount\n- back control\n- turtle\n- front headlock\n\n### Relative Orientation\nHow the athletes are positioned relative to one another.\n\nExamples:\n\n- chest-to-chest\n- chest-to-back\n- perpendicular\n- parallel\n- head-to-head\n- hip-to-hip\n\n### Control Structure\nWhat connections are currently shaping the position.\n\nExamples:\n\n- underhook\n- crossface\n- wrist control\n- head control\n- body lock\n- leg entanglement\n- frame\n- pin\n\n### Postural State\nThe structural posture of each athlete.\n\nExamples:\n\n- upright\n- flattened\n- seated\n- kneeling\n- extended\n- compressed\n- posted\n- folded\n\n### Constraint State\nWhat movement is currently restricted.\n\nExamples:\n\n- shoulder pinned\n- hips blocked\n- head controlled\n- posting arm occupied\n- inside position lost\n- retreat blocked by boundary\n\n## Stable vs Transitional State\nSynochi should distinguish between an established position and a transition.\n\nExample:\n\nHalf guard → knee-cut transition → hip clearance → side control\n\nThe middle phase should not be forced into either endpoint.\n\nTransitions are states in their own right.\n\nThis matters because many decisive technical errors occur during transitions rather than settled positions.\n\n## Hierarchical Recognition\nPosition should be represented hierarchically.\n\nExample:\n\nGrounded  \n→ Guard  \n→ Half Guard  \n→ Top Position  \n→ Chest-to-chest  \n→ Crossface present  \n→ Bottom athlete flattened\n\nThis allows Synochi to preserve useful information even when visual confidence is incomplete.\n\n## Confidence Degradation\nIf visibility is limited, Synochi should reduce specificity rather than invent detail.\n\nHigh confidence:\nTop half guard, bottom athlete flattened with crossface and underhook control.\n\nMedium confidence:\nTop half guard with likely upper-body control.\n\nLow confidence:\nGrounded guard exchange; exact configuration unclear.\n\n## Temporal Stability\nA positional state should usually persist across multiple frames.\n\nSynochi should avoid changing state labels because of temporary visual noise.\n\n**Frames → Candidate state → Temporal consistency → Confirmed state**\n\nA short movement does not necessarily mean the position has changed.\n\n## Diagnostic Value\nCorrect positional recognition allows Synochi to move beyond surface-level commentary.\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"You got passed.\"\n\nSynochi can identify:\n\n\"Once your near shoulder was flattened and your inside frame disappeared, your hip rotation became unavailable and the pass became much harder to stop.\"\n\n## Synochi Function\nPositional state acts as the latent structural context for everything that follows.\n\n**Visual input → Positional hypothesis → Structural features → Confidence → State confirmation → Transition detection → Updated state**\n\nTechnique recognition should sit on top of this layer, not replace it.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Combat Video Observation Schema]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Recovery Opportunity Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Recovery Opportunity Detection","body":"# Recovery Opportunity Detection\n\n## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nRecovery Opportunity Detection is Synochi’s ability to identify moments where an athlete can meaningfully restore structure, reduce constraint, regain initiative, or return to a more favourable state after deterioration has already begun.\n\nA recovery opportunity is not necessarily a full escape.\n\nIt is any viable moment where the athlete can improve the state of the exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\nDo not treat disadvantage as binary.\n\nBad positions often contain temporary recovery windows.\n\n**Deterioration → Recovery window → Restored option-space → Improved state**\n\n## Why It Matters\nCombat analysis can become too outcome-focused.\n\nAn athlete may lose a position but still have several meaningful chances to recover before the final outcome.\n\nExample:\n\nCrossface conceded  \n→ frame reappears  \n→ hip space opens  \n→ knee can recover  \n→ guard can potentially return\n\nIf Synochi only tracks the final pass, it misses the athlete’s best recovery moment.\n\n## Recovery Opportunity Components\n\n### Current Constraint State\nWhat is currently limiting the athlete?\n\nExamples:\n\n- flattened shoulders\n- trapped knee\n- lost inside position\n- compromised posture\n- isolated limb\n\n### Release Event\nWhat changed to create recovery potential?\n\nExamples:\n\n- opponent posts\n- grip loosens\n- weight shifts\n- head pressure reduces\n- knee line reopens\n- attachment breaks\n\n### Restorable Structure\nWhat can be recovered?\n\nExamples:\n\n- frame\n- base\n- posture\n- inside position\n- hip mobility\n- elbow-knee connection\n- head position\n\n### Recovery Direction\nWhere can the athlete realistically move from here?\n\nExamples:\n\n- re-guard\n- stand\n- turn in\n- turn away\n- re-pummel\n- disengage\n- counter-attack\n\n## Recovery Window\nRecovery opportunities are often temporary.\n\nThe window may close when:\n\n- opponent reconnects control\n- weight settles\n- second constraint is added\n- limb becomes isolated\n- angle is lost\n- boundary removes movement\n\nSynochi should therefore distinguish:\n\n**recovery available**\n\nfrom\n\n**recovery still available**\n\n## Partial Recovery\nRecovery does not need to restore the original position.\n\nExamples:\n\nMount → half guard\n\nBack control → turtle\n\nSide control → seated guard\n\nFront headlock → neutral standing\n\nThese may still represent successful recovery because the athlete restores meaningful option-space.\n\n## Recovery Hierarchy\nA useful recovery sequence may be:\n\n**Survive → Remove immediate threat → Restore structure → Regain movement → Recover position → Regain initiative**\n\nSynochi should not skip stages.\n\nAn athlete under a deep submission threat should not be criticised for failing to immediately reverse position.\n\n## Recovery Quality\nSynochi should assess whether a recovery action:\n\n- removes the highest-value constraint\n- restores multiple options\n- only delays deterioration\n- creates a counter-threat\n- exposes a new vulnerability\n- resets the exchange\n\n## Missed Recovery Opportunity\nA missed recovery opportunity occurs when:\n\n- the window was meaningfully available\n- the athlete had a viable action\n- the opportunity disappeared\n- no equivalent improvement occurred\n\nThis should remain probabilistic where visual evidence is incomplete.\n\n## False Recovery\nSome movements appear to improve position but do not restore meaningful control.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete creates temporary hip space but remains crossfaced, flattened, and unable to recover inside position.\n\nMovement occurred.\n\nFunctional recovery did not.\n\nSynochi should judge recovery by restored action-space, not motion alone.\n\n## Recurrent Recovery Pattern\nAcross repeated footage, Synochi can identify whether an athlete:\n\n- recognises recovery windows early\n- waits until constraint accumulates\n- recovers structure but not initiative\n- repeatedly chooses low-value recovery directions\n- excels at creating secondary recovery opportunities\n\nThese patterns should inform the Athlete Model.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You nearly recovered guard.\"\n\nRecovery-aware analysis:\n\n\"When his weight shifted forward, you briefly regained hip movement and your knee line reopened. That was the highest-value recovery window; once his chest reconnected, the opportunity closed.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nRecovery Opportunity Detection helps Synochi identify:\n\n- where escapes should begin\n- which structure should be restored first\n- whether the athlete is recognising windows\n- whether recovery sequencing is correct\n- when a bad position was still salvageable\n\nThis makes defensive coaching more precise.\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should track recovery dynamically:\n\n**Detect deterioration → monitor constraint release → identify restored option-space → detect viable recovery actions → observe response → evaluate recovery quality → update state**\n\nOver time, Synochi can learn which recovery windows the athlete reliably sees, misses, or converts.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Error Point Identification]]\n[[Decision Window Recognition]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Repeated Pattern Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Repeated Pattern Detection","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nRepeated Pattern Detection identifies recurring behaviours, situations, outcomes or technical tendencies across multiple exchanges.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nRepeated evidence is generally more useful for coaching than a single isolated event.\n\n## Pattern Types\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Repeated guard loss\n- Repeated underhook loss\n- Same passing direction\n- Same successful takedown\n- Same failed submission\n- Consistent back exposure\n- Repeated hesitation\n- Persistent head-position loss\n- Consistent reaction to pressure\n- Repeated success from one control system\n\n## Pattern Requirements\n\nFRAME should consider:\n\n- Number of occurrences\n- Similarity between occurrences\n- Context\n- Opponent variation\n- Confidence of each observation\n\n## Avoid Premature Patterns\n\nOne occurrence is an observation.\n\nTwo may indicate a possible pattern.\n\nRepeated occurrences increase confidence.\n\nFRAME should avoid converting isolated actions into permanent Athlete Model traits.\n\n## Positive Patterns\n\nPattern detection should identify strengths as well as weaknesses.\n\nExample:\n\n\"You repeatedly regain inside position before completing your pass.\"\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nRepeated patterns can influence:\n\n- Athlete Model\n- Weekly Mission\n- Drill prescription\n- Technical priorities\n- Competition preparation\n- Coaching conversations\n\n## Related\n\n[[Success Failure and Outcome Tagging]]\n[[Technical Error Observation]]\n[[Sequence and Exchange Reconstruction]]\n[[Video Observation to Coaching Inference]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Resolution State Classification.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Resolution State Classification","body":"## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nResolution State Classification is Synochi’s ability to identify how an exchange segment ends and what functional state remains once the immediate sequence has stabilised.\n\nNot every sequence ends in a clean win or loss.\n\nSome exchanges resolve into:\n\n- improved position\n- degraded position\n- neutral reset\n- unresolved scramble\n- submission completion\n- failed attack\n- successful escape\n- stalled control\n\n## Core Principle\nThe end of an event should be classified by functional consequence, not only by visible movement.\n\n**Sequence → Resolution → Resulting state → Strategic consequence**\n\n## Why It Matters\nWithout resolution classification, Synochi may detect events but fail to understand their outcome.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete attempts a sweep.\n\nPossible resolutions:\n\n- sweep succeeds\n- sweep fails but creates top position later\n- sweep fails but forces opponent to post\n- sweep fails and worsens position\n- sweep is abandoned into neutral reset\n\nThese outcomes are materially different.\n\n## Resolution Classes\n\n### Positive Resolution\nThe athlete improves their functional state.\n\nExamples:\n\n- passes guard\n- escapes mount\n- secures takedown\n- takes back\n- restores neutral position\n\n### Negative Resolution\nThe athlete ends in a worse functional state.\n\nExamples:\n\n- gets passed\n- loses back exposure\n- concedes takedown\n- becomes isolated in submission\n\n### Neutral Resolution\nThe exchange returns to broadly equivalent conditions.\n\nExamples:\n\n- disengagement\n- standing reset\n- grip exchange ends without advantage\n- scramble returns to neutral\n\n### Partial Resolution\nThe exchange ends with incomplete advantage.\n\nExamples:\n\n- dominant grip but no positional change\n- partial back exposure\n- knee line cleared but pass not consolidated\n\n### Unresolved State\nThe sequence remains active without clear stabilisation.\n\nExample:\n\nongoing scramble at clip end.\n\nSynochi should not invent a resolution if none is visible.\n\n## Technical Resolution vs Strategic Resolution\nAn action may technically fail but strategically succeed.\n\nExample:\n\nGuillotine attempt fails.\n\nBut:\n\nopponent rolls  \n→ top position gained\n\nTechnical outcome:\nsubmission failed.\n\nStrategic outcome:\nposition improved.\n\nSynochi should preserve both.\n\n## Local vs Global Resolution\nA local exchange may resolve positively while the broader sequence remains negative.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete escapes back control into turtle.\n\nLocal resolution:\nsuccessful escape from immediate back control.\n\nGlobal state:\nstill disadvantaged.\n\nThis distinction prevents oversimplification.\n\n## Consolidation Requirement\nA positional gain should not necessarily count as fully resolved until control stabilises.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete clears guard momentarily but opponent immediately re-guards.\n\nThis may be classified as:\n\ntemporary advancement\n\nrather than:\n\ncompleted pass\n\nSynochi should use temporal stability before assigning final resolution.\n\n## Resolution Confidence\nClassification confidence should depend on:\n\n- visibility\n- duration of stabilisation\n- clarity of control\n- clip completeness\n- rule context\n- whether the exchange continues off-camera\n\nWhere uncertain, Synochi should reduce specificity.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"The sweep failed.\"\n\nResolution-aware analysis:\n\n\"The sweep did not produce top position directly, but it forced the post that opened the back-take sequence. Technically unsuccessful, strategically productive.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nResolution State Classification helps Synochi distinguish:\n\n- productive failed attacks\n- costly successful-looking actions\n- neutral exchanges\n- partial gains\n- true control consolidation\n\nThis improves both diagnosis and reinforcement.\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should resolve event segments through:\n\n**Detect event → Track transition → Observe stabilisation → Classify local outcome → Classify resulting state → Estimate strategic consequence**\n\nResolution should then feed the Athlete Model and intervention-learning loop.\n\n## Outcome Learning\nRepeated resolution patterns can reveal whether certain behaviours actually work for the athlete.\n\nExample:\n\nRepeated front-headlock entries may show:\n\nhigh entry success  \n→ low finish rate  \n→ strong back-take conversion\n\nSynochi should learn the functional value of the sequence rather than judging it only by the original intent.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Escalation Point Recognition]]\n[[Recovery Opportunity Detection]]\n[[Error Point Identification]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]\n[[Outcome-Linked Intervention Learning]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Sequence and Exchange Reconstruction.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Sequence and Exchange Reconstruction","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nSequence and Exchange Reconstruction combines individual observations into a coherent representation of a complete combat exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nFRAME should understand sequences as connected chains rather than collections of isolated techniques.\n\n## Example Structure\n\nInitial state  \n→ Athlete A initiates  \n→ Athlete B responds  \n→ Control changes  \n→ Athlete A adjusts  \n→ Position changes  \n→ Outcome occurs\n\n## Sequence Elements\n\nA reconstructed exchange may include:\n\n- Starting position\n- Initiator\n- Action\n- Opponent response\n- Counter\n- Positional transition\n- Control change\n- Technical error\n- Opportunity\n- Outcome\n- Final state\n\n## Missing Information\n\nIf footage is obscured or skipped, FRAME should explicitly preserve gaps.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Guard exchange → [detail obscured] → athlete emerges in half guard top.\"\n\nDo not invent missing actions.\n\n## Exchange Boundaries\n\nExchange reconstruction should rely on [[Event Boundary Detection]] to determine when one meaningful sequence ends and another begins.\n\n## Hierarchical Analysis\n\nFRAME may represent video at several levels:\n\nFrame-level observation  \n→ Action  \n→ Interaction  \n→ Exchange  \n→ Match/session pattern\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nSequence reconstruction allows FRAME to explain:\n\n- How a position was lost\n- How an attack developed\n- Why a transition succeeded\n- What preceded a recurring problem\n- What the athlete could train as a complete chain\n\n## Related\n\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]\n[[Initiation and Response Pairing]]\n[[Transition and Positional Change Detection]]\n[[Repeated Pattern Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Success Failure and Outcome Tagging.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Success Failure and Outcome Tagging","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nSuccess Failure and Outcome Tagging classifies the functional result of an observed action or exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nSuccess should be determined relative to the intended objective of the action.\n\nAn action can fail technically while still creating a useful secondary outcome.\n\n## Outcome Categories\n\nPossible labels include:\n\n- Successful\n- Partially successful\n- Failed\n- Neutral\n- Interrupted\n- Reversed\n- Countered\n- Unclear\n\n## Examples\n\nA takedown may:\n\n- Score cleanly\n- Force a scramble\n- Create front headlock control\n- Fail completely\n- Expose the attacker to a counter\n\nThese outcomes should not all be treated identically.\n\n## Primary and Secondary Outcomes\n\nFRAME should distinguish:\n\nPrimary outcome:\nDid the intended action succeed?\n\nSecondary outcome:\nWhat else did the action create?\n\nExample:\n\nA failed arm drag may still create back exposure.\n\n## Delayed Outcomes\n\nSome techniques create advantages that appear several actions later.\n\nFRAME should avoid judging every action solely by the immediate next frame.\n\n## Confidence\n\nOutcome tagging should inherit uncertainty from:\n\n- Occlusion\n- Missing footage\n- Poor camera angle\n- Ambiguous positional state\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nOutcome tagging enables:\n\n- Technique success-rate analysis\n- Pattern recognition\n- Identification of reliable weapons\n- Identification of repeated breakdowns\n- Better drill prescription\n\n## Related\n\n[[Initiation and Response Pairing]]\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]\n[[Sequence and Exchange Reconstruction]]\n[[Repeated Pattern Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Technical Error Observation.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Technical Error Observation","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nTechnical Error Observation identifies visible technical behaviours that plausibly contribute to an undesirable outcome.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nFRAME should distinguish between what was directly observed and what is inferred to have caused the outcome.\n\n## Observation Before Diagnosis\n\nPreferred structure:\n\nObserved:\n\"Your right elbow separated from your ribs.\"\n\nPossible consequence:\n\"This appears to have created space for the underhook.\"\n\nAvoid:\n\n\"You lost because your elbow was wrong.\"\n\nUnless the causal relationship is strongly supported.\n\n## Error Categories\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Posture breakdown\n- Loss of base\n- Poor alignment\n- Inside-position loss\n- Grip failure\n- Frame collapse\n- Head-position loss\n- Hip-position error\n- Timing error\n- Directional error\n- Weight-distribution error\n- Excessive space\n- Overextension\n- Failure to consolidate control\n\n## Tactical vs Technical Error\n\nFRAME should distinguish:\n\nTechnical:\nThe action was executed poorly.\n\nTactical:\nThe action itself may have been inappropriate for the situation.\n\n## No-Error Outcomes\n\nNot every loss means a clear mistake occurred.\n\nSometimes the opponent simply executed effectively.\n\nFRAME should be able to state:\n\n\"No clear technical error is visible from this angle.\"\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nTechnical Error Observation should feed into:\n\n- Drill recommendations\n- Coaching prompts\n- Pattern tracking\n- Athlete Model updates\n\n## Related\n\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]\n[[Opportunity Recognition]]\n[[Repeated Pattern Detection]]\n[[Video Observation to Coaching Inference]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Threat Recognition.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Threat Recognition","body":"## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nThreat Recognition is Synochi’s ability to identify when an athlete creates a credible possibility of positional advancement, control gain, scoring action, submission, strike, takedown, or forced defensive reaction.\n\nA threat is not defined only by whether it succeeds.\n\nA credible threat changes the opponent’s decision space.\n\n## Core Principle\nA threat matters when the opponent must account for it.\n\n**Threat introduced → options narrow → response required → downstream opportunity changes**\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany exchanges are shaped by attacks that never finish.\n\nA submission attempt may force an elbow position change.\n\nA takedown entry may force stance adjustment.\n\nA guard pass threat may make the opponent expose an underhook.\n\nIf Synochi only tracks completed outcomes, it misses the mechanism that created them.\n\n## Threat Components\n\n### Threat Source\nThe action or structural condition generating danger.\n\nExamples:\n\n- exposed neck\n- isolated arm\n- compromised base\n- hip line crossed\n- dominant angle\n- inside control\n- body lock\n- leg entanglement\n\n### Threat Target\nWhat is currently at risk.\n\nExamples:\n\n- submission\n- positional loss\n- takedown\n- back exposure\n- sweep\n- strike\n- pin\n- balance\n\n### Threat Credibility\nHow likely the threat is to require a meaningful defensive response.\n\nCredibility increases when:\n\n- structure is mechanically sound\n- relevant control is present\n- distance is correct\n- escape options are limited\n- attacker has positional support\n- the defender actually responds\n\n### Threat Urgency\nHow quickly the defender must react.\n\nThreats may be:\n\n- immediate\n- developing\n- latent\n\n## Latent Threats\nSome threats exist before an obvious attack begins.\n\nExample:\n\nAn athlete controls the wrist and elbow while maintaining dominant angle.\n\nNo armbar has been initiated yet.\n\nBut the structural conditions for one may already influence the defender’s movement.\n\nSynochi should recognise these as latent threats where confidence is sufficient.\n\n## Threat vs Attack\nAn attack is an action.\n\nA threat is the consequence that action creates in the opponent’s available decision space.\n\nAn attack can fail to create a credible threat.\n\nA threat can exist without a visibly committed attack.\n\n## Threat Stacking\nMultiple threats can exist simultaneously.\n\nExample:\n\nFront headlock position may threaten:\n\n- guillotine\n- back take\n- snapdown continuation\n- go-behind\n- positional control\n\nThe combination can make the exchange harder to defend than any single threat alone.\n\nSynochi should therefore detect threat families rather than forcing one predicted outcome.\n\n## Threat Conversion\nA threat can convert into another opportunity through the opponent’s defensive reaction.\n\nExample:\n\nArmbar threat  \n→ opponent clasps hands  \n→ posture compresses  \n→ back exposure increases\n\nThe original threat may disappear while still having created the next attack.\n\n## False Threats\nSynochi should distinguish credible threats from low-impact gestures.\n\nSignals of weak credibility may include:\n\n- poor structural connection\n- excessive distance\n- no meaningful opponent reaction\n- repeated attacks with no constraint\n- mechanically incomplete positioning\n\nActivity alone should not inflate threat confidence.\n\n## Defensive Behaviour as Evidence\nThe opponent’s reaction can provide evidence that a threat is meaningful.\n\nExamples:\n\n- immediate hand fighting\n- posture change\n- retreat\n- frame insertion\n- grip break\n- base widening\n- turning away\n\nHowever, reaction should not prove the attacker’s intent.\n\nIt only increases confidence that a meaningful threat was perceived.\n\n## Threat Timing\nThreat quality depends partly on when it appears.\n\nEarly threat:\ncreates future options.\n\nLate threat:\nmay occur only after positional control is already lost.\n\nSynochi should therefore consider threat timing relative to state transitions.\n\n## Diagnostic Example\nSurface analysis:\n\n\"You nearly got the choke.\"\n\nThreat-aware analysis:\n\n\"The choke itself did not finish, but it forced his hands to the neck, removed his post, and created the angle that let you take the back.\"\n\n## Coaching Value\nThreat Recognition helps Synochi understand:\n\n- why opponents react\n- how combinations emerge\n- which attacks create useful downstream effects\n- whether attacks are credible\n- where threat sequencing breaks down\n- whether athletes are generating enough offensive pressure\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should model threat dynamically:\n\n**Observe structure → detect possible danger → estimate credibility → observe defensive response → update threat confidence → detect conversion or resolution**\n\nThreats should be tracked as changing probabilities, not binary labels.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Initiative and Reaction Detection]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Transition State Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Transition State Detection","body":"## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nTransition State Detection is Synochi’s ability to recognise when an exchange is moving between established positional states rather than forcing every moment into a fixed positional label.\n\nA transition is not visual noise between two positions.\n\nIt is often the most information-dense part of the exchange.\n\n## Core Principle\nPositions describe where the athletes are.\n\nTransitions reveal how control is being gained, lost, redirected, or recovered.\n\n**State A → Transition → State B**\n\nThe transition should be modelled explicitly.\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany decisive moments in combat occur before the final position becomes obvious.\n\nExamples:\n\n- guard retention failing before the pass is completed\n- back exposure occurring before hooks are established\n- balance breaking before the takedown\n- inside position being lost before the opponent secures control\n- defensive structure collapsing before the submission becomes threatening\n\nIf Synochi only recognises settled positions, it identifies outcomes after the causal moment has already passed.\n\n## Transition Components\n\n### Origin State\nThe position immediately before meaningful structural change begins.\n\n### Trigger\nThe event that initiates the transition.\n\nExamples:\n\n- frame removed\n- grip established\n- angle changed\n- weight shifted\n- post removed\n- level changed\n- hip line cleared\n\n### Intermediate Configuration\nThe unstable phase between positions.\n\nThis may contain:\n\n- partial control\n- contested balance\n- temporary exposure\n- incomplete attachment\n- changing orientation\n- scrambling\n\n### Destination State\nThe position the exchange stabilises into.\n\n### Transition Outcome\nThe transition may result in:\n\n- successful advancement\n- successful recovery\n- reversal\n- reset\n- failed attack\n- failed defence\n- unresolved scramble\n\n## Commitment Threshold\nNot every movement should count as a transition.\n\nSynochi should look for structural commitment.\n\nA movement becomes transitionally meaningful when it changes one or more of:\n\n- control\n- orientation\n- base\n- attachment\n- positional hierarchy\n- available action-space\n- exposure\n- balance\n\n## Transitional Advantage\nAdvantage can emerge before the destination position is secured.\n\nExample:\n\nDuring a knee cut, the passer may not yet have side control, but if the bottom athlete’s knee line has been cleared and shoulders are controlled, the transition is already strongly biased toward the passer.\n\nSynochi should therefore distinguish:\n\n**current position**\n\nfrom\n\n**transition momentum**\n\n## Transition Momentum\nTransition momentum estimates which athlete currently has greater structural influence over the direction of the exchange.\n\nSignals may include:\n\n- dominant angle\n- control of head or hips\n- removal of frames\n- control of inside space\n- successful off-balancing\n- constrained defensive options\n- forced reactions\n\nMomentum is not the same as outcome certainty.\n\n## Failed Transitions\nA failed transition remains diagnostically useful.\n\nExample:\n\nAthlete begins a guard pass, clears the knee line, but loses upper-body control and opponent recovers guard.\n\nThe correct interpretation is not simply:\n\n\"Guard retained.\"\n\nThe useful sequence is:\n\n**Pass initiated → knee line cleared → upper-body connection lost → guard recovered**\n\nThis exposes the actual failure point.\n\n## Temporal Segmentation\nSynochi should segment transitions around meaningful structural change.\n\nPossible sequence:\n\n**Stable state → transition initiation → transition development → critical decision point → resolution → new stable state**\n\nThis creates more useful coaching anchors than arbitrary timestamps.\n\n## Critical Decision Point\nWithin a transition, Synochi should attempt to identify the moment where available outcomes narrow significantly.\n\nThis may be:\n\n- the last viable frame\n- loss of a post\n- head position conceded\n- hip line crossed\n- elbow separated\n- balance broken\n- attachment lost\n\nThese moments often carry greater coaching value than the final outcome.\n\n## Confidence\nTransition detection should inherit uncertainty from the underlying visual evidence.\n\nHigh confidence:\nClear change from seated guard into completed single-leg entry.\n\nMedium confidence:\nLikely transition toward a single-leg, but lower-body connection partially obscured.\n\nLow confidence:\nScramble detected; exact transition path unclear.\n\n## Coaching Value\nTransition analysis shifts feedback from:\n\n\"You ended up in a bad position.\"\n\nto:\n\n\"The position became difficult three seconds earlier when your inside frame disappeared and your hips were turned away.\"\n\nThis moves coaching closer to causality.\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should maintain a continuously updating transition model:\n\n**Stable state → structural change → transition hypothesis → momentum estimate → critical event → resolution → updated positional state**\n\nThis allows video analysis to understand not only what positions occurred, but how one state became another.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Combat Video Observation Schema]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Transition and Positional Change Detection.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Transition and Positional Change Detection","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nTransition and Positional Change Detection identifies meaningful changes from one combat state to another.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nMany of the most important technical events occur between recognised positions rather than inside static positions.\n\nVideo analysis must therefore model transitions explicitly.\n\n## Examples\n\n- Standing → takedown\n- Guard → passing engagement\n- Half guard → side control\n- Side control → mount\n- Mount → back exposure\n- Turtle → front headlock\n- Guard → leg entanglement\n- Failed pass → guard recovery\n- Submission attempt → positional loss\n\n## Transition Components\n\nA transition may contain:\n\n1. Initial state\n2. Trigger\n3. Movement or technical action\n4. Control change\n5. New state\n6. Outcome\n\n## Transition Completion\n\nFRAME should distinguish between:\n\n- Attempted transition\n- Partial transition\n- Completed transition\n- Reversed transition\n- Interrupted transition\n\n## Positional Stability\n\nA new position should not automatically be considered established the instant it appears.\n\nFRAME should consider whether the athlete has:\n\n- Stabilised their base\n- Secured relevant control\n- Prevented immediate reversal\n- Maintained the state for sufficient time or functional control\n\n## Coaching Relevance\n\nTransition detection enables FRAME to find:\n\n- Where positional losses occur\n- How athletes enter dominant positions\n- Where scrambles repeatedly emerge\n- Whether transitions are controlled or chaotic\n- Which stages of an exchange need drilling\n\n## Related\n\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]\n[[Position and State Recognition]]\n[[Control Relationship Detection]]\n[[Sequence and Exchange Reconstruction]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Video Observation to Coaching Inference.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Video Observation to Coaching Inference","body":"[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\n\nVideo Observation to Coaching Inference governs how raw visual observations are converted into coaching-relevant conclusions and recommendations.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nFRAME must separate:\n\nObservation → Interpretation → Coaching Recommendation\n\nThese are different levels of certainty.\n\n## Layer 1 — Observation\n\nWhat is visibly supported?\n\nExample:\n\n\"Your left knee moved away from your opponent's hip.\"\n\n## Layer 2 — Interpretation\n\nWhat may this mean?\n\nExample:\n\n\"This appears to have created additional space for their guard recovery.\"\n\n## Layer 3 — Coaching Recommendation\n\nWhat should the athlete do with that information?\n\nExample:\n\n\"Consider drilling hip-control maintenance during the final phase of the pass.\"\n\n## Confidence Propagation\n\nA low-confidence observation should not produce a high-confidence coaching conclusion.\n\nUncertainty must propagate through the reasoning chain.\n\n## Recommendation Threshold\n\nFRAME should only prescribe a correction when:\n\n- The observation is sufficiently clear\n- The interpretation is technically plausible\n- The recommendation fits the athlete's context\n- The issue is meaningful enough to act on\n\n## Possible Outputs\n\nVideo observations may produce:\n\n- Technical feedback\n- Tactical feedback\n- Drill recommendation\n- Reflection prompt\n- Athlete Model update\n- Pattern flag\n- Competition note\n- No recommendation\n\n## No Forced Coaching\n\nSometimes the correct output is simply an observation.\n\nNot every visible event needs a correction.\n\n## Athlete Context\n\nRecommendations should consider:\n\n- Athlete level\n- Known game\n- Goals\n- Competition rules\n- Existing Athlete Model\n- Previous patterns\n- Physical constraints\n- Training phase\n\n## Functional Output\n\nUseful video observations should ideally lead toward one or more actions:\n\n- Work on this\n- Build a drill\n- Ask FRAME about this position\n- Review similar exchanges\n- Add to competition preparation\n- Monitor as a recurring pattern\n\n## Related\n\n[[Combat Video Observation Schema]]\n[[Visual Confidence Grading]]\n[[Technical Error Observation]]\n[[Opportunity Recognition]]\n[[Repeated Pattern Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Visual Confidence Grading.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Visual Confidence Grading","body":"## Purpose\n\nThis node defines how Synochi should judge the reliability of what it believes it can see in combat footage.\n\nVideo analysis should never treat unclear visual evidence as certain.\n\n## Core Principle\n\nBefore Synochi reasons from an observation, it should estimate how confidently that observation can actually be made.\n\nConfidence should reflect the quality of the visual evidence, not how plausible the interpretation feels.\n\n## Confidence Factors\n\nSynochi should consider:\n\n- Camera angle\n- Image quality\n- Distance from action\n- Motion blur\n- Obstruction\n- Lighting\n- Whether relevant limbs are visible\n- Whether grips or contact points can be seen\n- Whether the full sequence is captured\n- Whether the action occurs partly outside frame\n\n## Confidence Levels\n\n### High Confidence\n\nThe relevant action is clearly visible.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Takedown entry is fully visible\n- Opponent clearly posts with right hand\n- Guard pass completion is unobstructed\n- Submission finish is visually clear\n\n### Moderate Confidence\n\nThe main action is visible but some important detail is partially obscured.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Grip appears likely but hand placement is partly hidden\n- Hip position is visible but leg configuration is unclear\n- Transition is visible but initiation is partially missed\n\n### Low Confidence\n\nImportant information is missing or ambiguous.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Bodies obscure the relevant grip\n- Camera angle hides knee position\n- Exchange moves out of frame\n- Clip begins after the important setup\n- Motion blur prevents reliable identification\n\n## Unknown State\n\nWhen evidence is insufficient, Synochi should explicitly preserve:\n\nUnknown\n\nrather than selecting the most likely answer.\n\nUnknown is valid information.\n\n## Observation Rule\n\nConfidence should attach to the observation itself.\n\nExample:\n\nObservation:\nOpponent appears to establish an underhook.\n\nConfidence:\nModerate.\n\nInterpretation:\nThis may contribute to the positional reversal.\n\nThe interpretation should not carry greater certainty than the observation supporting it.\n\n## Confidence Propagation\n\nLow-confidence observations should reduce confidence in downstream reasoning.\n\nLow-confidence observation\n→ weaker pattern evidence\n→ weaker diagnosis\n→ less prescriptive intervention\n\nSynochi should not allow uncertain visual input to become a high-confidence coaching conclusion.\n\n## Multiple Evidence Sources\n\nConfidence may increase when video evidence aligns with:\n\n- Athlete report\n- Coach report\n- Repeated footage\n- Another camera angle\n- Similar events from other sessions\n\nHowever, additional evidence should support rather than overwrite what the footage itself shows.\n\n## False Precision\n\nSynochi should avoid overly precise claims when footage does not justify them.\n\nPrefer:\n\n“The athlete appears to lose inside position during the transition.”\n\nover:\n\n“The athlete loses the left underhook exactly because of a 20-degree hip-angle error.”\n\nunless the visual evidence genuinely supports that level of specificity.\n\n## Confidence and Feedback\n\nWhen confidence is low, Synochi may:\n\n- Ask for another angle\n- Request a longer clip\n- Ask the athlete what they felt\n- Present multiple plausible explanations\n- Avoid prescribing a specific technical correction\n\n## Design Objective\n\nSynochi should remain useful without pretending to see more than the footage actually reveals.\n\nThe goal is calibrated visual intelligence:\n\nClear evidence\n→ strong confidence\n\nAmbiguous evidence\n→ preserved uncertainty\n\n## Links\n\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n[[Combat Video Observation Schema]]\n\n[[Observation vs Inference]]\n\n[[Event Boundary Detection]]"},{"path":"COMBAT INTELLIGENCE/Video Observation Ontology/Weight Distribution Inference.md","folder":"Video Observation Ontology","title":"Weight Distribution Inference","body":"# Weight Distribution Inference\n\n## Type\nVideo Observation Ontology\n\n## Domain\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n\n## Definition\nWeight Distribution Inference is Synochi’s ability to estimate how an athlete’s body mass and pressure are likely distributed across their base, contact points, limbs, and opponent from visible posture and movement.\n\nThe system does not directly measure force.\n\nIt infers probable loading from observable structure.\n\n## Core Principle\nPosture reveals likely pressure pathways.\n\n**Body position → Base relationship → Contact structure → Probable loading → Available movement**\n\nWeight placement should therefore be treated as a probabilistic mechanical inference rather than a visually certain fact.\n\n## Why It Matters\nMany combat actions depend less on nominal position than on where weight is actually loaded.\n\nExamples:\n\n- a leg may be visually planted but lightly weighted\n- a hand may be posted because balance depends on it\n- hips may be high but pressure may still be transferred through the chest\n- an opponent may appear stable while most weight is committed to one side\n\nThese distinctions affect:\n\n- takedowns\n- sweeps\n- guard passing\n- escapes\n- pins\n- scrambles\n- submissions\n- striking balance\n\n## Observable Indicators\n\n### Centre of Mass Projection\nSynochi should estimate where the athlete’s centre of mass is likely projecting relative to the base.\n\nSignals include:\n\n- torso lean\n- hip position\n- head position\n- shoulder alignment\n- knee bend\n- foot placement\n\n### Base Width\nA wider or narrower base changes likely loading and balance options.\n\n### Joint Compression\nGreater flexion at the ankle, knee, hip, elbow, or shoulder may indicate increased loading through that structure.\n\n### Post Dependence\nIf removal or movement of a limb causes immediate balance correction, that limb was likely carrying meaningful load.\n\n### Contact Density\nMultiple strong contact points may distribute weight.\n\nFew contact points may concentrate it.\n\n### Opponent Deformation\nCompression, flattening, head displacement, or restricted hip motion may indicate pressure transfer into the opponent.\n\n### Recovery Movement\nHow quickly a limb can move may reveal whether it was heavily or lightly weighted.\n\n## Weight-Bearing Limb Detection\nSynochi should estimate which limbs are currently:\n\n- heavily loaded\n- moderately loaded\n- lightly loaded\n- unweighted\n- transitional\n\nExample:\n\nDuring a standing exchange, an opponent’s rear leg may appear planted but become rapidly movable.\n\nThis may indicate low loading despite visual contact with the mat.\n\n## Upper vs Lower Body Loading\nWeight distribution should not be reduced to feet alone.\n\nIn grappling, load may pass through:\n\n- knees\n- elbows\n- hands\n- chest\n- shoulder\n- hips\n- head\n- opponent contact\n\nExample:\n\nA passer may have both knees on the mat while most effective pressure travels through shoulder and chest connection.\n\n## Directional Loading\nSynochi should estimate not only where weight is placed but in which direction pressure is being applied.\n\nPossible directions:\n\n- forward\n- backward\n- lateral\n- rotational\n- downward\n- diagonally through the opponent\n\nDirectional loading can determine which movements are available or structurally expensive.\n\n## Dynamic Weight Shift\nWeight distribution changes continuously.\n\nSynochi should track:\n\n**initial loading → shift → redistribution → resulting action**\n\nExamples:\n\n- weight shifts forward before snapdown\n- weight transfers onto lead leg before shot\n- hips shift laterally before knee cut\n- pressure transfers across chest before mount transition\n\nThese shifts may reveal intent or opportunity, but intent should remain inferential.\n\n## Asymmetric Loading\nMany useful combat states are asymmetrical.\n\nExamples:\n\n- majority weight on one hip\n- one shoulder loaded more heavily\n- one leg acting as primary base\n- opponent pinned more heavily on one side\n\nAsymmetry often creates predictable light sides and heavy sides.\n\n## Light-Side Detection\nWhere weight is absent can be as useful as where it is present.\n\nA lightly loaded limb may be easier to:\n\n- lift\n- reap\n- pummel around\n- drag\n- redirect\n- remove from base\n\nSynochi should therefore identify both:\n\n**loaded structures**\n\nand\n\n**available structures**\n\n## Pressure Inference\nPressure should be inferred from the combination of:\n\n- body alignment\n- contact surface\n- force direction\n- opponent compression\n- movement restriction\n- persistence over time\n\nVisual closeness alone does not prove meaningful pressure.\n\n## Confidence Handling\nWeight inference should carry confidence levels.\n\nHigh confidence:\nClear unilateral stance with visible centre-of-mass shift over lead leg.\n\nMedium confidence:\nLikely greater loading through left hip based on torso angle and base.\n\nLow confidence:\nExact distribution unclear due to camera angle or occlusion.\n\nSynochi should avoid claims such as:\n\n\"80% of the weight is on the right leg\"\n\nunless actual sensor data exists.\n\n## Interaction With Technique\nTechnique interpretation should use inferred loading.\n\nExample:\n\nInstead of:\n\n\"He could have swept here.\"\n\nSynochi may reason:\n\n\"His opponent’s weight appears committed over the far knee, making the near-side post comparatively light and potentially available to remove.\"\n\nThis produces mechanically grounded analysis.\n\n## Diagnostic Value\nWeight Distribution Inference can help explain:\n\n- why a sweep succeeded\n- why a takedown failed\n- why a post could not move\n- why an escape direction was unavailable\n- why pressure felt structurally dominant\n- why a transition became possible\n\n## Synochi Function\nSynochi should infer weight distribution dynamically:\n\n**Observe posture → Identify base → Estimate centre-of-mass projection → Detect loaded contact points → Infer directional pressure → Track weight shift → Update mechanical possibilities**\n\nThe result should modify the current positional, constraint, threat, and decision models.\n\n## Links\n[[Combat Intelligence Hub]]\n[[Positional State Recognition]]\n[[Constraint Recognition]]\n[[Control Change Detection]]\n[[Transition State Detection]]\n[[Decision Window Recognition]]\n[[Observation vs Interpretation Separation]]"},{"path":"Recognition Decision Drill.md","folder":"Drill Templates","title":"Recognition Decision Drill","body":"# Recognition / Decision Drill\n\n## Purpose\nDevelop the ability to recognise relevant information and select an appropriate action under uncertainty.\n\n## Training Target\nAttention, cue recognition, timing, option selection and adaptation.\n\nEvaluate decisions using the information available when the action was selected, alongside the resulting execution and outcome.\n\n## Setup\nDefine:\n- The sport and applicable ruleset.\n- The tactical problem.\n- Starting posture, distance, contact and boundaries.\n- The available action options.\n- The opponent’s permitted behaviours.\n- Relevant cues and opportunities.\n- Success conditions and reset points.\n- Equipment and contact intensity.\n\nBegin with a manageable number of meaningful options. Include withholding action when no suitable opportunity exists.\n\n## Task\nThe athlete observes the opponent and situation, identifies an opportunity or threat and selects an appropriate response.\n\nThe partner varies their behaviour within the defined task without announcing the answer.\n\nAllow the exchange to continue far enough to reveal whether the selected action was viable.\n\n## Information and Resistance\nUse information that is available in the sport:\n- Distance and angle.\n- Posture and balance.\n- Grip, contact and pressure.\n- Movement direction and attacking commitment.\n- Openings, barriers and available space.\n\nDistinguish advance cues from information that appears only after commitment.\n\nArtificial signals may simplify an introductory task, but transfer must be assessed using opponent-generated information.\n\n## Constraints\nAdjust:\n- Number of available options.\n- Variety of opponent behaviours.\n- Time available to act.\n- Starting conditions.\n- Opponent resistance.\n- Permitted attacks and defensive actions.\n- Environmental boundaries.\n\nAvoid prescribing one universal response to a cue when several actions may be tactically valid.\n\n## Dosage\nUse brief exchanges with repeated opportunities to decide.\n\nAllow enough rest to preserve attention. Increase time pressure or fatigue only when it serves the intended training target.\n\nReview selected exchanges between sets without interrupting every attempt.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- Relevant information is recognised.\n- The selected action fits the opportunity, threat and ruleset.\n- Action occurs while the opportunity remains usable.\n- Commitment is proportionate to the available information.\n- The athlete can withhold, abandon or change an action appropriately.\n- Decisions remain adaptable rather than dependent on a fixed sequence.\n\nTrack decision quality separately from execution quality and final outcome.\n\n## Progression\n1. Distinguish between a small number of clear opponent behaviours.\n2. Vary the timing and starting conditions.\n3. Add plausible options and less obvious cues.\n4. Allow the opponent to disguise intentions.\n5. Introduce continuation and changing opportunities.\n6. Assess decisions within representative live exchanges.\n\nPreserve readable information. Difficulty should come from a meaningful problem rather than arbitrary guessing.\n\n## Regression\nReduce options, slow the exchange or make relevant information easier to perceive.\n\nRestore predictable starting conditions or reduce physical resistance while retaining the need to choose.\n\n## Diagnostic Use\nDistinguish between:\n- Failure to notice relevant information.\n- Misinterpretation of a cue.\n- Selection of an unsuitable action.\n- Delayed commitment.\n- Appropriate selection followed by poor execution.\n- Failure to update when the situation changes.\n\nA failed outcome does not automatically indicate a poor decision. A successful outcome does not automatically validate the decision.\n\n## Sport Adaptation\nPreserve the shared function while adapting the decision problem:\n- Grappling: select an entry, control, escape or transition from changes in grip, pressure and position.\n- Striking: select an attack, defence, angle change or disengagement from distance, movement and commitment.\n- Mixed combat: recognise opportunities and threats within and between striking, clinch and ground phases.\n\nUse the discipline’s rules, scoring and tactical priorities to determine which choices are appropriate.\n\n## Synochi Selection Logic\nSelect when an athlete can execute skills in predictable practice but chooses poorly, hesitates or misses opportunities during live exchanges.\n\nIdentify whether the main limitation concerns recognition, interpretation, selection, timing or execution before prescribing the task.\n\nAssess transfer through representative opponent behaviour rather than improved performance on artificial signals alone.\n\n## Safety\nDefine contact intensity, prohibited actions and stopping conditions.\n\nMatch speed and uncertainty to the athlete’s ability to defend safely.\n\nDo not combine unfamiliar decisions with uncontrolled contact or submission pressure.\n\n## Links\n- [[Entry Setup Drill]] — recognise when entry conditions are available.\n- [[Escape Recovery Drill]] — select recovery actions according to the developing threat.\n- [[Retention Stabilisation Drill]] — identify disruption and choose an appropriate adjustment.\n- [[Transition Drill]] — update decisions as the exchange changes.\n- [[Progressive-Resistance Drill]] — scale opposition while preserving meaningful choices.\n- [[Individualised Constrained Live Rounds]] — assess decision-making against the athlete’s recurring live problem."}];
